const DAYS = {"1":{"day":1,"week":"01","themeRu":"Whose book?","themeEn":"Кни́га бра́та","intro":"Welcome back — and congratulations: you're not a beginner anymore. In A1 you met some of Russian's cases in disguise, hidden inside ready phrases like <b>у меня́</b> and <b>нет вре́мени</b>. This month we lift the mask and turn them into a system you can steer. We begin with the case you'll reach for most — the <b>genitive</b>, the “of” case. And who better to start with than Эмма, back in Moscow for a whole new chapter.","introAudio":"Welcome back — and congratulations: you're not a beginner anymore. In A1 you met some of Russian's cases in disguise, hidden inside ready phrases like u menya and nyet vryemeni. This month we lift the mask and turn them into a system you can steer. We begin with the case you'll reach for most — the genitive, the of-case. And who better to start with than Emma, back in Moscow for a whole new chapter.","goals":["Say whose something is: <b>кни́га бра́та</b> — “the brother's book”, owner in the genitive","Form the genitive singular: he/it-words take <b>-а/-я</b>, she-words take <b>-ы/-и</b>","Ask <b>Чей? Чья? Чьё? Чьи?</b> — “whose?” — matching the thing, not the owner","Link any two things with “of”: <b>центр Москвы́</b>, <b>ча́шка ча́я</b>"],"learned":["Said whose something is: <b>кни́га бра́та</b>, with the owner in the genitive","Formed the genitive singular: <b>-а/-я</b> for he/it-words, <b>-ы/-и</b> for she-words","Asked <b>Чей? Чья? Чьё? Чьи?</b>, matching the thing","Linked two nouns with “of”: <b>центр Москвы́</b>, <b>ча́шка ча́я</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Before the new level, a one-minute warm-up on the Russian you already own — the present tense, the past, and “I have”. In a moment the genitive will grow straight out of that last one.","introAudio":"Before the new level, a one-minute warm-up on the Russian you already own — the present tense, the past, and I have. In a moment the genitive will grow straight out of that last one.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is all A1 — it should feel like greeting an old friend.","q":"“I read Russian books” — pick the natural sentence:","opts":["Я чита́ю ру́сские кни́ги.","Я чита́ть ру́сские кни́ги.","Я чита́ю ру́сский кни́ги."],"answer":0,"explain":"Conjugated <b>чита́ю</b> (not the infinitive чита́ть), and the adjective agrees in the plural: <b>ру́сские кни́ги</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"A woman says “Yesterday I was at home.”","opts":["Вчера́ я был до́ма.","Вчера́ я была́ до́ма.","Вчера́ я бу́ду до́ма."],"answer":1,"explain":"Past tense marks the speaker's gender: a woman says <b>была́</b>. Был is the man's form; бу́ду is the future."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I have a brother.”</b>","want":"У меня́ есть брат","answer":"У меня́ есть брат"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The genitive of possession","title":"Whose? <em>Кни́га бра́та</em> — “the brother's book”","paras":["To say <em>the brother's book</em>, Russian says <b>кни́га бра́та</b> — literally “book of-brother”. The thing comes first, the owner second, and the owner changes its ending: брат → бра́т<b>а</b>. No apostrophe, no separate little word for “of” — the ending does the whole job.","English hands you two tools for this — <em>the brother's book</em> and <em>the book of the brother</em>. Russian has one pattern for both, and the owner always lands in the <b>genitive</b>.","So the owner's name or noun takes a new tail. брат → бра́та, Ива́н → Ива́на, ма́ма → ма́мы, Ле́на → Ле́ны. The exact tail depends on whether the owner is a he/it-word or a she-word — that's the next rule."],"audio":"To say the brother's book, Russian says kniga brata — literally, book of-brother. The thing comes first, the owner second, and the owner changes its ending: brat becomes brata. No apostrophe, no separate little word for of — the ending does the whole job. English hands you two tools — the brother's book and the book of the brother. Russian has one pattern for both, and the owner always lands in the genitive. So the owner takes a new tail: brat becomes brata, Ivan becomes Ivana, mama becomes mamy, Lena becomes Leny.","table":{"rows":[["кни́га бра́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга брата\">🔊</button>","the brother's book"],["маши́на Ива́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"машина Ивана\">🔊</button>","Ivan's car"],["дом ма́мы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом мамы\">🔊</button>","Mum's house"],["телефо́н Ле́ны <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"телефон Лены\">🔊</button>","Lena's phone"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Whose book is this? — It's the brother's book.","en":"Чья э́то кни́га? — Э́то кни́га <b>бра́та</b>.","say":"Чья это книга? Это книга брата."},{"ru":"This is Ivan's car.","en":"Э́то маши́на <b>Ива́на</b>.","say":"Это машина Ивана."},{"ru":"Where is Lena's phone?","en":"Где телефо́н <b>Ле́ны</b>?","say":"Где телефон Лены?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"брат кни́га","right":"кни́га бра́та","why":"English word order (“brother book”) can't carry it. Russian puts the thing first and bends the owner: <b>кни́га бра́та</b>."},{"wrong":"Э́то кни́га брат.","right":"Э́то кни́га бра́та.","why":"Leaving брат in its plain form loses the “of”. The owner MUST step into the genitive: бра́т<b>а</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"How do you say “Ivan's car”?","opts":["Ива́н маши́на","маши́на Ива́на","маши́на Ива́н"],"answer":1,"explain":"Thing first, owner second and bent: <b>маши́на Ива́на</b>. Ива́н → Ива́на."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Mum's house”</b>","want":"дом ма́мы","answer":"дом ма́мы"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “Lena's phone” in Russian:","accept":["телефон лены","телефон лены."],"placeholder":"телефон…","explain":"телефо́н Ле́ны — Ле́на becomes Ле́ны in the genitive."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The endings","title":"He/it-words take <em>-а/-я</em>, she-words take <em>-ы/-и</em>","paras":["Which tail the owner takes depends on its gender — the он/она́/оно́ you learned in A1. <b>He-words and it-words</b> (masculine and neuter) take <b>-а</b> (or -я after a soft letter). <b>She-words</b> (feminine) swap their -а/-я for <b>-ы</b> (or -и).","One spelling habit carries over from A1: after <b>г, к, х, ж, ш, щ, ч</b> you write <b>-и</b>, never -ы. So кни́га → кни́г<b>и</b>, not “книгы”.","Read the table aloud once. You're not memorising a grid — you're training your ear to expect that little tail on the owner."],"audio":"Which tail the owner takes depends on its gender — the on, ona, ono you learned in A1. He-words and it-words, masculine and neuter, take -a, or -ya after a soft letter. She-words, feminine, swap their ending for -y, or -i. One spelling habit carries over from A1: after g, k, kh, zh, sh, shch, ch you write -i, never -y. So kniga becomes knigi, not knigy. Read the table aloud once — you're training your ear to expect that little tail on the owner.","table":{"rows":[["брат → бра́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брат, брата\">🔊</button>","brother → brother's (he-word, -а)"],["ме́сто → ме́ста <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"место, места\">🔊</button>","place → of the place (it-word, -а)"],["кни́га → кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга, книги\">🔊</button>","book → book's (she-word, -и after г)"],["Москва́ → Москвы́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Москва, Москвы\">🔊</button>","Moscow → of Moscow (she-word, -ы)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"the centre of the city","en":"центр <b>го́рода</b>","say":"центр города","gloss":"го́род → го́рода (he-word)"},{"ru":"the sister's room","en":"ко́мната <b>сестры́</b>","say":"комната сестры","gloss":"сестра́ → сестры́ (she-word)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"кни́га ма́ма","right":"кни́га ма́мы","why":"ма́ма is a she-word: its -а becomes <b>-ы</b>. кни́га ма́мы — “Mum's book”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“The sister's house” — the sister's ending is…","opts":["дом сестра́","дом сестре́","дом сестры́"],"answer":2,"explain":"сестра́ is a she-word → <b>сестры́</b>. Дом сестры́."},{"type":"choice","q":"Why is it кни́г<b>и</b> and never “книгы”?","opts":["Because кни́га is a he-word","Because after г you write -и, not -ы","Because it is plural"],"answer":1,"explain":"The A1 spelling rule: after г, к, х, ж, ш, щ, ч always -и. So кни́ги."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “the centre of the city” (го́род → …):","accept":["центр города","центр города."],"placeholder":"центр…","explain":"го́род is a he-word → го́рода. Центр го́рода."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Asking “whose?”","title":"<em>Чей? Чья? Чьё? Чьи?</em> — four for one “whose”","paras":["“Whose?” has four shapes in Russian, and — just like <b>мой</b> in A1 — it agrees with the <b>thing</b>, not with the owner. A he-word thing takes <b>чей</b>, a she-word <b>чья</b>, an it-word <b>чьё</b>, and any plural <b>чьи</b>.","To answer, you just name the owner in the genitive: <b>Чья э́то кни́га? — Э́то кни́га Ле́ны.</b> Question and answer click together.","Notice: чей looks at the thing being asked about (кни́га is a she-word → чья), never at whose it might turn out to be."],"audio":"Whose has four shapes in Russian, and — just like moy in A1 — it agrees with the thing, not with the owner. A he-word thing takes chey, a she-word chya, an it-word chyo, and any plural chyi. To answer, you name the owner in the genitive: chya eto kniga? Eto kniga Leny. Question and answer click together. Chey looks at the thing being asked about, never at whose it might turn out to be.","table":{"rows":[["Чей э́то телефо́н? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Чей это телефон?\">🔊</button>","Whose phone is this? (he-word)"],["Чья э́то кни́га? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Чья это книга?\">🔊</button>","Whose book is this? (she-word)"],["Чьё э́то письмо́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Чьё это письмо?\">🔊</button>","Whose letter is this? (it-word)"],["Чьи э́то ключи́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Чьи это ключи?\">🔊</button>","Whose keys are these? (plural)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Whose car is this? — It's Ivan's.","en":"<b>Чья</b> э́то маши́на? — Э́то маши́на Ива́на.","say":"Чья это машина? Это машина Ивана."},{"ru":"Whose keys are these? — Lena's.","en":"<b>Чьи</b> э́то ключи́? — Ле́ны.","say":"Чьи это ключи? Лены."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чей э́то кни́га?","right":"Чья э́то кни́га?","why":"кни́га is a she-word, so “whose” must be <b>чья</b>. Match the thing, not the owner."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«___ э́то маши́на?» (маши́на is a she-word)","opts":["Чей","Чья","Чьё"],"answer":1,"explain":"маши́на → she-word → <b>Чья</b>. Match the thing."},{"type":"choice","q":"«___ э́то ключи́?» (ключи́ — plural)","opts":["Чьи","Чей","Чья"],"answer":0,"explain":"A plural thing → <b>Чьи</b> э́то ключи́?"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and ask it out loud.","target":"Чья э́то кни́га?","sub":"point at a book and ask whose it is","want":"чья это книга"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · “Of” between two things","title":"<em>Центр Москвы́</em> — the genitive links any two nouns","paras":["The genitive isn't only for people's things. Any time English glues two nouns with “of”, Russian bends the second one: <b>центр Москвы́</b> (the centre of Moscow), <b>ча́шка ча́я</b> (a cup of tea), <b>нача́ло уро́ка</b> (the start of the lesson).","The rule is exactly the same: the first noun stays put, the second takes the genitive tail. Москва́ → Москвы́, ча́й → ча́я, уро́к → уро́ка.","This one little case already covers a huge slice of everyday Russian — possession, the “of” link, and (from tomorrow) “there isn't any”. It earns its place as first."],"audio":"The genitive isn't only for people's things. Any time English glues two nouns with of, Russian bends the second one: tsentr Moskvy, the centre of Moscow; chashka chaya, a cup of tea; nachalo uroka, the start of the lesson. The rule is the same: the first noun stays put, the second takes the genitive tail. Moskva becomes Moskvy, chay becomes chaya, urok becomes uroka. This one little case already covers a huge slice of everyday Russian.","examples":[{"ru":"We're in the centre of Moscow.","en":"Мы в це́нтре <b>Москвы́</b>.","say":"Мы в центре Москвы."},{"ru":"A cup of tea, please.","en":"<b>Ча́шку ча́я</b>, пожа́луйста.","say":"Чашку чая, пожалуйста.","gloss":"ча́й → ча́я"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"центр Москва́","right":"центр Москвы́","why":"“of Moscow” puts Moscow in the genitive: Москва́ → <b>Москвы́</b>. The first noun (центр) stays; the second bends."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“the start of the lesson” —","opts":["нача́ло уро́к","нача́ло уро́ка","уро́к нача́ло"],"answer":1,"explain":"уро́к → уро́ка in the genitive: <b>нача́ло уро́ка</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“the centre of Moscow”</b>","want":"центр Москвы́","answer":"центр Москвы́"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “a cup of tea” (ча́шка + ча́й → …):","accept":["чашка чая","чашку чая","чашка чая."],"placeholder":"ча́шка…","explain":"ча́й → ча́я. Ча́шка ча́я — a cup of tea."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma has just landed back in Moscow. Ivan meets her at Lena's café — and there's a book someone left on the table. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма! Ты сно́ва здесь! Как я рад!","ru":"Emma! You're here again! I'm so glad! (рад = glad, said by a man)"},{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, приве́т! Я о́чень ра́да.","ru":"Ivan, hi! I'm very glad. (ра́да — said by a woman)"},{"who":"B","en":"Смотри́, чья э́то кни́га на столе́?","ru":"Look, whose book is this on the table?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то не моя́ кни́га. Мо́жет, кни́га Ле́ны?","ru":"It's not my book. Maybe Lena's book?"},{"who":"B","en":"Нет, Ле́на до́ма. Э́то кни́га бра́та!","ru":"No, Lena's at home. It's my brother's book!"},{"who":"A","en":"У тебя́ есть брат?","ru":"You have a brother?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, Макси́м. Э́то его́ кни́га и его́ ключи́.","ru":"Yes, Maxim. It's his book and his keys."},{"who":"A","en":"Как хорошо́ сно́ва быть в Москве́!","ru":"How good it is to be in Moscow again! (быть = to be)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the reunion and the “of” case. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"сно́ва","ru":"again","ex":"Мы сно́ва вме́сте."},{"en":"вме́сте","ru":"together","ex":"Мы за́втракаем вме́сте."},{"en":"чей / чья","ru":"whose (he-word / she-word)","ex":"Чья э́то кни́га?"},{"en":"брат","ru":"brother","ex":"Э́то кни́га бра́та."},{"en":"сестра́","ru":"sister","ex":"Э́то ко́мната сестры́."},{"en":"друг","ru":"friend","ex":"Маши́на дру́га здесь."},{"en":"центр","ru":"centre","ex":"Мы в це́нтре Москвы́."},{"en":"ко́мната","ru":"room","ex":"Э́то ко́мната Ле́ны."},{"en":"ключ","ru":"key","ex":"Э́то ключи́ Ива́на."},{"en":"сосе́д","ru":"neighbour","ex":"Э́то соба́ка сосе́да."},{"en":"нача́ло","ru":"beginning, start","ex":"Э́то нача́ло уро́ка."},{"en":"ча́шка ча́я","ru":"a cup of tea","ex":"Ча́шка ча́я, пожа́луйста."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["кни́га бра́та","the brother's book"],["маши́на Ива́на","Ivan's car"],["центр Москвы́","the centre of Moscow"],["ча́шка ча́я","a cup of tea"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“The brother's book” in Russian:","opts":["брат кни́га","кни́га бра́та","кни́га брат"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Ivan's car” —","opts":["маши́на Ива́на","маши́на Ива́н","Ива́на маши́на"],"answer":0},{"q":"“Mum's house” — дом ___","opts":["ма́ма","ма́му","ма́мы"],"answer":2},{"q":"«___ э́то маши́на?» (маши́на is a she-word)","opts":["Чей","Чья","Чьё"],"answer":1},{"q":"“the centre of Moscow” —","opts":["центр Москвы́","центр Москва́","центр Москву́"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's first note of the new stay — read it aloud. Every genitive in it is one you can now make yourself.","title":"Сно́ва в Москве́!","sentences":["Я сно́ва в Москве́!","Э́то кварти́ра Ива́на и Ле́ны.","Моя́ ко́мната — в це́нтре го́рода.","На столе́ — кни́га бра́та и ключи́ Ле́ны.","За́втра нача́ло уро́ка. Я гото́ва!"],"translation":"I'm in Moscow again! This is Ivan and Lena's flat. My room is in the centre of the city. On the table are my brother's book and Lena's keys. Tomorrow is the start of the lesson. I'm ready!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put the genitive to work.","prompt":"Look around you. Name three things and whose they are, using the genitive (thing + owner with a new ending). Then say where you are.","hint":"The pattern is thing + owner-with-a-tail: кни́га бра́та, телефо́н ма́мы, маши́на дру́га. He/it-words take -а/-я, she-words take -ы/-и 🙂","example":"Э́то ко́мната дру́га. На столе́ — кни́га сестры́ и телефо́н Ива́на. Я в це́нтре Москвы́."}},"2":{"day":2,"week":"01","themeRu":"There isn't any","themeEn":"Нет вре́мени","intro":"Yesterday the genitive told you whose something was. Today it does the opposite job — it tells you what <b>isn't there</b>. In Russian, the moment something is missing, absent or denied, the thing that's gone slips into the genitive. And Emma's bare new flat gives us plenty to complain about.","introAudio":"Yesterday the genitive told you whose something was. Today it does the opposite job — it tells you what isn't there. In Russian, the moment something is missing, absent or denied, the thing that's gone slips into the genitive. And Emma's bare new flat gives us plenty to complain about.","goals":["Say something's missing: <b>нет + genitive</b> — <b>здесь нет ка́рты</b>, “there's no map here”","Say what you don't have: <b>у меня́ нет вре́мени</b> — the missing thing goes genitive","Use the genitive pronouns: <b>у меня́ / у тебя́ / у него́ / у неё</b>","Put absence in the past: <b>не́ было + genitive</b> — <b>не́ было воды́</b>, “there was no water”"],"learned":["Said something was missing with <b>нет + genitive</b>: <b>здесь нет ка́рты</b>","Said what you don't have: <b>у меня́ нет вре́мени</b>, the missing thing in the genitive","Used the genitive pronouns: <b>у меня́ / у тебя́ / у него́ / у неё</b>","Put absence in the past: <b>не́ было + genitive</b>, <b>не́ было воды́</b>"],"review":{"intro":"One quick look back at yesterday's genitive before we flip it around. The owner in the genitive — that's warm now.","introAudio":"One quick look back at yesterday's genitive before we flip it around. The owner in the genitive — that's warm now.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all from days you've already done.","q":"“Ivan's car” — pick it:","opts":["маши́на Ива́на","Ива́н маши́на","маши́на Ива́н"],"answer":0,"explain":"Thing first, owner second and bent: <b>маши́на Ива́на</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"A man says “I had a brother.” — «У меня́ ___ брат.»","opts":["был","была́","бы́ло"],"answer":0,"explain":"The past agrees with брат (a he-word): <b>был</b>. была́ is the she-form, бы́ло the it-form."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“the centre of Moscow”</b>","want":"центр Москвы́","answer":"центр Москвы́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · «нет» pulls the genitive","title":"<em>Нет ка́рты</em> — “there's no map”","paras":["In A1 you said <b>у меня́ есть ка́рта</b> — “I have a map”, with ка́рта in its plain form. Flip it to the negative and one thing changes: <b>у меня́ нет ка́рты</b>. The word <b>нет</b> (“there isn't”) reaches out and pulls the missing thing into the genitive.","This is a firm rule, not a habit. <b>нет</b> is <em>always</em> followed by the genitive, never the plain form: <b>Здесь нет апте́ки.</b> <b>В ко́мнате нет окна́.</b> The thing that's absent isn't the subject of the sentence, so it can't stay in its dictionary shape.","Compare the pair and you'll feel the switch: <b>есть</b> keeps the plain form, <b>нет</b> bends it. апте́ка → нет апте́к<b>и</b>; окно́ → нет окн<b>а́</b>; вода́ → нет вод<b>ы́</b>."],"audio":"In A1 you said u menya yest karta — I have a map, with karta in its plain form. Flip it to the negative and one thing changes: u menya nyet karty. The word nyet, there isn't, reaches out and pulls the missing thing into the genitive. This is a firm rule: nyet is always followed by the genitive, never the plain form. Zdyes nyet aptyeki. V komnatye nyet okna. The thing that's absent isn't the subject, so it can't stay in its dictionary shape. Compare the pair: yest keeps the plain form, nyet bends it. Aptyeka becomes aptyeki, okno becomes okna, voda becomes vody.","table":{"rows":[["есть ка́рта → нет ка́рты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть карта, нет карты\">🔊</button>","there's a map → there's no map"],["есть апте́ка → нет апте́ки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть аптека, нет аптеки\">🔊</button>","there's a pharmacy → there's no pharmacy"],["есть окно́ → нет окна́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть окно, нет окна\">🔊</button>","there's a window → there's no window"],["есть вода́ → нет воды́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть вода, нет воды\">🔊</button>","there's water → there's no water"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"There's no pharmacy here.","en":"Здесь <b>нет апте́ки</b>.","say":"Здесь нет аптеки."},{"ru":"There's no window in the room.","en":"В ко́мнате <b>нет окна́</b>.","say":"В комнате нет окна."},{"ru":"There's no water. (none at all)","en":"Здесь <b>нет воды́</b>.","say":"Здесь нет воды."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"здесь нет ка́рта","right":"здесь нет ка́рты","why":"<b>нет</b> always pulls the genitive: ка́рта → ка́рт<b>ы</b>. The missing thing can never stay in its plain form."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“There's no pharmacy here.”","opts":["Здесь нет апте́ка.","Здесь нет апте́ки.","Здесь нет апте́ку."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>нет</b> → genitive: апте́ка → апте́ки. (апте́ку is the accusative — the wrong case here.)"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“There's no window.”</b>","want":"нет окна́","answer":"нет окна́"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “there's no water” (вода́ → …):","accept":["нет воды","нет воды.","здесь нет воды"],"placeholder":"нет…","explain":"вода́ → воды́ after <b>нет</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · «У меня́ нет…» — I don't have","title":"<em>У меня́ нет вре́мени</em> — “I've no time”","paras":["“I have” was <b>у меня́ есть…</b>; “I don't have” is <b>у меня́ нет…</b> — and the same rule bites. Whatever you're short of steps into the genitive: <b>У меня́ нет вре́мени.</b> <b>У меня́ нет ключа́.</b>","Notice <b>вре́мя</b>, “time”. It's a special word (one of a small neuter group), and its genitive is <b>вре́мени</b>, not “врема”. You'll meet it constantly — <b>нет вре́мени</b> is one of the most-said phrases in Russian.","You can drop the <b>у меня́</b> and just deny the thing outright: <b>Ключа́ нет!</b> — “The key's gone!” The bare genitive already carries “there isn't (any)”."],"audio":"I have was u menya yest; I don't have is u menya nyet — and the same rule bites. Whatever you're short of steps into the genitive: u menya nyet vryemeni. U menya nyet klyucha. Notice vryemya, time. It's a special word, and its genitive is vryemeni, not vryema. You'll meet it constantly — nyet vryemeni is one of the most-said phrases in Russian. You can drop the u menya and just deny the thing outright: klyucha nyet — the key's gone. The bare genitive already carries there isn't any.","table":{"rows":[["есть вре́мя → нет вре́мени <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть время, нет времени\">🔊</button>","there's time → there's no time"],["есть ключ → нет ключа́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть ключ, нет ключа\">🔊</button>","there's a key → there's no key"],["есть брат → нет бра́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть брат, нет брата\">🔊</button>","there's a brother → there's no brother"],["есть хлеб → нет хле́ба <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть хлеб, нет хлеба\">🔊</button>","there's bread → there's no bread"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I've no time today.","en":"Сего́дня у меня́ <b>нет вре́мени</b>.","say":"Сегодня у меня нет времени."},{"ru":"I don't have a key.","en":"У меня́ <b>нет ключа́</b>.","say":"У меня нет ключа."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"у меня́ нет вре́мя","right":"у меня́ нет вре́мени","why":"<b>вре́мя</b> is a special word: its genitive is <b>вре́мени</b>. After <b>нет</b> it must bend like everything else."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I don't have a brother.” — У меня́ нет ___","opts":["брат","бра́та","бра́том"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>нет</b> → genitive: брат → бра́та. The plain form брат can't follow нет."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I don't have time.”</b>","want":"У меня́ нет вре́мени","answer":"У меня́ нет вре́мени"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · У меня́, у тебя́, у него́, у неё","title":"The genitive of the pronouns","paras":["The little words after <b>у</b> are pronouns already sitting in the genitive. You've said <b>у меня́</b> since A1; here's the full set: <b>у меня́</b> (me), <b>у тебя́</b> (you), <b>у него́</b> (him), <b>у неё</b> (her), <b>у нас</b> (us), <b>у вас</b> (you-plural), <b>у них</b> (them).","One spelling trap: after a preposition, “him / her / them” grow an <b>н-</b>. On their own they are его́ / её / их, but with <b>у</b> they become <b>у него́ / у неё / у них</b>. Every genitive preposition does this.","So “he doesn't have” is <b>у него́ нет…</b>, and “she has no time” is <b>у неё нет вре́мени</b>."],"audio":"The little words after u are pronouns already sitting in the genitive. You've said u menya since A1; here's the full set: u menya, me; u tyebya, you; u nyevo, him; u nyeyo, her; u nas, us; u vas, you plural; u nikh, them. One spelling trap: after a preposition, him, her and them grow an n. On their own they are yevo, yeyo, ikh, but with u they become u nyevo, u nyeyo, u nikh. Every genitive preposition does this. So he doesn't have is u nyevo nyet, and she has no time is u nyeyo nyet vryemeni.","table":{"rows":[["у меня́ / у тебя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у меня, у тебя\">🔊</button>","I have / you have"],["у него́ / у неё <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у него, у неё\">🔊</button>","he has / she has"],["у нас / у вас <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у нас, у вас\">🔊</button>","we have / you (pl) have"],["у них <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у них\">🔊</button>","they have"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"He doesn't have a car.","en":"<b>У него́</b> нет маши́ны.","say":"У него нет машины."},{"ru":"She has no time.","en":"<b>У неё</b> нет вре́мени.","say":"У неё нет времени."},{"ru":"We don't have a key.","en":"<b>У нас</b> нет ключа́.","say":"У нас нет ключа."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"у его́ нет вре́мени","right":"у него́ нет вре́мени","why":"After a preposition, его́ gains an <b>н-</b>: <b>у него́</b>. Same for её → у неё, их → у них."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“She doesn't have a car.” — ___ нет маши́ны.","opts":["У её","У неё","У она́"],"answer":1,"explain":"“she has” = <b>у неё</b> — её takes an <b>н-</b> after the preposition."},{"type":"choice","q":"“They don't have time.”","opts":["У них нет вре́мени.","У их нет вре́мени.","У они́ нет вре́мени."],"answer":0,"explain":"их → <b>у них</b> after <b>у</b>. And вре́мя → вре́мени."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"У меня́ нет вре́мени.","sub":"tell a friend you've no time right now","want":"у меня нет времени"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · «Не́ было» — there wasn't any","title":"<em>Не́ было воды́</em> — absence in the past","paras":["To say something <em>wasn't</em> there, use <b>не́ было</b> (“there wasn't”) — and yes, the missing thing still goes genitive: <b>Не́ было воды́.</b> <b>У меня́ не́ было вре́мени.</b>","Here's the neat part. “There was a pharmacy” agrees like a normal past: <b>Здесь была́ апте́ка</b> (feminine была́, апте́ка plain). Negate it and everything freezes: <b>Здесь не́ было апте́ки</b> — always <b>не́ было</b>, never “не был / не была́”, and the thing in the genitive. Absence has no subject to agree with.","And <b>бы́ло</b> never bends here: whether the missing thing is a he-word, a she-word or an it-word, past absence stays the one fixed shape <b>не́ было</b>. No subject, nothing to agree with."],"audio":"To say something wasn't there, use nye bylo, there wasn't — and yes, the missing thing still goes genitive: nye bylo vody. U menya nye bylo vryemeni. Here's the neat part. There was a pharmacy agrees like a normal past: zdyes byla aptyeka, feminine byla, aptyeka plain. Negate it and everything freezes: zdyes nye bylo aptyeki — always nye bylo, never nye byl or nye byla, and the thing in the genitive. Absence has no subject to agree with. The stress sits on the nye: nye bylo. Say it as one word.","table":{"rows":[["была́ апте́ка → не́ было апте́ки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"была аптека, не было аптеки\">🔊</button>","there was a pharmacy → there wasn't one"],["был хлеб → не́ было хле́ба <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"был хлеб, не было хлеба\">🔊</button>","there was bread → there wasn't any"],["бы́ло молоко́ → не́ было молока́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"было молоко, не было молока\">🔊</button>","there was milk → there wasn't any"],["была́ вода́ → не́ было воды́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"была вода, не было воды\">🔊</button>","there was water → there wasn't any"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"There was no water.","en":"<b>Не́ было воды́</b>.","say":"Не было воды."},{"ru":"I had no time yesterday.","en":"Вчера́ у меня́ <b>не́ было вре́мени</b>.","say":"Вчера у меня не было времени."},{"ru":"There was no bread in the shop.","en":"В магази́не <b>не́ было хле́ба</b>.","say":"В магазине не было хлеба."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"здесь не была́ апте́ка","right":"здесь не́ было апте́ки","why":"Negated “there was” is fixed: <b>не́ было</b> (no agreement), and the thing goes genitive: апте́ка → апте́ки."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“There was no milk.”","opts":["Не́ было молоко́.","Не́ было молока́.","Не́ был молоко́."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>не́ было</b> (fixed) + genitive: молоко́ → молока́."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I had no time” (start with «у меня́…»):","accept":["у меня не было времени","у меня не было времени.","у меня небыло времени"],"placeholder":"у меня́…","explain":"Past absence: <b>не́ было</b> + вре́мени."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“There was no bread.”</b>","want":"не́ было хле́ба","answer":"не́ было хле́ба"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma's first evening in her own rented room. Ivan helps her look around — and the place is a little… bare. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну как кварти́ра? Тебе́ нра́вится?","ru":"So how's the flat? Do you like it?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нра́вится! Но здесь нет ли́фта.","ru":"I like it! But there's no lift here."},{"who":"B","en":"А вода́? Есть горя́чая вода́?","ru":"And water? Is there hot water? (горя́чая = hot)"},{"who":"A","en":"Сейча́с нет воды́. Совсе́м нет!","ru":"Right now there's no water. None at all! (совсе́м = at all)"},{"who":"B","en":"А хлеб? Молоко́?","ru":"And bread? Milk?"},{"who":"A","en":"Хле́ба нет, молока́ нет. И вре́мени нет — уже́ ве́чер.","ru":"No bread, no milk. And no time either — it's evening already."},{"who":"B","en":"Не пробле́ма. У меня́ есть план.","ru":"No problem. I've got a plan."},{"who":"A","en":"У тебя́ всегда́ есть план!","ru":"You always have a plan!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for an empty flat and the “there isn't” case. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"нет","ru":"there isn't / no (+ genitive)","ex":"Здесь нет ка́рты."},{"en":"вре́мя","ru":"time (gen. вре́мени)","ex":"У меня́ нет вре́мени."},{"en":"ка́рта","ru":"map","ex":"Где ка́рта? — Ка́рты нет."},{"en":"апте́ка","ru":"pharmacy, chemist's","ex":"Здесь нет апте́ки."},{"en":"вода́","ru":"water","ex":"В кварти́ре нет воды́."},{"en":"хлеб","ru":"bread","ex":"До́ма нет хле́ба."},{"en":"молоко́","ru":"milk","ex":"У нас нет молока́."},{"en":"окно́","ru":"window","ex":"В ко́мнате нет окна́."},{"en":"лифт","ru":"lift, elevator","ex":"В до́ме нет ли́фта."},{"en":"интерне́т","ru":"internet","ex":"Здесь нет интерне́та."},{"en":"не́ было","ru":"there wasn't (+ genitive)","ex":"Вчера́ не́ было воды́."},{"en":"у неё / у него́","ru":"she has / he has","ex":"У неё нет вре́мени."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["У меня́ нет вре́мени.","I have no time."],["Здесь нет апте́ки.","There's no pharmacy here."],["У него́ нет ключа́.","He doesn't have a key."],["Не́ было воды́.","There was no water."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“There's no map here.”","opts":["Здесь нет ка́рты.","Здесь нет ка́рта.","Здесь нет ка́рту."],"answer":0},{"q":"“I don't have time.”","opts":["У меня́ нет вре́мя.","У меня́ нет вре́мени.","У меня́ нет вре́мена."],"answer":1},{"q":"“He doesn't have a key.”","opts":["У его́ нет ключа́.","У него́ нет ключ.","У него́ нет ключа́."],"answer":2},{"q":"“There was no water.” (in the past)","opts":["Не́ было воды́.","Не была́ вода́.","Нет воды́."],"answer":0},{"q":"“She has no time.”","opts":["У неё нет вре́мени.","У неё не вре́мени.","У ней нет вре́мени."],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's first note home about the new room — every “there isn't” in it is a genitive you can now make yourself.","title":"Моя́ но́вая ко́мната","sentences":["Э́то моя́ но́вая ко́мната в це́нтре Москвы́.","Ко́мната хоро́шая, но здесь нет ли́фта.","И сего́дня нет воды́ — совсе́м нет!","До́ма нет молока́ и нет хле́ба.","Но у меня́ есть Ива́н, а у Ива́на всегда́ есть план.","Э́то не пробле́ма. Я о́чень ра́да!"],"translation":"This is my new room in the centre of Moscow. The room's nice, but there's no lift here. And today there's no water — none at all! There's no milk and no bread at home. But I have Ivan, and Ivan always has a plan. It's not a problem. I'm very glad!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — list what's missing.","prompt":"You've just moved into a new place. Name three things that aren't there or that you don't have, using <b>нет + genitive</b>. Then say one thing you <em>do</em> have.","hint":"The pattern is <b>нет</b> + the thing with a genitive tail: нет воды́, нет ли́фта, нет вре́мени. For “I don't have”, start with <b>у меня́ нет…</b> 🙂","example":"Э́то моя́ но́вая кварти́ра. Здесь нет ли́фта, нет интерне́та и нет воды́. Но у меня́ есть окно́ и хоро́ший сосе́д!"}},"3":{"day":3,"week":"01","themeRu":"From where?","themeEn":"Из, от, до, с","intro":"Emma is always on the move — home to café, café to class, class to the metro. To say where she's coming <em>from</em>, Russian leans on the genitive again, this time behind four little prepositions. And one of them will trip your English ear.","introAudio":"Emma is always on the move — home to cafe, cafe to class, class to the metro. To say where she's coming from, Russian leans on the genitive again, this time behind four little prepositions. And one of them will trip your English ear.","goals":["Say where you're from with <b>из</b>: <b>из Москвы́</b>, out of a place you were <em>in</em>","Use <b>с</b> for coming off / from: <b>с рабо́ты</b>, from a place you were <em>at</em>","Use <b>от</b> for “from a person / away from”: <b>письмо́ от бра́та</b>","Use <b>до</b> for “as far as / until”: <b>до це́нтра</b>, <b>до за́втра</b>"],"learned":["Said where you're from with <b>из</b>: <b>из Москвы́</b>","Used <b>с</b> for coming off / from: <b>с рабо́ты</b>","Used <b>от</b> for “from a person / away from”: <b>письмо́ от бра́та</b>","Used <b>до</b> for “as far as / until”: <b>до це́нтра</b>, <b>до за́втра</b>"],"review":{"intro":"A minute on days 1 and 2 — whose things, and things that aren't there. The genitive is already doing a lot of work.","introAudio":"A minute on days one and two — whose things, and things that aren't there. The genitive is already doing a lot of work.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All from days you've already done.","q":"“There's no time.”","opts":["Нет вре́мя.","Нет вре́мени.","Не вре́мени."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>нет</b> → genitive: вре́мя → вре́мени."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“He doesn't have a car.”</b> (start with у него́)","want":"у него́ нет маши́ны","answer":"у него́ нет маши́ны"},{"type":"choice","q":"“the centre of the city”","opts":["центр го́рода","центр го́род","го́род центр"],"answer":0,"explain":"Owner in the genitive: го́род → го́рода. Центр го́рода."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · «Из» — out of","title":"<em>Из Москвы́</em> — out of a place you were in","paras":["To say you've come <em>from</em> somewhere you were <em>inside</em>, use <b>из</b> + genitive: <b>из Москвы́</b> (from Moscow), <b>из шко́лы</b> (from school), <b>из до́ма</b> (from home). <b>из</b> literally means “out of”.","There's a clean logic here. In A1 you went <em>into</em> a place with <b>в</b> + accusative: <b>в Москву́, в шко́лу</b>. To come back <em>out</em>, swap to <b>из</b> + genitive: <b>из Москвы́, из шко́лы</b>. Wherever you used <b>в</b>, you leave with <b>из</b>.","The place takes an ordinary genitive tail: Москва́ → Москв<b>ы́</b>, шко́ла → шко́л<b>ы</b>, дом → до́м<b>а</b>."],"audio":"To say you've come from somewhere you were inside, use iz plus genitive: iz Moskvy, from Moscow; iz shkoly, from school; iz doma, from home. Iz literally means out of. There's a clean logic: in A1 you went into a place with v plus accusative — v Moskvu, v shkolu. To come back out, swap to iz plus genitive: iz Moskvy, iz shkoly. Wherever you used v, you leave with iz. The place takes an ordinary genitive tail: Moskva becomes Moskvy, shkola becomes shkoly, dom becomes doma.","table":{"rows":[["в Москву́ → из Москвы́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в Москву, из Москвы\">🔊</button>","to Moscow → from Moscow"],["в шко́лу → из шко́лы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в школу, из школы\">🔊</button>","to school → from school"],["в магази́н → из магази́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в магазин, из магазина\">🔊</button>","to the shop → from the shop"],["в дом → из до́ма <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в дом, из дома\">🔊</button>","into the house → from home"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm from Moscow.","en":"Я <b>из Москвы́</b>.","say":"Я из Москвы."},{"ru":"She's walking home from school.","en":"Она́ идёт домо́й <b>из шко́лы</b>.","say":"Она идёт домой из школы."},{"ru":"This is bread from the shop.","en":"Э́то хлеб <b>из магази́на</b>.","say":"Это хлеб из магазина."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"я с Москвы́","right":"я из Москвы́","why":"You were <em>in</em> Moscow (<b>в Москве́</b>), so you come <b>из</b> Moscow. <b>с</b> is for places you were <em>on / at</em> — that's the next rule."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“She's coming from school.”","opts":["Она́ идёт с шко́лы.","Она́ идёт из шко́лы.","Она́ идёт от шко́лы."],"answer":1,"explain":"You're <em>in</em> school (<b>в шко́ле</b>), so you leave <b>из шко́лы</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm from Moscow.”</b>","want":"я из Москвы́","answer":"я из Москвы́"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “from the shop” (магази́н → …):","accept":["из магазина","из магазина."],"placeholder":"из…","explain":"магази́н → из магази́на."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · «С» — off, from a на-place","title":"<em>С рабо́ты</em> — the other “from”","paras":["Some places pair with <b>на</b>, not <b>в</b>: <b>на рабо́те</b> (at work), <b>на по́чте</b> (at the post office), <b>на у́лице</b> (outside). To come <em>from</em> those, don't use <b>из</b> — use <b>с</b> + genitive: <b>с рабо́ты, с по́чты, с у́лицы</b>.","This is the trap for English speakers. English has one “from”, but Russian splits it: <b>из</b> mirrors <b>в</b>, and <b>с</b> mirrors <b>на</b>. If you'd say <em>at</em> the place with <b>на</b>, you leave it with <b>с</b>.","So <b>в шко́ле → из шко́лы</b>, but <b>на рабо́те → с рабо́ты</b>. Learn each place with its own partner."],"audio":"Some places pair with na, not v: na rabotye, at work; na pochtye, at the post office; na ulitsye, outside. To come from those, don't use iz — use s plus genitive: s raboty, s pochty, s ulitsy. This is the trap for English speakers. English has one from, but Russian splits it: iz mirrors v, and s mirrors na. If you'd say at the place with na, you leave it with s. So v shkolye becomes iz shkoly, but na rabotye becomes s raboty. Learn each place with its own partner.","table":{"rows":[["на рабо́ту → с рабо́ты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на работу, с работы\">🔊</button>","to work → from work"],["на по́чту → с по́чты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на почту, с почты\">🔊</button>","to the post office → from it"],["на у́лицу → с у́лицы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на улицу, с улицы\">🔊</button>","outside → from the street"],["на уро́к → с уро́ка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на урок, с урока\">🔊</button>","to the lesson → from the lesson"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Ivan's coming home from work.","en":"Ива́н идёт домо́й <b>с рабо́ты</b>.","say":"Иван идёт домой с работы."},{"ru":"Emma's on her way from class.","en":"Э́мма идёт <b>с уро́ка</b>.","say":"Эмма идёт с урока."},{"ru":"The children are coming in from outside.","en":"Де́ти иду́т <b>с у́лицы</b>.","say":"Дети идут с улицы."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ива́н идёт из рабо́ты","right":"Ива́н идёт с рабо́ты","why":"You're <em>at</em> work (<b>на рабо́те</b>), so you come <b>с рабо́ты</b>, not “из”. <b>на</b>-places leave with <b>с</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“from work” — Ива́н идёт домо́й ___","opts":["из рабо́ты","с рабо́ты","от рабо́ты"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>на рабо́те → с рабо́ты</b>. на-places take <b>с</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Pick the matching pair:","opts":["в шко́ле → с шко́лы","на рабо́те → с рабо́ты","на по́чте → из по́чты"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>на</b> pairs with <b>с</b> (на рабо́те → с рабо́ты); <b>в</b> pairs with <b>из</b> (в шко́ле → из шко́лы)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“from the lesson”</b> (уро́к)","want":"с уро́ка","answer":"с уро́ка"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · «От» — from a person, away from","title":"<em>Письмо́ от бра́та</em> — from whom, from a start-point","paras":["The third “from” is <b>от</b> + genitive. Use it for coming <em>from a person</em> or moving <em>away from</em> a point: <b>письмо́ от бра́та</b> (a letter from my brother), <b>пода́рок от ма́мы</b> (a present from Mum), <b>от до́ма до метро́</b> (from the house to the metro).","The line to hold: <b>из / с</b> answer “out of / off <em>which place</em>”; <b>от</b> answers “from <em>whom</em>” or “starting from <em>which point</em>”. A letter comes <b>от</b> a person, never “из” a person.","Places and people take the same genitive tail: брат → бра́т<b>а</b>, ма́ма → ма́м<b>ы</b>, дом → до́м<b>а</b>."],"audio":"The third from is ot plus genitive. Use it for coming from a person or moving away from a point: pismo ot brata, a letter from my brother; podarok ot mamy, a present from Mum; ot doma do metro, from the house to the metro. The line to hold: iz and s answer out of or off which place; ot answers from whom or starting from which point. A letter comes ot a person, never iz a person. Places and people take the same genitive tail: brat becomes brata, mama becomes mamy, dom becomes doma.","table":{"rows":[["письмо́ от бра́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"письмо от брата\">🔊</button>","a letter from (my) brother"],["пода́рок от ма́мы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"подарок от мамы\">🔊</button>","a present from Mum"],["от до́ма до метро́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"от дома до метро\">🔊</button>","from the house to the metro"],["от Ива́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"от Ивана\">🔊</button>","from Ivan"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This is a present from Mum.","en":"Э́то пода́рок <b>от ма́мы</b>.","say":"Это подарок от мамы."},{"ru":"This is a letter from my brother.","en":"Э́то письмо́ <b>от бра́та</b>.","say":"Это письмо от брата."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"письмо́ из бра́та","right":"письмо́ от бра́та","why":"A letter comes <em>from a person</em> → <b>от</b>. <b>из</b> is “out of a place”, not out of a person."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“a present from Mum”","opts":["пода́рок из ма́мы","пода́рок от ма́мы","пода́рок с ма́мы"],"answer":1,"explain":"From a <em>person</em> → <b>от</b>: пода́рок от ма́мы."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “a letter from my brother” (брат → …):","accept":["письмо от брата","письмо от брата."],"placeholder":"письмо́…","explain":"<b>от</b> + genitive: брат → бра́та."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · «До» — up to, until","title":"<em>До це́нтра</em> — as far as, until","paras":["The last one, <b>до</b> + genitive, marks the far end: “up to, as far as, until”. In space: <b>до це́нтра</b> (as far as the centre), <b>до метро́</b> (up to the metro). In time: <b>до за́втра</b> (until tomorrow), <b>до сре́ды</b> (until Wednesday).","You already know one <b>до</b> by heart — <b>до свида́ния</b>, literally “until the next meeting”. Same word, same case.","A pairing you'll use for directions: <b>от … до …</b> — “from … to …”: <b>от до́ма до рабо́ты</b> (from home to work). And one indeclinable word to note: <b>метро́</b> never changes — <b>до метро́, из метро́</b>."],"audio":"The last one, do plus genitive, marks the far end: up to, as far as, until. In space: do tsentra, as far as the centre; do metro, up to the metro. In time: do zavtra, until tomorrow; do sredy, until Wednesday. You already know one do by heart — do svidaniya, literally until the next meeting. Same word, same case. A pairing for directions: ot to do, from to: ot doma do raboty, from home to work. And one word never changes: metro stays metro — do metro, iz metro.","table":{"rows":[["до це́нтра <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"до центра\">🔊</button>","as far as the centre"],["до метро́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"до метро\">🔊</button>","up to the metro"],["до за́втра <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"до завтра\">🔊</button>","until tomorrow"],["от до́ма до рабо́ты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"от дома до работы\">🔊</button>","from home to work"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"It's not far to the metro.","en":"<b>До метро́</b> недалеко́.","say":"До метро недалеко."},{"ru":"See you tomorrow!","en":"<b>До за́втра</b>!","say":"До завтра!"},{"ru":"from home to work","en":"<b>от до́ма до рабо́ты</b>","say":"от дома до работы"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"с до́ма до метро́","right":"от до́ма до метро́","why":"“from … to …” for a route is <b>от … до …</b>: <b>от до́ма до метро́</b>. <b>от</b> marks the start point, <b>до</b> the end."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“until tomorrow / see you tomorrow”","opts":["до за́втра","от за́втра","из за́втра"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>до</b> = “until”: до за́втра. The same word as <b>до свида́ния</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“from home to work”</b>","want":"от до́ма до рабо́ты","answer":"от до́ма до рабо́ты"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я из Москвы́, а ты?","sub":"say where you're from and ask back","want":"я из Москвы а ты"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma and Lena bump into each other near the metro. Everyone's coming from somewhere. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ле́на, приве́т! Ты отку́да?","ru":"Lena, hi! Where are you coming from? (отку́да = where from)"},{"who":"B","en":"С рабо́ты. А ты?","ru":"From work. And you?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я из до́ма. Иду́ в центр.","ru":"From home. I'm heading to the centre."},{"who":"B","en":"От до́ма до це́нтра далеко́?","ru":"Is it far from your place to the centre?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, недалеко́.","ru":"No, it's not far."},{"who":"B","en":"У меня́ письмо́ от бра́та. Он то́же в Москве́!","ru":"I've got a letter from my brother. He's in Moscow too!"},{"who":"A","en":"Как хорошо́! До за́втра, Ле́на?","ru":"How nice! See you tomorrow, Lena?"},{"who":"B","en":"До за́втра!","ru":"See you tomorrow!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for coming and going. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"из","ru":"from, out of (+ genitive)","ex":"Я из Москвы́."},{"en":"от","ru":"from (a person), away from (+ genitive)","ex":"Письмо́ от бра́та."},{"en":"до","ru":"up to, as far as, until (+ genitive)","ex":"До за́втра!"},{"en":"с (со)","ru":"off, from a на-place (+ genitive)","ex":"Ива́н с рабо́ты."},{"en":"рабо́та","ru":"work, job","ex":"Она́ идёт с рабо́ты."},{"en":"шко́ла","ru":"school","ex":"Де́ти из шко́лы."},{"en":"по́чта","ru":"post office; mail","ex":"Я иду́ с по́чты."},{"en":"у́лица","ru":"street","ex":"Де́ти с у́лицы."},{"en":"метро́","ru":"metro (never changes)","ex":"До метро́ недалеко́."},{"en":"письмо́","ru":"letter","ex":"Э́то письмо́ от ма́мы."},{"en":"пода́рок","ru":"present, gift","ex":"Пода́рок от дру́га."},{"en":"отку́да","ru":"where from?","ex":"Ты отку́да?"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я из Москвы́.","I'm from Moscow."],["Он с рабо́ты.","He's (coming) from work."],["Письмо́ от бра́та.","A letter from (my) brother."],["До це́нтра недалеко́.","It's not far to the centre."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“I'm from Moscow.”","opts":["Я с Москвы́.","Я из Москвы́.","Я от Москвы́."],"answer":1},{"q":"“Ivan's coming from work.” (he was на рабо́те)","opts":["Ива́н с рабо́ты.","Ива́н из рабо́ты.","Ива́н от рабо́ты."],"answer":0},{"q":"“a present from Mum”","opts":["пода́рок из ма́мы","пода́рок с ма́мы","пода́рок от ма́мы"],"answer":2},{"q":"“from home to the metro”","opts":["от до́ма до метро́","из до́ма от метро́","с до́ма до метро́"],"answer":0},{"q":"“She's coming from school.” (she was в шко́ле)","opts":["Она́ с шко́лы.","Она́ от шко́лы.","Она́ из шко́лы."],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short page from Emma's diary — she's coming and going all day. Read it aloud.","title":"Оди́н день Э́ммы","sentences":["У́тром я е́ду из до́ма на рабо́ту.","Ве́чером я иду́ с рабо́ты домо́й.","От до́ма до метро́ недалеко́.","Сего́дня у меня́ письмо́ от бра́та из Ло́ндона.","Он то́же хо́чет в Москву́!","Э́то хоро́ший день."],"translation":"In the morning I go from home to work. In the evening I walk home from work. It's not far from home to the metro. Today I have a letter from my brother, from London. He wants to come to Moscow too! It's a good day."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — map your comings and goings.","prompt":"Say where you go <em>from</em> and <em>to</em> in a day. Use <b>из</b>, <b>с</b>, <b>от</b> and <b>до</b> at least once each.","hint":"Remember the split: <b>в</b>-places leave with <b>из</b> (из шко́лы), <b>на</b>-places leave with <b>с</b> (с рабо́ты). People and start-points take <b>от</b>; end-points take <b>до</b> 🙂","example":"У́тром я иду́ из до́ма на рабо́ту. Ве́чером — с рабо́ты в центр. От до́ма до метро́ недалеко́. А сего́дня у меня́ пода́рок от дру́га!"}},"4":{"day":4,"week":"01","themeRu":"For you, without sugar","themeEn":"Для, без, по́сле","intro":"Emma's learning the small kindnesses of Moscow life — a coffee bought <em>for</em> a friend, <em>without</em> sugar, <em>after</em> class. Three more little words, three more genitives. And one of them, <b>для</b>, is a false friend for the English “for”.","introAudio":"Emma's learning the small kindnesses of Moscow life — a coffee bought for a friend, without sugar, after class. Three more little words, three more genitives. And one of them, dlya, is a false friend for the English for.","goals":["Say who something's for: <b>для + genitive</b> — <b>ко́фе для Ле́ны</b>","Say “without”: <b>без + genitive</b> — <b>без са́хара</b>","Say “after”: <b>по́сле + genitive</b> — <b>по́сле уро́ка</b>","Say “near / by / at someone's”: <b>о́коло до́ма</b>, <b>у окна́</b>"],"learned":["Said who something's for: <b>для + genitive</b>, <b>ко́фе для Ле́ны</b>","Said “without”: <b>без + genitive</b>, <b>без са́хара</b>","Said “after”: <b>по́сле + genitive</b>, <b>по́сле уро́ка</b>","Said “near / by / at someone's”: <b>о́коло до́ма</b>, <b>у окна́</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Quick recall from this week — the “of” link, absence, and yesterday's из / с.","introAudio":"Quick recall from this week — the of-link, absence, and yesterday's iz and s.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All from earlier days.","q":"“from work” (you were на рабо́те)","opts":["из рабо́ты","с рабо́ты","от рабо́ты"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>на</b>-places leave with <b>с</b>: с рабо́ты."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“There's no time.”</b>","want":"нет вре́мени","answer":"нет вре́мени"},{"type":"choice","q":"“Lena's phone”","opts":["телефо́н Ле́ны","телефо́н Ле́на","Ле́на телефо́н"],"answer":0,"explain":"Owner in the genitive: телефо́н Ле́ны."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · «Для» — for whom, for what","title":"<em>Для тебя́</em> — for you, for a thing's sake","paras":["<b>для</b> + genitive means “for the benefit of, meant for”: <b>ко́фе для Ле́ны</b> (a coffee for Lena), <b>пода́рок для дру́га</b> (a present for a friend), <b>вре́мя для рабо́ты</b> (time for work).","Here's the false friend. English pours “for” everywhere, but <b>для</b> covers only the <em>beneficiary of a thing</em> or a <em>purpose</em>. When “for” means the target of a verb — “I'm calling <em>you</em>, I'm helping <em>Mum</em>” — Russian reaches for a different case (you'll meet it next week), not <b>для</b>.","So a gift <b>для</b> someone is fine, but don't stretch <b>для</b> onto every English “for”. The noun after it just takes the genitive: Ле́на → Ле́н<b>ы</b>, друг → дру́г<b>а</b>."],"audio":"Dlya plus genitive means for the benefit of, meant for: kofe dlya Leny, a coffee for Lena; podarok dlya druga, a present for a friend; vryemya dlya raboty, time for work. Here's the false friend. English pours for everywhere, but dlya covers only the beneficiary of a thing or a purpose. When for means the target of a verb — I'm calling you, I'm helping Mum — Russian reaches for a different case, one you'll meet next week, not dlya. So a gift dlya someone is fine, but don't stretch dlya onto every English for. The noun after it just takes the genitive: Lena becomes Leny, drug becomes druga.","table":{"rows":[["ко́фе для Ле́ны <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кофе для Лены\">🔊</button>","a coffee for Lena"],["пода́рок для дру́га <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"подарок для друга\">🔊</button>","a present for a friend"],["стол для рабо́ты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стол для работы\">🔊</button>","a table for work"],["э́то для тебя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"это для тебя\">🔊</button>","this is for you"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This coffee is for Lena.","en":"Э́тот ко́фе <b>для Ле́ны</b>.","say":"Этот кофе для Лены."},{"ru":"This is for you!","en":"Э́то <b>для тебя́</b>!","say":"Это для тебя!"},{"ru":"A present for a friend.","en":"Пода́рок <b>для дру́га</b>.","say":"Подарок для друга."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"ко́фе для Ле́на","right":"ко́фе для Ле́ны","why":"<b>для</b> takes the genitive: Ле́на → Ле́н<b>ы</b>. The plain form can't follow it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“a present for a friend”","opts":["пода́рок для друг","пода́рок для дру́га","пода́рок для дру́гом"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>для</b> + genitive: друг → дру́га. <b>для</b> marks who a thing is <em>for</em>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which one uses <b>для</b> correctly?","opts":["Я звоню́ для ма́мы.","Я помога́ю для ма́мы.","Э́то ко́фе для ма́мы."],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>для</b> is for a thing meant for someone (a coffee for Mum). “Calling” or “helping” someone takes a different case — coming next week — not <b>для</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“This is for you.”</b>","want":"э́то для тебя́","answer":"э́то для тебя́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · «Без» — without","title":"<em>Без са́хара</em> — leaving something out","paras":["<b>без</b> + genitive is the clean opposite of “with”: “without”. <b>ко́фе без са́хара</b> (coffee without sugar), <b>чай без молока́</b> (tea without milk), <b>день без интерне́та</b> (a day without internet).","It works for people too: <b>без тебя́</b> (without you), <b>без Ива́на</b> (without Ivan). Whatever's missing takes the genitive tail — exactly the ending you drilled with <b>нет</b> yesterday.","Café Russian leans on this hourly: <b>без са́хара, без молока́</b>. Learn <b>без</b> and you can order almost anything “without”."],"audio":"Byez plus genitive is the clean opposite of with: without. Kofe byez sakhara, coffee without sugar; chai byez moloka, tea without milk; dyen byez interneta, a day without internet. It works for people too: byez tyebya, without you; byez Ivana, without Ivan. Whatever's missing takes the genitive tail — exactly the ending you drilled with nyet yesterday. Cafe Russian leans on this hourly: byez sakhara, byez moloka. Learn byez and you can order almost anything without.","table":{"rows":[["ко́фе без са́хара <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кофе без сахара\">🔊</button>","coffee without sugar"],["чай без молока́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"чай без молока\">🔊</button>","tea without milk"],["без тебя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"без тебя\">🔊</button>","without you"],["день без интерне́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"день без интернета\">🔊</button>","a day without internet"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"A coffee without sugar, please.","en":"Ко́фе <b>без са́хара</b>, пожа́луйста.","say":"Кофе без сахара, пожалуйста."},{"ru":"Tea without milk.","en":"Чай <b>без молока́</b>.","say":"Чай без молока."},{"ru":"I don't want to be here without you.","en":"Я не хочу́ здесь <b>без тебя́</b>.","say":"Я не хочу здесь без тебя."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"ко́фе без са́хар","right":"ко́фе без са́хара","why":"<b>без</b> pulls the genitive: са́хар → са́хар<b>а</b>. “Without” always bends the thing that's missing."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“tea without milk”","opts":["чай без молока́","чай без молоко́","чай без молоку́"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>без</b> + genitive: молоко́ → молока́."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“coffee without sugar”</b>","want":"ко́фе без са́хара","answer":"ко́фе без са́хара"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “without sugar” (са́хар → …):","accept":["без сахара","без сахара."],"placeholder":"без…","explain":"<b>без</b> + genitive: са́хар → са́хара."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · «По́сле» — after","title":"<em>По́сле уро́ка</em> — after this, before that","paras":["<b>по́сле</b> + genitive means “after”: <b>по́сле уро́ка</b> (after the lesson), <b>по́сле рабо́ты</b> (after work), <b>по́сле обе́да</b> (after lunch).","It answers “after <em>what</em>?”, so it takes a noun in the genitive, just like the others: уро́к → уро́к<b>а</b>, рабо́та → рабо́т<b>ы</b>, обе́д → обе́д<b>а</b>.","A handy everyday one: <b>по́сле обе́да</b> also means “in the afternoon”. And its partner from yesterday, <b>до</b>, means “before”: <b>до уро́ка</b> (before the lesson) — a whole timeline in two words."],"audio":"Poslye plus genitive means after: poslye uroka, after the lesson; poslye raboty, after work; poslye obeda, after lunch. It answers after what, so it takes a noun in the genitive, just like the others: urok becomes uroka, rabota becomes raboty, obed becomes obeda. A handy one: poslye obeda also means in the afternoon. And its partner from yesterday, do, means before: do uroka, before the lesson — a whole timeline in two words.","table":{"rows":[["по́сле уро́ка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"после урока\">🔊</button>","after the lesson"],["по́сле рабо́ты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"после работы\">🔊</button>","after work"],["по́сле обе́да <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"после обеда\">🔊</button>","after lunch / in the afternoon"],["до уро́ка ↔ по́сле уро́ка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"до урока, после урока\">🔊</button>","before the lesson ↔ after the lesson"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm free after work. (a woman)","en":"По́сле рабо́ты я <b>свобо́дна</b>.","say":"После работы я свободна."},{"ru":"After lunch — coffee.","en":"<b>По́сле обе́да</b> — ко́фе.","say":"После обеда — кофе."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"по́сле уро́к","right":"по́сле уро́ка","why":"<b>по́сле</b> takes the genitive: уро́к → уро́к<b>а</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“after work”","opts":["по́сле рабо́ты","по́сле рабо́та","по́сле рабо́ту"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>по́сле</b> + genitive: рабо́та → рабо́ты."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “after the lesson” (уро́к → …):","accept":["после урока","после урока."],"placeholder":"по́сле…","explain":"<b>по́сле</b> + genitive: уро́к → уро́ка."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · «О́коло» и «у» — near, by, at","title":"<em>О́коло до́ма, у окна́</em> — pinning down a spot","paras":["Two more genitive prepositions of place. <b>о́коло</b> + genitive means “near, close to”: <b>о́коло до́ма</b> (near the house), <b>о́коло метро́</b> (near the metro). <b>у</b> + genitive means “by, right at”: <b>у окна́</b> (by the window), <b>у две́ри</b> (at the door).","You already know <b>у</b> from <b>у меня́</b> — it's the same word. <b>у</b> + a person can also mean “at someone's place”: <b>у Ива́на</b> (at Ivan's), <b>у ма́мы</b> (at Mum's). “I have” is really “by-me there-is”.","Both take the plain genitive: дом → до́м<b>а</b>, окно́ → окн<b>а́</b>, Ива́н → Ива́н<b>а</b>. With these you can pin down almost any spot in the city."],"audio":"Two more genitive prepositions of place. Okolo plus genitive means near, close to: okolo doma, near the house; okolo metro, near the metro. U plus genitive means by, right at: u okna, by the window; u dveri, at the door. You already know u from u menya — same word. U plus a person can also mean at someone's place: u Ivana, at Ivan's; u mamy, at Mum's. I have is really by-me there-is. Both take the plain genitive: dom becomes doma, okno becomes okna, Ivan becomes Ivana.","table":{"rows":[["о́коло до́ма <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"около дома\">🔊</button>","near the house"],["о́коло метро́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"около метро\">🔊</button>","near the metro"],["у окна́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у окна\">🔊</button>","by the window"],["у Ива́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у Ивана\">🔊</button>","at Ivan's (place)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"There's a café near the house.","en":"<b>О́коло до́ма</b> есть кафе́.","say":"Около дома есть кафе."},{"ru":"Emma's sitting by the window.","en":"Э́мма сиди́т <b>у окна́</b>.","say":"Эмма сидит у окна."},{"ru":"Today we're at Ivan's.","en":"Сего́дня мы <b>у Ива́на</b>.","say":"Сегодня мы у Ивана."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"о́коло дом","right":"о́коло до́ма","why":"<b>о́коло</b> takes the genitive: дом → до́м<b>а</b>. “Near” bends the place like every other genitive preposition."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“by the window”","opts":["у окна́","у окно́","у окну́"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>у</b> + genitive: окно́ → окна́."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“near the house”</b>","want":"о́коло до́ма","answer":"о́коло до́ма"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and order it out loud.","target":"Ко́фе без са́хара, пожа́луйста.","sub":"order a coffee without sugar","want":"кофе без сахара пожалуйста"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma stops at Lena's café before class and wants to do something nice. Ivan's behind the counter today. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, оди́н ко́фе для Ле́ны, пожа́луйста. Без са́хара.","ru":"Ivan, one coffee for Lena, please. No sugar."},{"who":"B","en":"Для Ле́ны? Как ми́ло! А для тебя́?","ru":"For Lena? How sweet! And for you? (ми́ло = sweet, nice)"},{"who":"A","en":"Для меня́ чай без молока́.","ru":"For me, tea without milk."},{"who":"B","en":"Ле́ны сейча́с нет. Она́ здесь по́сле обе́да.","ru":"Lena's not here right now. She'll be here after lunch."},{"who":"A","en":"Ничего́, я здесь, у окна́.","ru":"No worries, I'm right here by the window."},{"who":"B","en":"У окна́ хоро́шее ме́сто.","ru":"By the window's a good spot."},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо, Ива́н. Ты о́чень до́брый!","ru":"Thank you, Ivan. You're very kind!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for small kindnesses. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"для","ru":"for (the sake of) (+ genitive)","ex":"Э́то для тебя́."},{"en":"без","ru":"without (+ genitive)","ex":"Ко́фе без са́хара."},{"en":"по́сле","ru":"after (+ genitive)","ex":"По́сле уро́ка."},{"en":"о́коло","ru":"near, close to (+ genitive)","ex":"О́коло до́ма."},{"en":"у","ru":"by, at; at someone's place (+ genitive)","ex":"Я у окна́."},{"en":"са́хар","ru":"sugar","ex":"Чай без са́хара."},{"en":"ко́фе","ru":"coffee (never changes)","ex":"Ко́фе для Ле́ны."},{"en":"обе́д","ru":"lunch, midday meal","ex":"По́сле обе́да."},{"en":"уро́к","ru":"lesson, class","ex":"По́сле уро́ка домо́й."},{"en":"окно́","ru":"window","ex":"Ме́сто у окна́."},{"en":"ми́лый","ru":"sweet, nice, dear","ex":"Как ми́ло!"},{"en":"до́брый","ru":"kind, good-hearted","ex":"Ты о́чень до́брый."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["ко́фе для Ле́ны","a coffee for Lena"],["чай без са́хара","tea without sugar"],["по́сле уро́ка","after the lesson"],["о́коло метро́","near the metro"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“a coffee for Lena”","opts":["ко́фе для Ле́ны","ко́фе для Ле́на","ко́фе для Ле́ну"],"answer":0},{"q":"“coffee without sugar”","opts":["ко́фе без са́хар","ко́фе без са́хара","ко́фе без са́харе"],"answer":1},{"q":"“after work”","opts":["по́сле рабо́ту","по́сле рабо́та","по́сле рабо́ты"],"answer":2},{"q":"“near the house”","opts":["о́коло до́ма","о́коло дом","о́коло до́му"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which one uses <b>для</b> correctly?","opts":["Я помога́ю для ма́мы.","Э́то пода́рок для ма́мы.","Я звоню́ для ма́мы."],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's little morning routine at the café. Read it aloud.","title":"Ко́фе для Ле́ны","sentences":["Ка́ждое у́тро я в кафе́ о́коло до́ма.","У́тром — ко́фе для меня́, без са́хара.","По́сле уро́ка — ко́фе для Ле́ны.","Ле́на лю́бит чай без молока́, а я — ко́фе.","Моё люби́мое ме́сто — у окна́.","Э́то хоро́шее у́тро для меня́."],"translation":"Every morning I'm in the café near the house. In the morning, a coffee for me, without sugar. After class, a coffee for Lena. Lena likes tea without milk, and I like coffee. My favourite spot is by the window. It's a good morning for me."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — describe a small kindness or a habit.","prompt":"Use <b>для</b> (for whom), <b>без</b> (without what), <b>по́сле</b> (after what), and one of <b>о́коло / у</b> (where).","hint":"Each little word wants the genitive after it: для Ле́ны, без са́хара, по́сле уро́ка, о́коло до́ма. Remember <b>для</b> is “for a thing/person's sake”, not every English “for” 🙂","example":"По́сле рабо́ты я покупа́ю ко́фе для Ива́на — без са́хара. Кафе́ о́коло метро́. Моё ме́сто — у окна́. Э́то ма́ленький пода́рок для дру́га."}},"5":{"day":5,"week":"01","themeRu":"How much? How many?","themeEn":"Мно́го, пять книг","intro":"Time to count. Emma's at the market, and Russian numbers do something English never dreams of: the number reaches into the noun and changes its case. Say “five” and the thing you're counting bends to obey. This is the genitive's biggest job — and its hardest endings.","introAudio":"Time to count. Emma's at the market, and Russian numbers do something English never dreams of: the number reaches into the noun and changes its case. Say five and the thing you're counting bends to obey. This is the genitive's biggest job — and its hardest endings.","goals":["Use quantity words: <b>мно́го / ма́ло / не́сколько / ско́лько + genitive</b>","Count with cases: <b>2–4</b> take genitive singular, <b>5+</b> take genitive plural","Form the genitive plural: he-words <b>-ов / -ей</b>, she/it-words drop to <b>nothing</b>","Say real amounts: <b>пять книг</b>, <b>мно́го домо́в</b>, <b>нет друзе́й</b>"],"learned":["Used the quantity words <b>мно́го / ма́ло / не́сколько / ско́лько</b> with the genitive","Counted with cases: <b>2–4</b> genitive singular, <b>5+</b> genitive plural","Formed the genitive plural: <b>-ов / -ей</b> or a bare stem","Said real amounts: <b>пять книг</b>, <b>мно́го домо́в</b>, <b>нет друзе́й</b>"],"review":{"intro":"One last warm-up on the week's little words before we count. без, для, по́сле — each still wants the genitive.","introAudio":"One last warm-up on the week's little words before we count. Byez, dlya, poslye — each still wants the genitive.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All from earlier days.","q":"“coffee without sugar”","opts":["ко́фе без са́хара","ко́фе без са́хар","ко́фе для са́хара"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>без</b> + genitive: са́хар → са́хара."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“after the lesson”</b>","want":"по́сле уро́ка","answer":"по́сле уро́ка"},{"type":"choice","q":"“a letter from my brother”","opts":["письмо́ из бра́та","письмо́ от бра́та","письмо́ с бра́та"],"answer":1,"explain":"From a person → <b>от</b>: письмо́ от бра́та."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Quantity words + genitive","title":"<em>Мно́го вре́мени</em> — how much, how many","paras":["Words of amount — <b>мно́го</b> (a lot), <b>ма́ло</b> (little / few), <b>не́сколько</b> (several), <b>ско́лько</b> (how much / many) — all take the genitive: <b>мно́го вре́мени</b>, <b>ма́ло де́нег</b>, <b>ско́лько вре́мени?</b>","For something you can't count — water, time, money — use the genitive <em>singular</em>: <b>мно́го воды́</b>, <b>ма́ло вре́мени</b>. For things you <em>can</em> count, you'll need the genitive <em>plural</em> — <b>мно́го книг</b>, <b>не́сколько домо́в</b> — which is the next two rules.","One phrase you'll use daily: <b>ско́лько сто́ит?</b> — “how much does it cost?”; and <b>ско́лько вре́мени?</b> — “what's the time?”"],"audio":"Words of amount — mnogo, a lot; malo, little or few; nyeskolko, several; skolko, how much or many — all take the genitive: mnogo vryemeni, malo dyenyeg, skolko vryemeni. For something you can't count — water, time, money — use the genitive singular: mnogo vody, malo vryemeni. For things you can count, you'll need the genitive plural — mnogo knig, nyeskolko domov — which is the next two rules. One phrase you'll use daily: skolko stoit, how much does it cost, and skolko vryemeni, what's the time.","table":{"rows":[["мно́го вре́мени <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"много времени\">🔊</button>","a lot of time"],["ма́ло воды́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мало воды\">🔊</button>","little water"],["не́сколько книг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"несколько книг\">🔊</button>","several books"],["ско́лько вре́мени? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сколько времени\">🔊</button>","how much time? / what's the time?"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"I don't have much time today.","en":"Сего́дня у меня́ <b>ма́ло вре́мени</b>.","say":"Сегодня у меня мало времени."},{"ru":"There are a lot of cafés here.","en":"Здесь <b>мно́го кафе́</b>.","say":"Здесь много кафе."},{"ru":"How much does it cost?","en":"<b>Ско́лько</b> сто́ит?","say":"Сколько стоит?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"мно́го вре́мя","right":"мно́го вре́мени","why":"Quantity words take the genitive: вре́мя → вре́мени. Never the plain form."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“a lot of water”","opts":["мно́го вода́","мно́го воды́","мно́го во́ду"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>мно́го</b> + genitive: вода́ → воды́."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“little time”</b> (ма́ло + вре́мя)","want":"ма́ло вре́мени","answer":"ма́ло вре́мени"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “how much time / what's the time?”:","accept":["сколько времени","сколько времени?"],"placeholder":"ско́лько…","explain":"<b>ско́лько</b> + genitive: вре́мя → вре́мени."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The number picks the case","title":"<em>Две кни́ги, пять книг</em> — 1, 2–4, 5+","paras":["In A1 you saw this in miniature; here's the full rule. <b>1</b> keeps the plain (nominative) form: <b>одна́ кни́га</b>. <b>2, 3, 4</b> take the genitive <em>singular</em>: <b>две кни́ги, три кни́ги, четы́ре кни́ги</b>. <b>5</b> and up switch to the genitive <em>plural</em>: <b>пять книг</b>.","So the same noun wears three coats depending on the number in front of it: <b>кни́га</b> (1) → <b>кни́ги</b> (2–4) → <b>книг</b> (5+). The number literally governs the case.","One agreement note you know from A1: “two” is <b>две</b> before she-words (<b>две кни́ги</b>) and <b>два</b> before he/it-words (<b>два до́ма, два окна́</b>)."],"audio":"In A1 you saw this in miniature; here's the full rule. One keeps the plain nominative form: odna kniga. Two, three, four take the genitive singular: dvye knigi, tri knigi, chetyrye knigi. Five and up switch to the genitive plural: pyat knig. So the same noun wears three coats depending on the number in front of it: kniga for one, knigi for two to four, knig for five and up. The number literally governs the case. One agreement note from A1: two is dvye before she-words, dvye knigi, and dva before he and it-words, dva doma, dva okna.","table":{"rows":[["одна́ кни́га <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"одна книга\">🔊</button>","one book (nominative)"],["две / три / четы́ре кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"две, три, четыре книги\">🔊</button>","2 / 3 / 4 books (genitive singular)"],["пять книг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пять книг\">🔊</button>","five books (genitive plural)"],["два до́ма → пять домо́в <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"два дома, пять домов\">🔊</button>","two houses → five houses"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"I have two brothers.","en":"У меня́ <b>два бра́та</b>.","say":"У меня два брата."},{"ru":"There are five rooms in the flat.","en":"В кварти́ре <b>пять ко́мнат</b>.","say":"В квартире пять комнат."},{"ru":"Three cups of tea, please.","en":"<b>Три ча́шки</b> ча́я, пожа́луйста.","say":"Три чашки чая, пожалуйста."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"пять кни́ги","right":"пять книг","why":"From <b>5</b> up you need the genitive <em>plural</em>: пять книг, not the 2–4 form кни́ги."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“five rooms” — пять ___","opts":["ко́мнаты","ко́мнат","ко́мната"],"answer":1,"explain":"5+ → genitive plural: ко́мната → ко́мнат (drop to the bare stem)."},{"type":"choice","q":"“two houses” — два ___","opts":["до́ма","домо́в","дом"],"answer":0,"explain":"2–4 → genitive singular: дом → до́ма. (домо́в is for 5+.)"},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“three books”</b>","want":"три кни́ги","answer":"три кни́ги"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The genitive plural","title":"<em>-ов, -ей</em>, or nothing at all","paras":["This is the toughest ending set in Russian, so take it in three clean groups. <b>He-words ending in a hard consonant</b> add <b>-ов</b>: дом → <b>домо́в</b>, стол → <b>столо́в</b>, магази́н → <b>магази́нов</b>.","<b>Words ending in -ь or in ж / ш / ч / щ</b> add <b>-ей</b>: рубль → <b>рубле́й</b>, слова́рь → <b>словаре́й</b>, дверь → <b>двере́й</b>. And <b>she-words in -а and it-words in -о</b> simply <em>drop the vowel</em> to nothing: кни́га → <b>книг</b>, ко́мната → <b>ко́мнат</b>, ме́сто → <b>мест</b>, окно́ → <b>о́кон</b>.","A few high-frequency words are irregular and worth learning whole: друг → <b>друзе́й</b>, брат → <b>бра́тьев</b>, челове́к → <b>люде́й</b> — and after a number, <b>пять челове́к</b> (a special counting form)."],"audio":"This is the toughest ending set in Russian, so take it in three clean groups. He-words ending in a hard consonant add ov: dom becomes domov, stol becomes stolov, magazin becomes magazinov. Words ending in a soft sign or in zh, sh, ch, shch add yey: rubl becomes rublyey, slovar becomes slovaryey, dvyer becomes dvyeryey. And she-words in a and it-words in o simply drop the vowel to nothing: kniga becomes knig, komnata becomes komnat, myesto becomes myest, okno becomes okon. A few high-frequency words are irregular and worth learning whole: drug becomes druzyey, brat becomes bratyev, chelovek becomes lyudyey, and after a number pyat chelovek, a special counting form.","table":{"rows":[["дом → домо́в <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом, домов\">🔊</button>","houses (hard he-word: add -ов)"],["рубль → рубле́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рубль, рублей\">🔊</button>","roubles (soft -ь: add -ей)"],["кни́га → книг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга, книг\">🔊</button>","books (she-word -а: drop to nothing)"],["друг → друзе́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друг, друзей\">🔊</button>","friends (irregular)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"There are many houses here.","en":"Здесь <b>мно́го домо́в</b>.","say":"Здесь много домов."},{"ru":"I have no friends in Moscow yet.","en":"У меня́ ещё <b>нет друзе́й</b> в Москве́.","say":"У меня ещё нет друзей в Москве."},{"ru":"That's a hundred roubles.","en":"Э́то <b>сто рубле́й</b>.","say":"Это сто рублей."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"мно́го дом","right":"мно́го домо́в","why":"A hard he-word takes <b>-ов</b> in the genitive plural: дом → домо́в. The bare form is only for she/it-words (кни́га → книг)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“many friends”","opts":["мно́го дру́га","мно́го друзе́й","мно́го дру́ги"],"answer":1,"explain":"друг is irregular: its genitive plural is <b>друзе́й</b>."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “many houses” (дом → …):","accept":["много домов","много домов."],"placeholder":"мно́го…","explain":"Hard he-word → -ов: дом → домо́в."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Counting for real","title":"<em>Ско́лько сто́ит?</em> — amounts in real life","paras":["Now put it together at the market. Prices come in roubles: <b>пять рубле́й, сто рубле́й</b>. People come in a special form: <b>два челове́ка</b> (2–4), but <b>пять челове́к</b> and <b>мно́го люде́й</b> (5+ / many).","Watch the whole ladder for one noun: <b>оди́н рубль → два рубля́ → пять рубле́й</b>. And for “day”: <b>оди́н день → два дня → пять дней</b>.","The takeaway: don't choose the ending by the noun alone — first look at the <em>number</em> or <em>quantity word</em>, and let it pick the case."],"audio":"Now put it together at the market. Prices come in roubles: pyat rublyey, sto rublyey. People come in a special form: dva cheloveka for two to four, but pyat chelovek and mnogo lyudyey for five or more. Watch the whole ladder for one noun: odin rubl, dva rublya, pyat rublyey. And for day: odin dyen, dva dnya, pyat dnyey. The takeaway: don't choose the ending by the noun alone — first look at the number or quantity word, and let it pick the case.","table":{"rows":[["оди́н рубль → два рубля́ → пять рубле́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"один рубль, два рубля, пять рублей\">🔊</button>","1 → 2 → 5 roubles"],["оди́н день → два дня → пять дней <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"один день, два дня, пять дней\">🔊</button>","1 → 2 → 5 days"],["два челове́ка → пять челове́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"два человека, пять человек\">🔊</button>","2 → 5 people"],["мно́го люде́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"много людей\">🔊</button>","many people"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"How much is a cup of coffee?","en":"<b>Ско́лько сто́ит</b> ча́шка ко́фе?","say":"Сколько стоит чашка кофе?"},{"ru":"It's two hundred roubles.","en":"Э́то <b>две́сти рубле́й</b>.","say":"Это двести рублей."},{"ru":"There are a lot of people here today.","en":"Сего́дня здесь <b>мно́го люде́й</b>.","say":"Сегодня здесь много людей."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"пять челове́ков","right":"пять челове́к","why":"After a number, “people” uses the special zero form <b>челове́к</b>: пять челове́к. (“Many” uses <b>мно́го люде́й</b>.)"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“five roubles”","opts":["пять рубля́","пять рубле́й","пять рубле́ю"],"answer":1,"explain":"5+ → genitive plural: рубль → рубле́й."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“many people”</b>","want":"мно́го люде́й","answer":"мно́го люде́й"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and ask it out loud.","target":"Ско́лько сто́ит ча́шка ко́фе?","sub":"ask how much a cup of coffee costs","want":"сколько стоит чашка кофе"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma's shopping at the market with Ivan, counting as she goes. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, ско́лько сто́ит хлеб?","ru":"Ivan, how much is the bread?"},{"who":"B","en":"Три́дцать рубле́й. Недо́рого.","ru":"Thirty roubles. Not expensive."},{"who":"A","en":"Хорошо́. И ещё молоко́ — ско́лько?","ru":"Good. And milk too — how much?"},{"who":"B","en":"Со́рок пять рубле́й. Здесь всегда́ мно́го люде́й.","ru":"Forty-five roubles. There are always lots of people here."},{"who":"A","en":"Да, о́чень мно́го! У меня́ ма́ло де́нег.","ru":"Yes, so many! I don't have much money."},{"who":"B","en":"Не пробле́ма. У меня́ есть сто рубле́й.","ru":"No problem. I've got a hundred roubles."},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо! Ты покупа́ешь хлеб, а я — молоко́.","ru":"Thanks! You get the bread, and I'll get the milk."},{"who":"B","en":"Договори́лись!","ru":"It's a deal! (договори́лись = agreed, deal)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for counting and shopping. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"мно́го","ru":"a lot, many (+ genitive)","ex":"Мно́го люде́й."},{"en":"ма́ло","ru":"little, few (+ genitive)","ex":"Ма́ло вре́мени."},{"en":"не́сколько","ru":"several, a few (+ genitive)","ex":"Не́сколько книг."},{"en":"ско́лько","ru":"how much / how many","ex":"Ско́лько сто́ит?"},{"en":"рубль","ru":"rouble (Russian currency)","ex":"Пять рубле́й."},{"en":"де́ньги","ru":"money (a plural noun)","ex":"Ма́ло де́нег."},{"en":"друг","ru":"friend","ex":"Мно́го друзе́й."},{"en":"челове́к","ru":"person (plural: people)","ex":"Пять челове́к."},{"en":"день","ru":"day","ex":"Пять дней."},{"en":"сто́ить","ru":"to cost","ex":"Ско́лько сто́ит ко́фе?"},{"en":"магази́н","ru":"shop, store","ex":"Мно́го магази́нов."},{"en":"недо́рого","ru":"cheap, not expensive","ex":"Э́то недо́рого."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["мно́го люде́й","a lot of people"],["пять книг","five books"],["нет друзе́й","no friends"],["ско́лько сто́ит?","how much does it cost?"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“a lot of houses”","opts":["мно́го домо́в","мно́го до́ма","мно́го дом"],"answer":0},{"q":"“five books”","opts":["пять кни́ги","пять книг","пять кни́га"],"answer":1},{"q":"“two brothers” — у меня́ два ___","opts":["бра́та","бра́тьев","брат"],"answer":0},{"q":"“no friends” (нет + …)","opts":["нет дру́га","нет дру́ги","нет друзе́й"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I have little money.” — У меня́ ма́ло ___","opts":["де́ньги","де́нег","де́ньга"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma writes about her first trip to a Moscow market. Read it aloud — every amount is a genitive.","title":"На ры́нке","sentences":["Сего́дня я на ры́нке.","Здесь мно́го люде́й и мно́го магази́нов.","Хлеб сто́ит три́дцать рубле́й, а молоко́ — со́рок пять.","У меня́ ма́ло де́нег, но э́то недо́рого.","Я покупа́ю две кни́ги и пять я́блок.","В Москве́ у меня́ уже́ не́сколько друзе́й!"],"translation":"Today I'm at the market. There are lots of people here and lots of shops. Bread costs thirty roubles, and milk forty-five. I have little money, but it's cheap. I'm buying two books and five apples. I already have several friends in Moscow!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — count things in your world.","prompt":"Say how many of something you have or see (use a number, or <b>мно́го / ма́ло / не́сколько</b>), and ask <b>ско́лько сто́ит…?</b> about one thing.","hint":"Let the number choose the case: 1 → plain (одна́ кни́га), 2–4 → genitive singular (две кни́ги), 5+ → genitive plural (пять книг). The plural endings: -ов (домо́в), -ей (рубле́й), or nothing (книг) 🙂","example":"У меня́ два бра́та и мно́го книг. В Москве́ у меня́ уже́ не́сколько друзе́й. Сего́дня я на ры́нке: ско́лько сто́ит хлеб? Три́дцать рубле́й — недо́рого!"}},"6":{"day":6,"week":"01","themeRu":"What's the date?","themeEn":"Пе́рвое ма́я","intro":"A wall calendar hangs in Emma's new kitchen, and half of Russian life runs on it — holidays, birthdays, the first day of term. Today you learn to read and say any date. Two little forms do all the work: <b>сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я</b> for “today is the 1st”, and <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b> for “on the 1st” — and yes, that genitive tail is the same “of” you built all last week.","introAudio":"A wall calendar hangs in Emma's new kitchen, and half of Russian life runs on it — holidays, birthdays, the first day of term. Today you learn to read and say any date. Two little forms do all the work: syevodnya pervoye maya for today is the first, and pervovo maya for on the first — and yes, that genitive tail is the same of you built all last week.","goals":["Ask and give today's date: <b>Како́е сего́дня число́?</b> — <b>Сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я</b> (the date in the nominative)","Name all twelve months and bend them after a date: май → <b>ма́я</b>, сентя́брь → <b>сентября́</b>","Say when something happens with a bare genitive — no “on”: <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b>, <b>деся́того сентября́</b>","Tell someone your birthday: <b>Мой день рожде́ния — деся́того ма́я</b>"],"learned":["Asked and gave today's date: <b>Како́е сего́дня число́?</b> — <b>Сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я</b>","Named the months and bent them after a date: май → <b>ма́я</b>, сентя́брь → <b>сентября́</b>","Said when something happens with a bare genitive: <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b>, no “on”","Told someone your birthday: <b>Мой день рожде́ния — деся́того ма́я</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you counted things with the genitive plural — пять книг, мно́го домо́в. A quick look back at last week before we fill the calendar.","introAudio":"Yesterday you counted things with the genitive plural — pyat knig, mnogo domov. A quick look back at last week before we fill the calendar.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of this is last week's genitive — an old friend by now.","q":"«У меня́ мно́го ___» (books) — after мно́го you need the genitive plural:","opts":["кни́ги","книг","кни́га"],"answer":1,"explain":"мно́го always pulls the genitive plural, and кни́га loses its ending to a bare stem: <b>книг</b>. Мно́го книг."},{"type":"choice","q":"“from Moscow” (day 3):","opts":["из Москва́","из Москвы́","с Москвы́"],"answer":1,"explain":"из + genitive, and Москва́ → <b>Москвы́</b>. You use с (not из) only for на-places like с рабо́ты."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“a cup of tea”</b> (day 1)","want":"ча́шка ча́я","answer":"ча́шка ча́я"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Today's date","title":"<em>Како́е сего́дня число́?</em> — “today is the 1st of May”","paras":["Russian asks the date with a neuter question, because the word it's really asking about — <b>число́</b> (“date, day-of-the-month”) — is a neuter it-word: <b>Како́е сего́дня число́?</b>, literally “what today [is the] date?”","You answer in the plain nominative — the naming form: <b>Сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я.</b> “Today is the first of May.” The ordinal <b>пе́рвое</b> stays neuter to match число́, and the month steps into the genitive — <b>ма́я</b>, “of May” — exactly the “of” you built all last week.","So a date is really two words: an ordinal (пе́рвое, второ́е, тре́тье…) plus the month in the genitive. Master those two and you can say any day of the year."],"audio":"Russian asks the date with a neuter question, because the word it's really asking about — chislo, date or day of the month — is a neuter it-word: kakoye syevodnya chislo? Literally, what today is the date? You answer in the plain nominative, the naming form: syevodnya pervoye maya. Today is the first of May. The ordinal pervoye stays neuter to match chislo, and the month steps into the genitive, maya, of May, exactly the of you built all last week. So a date is really two words: an ordinal — pervoye, vtoroye, tretye — plus the month in the genitive.","table":{"rows":[["Како́е сего́дня число́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Какое сегодня число?\">🔊</button>","What's the date today?"],["Сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сегодня первое мая\">🔊</button>","Today is the 1st of May."],["Сего́дня второ́е ию́ня. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сегодня второе июня\">🔊</button>","Today is the 2nd of June."],["Сего́дня деся́тое сентября́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сегодня десятое сентября\">🔊</button>","Today is the 10th of September."]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"What's the date today? — Today is the 1st of May.","en":"Како́е сего́дня число́? — Сего́дня <b>пе́рвое ма́я</b>.","say":"Какое сегодня число? Сегодня первое мая."},{"ru":"Today is the 3rd of March.","en":"Сего́дня <b>тре́тье ма́рта</b>.","say":"Сегодня третье марта."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Сего́дня пе́рвый ма́й.","right":"Сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я.","why":"The ordinal is neuter to match число́ — <b>пе́рвое</b>, not пе́рвый — and the month bends to the genitive: май → <b>ма́я</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap just shows you where to look.","q":"«___ сего́дня число́?» — asking today's date:","opts":["Како́е","Како́го","Кака́я"],"answer":0,"explain":"число́ is a neuter it-word → <b>Како́е</b>. Како́го is the genitive (for “on what date?”), and кака́я is for she-words."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Today is the 5th of May.”</b>","want":"Сего́дня пя́тое ма́я","answer":"Сего́дня пя́тое ма́я"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “Today is the 1st of January”:","accept":["сегодня первое января","сегодня первое января."],"placeholder":"Сего́дня…","explain":"Ordinal + month-in-genitive: <b>Сего́дня пе́рвое января́</b>. янва́рь → января́."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Ordinals & the months","title":"<em>пе́рвое, второ́е…</em> and май → <em>ма́я</em>","paras":["Two small sets power every date. First the <b>ordinals</b> for the day: пе́рвое (1st), второ́е (2nd), тре́тье (3rd), четвёртое (4th), пя́тое (5th)… — they end in <b>-ое</b> (with soft <b>тре́тье</b> the one outlier) and stay neuter to match число́.","Then the <b>twelve months</b>, which you'll almost always meet already in the genitive (right after a date). Watch the stress — several months hop it onto the ending: <b>янва́рь → января́</b>, <b>сентя́брь → сентября́</b>, <b>октя́брь → октября́</b>.","For a two-word day like “the 25th”, only the <b>last</b> word becomes an ordinal: <b>два́дцать пя́тое</b> — literally “twenty (plain) + fifth”."],"audio":"Two small sets power every date. First the ordinals for the day: pervoye, vtoroye, tretye, chetvyortoye, pyatoye — they end in -oye, with soft tretye the one outlier, and stay neuter to match chislo. Then the twelve months, which you'll almost always meet already in the genitive, right after a date. Watch the stress — several months hop it onto the ending: yanvar becomes yanvarya, sentyabr becomes sentyabrya, oktyabr becomes oktyabrya. For a two-word day like the twenty-fifth, only the last word becomes an ordinal: dvatsat pyatoye.","table":{"rows":[["янва́рь → января́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"январь, января\">🔊</button>","January → of January (stress hops to the end)"],["март → ма́рта <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"март, марта\">🔊</button>","March → of March"],["май → ма́я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"май, мая\">🔊</button>","May → of May"],["сентя́брь → сентября́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сентябрь, сентября\">🔊</button>","September → of September (stress hops)"],["дека́брь → декабря́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"декабрь, декабря\">🔊</button>","December → of December (stress hops)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"the 5th of April","en":"<b>пя́тое апре́ля</b>","say":"пятое апреля","gloss":"апре́ль → апре́ля"},{"ru":"the 25th of December","en":"два́дцать <b>пя́тое</b> декабря́","say":"двадцать пятое декабря","gloss":"only the last word is an ordinal"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"пя́тое дека́брь","right":"пя́тое декабря́","why":"After the day-number the month must go genitive: дека́брь → <b>декабря́</b> (and the stress jumps to the very end)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The genitive of “May” (the month right after a date) is:","opts":["ма́е","ма́я","май"],"answer":1,"explain":"май → <b>ма́я</b> for “of May”. ма́е is the in-form (в ма́е), май is the plain naming form."},{"type":"choice","q":"“of January” — which one keeps the stress-shift honest?","opts":["я́нваря","января́","янва́ря"],"answer":1,"explain":"The stress hops all the way to the ending: янва́рь → <b>января́</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Today is the 2nd of April.”</b>","want":"Сего́дня второ́е апре́ля","answer":"Сего́дня второ́е апре́ля"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · “On” a date","title":"<em>Пе́рвого ма́я</em> — the bare genitive, no “on”","paras":["To say <em>on</em> a date — when something happens — Russian doesn't add a word for “on” at all. It just puts the whole date in the genitive: <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b>, “on the first of May”. Both parts bend — the ordinal takes <b>-ого/-его</b> (пе́рвое → пе́рвого), the month is already genitive (ма́я).","The matching question is itself genitive: <b>Како́го числа́?</b> — “on what date?” Answer it the same way: <b>деся́того сентября́</b>.","This is the trap of the day. English reaches for “on May 1st”, so your hand wants <b>в</b> or <b>на</b> — resist it. No preposition. The bare genitive already carries “on”."],"audio":"To say on a date — when something happens — Russian doesn't add a word for on at all. It just puts the whole date in the genitive: pervovo maya, on the first of May. Both parts bend — the ordinal takes -ovo, pervoye becomes pervovo, and the month is already genitive, maya. The matching question is itself genitive: kakovo chisla? On what date? Answer it the same way: desyatovo sentyabrya. This is the trap of the day. English reaches for on May first, so your hand wants v or na — resist it. No preposition. The bare genitive already carries on.","table":{"rows":[["Како́го числа́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Какого числа?\">🔊</button>","On what date?"],["пе́рвого ма́я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"первого мая\">🔊</button>","on the 1st of May"],["деся́того сентября́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"десятого сентября\">🔊</button>","on the 10th of September"],["три́дцать пе́рвого декабря́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тридцать первого декабря\">🔊</button>","on the 31st of December"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"When's the concert? — On the 1st of May.","en":"Когда́ конце́рт? — <b>Пе́рвого ма́я</b>.","say":"Когда концерт? Первого мая."},{"ru":"I have a lesson on the 1st of September.","en":"У меня́ уро́к <b>пе́рвого сентября́</b>.","say":"У меня урок первого сентября."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"в пе́рвое ма́я","right":"пе́рвого ма́я","why":"No “on” word, and no nominative: “on a date” is the bare genitive. в/на here are the English “on” sneaking back — drop it and bend the date."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“on the 1st of May” —","opts":["пе́рвое ма́я","пе́рвого ма́я","в пе́рвое ма́я"],"answer":1,"explain":"“On a date” = the bare genitive: <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b>. пе́рвое ма́я is “today IS the 1st”; в… is the English calque."},{"type":"choice","q":"Emma wrote «на деся́тое ма́я» for “on May 10th”. The right form is:","opts":["деся́того ма́я","в деся́тое ма́я","деся́тое ма́я"],"answer":0,"explain":"Drop the preposition and bend the ordinal: <b>деся́того ма́я</b>. деся́тое ма́я alone means “today is the 10th”."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Мой день рожде́ния — деся́того ма́я.","sub":"say when your birthday is (any date is fine)","want":"мой день рождения десятого мая"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Dates in real life","title":"<em>Когда́ твой день рожде́ния?</em>","paras":["Now put dates to work. Ask <b>Когда́?</b> (“when?”) and answer with a genitive date — no “on”: <b>Когда́ твой день рожде́ния? — Деся́того ма́я.</b>","You already carry a genitive inside <b>день рожде́ния</b> — literally “day of birth”. Russian loves this: <b>пе́рвое сентября́</b> (the first of September) is the day the whole country goes back to school.","Keep the two forms apart: <b>сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я</b> (nominative — today <em>is</em> the date) versus <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b> (genitive — <em>on</em> that date). Same day, two different jobs."],"audio":"Now put dates to work. Ask kogda, when, and answer with a genitive date, no on: kogda tvoy den rozhdeniya? Desyatovo maya. You already carry a genitive inside den rozhdeniya — literally day of birth. Russian loves this: pervoye sentyabrya, the first of September, is the day the whole country goes back to school. Keep the two forms apart: syevodnya pervoye maya — nominative, today is the date — versus pervovo maya — genitive, on that date. Same day, two different jobs.","examples":[{"ru":"When is your birthday? — On the 10th of May.","en":"Когда́ твой день рожде́ния? — <b>Деся́того ма́я</b>.","say":"Когда твой день рождения? Десятого мая."},{"ru":"Today is the 1st of September — the first lesson.","en":"Сего́дня <b>пе́рвое сентября́</b> — пе́рвый уро́к.","say":"Сегодня первое сентября — первый урок."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Когда́ день рожде́ния? — В деся́того ма́я.","right":"Когда́ день рожде́ния? — Деся́того ма́я.","why":"The genitive date already means “on the 10th”. Adding <b>в</b> is the English “on” creeping back — leave it out."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Когда́ уро́к? — ___ сентября́.» (on the 1st):","opts":["Пе́рвое","Пе́рвого","Пе́рвом"],"answer":1,"explain":"“On the 1st” = genitive: <b>Пе́рвого сентября́</b>. Пе́рвое would be “today is the 1st”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“When is your birthday?”</b>","want":"Когда́ твой день рожде́ния","answer":"Когда́ твой день рожде́ния"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “on the 3rd of March”:","accept":["третьего марта","третьего марта."],"placeholder":"тре́тьего…","explain":"Bare genitive, both parts bent: <b>тре́тьего ма́рта</b>. No “on” word."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma and Ivan are at the kitchen table with the new calendar, planning the month ahead. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Смотри́ на кале́ндарь: ско́ро большо́й пра́здник!","ru":"Look at the calendar: a big holiday is coming soon!"},{"who":"A","en":"А како́е сего́дня число́?","ru":"And what's the date today?"},{"who":"B","en":"Сего́дня два́дцать пя́тое апре́ля.","ru":"Today is the 25th of April."},{"who":"A","en":"Зна́чит, пе́рвое ма́я уже́ ско́ро.","ru":"So the 1st of May is soon. (зна́чит = so, that means)"},{"who":"B","en":"Да! А когда́ твой день рожде́ния?","ru":"Yes! And when is your birthday?"},{"who":"A","en":"Деся́того ма́я. А твой когда́?","ru":"On the 10th of May. And when's yours?"},{"who":"B","en":"Тре́тьего ма́рта. Уже́ бы́ло.","ru":"On the 3rd of March. It's already passed."},{"who":"A","en":"Тогда́ пода́рок для тебя́ уже́ гото́в!","ru":"Then your present is ready already! (тогда́ = then)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the calendar and the year. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ме́сяц","ru":"month","ex":"Год — э́то двена́дцать ме́сяцев."},{"en":"число́","ru":"date, day of the month","ex":"Како́е сего́дня число́?"},{"en":"кале́ндарь","ru":"calendar","ex":"Кале́ндарь на столе́."},{"en":"день рожде́ния","ru":"birthday","ex":"Ско́ро мой день рожде́ния."},{"en":"пра́здник","ru":"holiday, celebration","ex":"Пе́рвое ма́я — большо́й пра́здник."},{"en":"янва́рь","ru":"January","ex":"Пе́рвого января́ — Но́вый год."},{"en":"март","ru":"March","ex":"Восьмо́е ма́рта — пра́здник."},{"en":"май","ru":"May","ex":"До ма́я ещё далеко́."},{"en":"сентя́брь","ru":"September","ex":"Пе́рвого сентября́ — пе́рвый уро́к."},{"en":"дека́брь","ru":"December","ex":"Три́дцать пе́рвого декабря́ — Но́вый год."},{"en":"Но́вый год","ru":"New Year","ex":"Мы о́чень лю́бим Но́вый год."},{"en":"пода́рок","ru":"present, gift","ex":"Э́то пода́рок для дру́га."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English. Watch the difference between “is the 1st” and “on the 1st”.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["пе́рвое ма́я","the 1st of May (today IS)"],["пе́рвого ма́я","on the 1st of May"],["Како́е сего́дня число́?","What's the date today?"],["день рожде́ния","birthday"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on dates. If one bites, the rule above has your back.","items":[{"q":"“What's the date today?” —","opts":["Како́е сего́дня число́?","Како́го сего́дня число́?","Где сего́дня число́?"],"answer":0},{"q":"“Today is the 1st of May” (the date IS) —","opts":["пе́рвого ма́я","пе́рвое ма́я","ма́я пе́рвое"],"answer":1},{"q":"“on the 1st of May” (something happens then) —","opts":["в пе́рвое ма́я","на пе́рвое ма́я","пе́рвого ма́я"],"answer":2},{"q":"“Today is the 3rd of March” —","opts":["Сего́дня тре́тье ма́рта","Сего́дня тре́тьего ма́рта","Сего́дня три ма́рта"],"answer":0},{"q":"“September” right after a date is:","opts":["сентя́брь","сентября́","сентябре́"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's little list of dates that matter this year. Read it aloud — every date is one you can now make yourself.","title":"Ва́жные да́ты","sentences":["Сего́дня два́дцать пя́тое апре́ля.","Ско́ро мой люби́мый пра́здник — пе́рвое ма́я.","Пе́рвого ма́я в Москве́ большо́й конце́рт.","А деся́того ма́я — мой день рожде́ния!","Пе́рвого сентября́ у меня́ пе́рвый уро́к в университе́те.","Э́то о́чень ва́жные да́ты для меня́."],"translation":"Today is the 25th of April. Soon it's my favourite holiday — the 1st of May. On the 1st of May there's a big concert in Moscow. And on the 10th of May it's my birthday! On the 1st of September I have my first lesson at university. These are very important dates for me."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put the calendar into words.","prompt":"Name three dates that matter in your year and what they are, using genitive dates (ordinal + month, both bent, no “on”): пе́рвого ма́я, деся́того сентября́.","hint":"A date is ordinal + month, both bent: пе́рвого января́, деся́того ма́я. No little word for “on” — the genitive already says it 🙂","example":"Мой день рожде́ния — деся́того ма́я. Пе́рвого января́ — Но́вый год, мой люби́мый пра́здник. А пе́рвого сентября́ у меня́ пе́рвый уро́к."}},"7":{"day":7,"week":"01","themeRu":"Review 1","themeEn":"Повторе́ние №1","intro":"Six days, one case, and already a huge slice of Russian is yours. This is a victory lap, not a test: we gather the whole genitive — the endings, the little trigger words, counting, and dates — into one clear picture, then Эмма closes her first week over coffee. Nothing new today, just the quiet satisfaction of it all fitting together.","introAudio":"Six days, one case, and already a huge slice of Russian is yours. This is a victory lap, not a test: we gather the whole genitive — the endings, the little trigger words, counting, and dates — into one clear picture, then Emma closes her first week over coffee. Nothing new today, just the quiet satisfaction of it all fitting together.","goals":["Steer the genitive singular endings from memory: <b>-а/-я</b> for he/it-words, <b>-ы/-и</b> for she-words","Trigger the genitive after <b>нет</b>, <b>из/от/до/с</b> and <b>для/без/по́сле</b> without thinking","Count with confidence: <b>пять книг</b>, <b>мно́го домо́в</b>, <b>ма́ло вре́мени</b>","Give any date two ways: <b>сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я</b> and <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b>"],"learned":["Steered the genitive singular endings from memory: <b>-а/-я</b> and <b>-ы/-и</b>","Triggered the genitive after <b>нет</b>, <b>из/от/до/с</b> and <b>для/без/по́сле</b>","Counted with confidence: <b>пять книг</b>, <b>мно́го домо́в</b>, <b>ма́ло вре́мени</b>","Gave dates two ways: <b>сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я</b> and <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b>"],"review":{"intro":"No warm-up from a single day this time — the whole week is our warm-up. Five quick retrievals across everything you built.","introAudio":"No warm-up from a single day this time — the whole week is our warm-up. Five quick retrievals across everything you built.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all this week's genitive — pull it from memory.","q":"«У меня́ нет ___» (time):","opts":["вре́мя","вре́мени","времена́"],"answer":1,"explain":"нет always pulls the genitive: вре́мя → <b>вре́мени</b>. вре́мя is the plain form, времена́ is the plural."},{"type":"choice","q":"«ко́фе ___ са́хара» (without):","opts":["без","для","по́сле"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>без</b> = without (+ genitive): ко́фе без са́хара. для is “for”, по́сле is “after”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Ivan's car”</b>","want":"маши́на Ива́на","answer":"маши́на Ива́на"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"Type “from Moscow” (out of):","accept":["из москвы","из москвы."],"placeholder":"из…","explain":"из + genitive: <b>из Москвы́</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"«___ ма́я» (on the 1st):","opts":["Пе́рвого","Пе́рвое","Пе́рвом"],"answer":0,"explain":"“On a date” = bare genitive: <b>Пе́рвого ма́я</b>. Пе́рвое ма́я means “today is the 1st”."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The endings, one more look","title":"He/it-words <em>-а/-я</em>, she-words <em>-ы/-и</em>","paras":["One week, one case — and look how far it carries you. The genitive singular has two tidy sets of tails: he/it-words take <b>-а/-я</b> (брат → бра́та, ме́сто → ме́ста), she-words swap for <b>-ы/-и</b> (Москва́ → Москвы́, кни́га → кни́ги).","It does the “of” job in every direction: possession (<b>кни́га бра́та</b>), the link between two nouns (<b>центр Москвы́</b>), and the answer to <b>чей?</b> — which still agrees with the thing, never the owner.","One spelling habit rides along from A1: after г, к, х, ж, ш, щ, ч you write <b>-и</b>, never -ы — кни́ги, not “книгы”."],"audio":"One week, one case — and look how far it carries you. The genitive singular has two tidy sets of tails: he and it-words take -a or -ya, brat becomes brata, mesto becomes mesta; she-words swap for -y or -i, Moskva becomes Moskvy, kniga becomes knigi. It does the of job in every direction: possession, kniga brata; the link between two nouns, tsentr Moskvy; and the answer to chey, which still agrees with the thing, never the owner. One spelling habit rides along from A1: after g, k, kh, zh, sh, shch, ch you write -i, never -y.","table":{"rows":[["брат → бра́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брат, брата\">🔊</button>","brother → of the brother (he-word, -а)"],["письмо́ → письма́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"письмо, письма\">🔊</button>","letter → of the letter (it-word, -а)"],["Москва́ → Москвы́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Москва, Москвы\">🔊</button>","Moscow → of Moscow (she-word, -ы)"],["кни́га → кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга, книги\">🔊</button>","book → of the book (-и after г)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Ivan's car","en":"маши́на <b>Ива́на</b>","say":"машина Ивана"},{"ru":"the centre of the city","en":"центр <b>го́рода</b>","say":"центр города","gloss":"го́род → го́рода"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"центр Москва́","right":"центр Москвы́","why":"The second noun bends: Москва́ → <b>Москвы́</b>. The first noun stays put, the second takes the genitive."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap. A miss just points you at the rule.","q":"“The sister's room” — the owner's ending is…","opts":["ко́мната сестра́","ко́мната сестры́","ко́мната сестре́"],"answer":1,"explain":"сестра́ is a she-word → <b>сестры́</b>. Ко́мната сестры́."},{"type":"choice","q":"«___ э́то письмо́?» (письмо́ is an it-word):","opts":["Чья","Чей","Чьё"],"answer":2,"explain":"“Whose” agrees with the thing: письмо́ is an it-word → <b>Чьё</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Mum's house”</b>","want":"дом ма́мы","answer":"дом ма́мы"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “the centre of the city” (го́род → …):","accept":["центр города","центр города."],"placeholder":"центр…","explain":"го́род is a he-word → го́рода. <b>Центр го́рода</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The genitive magnets","title":"<em>нет, из/от/до/с, для/без/по́сле</em>","paras":["The genitive also springs up after a whole family of trigger words. <b>нет</b> and <b>не́ было</b> pull it every time — the missing thing is never the subject: <b>здесь нет ка́рты</b>, <b>у меня́ не́ было вре́мени</b>.","So do the little place-words <b>из / от / до / с</b> (out of, from, up to, off): <b>из Москвы́</b>, <b>от до́ма</b>, <b>до це́нтра</b>, <b>с рабо́ты</b>. Pair them up — <b>из</b> answers в-places, <b>с</b> answers на-places.","And <b>для / без / по́сле / о́коло / у</b> take it too: <b>для тебя́</b>, <b>без са́хара</b>, <b>по́сле уро́ка</b>, <b>о́коло до́ма</b>."],"audio":"The genitive also springs up after a whole family of trigger words. Nyet and nye bylo pull it every time — the missing thing is never the subject: zdyes nyet karty, u menya nye bylo vryemeni. So do the little place-words iz, ot, do, s — out of, from, up to, off: iz Moskvy, ot doma, do tsentra, s raboty. Pair them up: iz answers v-places, s answers na-places. And dlya, byez, poslye, okolo, u take it too: dlya tebya, byez sakhara, poslye uroka, okolo doma.","table":{"rows":[["нет ка́рты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нет карты\">🔊</button>","there's no map (нет + genitive)"],["из Москвы́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"из Москвы\">🔊</button>","from (out of) Moscow"],["с рабо́ты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с работы\">🔊</button>","from work (off a на-place)"],["без са́хара <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"без сахара\">🔊</button>","without sugar"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I don't have time.","en":"У меня́ нет <b>вре́мени</b>.","say":"У меня нет времени."},{"ru":"tea without sugar","en":"чай без <b>са́хара</b>","say":"чай без сахара"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Здесь нет ка́рта.","right":"Здесь нет ка́рты.","why":"нет always drags the genitive — the missing thing is not a subject: ка́рта → <b>ка́рты</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“from work” (work is a на-place) —","opts":["из рабо́ты","с рабо́ты","от рабо́ты"],"answer":1,"explain":"You go на рабо́ту, so you come <b>с рабо́ты</b>. из is for в-places, от is for people/points."},{"type":"choice","q":"«У меня́ нет ___» (money):","opts":["де́ньги","де́нег","де́ньгам"],"answer":1,"explain":"нет + genitive plural: де́ньги → <b>де́нег</b>. У меня́ нет де́нег."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“after the lesson”</b>","want":"по́сле уро́ка","answer":"по́сле уро́ка"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"У меня́ нет вре́мени.","sub":"tell a friend you have no time","want":"у меня нет времени"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Counting & dating","title":"<em>пять книг</em> and <em>пе́рвого ма́я</em>","paras":["Numbers steer the case. <b>2, 3, 4</b> take the genitive singular (<b>две кни́ги</b>, <b>три дня</b>), but <b>5 and up</b> — plus <b>мно́го / ма́ло / не́сколько / ско́лько</b> — take the genitive <b>plural</b>: <b>пять книг</b>, <b>мно́го домо́в</b>, <b>ма́ло вре́мени</b>.","That genitive plural is the trickiest set: some words end in <b>-ов/-ев</b> (домо́в), some in <b>-ей</b> (друзе́й), and many she/it-words end in a bare stem with no tail at all (книг, мест).","Dates ride the same case. <b>сего́дня пе́рвое ма́я</b> names today (nominative), while <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b> — bare genitive, no “on” — says when. Two forms, one calendar."],"audio":"Numbers steer the case. Two, three, four take the genitive singular — dvye knigi, tri dnya — but five and up, plus mnogo, malo, nyeskolko, skolko, take the genitive plural: pyat knig, mnogo domov, malo vryemeni. That genitive plural is the trickiest set: some words end in -ov or -ev, domov; some in -ey, druzey; and many she and it-words end in a bare stem with no tail at all, knig, myest. Dates ride the same case. Syevodnya pervoye maya names today, nominative, while pervovo maya — bare genitive, no on — says when. Two forms, one calendar.","table":{"rows":[["две кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"две книги\">🔊</button>","two books (2–4 → genitive singular)"],["пять книг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пять книг\">🔊</button>","five books (5+ → genitive plural)"],["мно́го домо́в <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"много домов\">🔊</button>","many houses (-ов ending)"],["пе́рвого ма́я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"первого мая\">🔊</button>","on the 1st of May (bare genitive)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I have little time.","en":"У меня́ ма́ло <b>вре́мени</b>.","say":"У меня мало времени."},{"ru":"On the 10th of September — my birthday.","en":"<b>Деся́того сентября́</b> — мой день рожде́ния.","say":"Десятого сентября — мой день рождения."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"пять кни́ги","right":"пять книг","why":"5 and up take the genitive <b>plural</b>: кни́га → <b>книг</b> (bare stem). пять кни́ги borrows the 2–4 form by mistake."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“five books” —","opts":["пять кни́га","пять кни́ги","пять книг"],"answer":2,"explain":"5+ → genitive plural, bare stem: <b>пять книг</b>. пять кни́ги is the 2–4 form."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“many houses”</b>","want":"мно́го домо́в","answer":"мно́го домо́в"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “on the 1st of May” (bare genitive):","accept":["первого мая","первого мая."],"placeholder":"пе́рвого…","explain":"Both parts bent, no “on”: <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b>."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"End of week one. Emma drops into Lena's café, where Ivan is already at a table. A small victory lap over coffee. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну как твоя́ пе́рвая неде́ля в Москве́?","ru":"So how's your first week in Moscow?"},{"who":"A","en":"Отли́чно! То́лько у меня́ совсе́м нет вре́мени.","ru":"Great! Only I have no time at all. (совсе́м = at all)"},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то Москва́! Хо́чешь ча́шку ко́фе?","ru":"That's Moscow! Want a cup of coffee?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, но без са́хара, пожа́луйста.","ru":"Yes, but without sugar, please."},{"who":"B","en":"А э́то что у тебя́?","ru":"And what's that you've got?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ма́ленький пода́рок для тебя́ и для Ле́ны.","ru":"A little present for you and for Lena."},{"who":"B","en":"Спаси́бо! А когда́ твой день рожде́ния?","ru":"Thank you! And when's your birthday?"},{"who":"A","en":"Деся́того ма́я. Уже́ ско́ро!","ru":"On the 10th of May. Soon now!"},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, у нас ещё оди́н пра́здник.","ru":"So we've got one more holiday. (зна́чит = so, that means)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words from your first week — the genitive family. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"без","ru":"without (+ genitive)","ex":"Ко́фе без са́хара."},{"en":"по́сле","ru":"after (+ genitive)","ex":"По́сле уро́ка я до́ма."},{"en":"о́коло","ru":"near, about (+ genitive)","ex":"Кафе́ о́коло до́ма."},{"en":"не́сколько","ru":"several, a few (+ gen. pl.)","ex":"У меня́ не́сколько книг."},{"en":"ма́ло","ru":"little, few (+ genitive)","ex":"У меня́ ма́ло вре́мени."},{"en":"вре́мя","ru":"time","ex":"У меня́ нет вре́мени."},{"en":"де́ньги","ru":"money","ex":"У меня́ ма́ло де́нег."},{"en":"неде́ля","ru":"week","ex":"Э́то моя́ пе́рвая неде́ля."},{"en":"вопро́с","ru":"question","ex":"У меня́ нет вопро́сов."},{"en":"пода́рок","ru":"present, gift","ex":"Пода́рок для дру́га."},{"en":"ко́фе","ru":"coffee","ex":"Ча́шка ко́фе, пожа́луйста."},{"en":"магази́н","ru":"shop","ex":"Э́то ключи́ от магази́на."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — one from each corner of the week.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["нет вре́мени","there's no time"],["из Москвы́","from Moscow"],["для тебя́","for you"],["пе́рвого ма́я","on the 1st of May"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions across the whole week. Eighty percent is a pass — and you're ready.","items":[{"q":"“the brother's book” —","opts":["кни́га бра́та","брат кни́га","кни́га брат"],"answer":0},{"q":"“there's no time” —","opts":["нет вре́мя","нет вре́мени","не вре́мя"],"answer":1},{"q":"“from work” (off a на-place) —","opts":["с рабо́ты","из рабо́ты","от рабо́ты"],"answer":0},{"q":"“for you” —","opts":["для тебе́","для тебя́","для ты"],"answer":1},{"q":"“without sugar” —","opts":["без са́харом","без са́хара","без са́хар"],"answer":1},{"q":"“five books” —","opts":["пять кни́га","пять кни́ги","пять книг"],"answer":2},{"q":"“many houses” —","opts":["мно́го до́ма","мно́го домо́в","мно́го дома́"],"answer":1},{"q":"“on the 1st of May” —","opts":["в пе́рвое ма́я","пе́рвое ма́я","пе́рвого ма́я"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's own week-one note, all in the genitive you now own. Read it aloud.","title":"Пе́рвая неде́ля","sentences":["Э́то моя́ пе́рвая неде́ля в Москве́.","У меня́ ма́ло вре́мени, но мно́го дел.","Я о́чень люблю́ ко́фе без са́хара.","Э́то кафе́ Ле́ны — здесь всегда́ хорошо́.","Пе́рвого сентября́ у меня́ пе́рвый уро́к в университе́те.","А деся́того ма́я — мой день рожде́ния, мой люби́мый пра́здник."],"translation":"This is my first week in Moscow. I have little time, but a lot to do. I love coffee without sugar. This is Lena's café — it's always nice here. On the 1st of September I have my first lesson at university. And on the 10th of May it's my birthday, my favourite holiday."},"essay":{"intro":"Close your own first week the way Emma did.","prompt":"Sum up your week: one thing you have a lot of and one you have little of (мно́го/ма́ло + genitive), one thing you don't have (нет + genitive), and one date coming up.","hint":"Lean on your genitive magnets: мно́го дел, ма́ло вре́мени, нет пла́нов, and a date like пе́рвого ма́я 🙂","example":"У меня́ мно́го дел и ма́ло вре́мени. Сего́дня у меня́ нет пла́нов на ве́чер. Но пе́рвого ма́я большо́й пра́здник — э́то уже́ ско́ро!"}},"8":{"day":8,"week":"02","themeRu":"To whom?","themeEn":"Кому́? Пишу́ дру́гу","intro":"Last week everything answered “of whom?” — this week we turn to the people on the receiving end. When Эмма writes to her mum, calls Иван, or hands Лена a bunch of flowers, each of those people steps into a new case: the <b>dative</b>, the case of the receiver. Its question is <b>кому́?</b> — “to whom?” — and you've secretly used it since A1, every time you said <b>мне нра́вится</b>.","introAudio":"Last week everything answered of whom — this week we turn to the people on the receiving end. When Emma writes to her mum, calls Ivan, or hands Lena a bunch of flowers, each of those people steps into a new case: the dative, the case of the receiver. Its question is komu — to whom — and you've secretly used it since A1, every time you said mnye nravitsya.","goals":["Point to the receiver with <b>кому́?</b> (“to whom?”) and answer in the dative: <b>пишу́ дру́гу</b>","Form the dative singular: he/it-words take <b>-у/-ю</b>, she-words take <b>-е</b>","Use the dative pronouns <b>мне, тебе́, ему́, ей</b> — the “to me / to you” you've had since A1","Handle the dative verbs <b>звони́ть, помога́ть, дари́ть</b> — the person is dative, not an object"],"learned":["Pointed to the receiver with <b>кому́?</b> and answered in the dative: <b>пишу́ дру́гу</b>","Formed the dative singular: <b>-у/-ю</b> for he/it-words, <b>-е</b> for she-words","Used the dative pronouns <b>мне, тебе́, ему́, ей</b>","Handled <b>звони́ть, помога́ть, дари́ть</b> with the person in the dative, not the accusative"],"review":{"intro":"One last genitive warm-up before we change cases — the “of / from / without” family you spent all week building. Cases don't retire; they just make room.","introAudio":"One last genitive warm-up before we change cases — the of, from, without family you spent all week building. Cases don't retire; they just make room.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is last week's genitive — quick and familiar.","q":"«У меня́ нет ___» (a car):","opts":["маши́на","маши́ны","маши́ну"],"answer":1,"explain":"нет + genitive: маши́на → <b>маши́ны</b>. маши́ну is the accusative."},{"type":"choice","q":"“without sugar” —","opts":["без са́хар","без са́хара","без са́харом"],"answer":1,"explain":"без + genitive: са́хар → <b>са́хара</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“on the 1st of September”</b>","want":"пе́рвого сентября́","answer":"пе́рвого сентября́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The case of the receiver","title":"<em>Кому́?</em> — “to whom?” <em>Пишу́ дру́гу</em>","paras":["Meet the case of the receiver. When something goes <em>to</em> someone — a letter, a call, a gift, a word — that person lands in the <b>dative</b>, and the question is <b>кому́?</b> (“to whom?”): <b>Я пишу́ письмо́ дру́гу.</b> — “I'm writing a letter to a friend.”","Two jobs sit in that one sentence: <b>письмо́</b> is the thing written (accusative — the object you met in A1), and <b>дру́гу</b> is the one it's going to (dative — the receiver). English often hides this with word order — “I write my friend a letter” — but Russian marks the receiver with an ending.","You've quietly used the dative since A1: <b>мне нра́вится</b>, <b>мне 20 лет</b> — that <b>мне</b> is “to me”. Today it becomes a system you can steer."],"audio":"Meet the case of the receiver. When something goes to someone — a letter, a call, a gift, a word — that person lands in the dative, and the question is komu, to whom: ya pishu pismo drugu, I'm writing a letter to a friend. Two jobs sit in that one sentence: pismo is the thing written, accusative, the object you met in A1, and drugu is the one it's going to, dative, the receiver. English often hides this with word order — I write my friend a letter — but Russian marks the receiver with an ending. You've quietly used the dative since A1: mnye nravitsya, mnye dvadtsat lyet — that mnye is to me. Today it becomes a system you can steer.","table":{"rows":[["Кому́ ты пи́шешь? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Кому ты пишешь?\">🔊</button>","Who are you writing to?"],["Я пишу́ дру́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я пишу другу\">🔊</button>","I'm writing to a friend."],["Я звоню́ ма́ме. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я звоню маме\">🔊</button>","I'm calling Mum."],["Пода́рок Ле́не. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Подарок Лене\">🔊</button>","A present for Lena."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Who are you writing to? — To a friend.","en":"Кому́ ты пи́шешь? — <b>Дру́гу</b>.","say":"Кому ты пишешь? Другу."},{"ru":"I'm writing a letter to Mum.","en":"Я пишу́ письмо́ <b>ма́ме</b>.","say":"Я пишу письмо маме."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я пишу́ письмо́ ма́ма.","right":"Я пишу́ письмо́ ма́ме.","why":"The receiver can't stay in its plain form: ма́ма → <b>ма́ме</b>. Кому́? — ма́ме, in the dative."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap just marks the spot to review.","q":"«Я пишу́ письмо́ ___» (to a friend) — the receiver goes in the dative:","opts":["друг","дру́га","дру́гу"],"answer":2,"explain":"Кому́? → dative: друг → <b>дру́гу</b>. дру́га is the genitive/accusative form."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm writing a letter to a friend.”</b>","want":"Я пишу́ письмо́ дру́гу","answer":"Я пишу́ письмо́ дру́гу"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"Type “to me” (the dative pronoun):","accept":["мне"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>мне</b> = “to me” — the dative you've owned since A1's мне нра́вится."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The endings","title":"He/it-words <em>-у/-ю</em>, she-words <em>-е</em>","paras":["The dative singular is one of the friendlier sets. <b>He-words and it-words</b> take <b>-у</b> (or <b>-ю</b> after a soft letter): брат → бра́т<b>у</b>, друг → дру́г<b>у</b>, Ива́н → Ива́н<b>у</b>, учи́тель → учи́тел<b>ю</b>.","<b>She-words</b> take <b>-е</b>: Ле́на → Ле́н<b>е</b>, ма́ма → ма́м<b>е</b>, сестра́ → сестр<b>е́</b>. That's the very same <b>-е</b> you know from the “in a place” form — в шко́ле, о рабо́те.","So the receiver just swaps its tail: to a he-word add -у/-ю, to a she-word add -е. Read the grid aloud once and let your ear expect it."],"audio":"The dative singular is one of the friendlier sets. He-words and it-words take -u, or -yu after a soft letter: brat becomes bratu, drug becomes drugu, Ivan becomes Ivanu, uchitel becomes uchitelyu. She-words take -ye: Lena becomes Lenye, mama becomes mamye, sestra becomes sestrye. That's the very same -ye you know from the in-a-place form, v shkolye, o rabotye. So the receiver just swaps its tail: to a he-word add -u or -yu, to a she-word add -ye.","table":{"rows":[["брат → бра́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брат, брату\">🔊</button>","brother → to the brother (he-word, -у)"],["друг → дру́гу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друг, другу\">🔊</button>","friend → to a friend (-у)"],["учи́тель → учи́телю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"учитель, учителю\">🔊</button>","teacher → to the teacher (soft, -ю)"],["Ле́на → Ле́не <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Лена, Лене\">🔊</button>","Lena → to Lena (she-word, -е)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm calling the teacher.","en":"Я звоню́ <b>учи́телю</b>.","say":"Я звоню учителю.","gloss":"учи́тель → учи́телю (soft -ю)"},{"ru":"a present for the sister","en":"пода́рок <b>сестре́</b>","say":"подарок сестре"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"пода́рок Ле́ну","right":"пода́рок Ле́не","why":"Ле́ну is the accusative (the object). The receiver of a gift is dative: Ле́на → <b>Ле́не</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“to Lena” (Ле́на is a she-word) —","opts":["Ле́ну","Ле́не","Ле́ны"],"answer":1,"explain":"She-words take -е: <b>Ле́не</b>. Ле́ну is accusative, Ле́ны is genitive."},{"type":"choice","q":"“to the brother” —","opts":["бра́ту","бра́та","бра́те"],"answer":0,"explain":"He-word → -у: <b>бра́ту</b>. бра́та is genitive/accusative, бра́те is the in-form."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“a present for the teacher”</b>","want":"пода́рок учи́телю","answer":"пода́рок учи́телю"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The dative pronouns","title":"<em>мне, тебе́, ему́, ей</em>","paras":["The pronouns have their own dative shapes, and you already own the first two: <b>мне</b> (to me), <b>тебе́</b> (to you), <b>ему́</b> (to him / to it), <b>ей</b> (to her).","They slot in wherever a receiver goes: <b>Я звоню́ тебе́.</b> (I'm calling you.) <b>Э́то пода́рок ему́.</b> (This is a present for him.) <b>Я пишу́ ей.</b> (I'm writing to her.)","One tidy echo: <b>ему́</b> covers both “to him” and “to it”, just as он and оно́ share forms elsewhere."],"audio":"The pronouns have their own dative shapes, and you already own the first two: mnye, to me; tebye, to you; yemu, to him or to it; yey, to her. They slot in wherever a receiver goes: ya zvonyu tebye, I'm calling you; eto podarok yemu, this is a present for him; ya pishu yey, I'm writing to her. One tidy echo: yemu covers both to him and to it, just as on and ono share forms elsewhere.","table":{"rows":[["мне <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мне\">🔊</button>","to me"],["тебе́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тебе\">🔊</button>","to you"],["ему́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ему\">🔊</button>","to him / to it"],["ей <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ей\">🔊</button>","to her"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm calling you.","en":"Я звоню́ <b>тебе́</b>.","say":"Я звоню тебе."},{"ru":"This is a present for him.","en":"Э́то пода́рок <b>ему́</b>.","say":"Это подарок ему."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я звоню́ тебя́.","right":"Я звоню́ тебе́.","why":"тебя́ is “you” as an object (genitive/accusative). Here you're calling <em>to</em> you → dative <b>тебе́</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Я звоню́ ___» (to you) —","opts":["тебя́","тебе́","ты"],"answer":1,"explain":"To you = the dative <b>тебе́</b>. тебя́ is the genitive/accusative, ты is the naming form."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm writing to her” — «Я пишу́ ___»","opts":["её","ей","ему́"],"answer":1,"explain":"To her = <b>ей</b>. её is “her” as an object; ему́ is “to him”."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я звоню́ тебе́.","sub":"tell a friend you're calling them","want":"я звоню тебе"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The dative verbs","title":"<em>звоню́ бра́ту, помога́ю сестре́</em> — not the accusative","paras":["A handful of very common verbs send their action <em>to</em> someone, so their person is always dative — even when English makes it look like a plain object: <b>звони́ть</b> (to call), <b>помога́ть</b> (to help), <b>писа́ть</b> (to write to), <b>дари́ть</b> (to give a gift), <b>говори́ть/сказа́ть</b> (to tell).","Here's the trap. English says “call my brother”, “help my sister” with no “to”, so your hand reaches for the accusative. Russian hears “give a call <em>to</em> the brother”, “give help <em>to</em> the sister”: <b>звоню́ бра́ту</b>, <b>помога́ю сестре́</b> — dative, not бра́та / сестру́.","When a gift is involved, both cases appear at once: <b>Я дарю́ Ле́не цветы́</b> — цветы́ is the thing (accusative), Ле́не is the receiver (dative)."],"audio":"A handful of very common verbs send their action to someone, so their person is always dative — even when English makes it look like a plain object: zvonit, to call; pomogat, to help; pisat, to write to; darit, to give a gift; govorit or skazat, to tell. Here's the trap. English says call my brother, help my sister with no to, so your hand reaches for the accusative. Russian hears give a call to the brother, give help to the sister: zvonyu bratu, pomogayu sestrye — dative, not brata or sestru. When a gift is involved, both cases appear at once: ya daryu Lenye tsvety — tsvety is the thing, accusative, Lenye is the receiver, dative.","table":{"rows":[["Я звоню́ бра́ту. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я звоню брату\">🔊</button>","I'm calling my brother. (звони́ть + dative)"],["Я помога́ю ма́ме. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я помогаю маме\">🔊</button>","I help Mum. (помога́ть + dative)"],["Я дарю́ Ле́не цветы́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я дарю Лене цветы\">🔊</button>","I'm giving Lena flowers. (dat. + acc.)"],["Он говори́т дру́гу пра́вду. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он говорит другу правду\">🔊</button>","He tells a friend the truth."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm calling my brother.","en":"Я звоню́ <b>бра́ту</b>.","say":"Я звоню брату."},{"ru":"Mum helps my sister.","en":"Ма́ма помога́ет <b>сестре́</b>.","say":"Мама помогает сестре."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я звоню́ бра́та.","right":"Я звоню́ бра́ту.","why":"звони́ть sends a call <em>to</em> someone — the person is dative: брат → <b>бра́ту</b>. бра́та is the accusative, wrong here."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ма́ма помога́ет ___» (the sister) — help takes the dative:","opts":["сестру́","сестры́","сестре́"],"answer":2,"explain":"помога́ть + dative: <b>сестре́</b>. сестру́ is the accusative, сестры́ the genitive — both wrong here."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm calling Ivan.”</b>","want":"Я звоню́ Ива́ну","answer":"Я звоню́ Ива́ну"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I help my brother” (help → dative brother):","accept":["я помогаю брату","помогаю брату"],"placeholder":"Я помога́ю…","explain":"помога́ть + dative: <b>бра́ту</b>, never бра́та."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"A quiet evening at the flat. Emma is at the table with her phone and some flowers, sorting out messages and gifts, while Ivan looks on. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, я пишу́ письмо́ ма́ме.","ru":"Ivan, I'm writing a letter to Mum."},{"who":"B","en":"А пото́м? Ты звони́шь Ле́не?","ru":"And after? Are you calling Lena?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, я звоню́ Ле́не и дарю́ ей цветы́.","ru":"Yes, I'm calling Lena and giving her flowers. (цветы́ = flowers)"},{"who":"B","en":"Как ми́ло! А мне?","ru":"How sweet! And me?"},{"who":"A","en":"Тебе́ — вот кни́га. Э́то пода́рок дру́гу.","ru":"For you — here's a book. It's a present for a friend."},{"who":"B","en":"Спаси́бо! Я то́же помога́ю тебе́ ка́ждый день.","ru":"Thanks! I help you every day too."},{"who":"A","en":"Пра́вда. Без тебя́ здесь тру́дно.","ru":"True. Without you it's hard here."},{"who":"B","en":"Тогда́ я звоню́ бра́ту, Макси́му.","ru":"Then I'll call my brother, Maxim."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for writing, calling and giving. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"кому́","ru":"to whom","ex":"Кому́ ты пи́шешь?"},{"en":"звони́ть","ru":"to call, to phone","ex":"Я звоню́ дру́гу."},{"en":"помога́ть","ru":"to help","ex":"Я помога́ю ма́ме."},{"en":"дари́ть","ru":"to give (as a gift)","ex":"Я дарю́ Ле́не цветы́."},{"en":"писа́ть","ru":"to write","ex":"Я пишу́ письмо́ бра́ту."},{"en":"говори́ть","ru":"to say, to tell","ex":"Он говори́т дру́гу пра́вду."},{"en":"сказа́ть","ru":"to say, to tell (once)","ex":"Он сказа́л мне пра́вду."},{"en":"письмо́","ru":"letter","ex":"Э́то письмо́ ма́ме."},{"en":"цветы́","ru":"flowers","ex":"Я дарю́ цветы́ сестре́."},{"en":"пра́вда","ru":"truth","ex":"Он говори́т пра́вду."},{"en":"учи́тель","ru":"teacher","ex":"Я помога́ю учи́телю."},{"en":"сосе́д","ru":"neighbour","ex":"Я звоню́ сосе́ду."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English. Every receiver is in the dative.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я звоню́ дру́гу","I'm calling a friend"],["Я помога́ю ма́ме","I help Mum"],["пода́рок Ле́не","a present for Lena"],["Кому́ ты пи́шешь?","Who are you writing to?"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the dative. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“I'm writing to a friend” — Я пишу́ ___","opts":["дру́гу","друг","дру́га"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Я звоню́ ___» (my brother) —","opts":["бра́та","бра́ту","бра́те"],"answer":1},{"q":"“to Lena” (a she-word) —","opts":["Ле́не","Ле́ну","Ле́ны"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I'm calling you” — Я звоню́ ___","opts":["тебя́","тебе́","ты"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I help my sister” —","opts":["помога́ю сестру́","помога́ю сестры́","помога́ю сестре́"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note about the people she keeps in touch with. Read it aloud — every receiver is in the dative.","title":"Кому́ я пишу́","sentences":["Ка́ждый день я пишу́ письмо́ ма́ме.","Ка́ждый ве́чер я звоню́ бра́ту и́ли дру́гу.","За́втра день рожде́ния Ле́ны, и я дарю́ ей цветы́.","Ива́н всегда́ помога́ет мне здесь, в Москве́.","А я помога́ю ему́: я хорошо́ зна́ю англи́йский.","А кому́ ты звони́шь и пи́шешь ка́ждый день?"],"translation":"Every day I write a letter to Mum. Every evening I call my brother or a friend. Tomorrow is Lena's birthday, and I'm giving her flowers. Ivan always helps me here in Moscow. And I help him: I know English well. And who do you call and write to every day?"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put the dative to work on real people.","prompt":"Name three people in your life and what you do for them, using the dative: who you call (звони́ть), who you help (помога́ть), who you write to or give something to (писа́ть / дари́ть).","hint":"The receiver takes the dative: he-words -у/-ю (бра́ту, дру́гу), she-words -е (ма́ме, Ле́не). Remember звони́ть and помога́ть take it too 🙂","example":"Ка́ждый день я звоню́ ма́ме и пишу́ бра́ту. Я ча́сто помога́ю дру́гу. А сестре́ я дарю́ цветы́ — за́втра её день рожде́ния."}},"9":{"day":9,"week":"02","themeRu":"I need to…","themeEn":"Мне на́до, к врачу́","intro":"Эмма wakes up feeling rotten — and that turns out to be the perfect way into today's Russian. To say “I have to go”, “I need a doctor”, “I'm off to the doctor's”, Russian keeps the person in yesterday's dative but drops the “I” entirely. <b>Мне на́до идти́</b> is literally “to me it's necessary to go.” Strange to an English ear at first — and then wonderfully simple.","introAudio":"Emma wakes up feeling rotten — and that turns out to be the perfect way into today's Russian. To say I have to go, I need a doctor, I'm off to the doctor's, Russian keeps the person in yesterday's dative but drops the I entirely. Mnye nado idti is literally to me it's necessary to go. Strange to an English ear at first — and then wonderfully simple.","goals":["Say what you have to do — impersonal, no “I”: <b>Мне на́до идти́</b>, <b>Тебе́ ну́жно отдыха́ть</b>","Ask and give permission: <b>Здесь мо́жно?</b> and its opposite <b>нельзя́</b>","Say you need a thing, with “need” agreeing: <b>Мне ну́жен врач</b>, <b>Мне нужна́ кни́га</b>","Head somewhere with <b>к + dative</b> (<b>к врачу́</b>) and use <b>по + dative</b> (<b>по телефо́ну</b>)"],"learned":["Said what you have to do, impersonally: <b>Мне на́до идти́</b>, <b>Тебе́ ну́жно отдыха́ть</b>","Asked and gave permission: <b>мо́жно</b> and <b>нельзя́</b>","Said you need a thing, with “need” agreeing: <b>Мне ну́жен врач</b>, <b>Мне нужна́ кни́га</b>","Used <b>к + dative</b> (<b>к врачу́</b>) and <b>по + dative</b> (<b>по телефо́ну</b>)"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you handed things to people in the dative — пишу́ дру́гу, звоню́ Ле́не. A quick round on that (and a little genitive) before today's dative goes modal.","introAudio":"Yesterday you handed things to people in the dative — pishu drugu, zvonyu Lenye. A quick round on that, and a little genitive, before today's dative goes modal.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's dative and last week's genitive — both fair game.","q":"«Я звоню́ ___» (my brother) — the receiver is dative:","opts":["бра́та","бра́ту","бра́те"],"answer":1,"explain":"звони́ть + dative: брат → <b>бра́ту</b>. бра́та is the accusative — the day-8 trap."},{"type":"choice","q":"“for you” (day 4, genitive) —","opts":["для тебе́","для тебя́","для ты"],"answer":1,"explain":"для + genitive: <b>для тебя́</b>. для тебе́ borrows the dative by mistake."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“a present for Lena”</b>","want":"пода́рок Ле́не","answer":"пода́рок Ле́не"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Have to, need to","title":"<em>Мне на́до идти́</em> — no “I” in it","paras":["You've said <b>Мне 21 год</b> since A1 — “to me [there are] 21 years”. That <b>мне</b> is the dative, and Russian uses the very same frame to say what you <em>have to</em> do: <b>Мне на́до идти́.</b> — literally “to-me [it is] necessary to go.”","Here's the big shift for an English speaker: there is <b>no “I”</b> in it. English builds “<b>I</b> must go / <b>I</b> need to go” around a subject; Russian makes it impersonal and puts the person in the dative. <b>на́до</b> and <b>ну́жно</b> are twins here — both “need to / have to” — and both take a plain infinitive: <b>Мне ну́жно рабо́тать.</b>","Change the person, keep the frame: <b>Тебе́ на́до отдыха́ть.</b> (You need to rest.) <b>Ему́ на́до спать.</b> (He needs to sleep.)"],"audio":"You've said mnye dvadtsat odin god since A1 — to me there are twenty-one years. That mnye is the dative, and Russian uses the very same frame to say what you have to do: mnye nado idti, literally to me it is necessary to go. Here's the big shift for an English speaker: there is no I in it. English builds I must go, I need to go around a subject; Russian makes it impersonal and puts the person in the dative. Nado and nuzhno are twins here — both need to, have to — and both take a plain infinitive: mnye nuzhno rabotat. Change the person, keep the frame: tebye nado otdykhat, you need to rest; yemu nado spat, he needs to sleep.","table":{"rows":[["Мне на́до идти́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне надо идти\">🔊</button>","I have to go."],["Тебе́ ну́жно рабо́тать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Тебе нужно работать\">🔊</button>","You need to work."],["Ему́ на́до спать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ему надо спать\">🔊</button>","He needs to sleep."],["Мне 21 год. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне двадцать один год\">🔊</button>","I'm 21 — the same dative мне."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I have to go, it's already late.","en":"<b>Мне на́до</b> идти́, уже́ по́здно.","say":"Мне надо идти, уже поздно."},{"ru":"You need to rest.","en":"<b>Тебе́ ну́жно</b> отдыха́ть.","say":"Тебе нужно отдыхать."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я на́до идти́.","right":"Мне на́до идти́.","why":"There's no “I” subject here. Necessity is impersonal + dative: <b>Мне</b> на́до идти́ — “to me it's necessary to go.”"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap. A wrong tap just points at the rule.","q":"“I have to go.” —","opts":["Я на́до идти́","Мне на́до идти́","Мне на́до иду́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Impersonal + dative + infinitive: <b>Мне на́до идти́</b>. No “I”, and the verb stays an infinitive (not иду́)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“You need to rest.”</b>","want":"Тебе́ ну́жно отдыха́ть","answer":"Тебе́ ну́жно отдыха́ть"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the sentence. на́до or ну́жно — either is fine.","q":"Type “He needs to sleep.”","accept":["ему надо спать","ему нужно спать"],"placeholder":"Ему́…","explain":"Dative person + на́до/ну́жно + infinitive: <b>Ему́ на́до спать</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · May and may-not","title":"<em>мо́жно</em> and <em>нельзя́</em>","paras":["The same frame gives you permission and prohibition. <b>мо́жно</b> means “may / it's allowed”; <b>нельзя́</b> means “may not / it's not allowed”. Each takes an infinitive: <b>Здесь мо́жно кури́ть?</b> — “May one smoke here?” <b>Нет, здесь нельзя́.</b>","Add a dative person to aim it: <b>Тебе́ нельзя́ спать сейча́с.</b> (You mustn't sleep right now.) <b>Мне мо́жно ждать здесь?</b> (May I wait here?) No “I” or “you” subject — just the dative and the infinitive.","Leave the person out and it goes general: <b>здесь нельзя́</b> — “you can't do that here”, for anyone. Half the signs in Russia are exactly this word."],"audio":"The same frame gives you permission and prohibition. Mozhno means may, it's allowed; nyelzya means may not, it's not allowed. Each takes an infinitive: zdyes mozhno kurit? May one smoke here? Nyet, zdyes nyelzya. Add a dative person to aim it: tebye nyelzya spat syeychas, you mustn't sleep right now; mnye mozhno zhdat zdyes? May I wait here? No I or you subject — just the dative and the infinitive. Leave the person out and it goes general: zdyes nyelzya, you can't do that here, for anyone. Half the signs in Russia are exactly this word.","table":{"rows":[["Здесь мо́жно кури́ть? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Здесь можно курить?\">🔊</button>","May one smoke here?"],["Здесь нельзя́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Здесь нельзя\">🔊</button>","You can't — it's not allowed here."],["Мне мо́жно ждать здесь? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне можно ждать здесь?\">🔊</button>","May I wait here?"],["Тебе́ нельзя́ спать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Тебе нельзя спать\">🔊</button>","You mustn't sleep."]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"May I wait here? — Yes, of course.","en":"<b>Мне мо́жно</b> ждать здесь? — Да, коне́чно.","say":"Мне можно ждать здесь? Да, конечно."},{"ru":"You can't smoke here.","en":"Здесь <b>нельзя́</b> кури́ть.","say":"Здесь нельзя курить."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Здесь не мо́жно кури́ть.","right":"Здесь нельзя́ кури́ть.","why":"“Not allowed” has its own word — <b>нельзя́</b> — and мо́жно never takes не in front of it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“May I wait here?” —","opts":["Я мо́жно ждать здесь?","Мне мо́жно ждать здесь?","Мне мо́жно жду здесь?"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dative + мо́жно + infinitive: <b>Мне мо́жно ждать здесь?</b> No “I”, and the verb stays an infinitive."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Not allowed” is one word:","opts":["не мо́жно","нельзя́","нет мо́жно"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>нельзя́</b> = “not allowed”. There is no “не мо́жно”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“You mustn't sleep.”</b>","want":"Тебе́ нельзя́ спать","answer":"Тебе́ нельзя́ спать"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Needing a thing","title":"<em>Мне ну́жен врач, мне нужна́ кни́га</em>","paras":["To say you need a <em>thing</em> (not to do something), Russian flips the picture. The <b>thing</b> becomes the subject in the nominative, the person stays dative, and the word for “need” <b>agrees with the thing</b>: <b>Мне ну́жен врач</b> (m), <b>Мне нужна́ кни́га</b> (f), <b>Мне ну́жно лека́рство</b> (n), <b>Мне нужны́ де́ньги</b> (pl).","Literally it's “to me [is] needed a doctor” — the doctor is what's “needed”, so ну́жен takes the doctor's gender. This is the trap: “need” agrees with the <em>thing you need</em>, never with you.","Compare the two “needs”: <b>Мне на́до отдыха́ть</b> (need to <em>do</em> — infinitive) versus <b>Мне ну́жен о́тдых</b> (need a <em>thing</em> — nominative). Same мне, two shapes."],"audio":"To say you need a thing, not to do something, Russian flips the picture. The thing becomes the subject in the nominative, the person stays dative, and the word for need agrees with the thing: mnye nuzhen vrach for a doctor, mnye nuzhna kniga for a book, mnye nuzhno lyekarstvo for medicine, mnye nuzhny dyengi for money. Literally it's to me is needed a doctor — the doctor is what's needed, so nuzhen takes the doctor's gender. This is the trap: need agrees with the thing you need, never with you. Compare the two needs: mnye nado otdykhat, need to do, an infinitive, versus mnye nuzhen otdykh, need a thing, in the nominative. Same mnye, two shapes.","table":{"rows":[["Мне ну́жен врач. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нужен врач\">🔊</button>","I need a doctor. (he-word)"],["Мне нужна́ кни́га. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нужна книга\">🔊</button>","I need a book. (she-word)"],["Мне ну́жно лека́рство. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нужно лекарство\">🔊</button>","I need medicine. (it-word)"],["Мне нужны́ де́ньги. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нужны деньги\">🔊</button>","I need money. (plural)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I need a doctor.","en":"Мне <b>ну́жен</b> врач.","say":"Мне нужен врач."},{"ru":"Do you need help?","en":"Тебе́ <b>нужна́</b> по́мощь?","say":"Тебе нужна помощь?","gloss":"по́мощь is a she-word → нужна́"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Мне ну́жен кни́га.","right":"Мне нужна́ кни́га.","why":"“Need” agrees with the thing needed: кни́га is a she-word → <b>нужна́</b>. ну́жен is for he-words like врач."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I need a book” (кни́га — a she-word) —","opts":["Мне ну́жен кни́га","Мне нужна́ кни́га","Мне нужны́ кни́га"],"answer":1,"explain":"“Need” agrees with the thing: кни́га → <b>нужна́</b>. ну́жен is for he-words, нужны́ for plurals."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I need money” (де́ньги — plural) —","opts":["Мне ну́жен де́ньги","Мне нужна́ де́ньги","Мне нужны́ де́ньги"],"answer":2,"explain":"A plural thing → <b>нужны́</b>: Мне нужны́ де́ньги."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I need a doctor” (врач — a he-word):","accept":["мне нужен врач"],"placeholder":"Мне…","explain":"врач is a he-word → <b>ну́жен</b>: Мне ну́жен врач."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · к and по + dative","title":"<em>к врачу́, по телефо́ну</em>","paras":["Two little prepositions live on the dative. <b>к</b> means “to / towards / to see” a person or place: <b>Я иду́ к врачу́.</b> (I'm going to the doctor's.) <b>к дру́гу</b>, <b>к ма́ме</b>. Watch the stress hop: врач → <b>к врачу́</b>.","<b>по</b> is the everyday “along / around / on / by”: <b>по у́лице</b> (along the street), <b>по телефо́ну</b> (on the phone), <b>по ра́дио</b> (on the radio). Both take the dative ending you learned yesterday.","So a whole errand strings together: <b>Мне на́до к врачу́</b>, and you tell a friend <b>по телефо́ну</b> that you're on your way."],"audio":"Two little prepositions live on the dative. K means to, towards, to see a person or place: ya idu k vrachu, I'm going to the doctor's; k drugu, k mame. Watch the stress hop: vrach becomes k vrachu. Po is the everyday along, around, on, by: po ulitse, along the street; po tyelyefonu, on the phone; po radio, on the radio. Both take the dative ending you learned yesterday. So a whole errand strings together: mnye nado k vrachu, and you tell a friend po tyelyefonu that you're on your way.","table":{"rows":[["к врачу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"к врачу\">🔊</button>","to the doctor('s) — stress hops to the end"],["к дру́гу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"к другу\">🔊</button>","to a friend's"],["по телефо́ну <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по телефону\">🔊</button>","on the phone"],["по у́лице <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по улице\">🔊</button>","along the street"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm going to the doctor's.","en":"Я иду́ <b>к врачу́</b>.","say":"Я иду к врачу."},{"ru":"We're talking on the phone.","en":"Мы говори́м <b>по телефо́ну</b>.","say":"Мы говорим по телефону."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я иду́ в врача́.","right":"Я иду́ к врачу́.","why":"“To a person” is <b>к</b> + dative, not в + accusative. в/на are for places; к врачу́ heads toward the person."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“to the doctor's” —","opts":["к врачу́","в врача́","к врач"],"answer":0,"explain":"к + dative, and врач → <b>врачу́</b> (stress on the end). в врача́ would be a place-preposition on a person — wrong."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“We're talking on the phone.”</b>","want":"Мы говори́м по телефо́ну","answer":"Мы говори́м по телефо́ну"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Мне на́до к врачу́.","sub":"say you need to go to the doctor","want":"мне надо к врачу"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma has woken up unwell. Ivan finds her at the kitchen table, still in her coat, looking miserable. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, мне пло́хо. Мне на́до к врачу́.","ru":"Ivan, I feel awful. I need to go to the doctor. (пло́хо = bad, unwell)"},{"who":"B","en":"Тебе́ нужна́ по́мощь? Я звоню́ врачу́.","ru":"Do you need help? I'll call the doctor. (по́мощь = help)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо. А мне мо́жно сейча́с идти́?","ru":"Thanks. And may I go now?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, но снача́ла тебе́ на́до отдыха́ть.","ru":"Yes, but first you need to rest. (снача́ла = first)"},{"who":"A","en":"Мне ну́жно лека́рство и ча́шка ча́я.","ru":"I need medicine and a cup of tea."},{"who":"B","en":"Хорошо́. Я иду́ в апте́ку, э́то ря́дом.","ru":"OK. I'm going to the pharmacy, it's nearby. (апте́ка = pharmacy; ря́дом = nearby)"},{"who":"A","en":"А пото́м мы говори́м по телефо́ну?","ru":"And then we'll talk on the phone?"},{"who":"B","en":"Коне́чно. Тебе́ нельзя́ мно́го рабо́тать сего́дня!","ru":"Of course. You mustn't work a lot today!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for needs, permission and a trip to the doctor. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"на́до","ru":"need to, have to (+ inf.)","ex":"Мне на́до идти́."},{"en":"ну́жно","ru":"need to (twin of на́до)","ex":"Тебе́ ну́жно отдыха́ть."},{"en":"мо́жно","ru":"may, it's allowed","ex":"Здесь мо́жно кури́ть?"},{"en":"нельзя́","ru":"may not, not allowed","ex":"Здесь нельзя́ кури́ть."},{"en":"ну́жен","ru":"needed (he-word)","ex":"Мне ну́жен врач."},{"en":"врач","ru":"doctor","ex":"Мне на́до к врачу́."},{"en":"лека́рство","ru":"medicine","ex":"Мне ну́жно лека́рство."},{"en":"по́мощь","ru":"help","ex":"Тебе́ нужна́ по́мощь?"},{"en":"апте́ка","ru":"pharmacy","ex":"Апте́ка ря́дом."},{"en":"к","ru":"to, towards (+ dative)","ex":"Я иду́ к дру́гу."},{"en":"по","ru":"along, on, by (+ dative)","ex":"Мы говори́м по телефо́ну."},{"en":"отдыха́ть","ru":"to rest","ex":"Мне на́до отдыха́ть."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English. Every line rides on the dative.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Мне на́до идти́","I have to go"],["Мне нужна́ по́мощь","I need help"],["к врачу́","to the doctor's"],["по телефо́ну","on the phone"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on needs and permission. If one bites, the rule above has your back.","items":[{"q":"“I have to go” (impersonal) —","opts":["Мне на́до идти́","Я на́до идти́","Мне на́до иду́"],"answer":0},{"q":"“Not allowed” — one word:","opts":["не мо́жно","нельзя́","нет мо́жно"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I need a book” (кни́га — a she-word) —","opts":["Мне нужна́ кни́га","Мне ну́жен кни́га","Мне нужны́ кни́га"],"answer":0},{"q":"“to the doctor's” —","opts":["в врача́","к врачу́","к врач"],"answer":1},{"q":"“on the phone” —","opts":["по телефо́на","по телефо́ну","по телефо́н"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's account of a rough morning — every line leans on today's dative. Read it aloud.","title":"У врача́","sentences":["Сего́дня мне пло́хо, и мне на́до к врачу́.","Ива́н говори́т: «Тебе́ нужна́ по́мощь, я звоню́ врачу́».","В апте́ке мне ну́жно лека́рство и вода́.","Врач говори́т, что мне нельзя́ мно́го рабо́тать.","Мне на́до отдыха́ть и спать. Э́то тру́дно для меня́!","За́втра мне мо́жно идти́ на рабо́ту."],"translation":"Today I feel unwell, and I need to go to the doctor. Ivan says: “You need help, I'll call the doctor.” At the pharmacy I need medicine and water. The doctor says I mustn't work a lot. I need to rest and sleep. That's hard for me! Tomorrow I can go to work."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — say what today asks of you.","prompt":"Say what you need today: one thing you have to do (на́до/ну́жно + infinitive), one thing you need (ну́жен/нужна́/ну́жно/нужны́ + a thing), and one place or person you're heading to (к + dative).","hint":"“Have to do” = на́до + infinitive (мне на́до рабо́тать). “Need a thing” = ну́жен/нужна́/нужны́ agreeing with the thing (мне нужна́ кни́га). And к + dative for where you're off to 🙂","example":"Сего́дня мне на́до рабо́тать, но снача́ла мне ну́жен ко́фе. Пото́м мне на́до к врачу́. А по́сле рабо́ты мне ну́жен о́тдых."}},"10":{"day":10,"week":"02","themeRu":"With whom? With what?","themeEn":"С кем? С дру́гом","intro":"Here is a case A1 never showed you — not even in disguise. The <b>instrumental</b> answers <b>с кем?</b> (with whom?) and <b>с чем?</b> (with what?), and it does two jobs at once: it names the friend you're <em>with</em> and the pen you write <em>with</em>. Эмма is planning a trip across Moscow today — the perfect place to meet it.","introAudio":"Here is a case A1 never showed you — not even in disguise. The instrumental answers s kyem (with whom?) and s chyem (with what?), and it does two jobs at once: it names the friend you're with and the pen you write with. Emma is planning a trip across Moscow today — the perfect place to meet it.","goals":["Say who you do things with: <b>с дру́гом</b>, <b>с сестро́й</b> — “with” + the instrumental","Form the instrumental: he/it-words take <b>-ом/-ем</b>, she-words <b>-ой/-ей</b>, plurals <b>-ами/-ями</b>","Say how or with what — the bare instrumental, no “with”: <b>пишу́ ру́чкой</b>, <b>е́ду авто́бусом</b>","Tell company from tool: <b>с</b> for the people you're with, nothing for the tool you use"],"learned":["Said who you do things with: <b>с дру́гом</b>, <b>с сестро́й</b> — “with” + the instrumental","Formed the instrumental: <b>-ом/-ем</b> for he/it-words, <b>-ой/-ей</b> for she-words, <b>-ами/-ями</b> for plurals","Said how or with what — the bare instrumental: <b>пишу́ ру́чкой</b>, <b>е́ду авто́бусом</b>","Told company from tool: <b>с</b> for people, nothing for the tool"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on the two cases already living in your head — the genitive (“of / from”) and the dative (“to whom”). The instrumental is about to join them.","introAudio":"One minute on the two cases already living in your head — the genitive, of or from, and the dative, to whom. The instrumental is about to join them.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all earlier material — treat it as a warm-up.","q":"“I'm phoning my brother.” — Я звоню́ ___","opts":["бра́та","бра́ту","брат"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>звони́ть</b> takes the dative — “to whom”: <b>бра́ту</b>. бра́та would be the genitive/accusative."},{"type":"choice","q":"“She's from Moscow.” — Она́ ___","opts":["из Москва́","из Москвы́","из Москву́"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>из</b> pulls the genitive: Москва́ → <b>из Москвы́</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm phoning Mum.”</b>","want":"Я звоню́ ма́ме","answer":"Я звоню́ ма́ме"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · With whom? — с кем?","title":"<em>С дру́гом, с сестро́й</em> — “with” a person","paras":["When you do something together with a person, Russian uses <b>с</b> (“with”) — and the person steps into a brand-new case, the <b>instrumental</b>. He-words add <b>-ом</b>: друг → <b>с дру́гом</b>, Ива́н → <b>с Ива́ном</b>. She-words swap their -а for <b>-ой</b>: сестра́ → <b>с сестро́й</b>, ма́ма → <b>с ма́мой</b>.","The question word is <b>с кем?</b> — “with whom?”. Ask it, and the answer is <b>с</b> + this new ending: <b>С кем ты в кафе́? — С Ле́ной.</b>","The pronouns are worth learning as a little set: <b>со мной</b> (with me), <b>с тобо́й</b> (with you), <b>с ним / с ней</b> (with him / her), <b>с на́ми</b> (with us). Notice <b>со</b> мной grows an extra vowel — со, not с — just to be easy to say."],"audio":"When you do something together with a person, Russian uses s, with, and the person steps into a brand-new case, the instrumental. He-words add -om: drug becomes s drugom, Ivan becomes s Ivanom. She-words swap their -a for -oy: sestra becomes s sestroy, mama becomes s mamoy. The question word is s kyem, with whom. Ask it, and the answer is s plus this new ending. The pronouns come as a set: so mnoy with me, s toboy with you, s nim or s nyey with him or her, s nami with us. So mnoy grows an extra vowel just to be easy to say.","table":{"rows":[["с дру́гом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с другом\">🔊</button>","with a friend (he-word, -ом)"],["с Ива́ном <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с Иваном\">🔊</button>","with Ivan (name, -ом)"],["с сестро́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с сестрой\">🔊</button>","with a sister (she-word, -ой)"],["со мной <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"со мной\">🔊</button>","with me (pronoun)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Who are you at the café with? — With Lena.","en":"С кем ты в кафе́? — <b>С Ле́ной</b>.","say":"С кем ты в кафе? С Леной."},{"ru":"I'm going to the centre with Ivan.","en":"Я е́ду в центр <b>с Ива́ном</b>.","say":"Я еду в центр с Иваном."},{"ru":"Are you with me or with them?","en":"Ты <b>со мной</b> и́ли <b>с ни́ми</b>?","say":"Ты со мной или с ними?","gloss":"со мной, с ни́ми — instrumental pronouns"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я е́ду в центр с Ива́н.","right":"Я е́ду в центр с Ива́ном.","why":"After <b>с</b> the person must take the instrumental ending: Ива́н → <b>Ива́ном</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. A wrong tap just shows you the note underneath — no harm done.","q":"“I'm at the café with a friend.” — Я в кафе́ ___","opts":["с друг","с дру́гом","с дру́га"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>с</b> + the instrumental: друг → <b>с дру́гом</b>. с дру́га would be the genitive."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“with a sister”</b>","want":"с сестро́й","answer":"с сестро́й"},{"type":"choice","q":"“With whom are you going?” — the question word is…","opts":["С кем?","С чем?","Кому́?"],"answer":0,"explain":"People → <b>с кем?</b> (with whom). с чем? is “with what”; кому́? is “to whom” (the dative)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The instrumental endings","title":"He/it-words <em>-ом/-ем</em>, she-words <em>-ой/-ей</em>, plurals <em>-ами/-ями</em>","paras":["Here is the whole case on one card. <b>He-words and it-words</b> add <b>-ом</b> (or <b>-ем</b> after a soft letter): брат → <b>бра́том</b>, окно́ → <b>окно́м</b>, чай → <b>ча́ем</b>. <b>She-words</b> trade -а/-я for <b>-ой</b> (or <b>-ей</b>): ма́ма → <b>ма́мой</b>, Ка́тя → <b>Ка́тей</b>. <b>Plurals</b> take <b>-ами</b> (or -ями): друзья́ → <b>друзья́ми</b>.","The soft/hushing choice mirrors A1 spelling: after a soft consonant or ж, ш, ч, щ, ц you lean to <b>-ем / -ей</b> when the ending is unstressed. Don't over-drill it — your ear will settle it with use.","Read the grid aloud once. You're not memorising a table — you're teaching your ear to hear that <b>-ом / -ой</b> tail as “by means of / together with”."],"audio":"Here is the whole case on one card. He-words and it-words add -om, or -yem after a soft letter: brat becomes bratom, okno becomes oknom, chay becomes chayem. She-words trade their ending for -oy, or -yey: mama becomes mamoy, Katya becomes Katyey. Plurals take -ami, or -yami: druzya becomes druzyami. The soft choice mirrors A1 spelling — after a soft consonant or the hushing letters you lean to -yem or -yey when the ending is unstressed. Read the grid aloud once: you're teaching your ear to hear that tail as by-means-of or together-with.","table":{"rows":[["брат → бра́том <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брат, братом\">🔊</button>","he-word, -ом"],["окно́ → окно́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"окно, окном\">🔊</button>","it-word, -ом"],["ма́ма → ма́мой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мама, мамой\">🔊</button>","she-word, -ой"],["друзья́ → друзья́ми <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друзья, друзьями\">🔊</button>","plural, -ами"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"with Mum and Dad","en":"<b>с ма́мой</b> и <b>с па́пой</b>","say":"с мамой и с папой"},{"ru":"with friends","en":"<b>с друзья́ми</b>","say":"с друзьями","gloss":"друзья́ → друзья́ми (plural)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"с ма́ма","right":"с ма́мой","why":"A she-word swaps -а for <b>-ой</b>: ма́ма → <b>ма́мой</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“with parents” — с ___ (роди́тели)","opts":["с роди́тели","с роди́телями","с роди́телей"],"answer":1,"explain":"A plural takes <b>-ями</b>: роди́тели → <b>роди́телями</b>. с роди́тели keeps the nominative; с роди́телей is the genitive."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are optional — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “with friends” (друзья́ → …):","accept":["с друзьями","с друзьями."],"placeholder":"с…","explain":"друзья́ → <b>друзья́ми</b>, the plural instrumental ending -ями."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which ending does a she-word take? — ма́ма → ___","opts":["ма́мой","ма́мом","ма́мами"],"answer":0,"explain":"She-words take <b>-ой</b>: <b>ма́мой</b>. -ом is for he/it-words, -ами is the plural."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · How? With what? — no “with” at all","title":"<em>Пишу́ ру́чкой, е́ду авто́бусом</em> — the bare instrumental","paras":["Now the surprise. When the noun is the <b>tool or means</b> you use — the pen you write with, the bus you travel by — Russian uses the very same instrumental, but with <b>no preposition at all</b>. пишу́ <b>ру́чкой</b> = “I write with a pen”. е́ду <b>авто́бусом</b> = “I go by bus”.","This is the day's trap. English reaches for a little word — <em>with</em> a pen, <em>by</em> bus — and there is nothing here to translate it into. The bare ending <em>is</em> the “with / by”. Slip in a <b>с</b> and it turns wrong.","So the same shape does two jobs, split by one word: <b>с ру́чкой</b> would mean “carrying a pen around” (company!), while <b>ру́чкой</b> means “using a pen” (tool). For tools and transport, drop the <b>с</b>."],"audio":"Now the surprise. When the noun is the tool or means you use — the pen you write with, the bus you travel by — Russian uses the very same instrumental, but with no preposition at all. Pishu ruchkoy means I write with a pen. Yedu avtobusom means I go by bus. This is the day's trap. English reaches for a little word — with a pen, by bus — and there's nothing here to translate it into. The bare ending is the with or by. Slip in an s and it turns wrong. The same shape does two jobs: s ruchkoy would mean carrying a pen around, company, while ruchkoy means using a pen, tool. For tools and transport, drop the s.","table":{"rows":[["пишу́ ру́чкой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пишу ручкой\">🔊</button>","I write with a pen (tool)"],["е́ду авто́бусом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"еду автобусом\">🔊</button>","I go by bus (means)"],["ем ви́лкой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ем вилкой\">🔊</button>","I eat with a fork (tool)"],["е́ду по́ездом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"еду поездом\">🔊</button>","I go by train (means)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"How do you write — with a pen or a pencil?","en":"Чем ты пи́шешь — <b>ру́чкой</b> и́ли <b>карандашо́м</b>?","say":"Чем ты пишешь ручкой или карандашом?"},{"ru":"I go to work by bus.","en":"Я е́ду на рабо́ту <b>авто́бусом</b>.","say":"Я еду на работу автобусом."},{"ru":"We eat soup with a spoon.","en":"Суп мы еди́м <b>ло́жкой</b>.","say":"Суп мы едим ложкой."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я пишу́ с ру́чкой.","right":"Я пишу́ ру́чкой.","why":"The tool takes the <b>bare</b> instrumental — no с. с ру́чкой would mean “carrying a pen”, not writing with one."},{"wrong":"Я е́ду на авто́бус.","right":"Я е́ду авто́бусом.","why":"“by bus” is the bare instrumental <b>авто́бусом</b>. (на авто́бус would be “onto the bus”.)"}],"mnemonic":"Tool or wheels? Drop the с. A person to join? Keep the с.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I write with a pen.” — Я пишу́ ___","opts":["с ру́чкой","ру́чкой","ру́чку"],"answer":1,"explain":"The tool is the <b>bare</b> instrumental: <b>ру́чкой</b>, no с. с ру́чкой = carrying a pen; ру́чку is the accusative."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I go by bus.”</b>","want":"Я е́ду авто́бусом","answer":"Я е́ду авто́бусом"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. No preposition before авто́бусом — the ending itself is the “by”.","target":"Я е́ду в центр авто́бусом.","sub":"say how you get to the centre — by bus","want":"Я еду в центр автобусом"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · One case, two jobs","title":"<em>Ко́фе с молоко́м</em> vs <em>пишу́ ру́чкой</em>","paras":["Let the single word <b>с</b> sort them for you. <b>Together-with</b> — company, or one thing accompanying another — keeps <b>с</b>: ко́фе <b>с молоко́м</b> (coffee with milk), хлеб <b>с ма́слом</b> (bread with butter), я <b>с дру́гом</b> (me with a friend).","<b>By means of</b> — a tool, a vehicle — goes <b>bare</b>: пишу́ <b>ру́чкой</b>, е́ду <b>авто́бусом</b>. Same endings, no с.","A tidy test: could you slip in “together with”? Then keep <b>с</b>. Could you say “by means of / using”? Then drop it."],"audio":"Let the single word s sort them for you. Together-with — company, or one thing accompanying another — keeps s: kofye s molokom, coffee with milk; hlyeb s maslom, bread with butter; ya s drugom, me with a friend. By means of — a tool, a vehicle — goes bare: pishu ruchkoy, yedu avtobusom. Same endings, no s. A tidy test: could you slip in together-with? Keep s. Could you say by-means-of, or using? Drop it.","examples":[{"ru":"A coffee with milk, please.","en":"<b>Ко́фе с молоко́м</b>, пожа́луйста.","say":"Кофе с молоком, пожалуйста."},{"ru":"Emma writes to Ivan with a pen.","en":"Э́мма пи́шет Ива́ну <b>ру́чкой</b>.","say":"Эмма пишет Ивану ручкой.","gloss":"Ива́ну = to Ivan (dative); ру́чкой = with a pen (bare)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"ко́фе молоко́м","right":"ко́фе с молоко́м","why":"Milk here <b>accompanies</b> the coffee — that's company, so keep <b>с</b>: ко́фе с молоко́м."}],"tasks":[{"type":"type","q":"Type “coffee with milk”:","accept":["кофе с молоком","кофе с молоком."],"placeholder":"ко́фе…","explain":"Milk accompanies the coffee → keep <b>с</b>: ко́фе с молоко́м."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“bread with butter”</b>","want":"хлеб с ма́слом","answer":"хлеб с ма́слом"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ivan is planning a museum trip. Emma wants to know who's coming and how they'll get there. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, за́втра мы е́дем в музе́й.","ru":"Emma, tomorrow we're going to a museum."},{"who":"A","en":"Здо́рово! С кем?","ru":"Great! With whom?"},{"who":"B","en":"Со мной, с Ле́ной и с бра́том.","ru":"With me, with Lena and with our brother."},{"who":"A","en":"А как? Авто́бусом?","ru":"And how? By bus?"},{"who":"B","en":"Мо́жно авто́бусом, но я люблю́ метро́.","ru":"We can go by bus, but I like the metro."},{"who":"A","en":"Хорошо́, е́дем на метро́ вме́сте.","ru":"Good, we'll go by metro together."},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то наш брат Макси́м. Ты его́ зна́ешь.","ru":"That is our brother Maxim. You know him."},{"who":"A","en":"Макси́м? Коне́чно! Пи́шем ему́ сейча́с.","ru":"Maxim? Of course! Let's write to him now."},{"who":"B","en":"Дава́й. Где моя́ ру́чка? А, вот — пишу́ ру́чкой.","ru":"Let's. Where's my pen? Ah, here — I'm writing with a pen."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for tools, transport and company — the world of the instrumental. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ру́чка","ru":"pen","ex":"Я пишу́ ру́чкой."},{"en":"каранда́ш","ru":"pencil","ex":"Ле́на пи́шет карандашо́м."},{"en":"авто́бус","ru":"bus","ex":"Я е́ду авто́бусом."},{"en":"по́езд","ru":"train","ex":"Мы е́дем по́ездом."},{"en":"метро́","ru":"metro, underground","ex":"Ива́н е́дет на метро́."},{"en":"такси́","ru":"taxi","ex":"Ве́чером мы на такси́."},{"en":"молоко́","ru":"milk","ex":"Ко́фе с молоко́м, пожа́луйста."},{"en":"нож","ru":"knife","ex":"Нож и ви́лка на столе́."},{"en":"ви́лка","ru":"fork","ex":"Я ем ви́лкой."},{"en":"ло́жка","ru":"spoon","ex":"Суп еди́м ло́жкой."},{"en":"друзья́","ru":"friends","ex":"Я в кафе́ с друзья́ми."},{"en":"роди́тели","ru":"parents","ex":"Я живу́ с роди́телями."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English. Watch for the ones with с and the ones without.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["с дру́гом","with a friend"],["ру́чкой","with a pen"],["авто́бусом","by bus"],["ко́фе с молоко́м","coffee with milk"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“with a friend” (company) —","opts":["дру́гом","с дру́гом","с дру́га"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I write with a pen.” — Я пишу́ ___","opts":["ру́чкой","с ру́чкой","ру́чку"],"answer":0},{"q":"“with a sister” — с ___ (сестра́)","opts":["сестра́","сестро́й","сестры́"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I go by bus.” — Я е́ду ___","opts":["на авто́бус","с авто́бусом","авто́бусом"],"answer":2},{"q":"“coffee with milk” —","opts":["ко́фе молоко́","ко́фе с молоко́м","ко́фе молоко́м"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note on how she moves around the city and who with. Read it aloud — every instrumental in it is one you can now make.","title":"По Москве́ с друзья́ми","sentences":["У́тром я е́ду на рабо́ту авто́бусом.","Ива́н е́дет на метро́, а Ле́на — маши́ной.","В кафе́ я пишу́ ру́чкой, а Ле́на — карандашо́м.","Мы пьём ко́фе с молоко́м вме́сте.","Ве́чером я е́ду домо́й с друзья́ми.","Как хорошо́ быть вме́сте!"],"translation":"In the morning I go to work by bus. Ivan goes by metro, and Lena by car. At the café I write with a pen, and Lena with a pencil. We drink coffee with milk together. In the evening I go home with friends. How good it is to be together!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put the instrumental to work.","prompt":"Say how you get around your city and who you do things with. Use at least one bare instrumental (a tool or transport) and one с + a person.","hint":"Tools and transport go bare: <b>ру́чкой</b>, <b>авто́бусом</b>. People take <b>с</b>: <b>с дру́гом</b>, <b>с сестро́й</b> 🙂","example":"Я е́ду на рабо́ту авто́бусом, а домо́й — с дру́гом. В кафе́ я пишу́ ру́чкой и пью ко́фе с молоко́м. По́сле рабо́ты я до́ма с роди́телями."}},"11":{"day":11,"week":"02","themeRu":"I work as…","themeEn":"Рабо́таю инжене́ром","intro":"Эмма starts a new job this week, and with it comes a very Russian puzzle: to say what you <em>do</em>, you bend the job into yesterday's case. <b>Рабо́таю инжене́ром</b> — “I work as an engineer”, the instrumental again. Today: jobs, what people used to be, what they're into, and where things sit in a room.","introAudio":"Emma starts a new job this week, and with it comes a very Russian puzzle: to say what you do, you bend the job into yesterday's case. Rabotayu inzhenyerom — I work as an engineer, the instrumental again. Today: jobs, what people used to be, what they're into, and where things sit in a room.","goals":["Say what you do for a living: <b>Я рабо́таю инжене́ром</b> — “work as” + the instrumental","Say what someone <em>was</em> or <em>became</em>: <b>был врачо́м</b>, <b>стал учи́телем</b>","Say what you're into: <b>Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой</b> — “interested in” + the instrumental","Place things in space: <b>за</b>, <b>над</b>, <b>под</b>, <b>пе́ред</b>, <b>ме́жду</b> + the instrumental"],"learned":["Said what you do for a living: <b>Я рабо́таю инжене́ром</b>, “work as” + the instrumental","Said what someone <em>was</em> or <em>became</em>: <b>был врачо́м</b>, <b>стал учи́телем</b>","Said what you're into: <b>Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой</b>, “interested in” + the instrumental","Placed things in space with <b>за</b>, <b>над</b>, <b>под</b>, <b>пе́ред</b>, <b>ме́жду</b> + the instrumental"],"review":{"intro":"A quick loop back before the new job. Yesterday's instrumental (“with” a person, or a bare tool) and the dative from earlier this week — let's keep them warm.","introAudio":"A quick loop back before the new job. Yesterday's instrumental, with a person or a bare tool, and the dative from earlier this week — let's keep them warm.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of it is from earlier days.","q":"“I go by bus.” — Я е́ду ___","opts":["на авто́бус","авто́бусом","с авто́бусом"],"answer":1,"explain":"Transport is the <b>bare</b> instrumental: <b>авто́бусом</b>. на авто́бус = onto the bus; с авто́бусом = together with a bus."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm helping Mum.” — Я помога́ю ___","opts":["ма́ме","ма́му","ма́мы"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>помога́ть</b> takes the dative — “to whom”: <b>ма́ме</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“many books”</b>","want":"мно́го книг","answer":"мно́го книг"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · I work as… — рабо́таю инжене́ром","title":"<em>Рабо́таю инжене́ром</em> — the job goes instrumental","paras":["To say what you do for a living, Russian uses <b>рабо́тать</b> (“to work”) + the job in the <b>instrumental</b> — the same case as yesterday: <b>Я рабо́таю инжене́ром.</b> Think of it as “I work <em>as</em> an engineer”, where the ending carries that “as”.","The endings are the ones you already met. He-word jobs add <b>-ом/-ем</b>: врач → <b>врачо́м</b>, учи́тель → <b>учи́телем</b>, инжене́р → <b>инжене́ром</b>. She-word jobs take <b>-ой</b>: медсестра́ → <b>медсестро́й</b>.","Here is the trap that catches every English speaker. “I <em>am</em> an engineer” in the present has <b>no verb</b> and stays nominative: <b>Я инжене́р.</b> But the moment a verb like рабо́тать steps in, the job bends: <b>Я рабо́таю инжене́ром.</b> The verb, not the meaning, chooses the case."],"audio":"To say what you do for a living, Russian uses rabotat, to work, plus the job in the instrumental — the same case as yesterday: Ya rabotayu inzhenyerom. Think of it as I work as an engineer, where the ending carries that as. The endings are the ones you met: he-word jobs add -om or -yem: vrach becomes vrachom, uchityel becomes uchityelem, inzhenyer becomes inzhenyerom. She-word jobs take -oy: myedsyestra becomes myedsyestroy. Here is the trap that catches every English speaker. I am an engineer in the present has no verb and stays nominative: Ya inzhenyer. But the moment a verb like rabotat steps in, the job bends: Ya rabotayu inzhenyerom. The verb, not the meaning, chooses the case.","table":{"rows":[["Я рабо́таю инжене́ром. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я работаю инженером\">🔊</button>","I work as an engineer"],["Он рабо́тает врачо́м. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он работает врачом\">🔊</button>","He works as a doctor"],["Она́ рабо́тает учи́телем. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она работает учителем\">🔊</button>","She works as a teacher"],["Я инжене́р. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я инженер\">🔊</button>","I am an engineer (present — nominative!)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"What do you work as? — As a doctor.","en":"Кем ты рабо́таешь? — <b>Врачо́м</b>.","say":"Кем ты работаешь? Врачом.","gloss":"Кем? = “as what/whom?” (instrumental)"},{"ru":"Emma works as a translator.","en":"Э́мма рабо́тает <b>перево́дчиком</b>.","say":"Эмма работает переводчиком."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я рабо́таю инжене́р.","right":"Я рабо́таю инжене́ром.","why":"рабо́тать pulls the job into the instrumental: инжене́р → <b>инжене́ром</b>."},{"wrong":"Я инжене́ром.","right":"Я инжене́р.","why":"With no verb, “I am an engineer” stays nominative: <b>Я инжене́р.</b> The instrumental needs a verb like рабо́таю."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. The note underneath explains any miss.","q":"“I work as a doctor.” — Я рабо́таю ___","opts":["врач","врачо́м","врача́"],"answer":1,"explain":"рабо́тать + instrumental: врач → <b>врачо́м</b>. врача́ is the genitive/accusative."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I am a teacher.” (present, no verb) —","opts":["Я учи́тель.","Я учи́телем.","Я рабо́таю учи́тель."],"answer":0,"explain":"Present “am” has no verb → nominative: <b>Я учи́тель.</b> Only a verb like рабо́таю would bend it to учи́телем."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“She works as a translator.”</b>","want":"Она́ рабо́тает перево́дчиком","answer":"Она́ рабо́тает перево́дчиком"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Was, became — был врачо́м","title":"<em>Был инжене́ром, стал учи́телем</em> — the past takes it too","paras":["Two more verbs hand the job to the instrumental: <b>быть</b> (“to be”) and <b>стать</b> (“to become”). In the <b>past</b> they mark gender, like any A1 past: <b>Он был врачо́м.</b> <b>Она́ была́ врачо́м.</b> <b>Он стал учи́телем.</b> <b>Она́ ста́ла учи́телем.</b>","So the same person is <b>Я инжене́р</b> today (present, nominative) but <b>Я был инжене́ром</b> about the past — because now there's a verb, был, and it wants the instrumental. Watch the case flip the moment you leave the present.","You'll meet this everywhere: <b>Ра́ньше он был студе́нтом, а тепе́рь рабо́тает программи́стом.</b> Was a student, now works as a programmer — two verbs, two instrumentals."],"audio":"Two more verbs hand the job to the instrumental: byt, to be, and stat, to become. In the past they mark gender, like any A1 past: On byl vrachom. Ona byla vrachom. On stal uchityelem. Ona stala uchityelem. So the same person is Ya inzhenyer today, present, nominative, but Ya byl inzhenyerom about the past — because now there's a verb, byl, and it wants the instrumental. Watch the case flip the moment you leave the present. Ranshe on byl studyentom, a tyepyer rabotayet programmistom — was a student, now works as a programmer: two verbs, two instrumentals.","table":{"rows":[["Он был врачо́м. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он был врачом\">🔊</button>","He was a doctor"],["Она́ была́ учи́телем. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она была учителем\">🔊</button>","She was a teacher"],["Он стал программи́стом. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он стал программистом\">🔊</button>","He became a programmer"],["Ра́ньше — студе́нт, тепе́рь — врач. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Раньше студент, теперь врач\">🔊</button>","Once a student, now a doctor (present, nominative)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Before, Ivan was a student.","en":"Ра́ньше Ива́н был <b>студе́нтом</b>.","say":"Раньше Иван был студентом."},{"ru":"My mum became a doctor.","en":"Моя́ ма́ма ста́ла <b>врачо́м</b>.","say":"Моя мама стала врачом."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он был врач.","right":"Он был врачо́м.","why":"был is a verb, so the job goes instrumental: <b>врачо́м</b>. (Only the verb-less present keeps врач.)"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“He was a teacher.” — Он был ___","opts":["учи́тель","учи́телем","учи́теля"],"answer":1,"explain":"был + instrumental: учи́тель → <b>учи́телем</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type “She became a doctor” (стать → ста́ла):","accept":["она стала врачом","она стала врачом."],"placeholder":"Она́ ста́ла…","explain":"стать + instrumental: <b>ста́ла врачо́м</b>. A woman says ста́ла."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Ivan was a student.”</b>","want":"Ива́н был студе́нтом","answer":"Ива́н был студе́нтом"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · What are you into? — интересу́юсь му́зыкой","title":"<em>Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой</em> — “interested in” takes the instrumental","paras":["To say what you're <b>interested in</b>, Russian uses <b>интересова́ться</b> + the instrumental — there's no “in” to translate, the ending does it: <b>Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой.</b> (interested in music), <b>Ты интересу́ешься спо́ртом?</b> (into sport?).","The verb wears a little tail <b>-ся</b> on the end — for now just learn the shapes you need: <b>я интересу́юсь</b>, <b>ты интересу́ешься</b>, <b>он / она́ интересу́ется</b>. That tail is glued on; there's a whole day on such verbs later.","It works with any interest: <b>исто́рией</b> (history), <b>кни́гами</b> (books), <b>теа́тром</b> (theatre). Same endings as always — she-words -ой/-ей, he/it-words -ом/-ем, plurals -ами."],"audio":"To say what you're interested in, Russian uses intyeryesovatsa plus the instrumental — there's no in to translate, the ending does it: Ya intyeryesuyus muzykoy, interested in music; Ty intyeryesuyeshsya sportom, into sport? The verb wears a little tail -sya on the end — for now just learn the shapes you need: ya intyeryesuyus, ty intyeryesuyeshsya, on or ona intyeryesuyetsa. That tail is glued on; there's a whole day on such verbs later. It works with any interest: istoriyey, history; knigami, books; tyeatrom, theatre. Same endings as always.","examples":[{"ru":"I'm interested in history.","en":"Я интересу́юсь <b>исто́рией</b>.","say":"Я интересуюсь историей."},{"ru":"What are you into? — Music and theatre.","en":"Чем ты интересу́ешься? — <b>Му́зыкой</b> и <b>теа́тром</b>.","say":"Чем ты интересуешься? Музыкой и театром.","gloss":"Чем? = “in what?” (instrumental)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я интересу́юсь му́зыку.","right":"Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой.","why":"интересова́ться takes the instrumental, not the accusative: му́зыка → <b>му́зыкой</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm interested in sport.” — Я интересу́юсь ___","opts":["спорт","спо́ртом","спо́рта"],"answer":1,"explain":"интересова́ться + instrumental: спорт → <b>спо́ртом</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm interested in history.”</b>","want":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией","answer":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Where exactly? — за, над, под, пе́ред, ме́жду","title":"<em>За до́мом, под столо́м</em> — five prepositions of place","paras":["A neat family of place-words all take the instrumental: <b>за</b> (behind), <b>над</b> (above), <b>под</b> (under), <b>пе́ред</b> (in front of), <b>ме́жду</b> (between). <b>Маши́на за до́мом.</b> — The car is behind the house.","The noun after them takes the endings you've drilled all day: дом → <b>до́мом</b>, стол → <b>столо́м</b>, окно́ → <b>окно́м</b>. <b>Ла́мпа над столо́м, су́мка под столо́м.</b>","<b>ме́жду</b> usually links two things, both instrumental: <b>ме́жду до́мом и шко́лой</b> — between the house and the school."],"audio":"A neat family of place-words all take the instrumental: za, behind; nad, above; pod, under; pyeryed, in front of; myezhdu, between. Mashina za domom — the car is behind the house. The noun after them takes the endings you've drilled all day: dom becomes domom, stol becomes stolom, okno becomes oknom. Lampa nad stolom, sumka pod stolom. Myezhdu usually links two things, both instrumental: myezhdu domom i shkoloy — between the house and the school.","table":{"rows":[["за до́мом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"за домом\">🔊</button>","behind the house"],["над столо́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"над столом\">🔊</button>","above the table"],["под столо́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"под столом\">🔊</button>","under the table"],["ме́жду до́мом и шко́лой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"между домом и школой\">🔊</button>","between the house and the school"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"The cat is under the table.","en":"Ко́шка <b>под столо́м</b>.","say":"Кошка под столом."},{"ru":"Ivan is standing in front of the café.","en":"Ива́н стои́т <b>пе́ред кафе́</b>.","say":"Иван стоит перед кафе."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"под стол","right":"под столо́м","why":"For location, под takes the instrumental: <b>под столо́м</b> (under the table). под стол would mean motion, “to under the table”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“behind the house” —","opts":["за дом","за до́мом","за до́ма"],"answer":1,"explain":"за (behind) for location + instrumental: дом → <b>за до́мом</b>."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “under the table” (стол → …):","accept":["под столом","под столом."],"placeholder":"под…","explain":"под for location takes the instrumental: <b>под столо́м</b>."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Кни́га под столо́м.","sub":"say where the book is — under the table","want":"Книга под столом"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ivan visits Emma at her new office and meets the team. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну как, Э́мма, нра́вится но́вая рабо́та?","ru":"Well, Emma, do you like the new job?"},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень! Я рабо́таю перево́дчиком.","ru":"Very much! I work as a translator."},{"who":"B","en":"Здо́рово! А кто твой нача́льник?","ru":"Great! And who's your boss?"},{"who":"A","en":"Мой нача́льник ра́ньше был учи́телем.","ru":"My boss used to be a teacher."},{"who":"B","en":"А э́то кто, за столо́м?","ru":"And who's that, behind the desk?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то Ка́тя, она́ рабо́тает программи́стом.","ru":"That's Katya, she works as a programmer."},{"who":"B","en":"А чем она́ интересу́ется?","ru":"And what's she into?"},{"who":"A","en":"Му́зыкой. Она́ хо́чет стать арти́сткой.","ru":"Music. She wants to become a performer."},{"who":"A","en":"А я ра́да, что рабо́таю здесь, в Москве́.","ru":"And I'm glad I work here, in Moscow."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for jobs, interests and where things sit. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"инжене́р","ru":"engineer","ex":"Я рабо́таю инжене́ром."},{"en":"врач","ru":"doctor","ex":"Он стал врачо́м."},{"en":"учи́тель","ru":"teacher","ex":"Она́ рабо́тает учи́телем."},{"en":"программи́ст","ru":"programmer","ex":"Ка́тя — программи́ст."},{"en":"перево́дчик","ru":"translator","ex":"Э́мма рабо́тает перево́дчиком."},{"en":"арти́ст","ru":"performer, actor (not a painter!)","ex":"Он арти́ст в теа́тре."},{"en":"нача́льник","ru":"boss, chief","ex":"Э́то мой нача́льник."},{"en":"стать","ru":"to become","ex":"Я хочу́ стать врачо́м."},{"en":"интересова́ться","ru":"to be interested (in)","ex":"Чем ты интересу́ешься?"},{"en":"му́зыка","ru":"music","ex":"Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой."},{"en":"за","ru":"behind","ex":"Маши́на за до́мом."},{"en":"под","ru":"under","ex":"Ко́шка под столо́м."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English. Every noun here is in the instrumental.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["рабо́таю врачо́м","I work as a doctor"],["был студе́нтом","was a student"],["интересу́юсь му́зыкой","interested in music"],["под столо́м","under the table"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions to close the day. If one bites, the rule above has your back.","items":[{"q":"“I work as an engineer.” — Я рабо́таю ___","opts":["инжене́р","инжене́ром","инжене́ра"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I am a doctor.” (present, no verb) —","opts":["Я врач.","Я врачо́м.","Я рабо́таю врач."],"answer":0},{"q":"“He became a teacher.” — Он стал ___","opts":["учи́тель","учи́теля","учи́телем"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I'm interested in music.” — Я интересу́юсь ___","opts":["му́зыку","му́зыкой","му́зыки"],"answer":1},{"q":"“under the table” —","opts":["под стол","под столо́м","под стола́"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma writes home about her new job. Read it aloud — the instrumental is doing most of the work.","title":"Моя́ но́вая рабо́та","sentences":["Тепе́рь я рабо́таю перево́дчиком в це́нтре Москвы́.","Ра́ньше я была́ студе́нткой, а сейча́с у меня́ есть рабо́та.","Мой нача́льник — до́брый челове́к, он был учи́телем.","Ря́дом рабо́тает Ка́тя, она́ программи́ст и интересу́ется му́зыкой.","Мой стол у окна́, а за окно́м — большо́й го́род.","Я о́чень ра́да, что рабо́таю здесь."],"translation":"Now I work as a translator in the centre of Moscow. Before, I was a student, and now I have a job. My boss is a kind person; he used to be a teacher. Katya works next to me — she's a programmer and is interested in music. My desk is by the window, and behind the window is a big city. I'm very glad I work here."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — talk about work and interests.","prompt":"Say what you do (or want to become), what you were before, and one thing you're interested in. Use the instrumental after рабо́таю / был(а) / стал(а) / интересу́юсь.","hint":"The verb bends the job: <b>рабо́таю инжене́ром</b>, <b>был(а) студе́нтом</b>, <b>интересу́юсь му́зыкой</b>. But a plain “I am…” stays nominative: <b>Я врач</b> 🙂","example":"Ра́ньше я был студе́нтом, а тепе́рь рабо́таю инжене́ром. Я хочу́ стать нача́льником. Я интересу́юсь спо́ртом и му́зыкой."}},"12":{"day":12,"week":"02","themeRu":"Thinking about you","themeEn":"Ду́маю о тебе́","intro":"Some evenings are all talk. Tonight Эмма, Иван and Лена sit late over tea, and every sentence is <em>about</em> something — the day, the city, each other. To say “about”, Russian reaches for a case you already know from A1: the <b>prepositional</b>, the one you used for “in / at”. Now it picks up a second job.","introAudio":"Some evenings are all talk. Tonight Emma, Ivan and Lena sit late over tea, and every sentence is about something — the day, the city, each other. To say about, Russian reaches for a case you already know from A1: the prepositional, the one you used for in and at. Now it picks up a second job.","goals":["Say what you think about: <b>Я ду́маю о тебе́</b> — “about” + the prepositional","Choose <b>о / об / обо</b>: <b>о рабо́те</b>, <b>об А́нне</b>, <b>о́бо мне</b>","Talk and tell about things: <b>говорю́ о Москве́</b>, <b>расска́зываю о дру́ге</b>","Feel one case do two jobs: place (<b>в шко́ле</b>) and topic (<b>о шко́ле</b>)"],"learned":["Said what you think about: <b>Я ду́маю о тебе́</b>, “about” + the prepositional","Chose <b>о / об / обо</b>: <b>о рабо́те</b>, <b>об А́нне</b>, <b>о́бо мне</b>","Talked and told about things: <b>говорю́ о Москве́</b>, <b>расска́зываю о дру́ге</b>","Felt one case do two jobs: place (<b>в шко́ле</b>) and topic (<b>о шко́ле</b>)"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's instrumental is still warm — jobs and interests — and this week's dative too. One quick lap, then we open the “about” case.","introAudio":"Yesterday's instrumental is still warm — jobs and interests — and this week's dative too. One quick lap, then we open the about case.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all from earlier days.","q":"“I work as a doctor.” — Я рабо́таю ___","opts":["врач","врачо́м","врача́"],"answer":1,"explain":"рабо́тать + instrumental: врач → <b>врачо́м</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm at work.” — Я ___","opts":["на рабо́ту","на рабо́те","на рабо́ты"],"answer":1,"explain":"Location = the prepositional: <b>на рабо́те</b>. на рабо́ту would mean motion, “to work”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm writing to a friend.”</b>","want":"Я пишу́ дру́гу","answer":"Я пишу́ дру́гу"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · About what? About whom? — о чём, о ком","title":"<em>Ду́маю о тебе́</em> — “about” is the prepositional","paras":["To say you think, talk or read <b>about</b> something, Russian uses <b>о</b> (“about”) + the <b>prepositional</b> — the very case A1 used for “in / at” a place. <b>Я ду́маю о тебе́.</b> — I'm thinking about you.","The question words are <b>о чём?</b> (about what?) and <b>о ком?</b> (about whom?). Most nouns simply take <b>-е</b>: рабо́та → <b>о рабо́те</b>, Москва́ → <b>о Москве́</b>, дом → <b>о до́ме</b>.","Here's the day's trap. In English “think about” splits into two words; in Russian the “about” is <b>о</b> + the ending, and it never drops. <b>Я ду́маю о тебе́</b> — never <em>ду́маю тебя́</em>, which drops the “about” entirely and just means “I think you”."],"audio":"To say you think, talk or read about something, Russian uses o, about, plus the prepositional — the very case A1 used for in or at a place. Ya dumayu o tyebye — I'm thinking about you. The question words are o chyom, about what, and o kom, about whom. Most nouns simply take -ye: rabota becomes o rabotye, Moskva becomes o Moskvye, dom becomes o domye. Here's the day's trap. In English think about splits into two words; in Russian the about is o plus the ending, and it never drops. Ya dumayu o tyebye — never dumayu tyebya, which drops the about entirely and just means I think you.","table":{"rows":[["Я ду́маю о тебе́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я думаю о тебе\">🔊</button>","I'm thinking about you"],["о рабо́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о работе\">🔊</button>","about work"],["о Москве́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о Москве\">🔊</button>","about Moscow"],["о до́ме <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о доме\">🔊</button>","about home"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"What are you thinking about? — About the trip.","en":"О чём ты ду́маешь? — <b>О пое́здке</b>.","say":"О чём ты думаешь? О поездке.","gloss":"пое́здка → о пое́здке"},{"ru":"I often think about my family.","en":"Я ча́сто ду́маю <b>о семье́</b>.","say":"Я часто думаю о семье."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я ду́маю тебя́.","right":"Я ду́маю о тебе́.","why":"“think about” needs <b>о</b> + the prepositional: <b>о тебе́</b>. ду́маю тебя́ has no “about” in it at all."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — the note underneath explains any miss.","q":"“I'm thinking about work.” — Я ду́маю ___","opts":["о рабо́ту","о рабо́те","рабо́ту"],"answer":1,"explain":"о + prepositional: рабо́та → <b>о рабо́те</b>. о рабо́ту mixes the wrong ending; рабо́ту is the accusative."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm thinking about you.”</b>","want":"Я ду́маю о тебе́","answer":"Я ду́маю о тебе́"},{"type":"choice","q":"“about what?” — the question word is…","opts":["О чём?","О ком?","Чем?"],"answer":0,"explain":"Things → <b>о чём?</b> (about what). о ком? is “about whom”; чем? (no о) is the instrumental, “with what”."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · о, об or обо?","title":"<em>О рабо́те, об А́нне, о́бо мне</em>","paras":["The little word “about” has three shapes, chosen only for smooth sound. Before a consonant it's <b>о</b>: <b>о рабо́те</b>, <b>о до́ме</b>, <b>о Ле́не</b>.","Before a word that <em>starts with a vowel sound</em> (а, э, и, о, у) it grows a <b>б</b> → <b>об</b>: <b>об Ива́не</b>, <b>об А́нне</b>, <b>об окне́</b>. Say “о Анне” aloud and you'll feel why the б sneaks in.","And one fixed favourite: <b>о́бо мне</b> (about me). Learn it as a set with the pronouns — <b>о тебе́</b>, <b>о нём</b>, <b>о ней</b>, <b>о нас</b>, <b>о них</b>."],"audio":"The little word about has three shapes, chosen only for smooth sound. Before a consonant it's o: o rabotye, o domye, o Lyenye. Before a word that starts with a vowel sound — a, e, i, o, u — it grows a b, ob: ob Ivanye, ob Annye, ob oknye. Say o Annye aloud and you'll feel why the b sneaks in. And one fixed favourite: obo mnye, about me. Learn it with the pronouns: o tyebye, o nyom, o nyey, o nas, o nih.","table":{"rows":[["о рабо́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о работе\">🔊</button>","about work (before a consonant)"],["об Ива́не <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"об Иване\">🔊</button>","about Ivan (before a vowel)"],["об окне́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"об окне\">🔊</button>","about the window (before a vowel)"],["о́бо мне <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обо мне\">🔊</button>","about me (fixed form)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Ivan is talking about Anna.","en":"Ива́н говори́т <b>об А́нне</b>.","say":"Иван говорит об Анне."},{"ru":"And what about me?","en":"А что <b>о́бо мне</b>?","say":"А что обо мне?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"о Ива́не","right":"об Ива́не","why":"Ива́н starts with a vowel, so “about” grows a б: <b>об Ива́не</b>. Purely for smooth sound."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“about Anna” —","opts":["о А́нне","об А́нне","о́бо А́нне"],"answer":1,"explain":"А́нна starts with a vowel → <b>об А́нне</b>. обо is only for a few fixed forms like о́бо мне."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type “about me”:","accept":["обо мне","обо мне."],"placeholder":"обо…","explain":"The fixed form is <b>обо мне</b> — about me."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“about Ivan”</b>","want":"об Ива́не","answer":"об Ива́не"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Talking and telling — говори́ть, расска́зывать о…","title":"<em>Говорю́ о Москве́, расска́зываю о дру́ге</em>","paras":["The “about” pattern powers the verbs of speech. <b>говори́ть о</b> — to talk about, <b>расска́зывать о</b> — to tell / recount about, <b>чита́ть о</b> — to read about. All of them take <b>о</b> + the prepositional. <b>Мы говори́м о Москве́.</b>","<b>расска́зывать</b> is your storytelling verb: <b>Я расска́зываю о дру́ге.</b> — I'm telling (a story) about a friend. Present tense: я расска́зываю, ты расска́зываешь, он расска́зывает.","See how naturally the topic sits after о: <b>Он говори́т о рабо́те, о семье́, о жи́зни.</b> (жизнь → о жи́зни — a she-word ending in -ь takes -и, not -е.)"],"audio":"The about pattern powers the verbs of speech. Govorit o, to talk about; rasskazyvat o, to tell about; chitat o, to read about — all take o plus the prepositional. My govorim o Moskvye. Rasskazyvat is your storytelling verb: Ya rasskazyvayu o drugye — I'm telling a story about a friend. Present tense: ya rasskazyvayu, ty rasskazyvayesh, on rasskazyvayet. See how naturally the topic sits after o: On govorit o rabotye, o syemye, o zhizni. Zhizn becomes o zhizni — a she-word ending in a soft sign takes -i, not -ye.","examples":[{"ru":"We're talking about the film.","en":"Мы говори́м <b>о фи́льме</b>.","say":"Мы говорим о фильме."},{"ru":"Emma is telling us about London.","en":"Э́мма расска́зывает <b>о Ло́ндоне</b>.","say":"Эмма рассказывает о Лондоне."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я говорю́ Москву́.","right":"Я говорю́ о Москве́.","why":"“talk about Moscow” is <b>о</b> + prepositional: <b>о Москве́</b>. говорю́ Москву́ (accusative) means nothing here."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“We're talking about work.” — Мы говори́м ___","opts":["рабо́ту","о рабо́те","о рабо́ту"],"answer":1,"explain":"говори́ть о + prepositional: <b>о рабо́те</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Emma is telling about Moscow.”</b>","want":"Э́мма расска́зывает о Москве́","answer":"Э́мма расска́зывает о Москве́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · One case, two jobs — place and topic","title":"<em>В шко́ле</em> (place) vs <em>о шко́ле</em> (topic)","paras":["You now own the prepositional twice over. With <b>в / на</b> it means <b>where</b>: <b>в шко́ле</b> (at school), <b>на рабо́те</b> (at work). With <b>о</b> it means <b>what about</b>: <b>о шко́ле</b> (about school), <b>о рабо́те</b> (about work). Same ending — a different little word in front.","So one form, шко́ле, waits for its preposition to reveal the job. <b>Я в шко́ле</b> = I'm at school. <b>Я ду́маю о шко́ле</b> = I'm thinking about school. Let the preposition steer.","That's why the prepositional is sometimes called “the о-case as well as the в/на-case”: one set of endings, three little words — в, на, о — riding on top."],"audio":"You now own the prepositional twice over. With v or na it means where: v shkolye, at school; na rabotye, at work. With o it means what about: o shkolye, about school; o rabotye, about work. Same ending, a different little word in front. So one form, shkolye, waits for its preposition to reveal the job. Ya v shkolye — I'm at school. Ya dumayu o shkolye — I'm thinking about school. Let the preposition steer. That's why the prepositional is sometimes called the o-case as well as the v-na-case: one set of endings, three little words riding on top.","table":{"rows":[["в шко́ле <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в школе\">🔊</button>","at school (place)"],["о шко́ле <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о школе\">🔊</button>","about school (topic)"],["на рабо́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на работе\">🔊</button>","at work (place)"],["о рабо́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о работе\">🔊</button>","about work (topic)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm at home and thinking about work.","en":"Я до́ма и ду́маю <b>о рабо́те</b>.","say":"Я дома и думаю о работе."},{"ru":"We were in Moscow, and we often talk about Moscow.","en":"Мы бы́ли в Москве́ и ча́сто говори́м <b>о Москве́</b>.","say":"Мы были в Москве и часто говорим о Москве."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я ду́маю в рабо́те.","right":"Я ду́маю о рабо́те.","why":"For a topic use <b>о</b>, not в: <b>о рабо́те</b> (about work). в рабо́те would try to put you inside the work."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which one means “about the city” (the topic)?","opts":["в го́роде","го́род","о го́роде"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>о</b> + prepositional = the topic: <b>о го́роде</b>. в го́роде means “in the city”."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “about the city”:","accept":["о городе","о городе."],"placeholder":"о…","explain":"о + prepositional: <b>о го́роде</b>."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я ду́маю о Москве́.","sub":"say you're thinking about Moscow","want":"Я думаю о Москве"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Late evening, tea long finished. Emma and Ivan are just talking — about everything and nothing. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Как хорошо́ ве́чером сиде́ть и говори́ть о жи́зни.","ru":"How nice it is to sit in the evening and talk about life."},{"who":"A","en":"Да. О чём ты сейча́с ду́маешь?","ru":"Yes. What are you thinking about right now?"},{"who":"B","en":"О рабо́те, коне́чно. Я сли́шком мно́го ду́маю о рабо́те.","ru":"About work, of course. I think about work too much."},{"who":"A","en":"А я — о до́ме и о семье́.","ru":"And I — about home and about family."},{"who":"B","en":"Ты ча́сто говори́шь о семье́?","ru":"Do you often talk about your family?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да. И я мечта́ю о большо́й пое́здке.","ru":"Yes. And I dream about a big trip."},{"who":"B","en":"О пое́здке? А куда́?","ru":"About a trip? Where to?"},{"who":"A","en":"Не зна́ю. Но я ду́маю об э́том ча́сто.","ru":"I don't know. But I think about it often. (об э́том = about it)"},{"who":"B","en":"А о́бо мне ты ду́маешь? Шучу́!","ru":"And do you think about me? Just kidding! (Шучу́ = I'm joking)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for a long evening of talk. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ду́мать","ru":"to think","ex":"Я ду́маю о тебе́."},{"en":"расска́зывать","ru":"to tell, recount","ex":"Он расска́зывает о дру́ге."},{"en":"мечта́ть","ru":"to dream (about)","ex":"Я мечта́ю о Москве́."},{"en":"пое́здка","ru":"trip, journey","ex":"Мы ду́маем о пое́здке."},{"en":"семья́","ru":"family","ex":"Мы говори́м о семье́."},{"en":"жизнь","ru":"life","ex":"Э́то кни́га о жи́зни."},{"en":"фильм","ru":"film","ex":"Расска́зываю о фи́льме."},{"en":"пого́да","ru":"weather","ex":"Все говоря́т о пого́де."},{"en":"пра́вда","ru":"truth","ex":"Э́то пра́вда о Москве́."},{"en":"вопро́с","ru":"question","ex":"У меня́ вопро́с о рабо́те."},{"en":"себя́","ru":"oneself","ex":"Он ду́мает то́лько о себе́."},{"en":"исто́рия","ru":"story, history","ex":"Интере́сная исто́рия о Москве́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English. Every phrase here is “about” something.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["ду́маю о тебе́","I think about you"],["об Ива́не","about Ivan"],["о́бо мне","about me"],["говори́м о рабо́те","we talk about work"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions to finish. If one bites, the rule above has your back.","items":[{"q":"“I'm thinking about you.” — Я ду́маю ___","opts":["тебя́","о тебя́","о тебе́"],"answer":2},{"q":"“about Ivan” —","opts":["о Ива́не","об Ива́не","о́бо Ива́не"],"answer":1},{"q":"“about me” —","opts":["о́бо мне","о мне","о меня́"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which one means “about the city” (topic)?","opts":["в го́роде","го́род","о го́роде"],"answer":2},{"q":"“We talk about work.” — Мы говори́м ___","opts":["рабо́ту","о рабо́те","на рабо́те"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's diary line about their evenings of talk. Read it aloud — every “about” rides on the prepositional.","title":"О чём мы говори́м","sentences":["Ве́чером мы ча́сто сиди́м в кафе́ и говори́м о жи́зни.","Ива́н расска́зывает о рабо́те, а я — о семье́.","Ле́на мечта́ет о большо́й пое́здке, о Пари́же и о Ри́ме.","Мы говори́м о фи́льме, о кни́ге и о Москве́.","А по́здно ве́чером я ду́маю о до́ме и о дру́ге.","Как хорошо́ ду́мать и говори́ть о до́ме!"],"translation":"In the evening we often sit in a café and talk about life. Ivan tells us about work, and I about family. Lena dreams about a big trip, about Paris and about Rome. We talk about a film, a book and Moscow. And late in the evening I think about home and about a friend. How good it is to think and talk about home!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — say what fills your thoughts.","prompt":"Say what you often think about, what you like to talk about, and one thing you dream about. Use о / об / обо + the prepositional each time.","hint":"It's <b>о</b> + the -е ending: <b>о рабо́те</b>, <b>о семье́</b>. Before a vowel use <b>об</b> (<b>об Ива́не</b>), and “about me” is <b>о́бо мне</b> 🙂","example":"Ча́сто я ду́маю о семье́ и о до́ме. С друзья́ми я люблю́ говори́ть о фи́льмах и о Москве́. А мечта́ю я о большо́й пое́здке."}},"13":{"day":13,"week":"02","themeRu":"I see my brother","themeEn":"Ви́жу бра́та","intro":"Эмма spends a lot of this week waiting for people and running into them — <b>жду Макси́ма</b>, <b>ви́жу дру́га</b>. That throws a spotlight on the last corner of the accusative you haven't met: what happens when the thing you see or await is a <em>person</em>. Along the way, the little words for “when” — on Monday, every day, all day.","introAudio":"Emma spends a lot of this week waiting for people and running into them — zhdu Maksima, vizhu druga. That throws a spotlight on the last corner of the accusative you haven't met: what happens when the thing you see or await is a person. Along the way, the little words for when — on Monday, every day, all day.","goals":["See and await people: <b>ви́жу бра́та</b>, <b>жду дру́га</b> — animate he-words copy the genitive","Keep the rest of the accusative straight: things don't change (<b>ви́жу стол</b>), she-words take -у (<b>ви́жу ма́му</b>)","Say when: <b>в понеде́льник</b>, <b>в час</b> — “on / at” + the accusative","Say how often and how long: <b>ка́ждый день</b>, <b>весь день</b>"],"learned":["Saw and awaited people: <b>ви́жу бра́та</b>, <b>жду дру́га</b> — animate he-words copy the genitive","Kept the rest of the accusative straight: things unchanged (<b>ви́жу стол</b>), she-words -у (<b>ви́жу ма́му</b>)","Said when: <b>в понеде́льник</b>, <b>в час</b>, “on / at” + the accusative","Said how often and how long: <b>ка́ждый день</b>, <b>весь день</b>"],"review":{"intro":"The cases keep stacking up. A quick lap over yesterday's “about” and this week's instrumental — then the accusative gets its final piece.","introAudio":"The cases keep stacking up. A quick lap over yesterday's about and this week's instrumental — then the accusative gets its final piece.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of it is from earlier days.","q":"“I'm thinking about you.” — Я ду́маю ___","opts":["тебя́","о тебе́","о тебя́"],"answer":1,"explain":"“think about” = <b>о</b> + prepositional: <b>о тебе́</b>. тебя́ (accusative) drops the “about”."},{"type":"choice","q":"“He was a doctor.” — Он был ___","opts":["врач","врачо́м","врача́"],"answer":1,"explain":"был is a verb, so the job goes instrumental: <b>врачо́м</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“the brother's book”</b>","want":"кни́га бра́та","answer":"кни́га бра́та"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Seeing a person — ви́жу бра́та","title":"<em>Ви́жу бра́та</em> — a living he-word copies the genitive","paras":["In A1 the accusative (the “direct object” case) was gentle: she-words took -у (<b>ви́жу ма́му</b>), and he-word <em>things</em> didn't change at all (<b>ви́жу стол</b>). Today the last piece clicks in: a he-word that is a <b>living being</b> — a person or an animal — takes the <b>genitive</b> ending instead.","So брат becomes <b>бра́та</b>, exactly like yesterday's… no, like the genitive from week one: <b>Я ви́жу бра́та.</b> <b>Я зна́ю Ива́на.</b> <b>Я жду дру́га.</b> The clue is life: if it breathes and it's a he-word, reach for -а/-я.","Things stay their calm selves: <b>ви́жу стол</b>, <b>ви́жу дом</b> — no change. Only <em>living</em> he-words bend. That single split — animate vs inanimate — is the whole new idea for today."],"audio":"In A1 the accusative, the direct-object case, was gentle: she-words took -u, vizhu mamu, and he-word things didn't change at all, vizhu stol. Today the last piece clicks in: a he-word that is a living being, a person or an animal, takes the genitive ending instead. So brat becomes brata, just like the genitive from week one: Ya vizhu brata. Ya znayu Ivana. Ya zhdu druga. The clue is life: if it breathes and it's a he-word, reach for -a or -ya. Things stay calm: vizhu stol, vizhu dom, no change. Only living he-words bend. That single split, animate versus inanimate, is the whole new idea for today.","table":{"rows":[["Я ви́жу бра́та. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я вижу брата\">🔊</button>","I see my brother (he-word, alive → -а)"],["Я ви́жу Ива́на. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я вижу Ивана\">🔊</button>","I see Ivan (alive → -а)"],["Я ви́жу стол. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я вижу стол\">🔊</button>","I see a table (thing → no change)"],["Я ви́жу ма́му. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я вижу маму\">🔊</button>","I see Mum (she-word → -у)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Do you know Maxim? — Yes, I know Maxim.","en":"Ты зна́ешь Макси́ма? — Да, я зна́ю <b>Макси́ма</b>.","say":"Ты знаешь Максима? Да, я знаю Максима."},{"ru":"Emma loves her brother and her dog.","en":"Э́мма лю́бит <b>бра́та</b> и <b>соба́ку</b>.","say":"Эмма любит брата и собаку.","gloss":"бра́та (he-word, alive → -а); соба́ку (she-word → -у)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я ви́жу брат.","right":"Я ви́жу бра́та.","why":"брат is a living he-word, so the accusative copies the genitive: <b>бра́та</b>. Only <em>things</em> stay unchanged."},{"wrong":"Я ви́жу столо́м.","right":"Я ви́жу стол.","why":"стол is a thing — the accusative leaves it untouched: <b>ви́жу стол</b>. (столо́м would be the instrumental.)"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — the note underneath explains any miss.","q":"“I see my brother.” — Я ви́жу ___","opts":["брат","бра́та","бра́том"],"answer":1,"explain":"A living he-word copies the genitive: <b>бра́та</b>. брат stays plain only for things; бра́том is the instrumental."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I see a table.” — Я ви́жу ___","opts":["стол","стола́","столо́м"],"answer":0,"explain":"стол is a thing → no change: <b>ви́жу стол</b>. стола́ (genitive) and столо́м (instrumental) don't belong here."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I know Ivan.”</b>","want":"Я зна́ю Ива́на","answer":"Я зна́ю Ива́на"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Waiting, knowing, loving — жду дру́га","title":"<em>Жду дру́га</em> — the verbs that grab a direct object","paras":["A whole crowd of everyday verbs point straight at an object in the accusative: <b>ви́деть</b> (see), <b>ждать</b> (wait for), <b>знать</b> (know), <b>люби́ть</b> (love), <b>слу́шать</b> (listen to). With a living he-word they all use that -а/-я form: <b>Я жду дру́га.</b>","<b>ждать</b> is a favourite trap-melter: English says “wait <em>for</em>”, but Russian just puts the person in the accusative — no “for” at all. <b>Жду Макси́ма</b> = “I'm waiting for Maxim”. The “for” vanishes into the ending, like yesterday's “about” into о.","Present tense of the two stars: <b>ви́жу, ви́дишь</b> (see) and <b>жду, ждёшь</b> (wait). <b>Я жду тебя́!</b> — тебя́ is the accusative of ты, and it looks just like the genitive."],"audio":"A whole crowd of everyday verbs point straight at an object in the accusative: vidyet, see; zhdat, wait for; znat, know; lyubit, love; slushat, listen to. With a living he-word they all use that -a or -ya form: Ya zhdu druga. Zhdat is a favourite trap-melter: English says wait for, but Russian just puts the person in the accusative, no for at all. Zhdu Maksima means I'm waiting for Maxim. The for vanishes into the ending, like yesterday's about into o. Present tense of the two stars: vizhu, vidish, see; and zhdu, zhdyosh, wait. Ya zhdu tyebya — tyebya is the accusative of ty, and it looks just like the genitive.","table":{"rows":[["Я жду дру́га. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я жду друга\">🔊</button>","I'm waiting for a friend"],["Я жду Макси́ма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я жду Максима\">🔊</button>","I'm waiting for Maxim"],["Я зна́ю его́ бра́та. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я знаю его брата\">🔊</button>","I know his brother"],["Я жду тебя́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я жду тебя\">🔊</button>","I'm waiting for you"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Who are you waiting for? — For Ivan.","en":"Кого́ ты ждёшь? — <b>Ива́на</b>.","say":"Кого ты ждёшь? Ивана.","gloss":"Кого́? = “whom?” (accusative)"},{"ru":"We love our teacher.","en":"Мы лю́бим на́шего <b>учи́теля</b>.","say":"Мы любим нашего учителя.","gloss":"учи́тель → учи́теля (living he-word)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я жду для дру́га.","right":"Я жду дру́га.","why":"“wait for” takes a plain accusative — no “for” word: <b>жду дру́га</b>. (для дру́га would mean “for a friend's sake”.)"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm waiting for a friend.” — Я жду ___","opts":["друг","дру́га","дру́гу"],"answer":1,"explain":"ждать + accusative; a living he-word copies the genitive: <b>дру́га</b>. дру́гу is the dative."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type “I'm waiting for Maxim” (Макси́м → …):","accept":["я жду максима","я жду максима."],"placeholder":"Я жду…","explain":"жду + the accusative of a living he-word: <b>Макси́ма</b> (a copy of the genitive)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm waiting for you.”</b>","want":"Я жду тебя́","answer":"Я жду тебя́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · When? — в понеде́льник, в час","title":"<em>В понеде́льник, в час</em> — “on / at” takes the accusative","paras":["To say <b>on</b> a day of the week, Russian uses <b>в</b> + the day in the accusative: <b>в понеде́льник</b> (on Monday), <b>во вто́рник</b> (on Tuesday), <b>в сре́ду</b> (on Wednesday — среда́ → сре́ду). The same little в you know from places, now pointing at time.","Most days are he-words and don't change (понеде́льник, вто́рник, четве́рг); the she-word days take -у: среда́ → <b>сре́ду</b>, пя́тница → <b>пя́тницу</b>, суббо́та → <b>суббо́ту</b>.","For clock time it's <b>в</b> + the hour: <b>в час</b> (at one), <b>в два</b> (at two), <b>в пять</b> (at five). <b>Я жду Ива́на в час.</b> — I'm meeting Ivan at one."],"audio":"To say on a day of the week, Russian uses v plus the day in the accusative: v ponyedyelnik, on Monday; vo vtornik, on Tuesday; v sryedu, on Wednesday — sryeda becomes sryedu. The same little v you know from places, now pointing at time. Most days are he-words and don't change: ponyedyelnik, vtornik, chetvyerg; the she-word days take -u: sryeda becomes sryedu, pyatnitsa becomes pyatnitsu, subbota becomes subbotu. For clock time it's v plus the hour: v chas, at one; v dva, at two; v pyat, at five. Ya zhdu Ivana v chas — I'm meeting Ivan at one.","table":{"rows":[["в понеде́льник <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в понедельник\">🔊</button>","on Monday (he-word, no change)"],["в сре́ду <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в среду\">🔊</button>","on Wednesday (среда́ → сре́ду)"],["в суббо́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в субботу\">🔊</button>","on Saturday (суббо́та → суббо́ту)"],["в час <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в час\">🔊</button>","at one o'clock"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"On Monday I'm meeting Ivan.","en":"В понеде́льник я жду <b>Ива́на</b>.","say":"В понедельник я жду Ивана."},{"ru":"The lesson is at nine.","en":"Уро́к <b>в де́вять</b>.","say":"Урок в девять.","gloss":"в де́вять — at nine"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"на понеде́льник","right":"в понеде́льник","why":"“on Monday” uses <b>в</b> + accusative, not на: <b>в понеде́льник</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“at one o'clock” —","opts":["в час","на час","о ча́се"],"answer":0,"explain":"Clock time = <b>в</b> + the hour: <b>в час</b>. (на час = “for an hour”; о ча́се = “about the hour”.)"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “on Wednesday” (среда́ → …):","accept":["в среду","в среду."],"placeholder":"в…","explain":"в + accusative; среда́ is a she-word → <b>в сре́ду</b>. (в среде́ would be the prepositional.)"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Every day, all day — ка́ждый день, весь день","title":"<em>Ка́ждый день, весь день</em> — how often, how long","paras":["Two handy time-phrases both sit in the accusative with no preposition at all. <b>ка́ждый день</b> = every day (how often); <b>весь день</b> = all day / the whole day (how long). <b>Я ви́жу его́ ка́ждый день.</b> <b>Я жду тебя́ весь день!</b>","They flex to match the noun. <b>ка́ждый</b> and <b>весь</b> are he-word here (день), but with a she-word they become <b>ка́ждую</b>, <b>всю</b>: <b>ка́ждую неде́лю</b> (every week), <b>всю неде́лю</b> (all week).","No “for”, no “on” — the bare accusative carries “every” and “whole” by itself. English piles on extra words; Russian just leans on the ending plus that little ка́ждый / весь."],"audio":"Two handy time-phrases both sit in the accusative with no preposition at all. Kazhdyy dyen means every day, how often; vyes dyen means all day, the whole day, how long. Ya vizhu yego kazhdyy dyen. Ya zhdu tyebya vyes dyen. They flex to match the noun. Kazhdyy and vyes are he-word here, with dyen, but with a she-word they become kazhduyu, vsyu: kazhduyu nyedyelyu, every week; vsyu nyedyelyu, all week. No for, no on — the bare accusative carries every and whole by itself.","table":{"rows":[["ка́ждый день <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"каждый день\">🔊</button>","every day"],["весь день <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"весь день\">🔊</button>","all day, the whole day"],["ка́ждую неде́лю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"каждую неделю\">🔊</button>","every week (неде́ля → неде́лю)"],["всю неде́лю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"всю неделю\">🔊</button>","all week"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I see Ivan every day.","en":"Я ви́жу Ива́на <b>ка́ждый день</b>.","say":"Я вижу Ивана каждый день."},{"ru":"I was at home all day.","en":"Я был до́ма <b>весь день</b>.","say":"Я был дома весь день.","gloss":"весь день — all day (no preposition)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"ка́ждый неде́лю","right":"ка́ждую неде́лю","why":"неде́ля is a she-word, so ка́ждый agrees as <b>ка́ждую</b>: ка́ждую неде́лю."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“every day” —","opts":["в день","ка́ждый день","ка́ждый дня"],"answer":1,"explain":"Frequency in the bare accusative: <b>ка́ждый день</b> — no preposition. (в день = “per day”.)"},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“all day”</b>","want":"весь день","answer":"весь день"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я ви́жу дру́га ка́ждый день.","sub":"say you see your friend every day","want":"Я вижу друга каждый день"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At the metro exit, Emma and Ivan are waiting to meet Maxim. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Кого́ мы ждём здесь, Э́мма?","ru":"Who are we waiting for here, Emma?"},{"who":"A","en":"Макси́ма. Ты его́ зна́ешь?","ru":"Maxim. Do you know him?"},{"who":"B","en":"Коне́чно, я зна́ю Макси́ма. Мы ви́дим его́ ка́ждую неде́лю.","ru":"Of course I know Maxim. We see him every week."},{"who":"A","en":"Он всегда́ здесь в час.","ru":"He's always here at one."},{"who":"B","en":"Уже́ час. А вот и он! Ви́жу Макси́ма!","ru":"It's one already. And here he is! I see Maxim!"},{"who":"A","en":"Где? Я ви́жу то́лько авто́бус и большо́й дом.","ru":"Where? I only see a bus and a big building."},{"who":"B","en":"Вон, за авто́бусом. Он ждёт нас.","ru":"There, behind the bus. He's waiting for us."},{"who":"A","en":"А, ви́жу! Приве́т, Макси́м! Мы ждём тебя́ весь день!","ru":"Ah, I see him! Hi, Maxim! We've been waiting for you all day!"},{"who":"B","en":"Не весь день, Э́мма. То́лько час!","ru":"Not all day, Emma. Only an hour!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for meeting people and marking the week. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ви́деть","ru":"to see","ex":"Я ви́жу бра́та."},{"en":"ждать","ru":"to wait (for)","ex":"Я жду дру́га."},{"en":"знать","ru":"to know","ex":"Я зна́ю Ива́на."},{"en":"встреча́ть","ru":"to meet","ex":"Я встреча́ю Ле́ну в час."},{"en":"сын","ru":"son","ex":"Она́ ждёт сы́на."},{"en":"челове́к","ru":"person","ex":"Я ви́жу челове́ка."},{"en":"сосе́д","ru":"neighbour","ex":"Я жду сосе́да."},{"en":"понеде́льник","ru":"Monday","ex":"В понеде́льник я рабо́таю."},{"en":"неде́ля","ru":"week","ex":"Ка́ждую неде́лю я в кафе́."},{"en":"ка́ждый","ru":"every, each","ex":"Ка́ждый день я в це́нтре."},{"en":"весь","ru":"all, the whole","ex":"Я жду весь день."},{"en":"час","ru":"hour, o'clock","ex":"Уро́к в час."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English. Watch which he-words bend and which stay put.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["ви́жу бра́та","I see my brother"],["жду дру́га","I wait for a friend"],["в понеде́льник","on Monday"],["ка́ждый день","every day"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions to close the week's grammar. If one bites, the rule above has your back.","items":[{"q":"“I see my brother.” — Я ви́жу ___","opts":["брат","бра́та","бра́том"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I see a table.” — Я ви́жу ___","opts":["стол","стола́","столо́м"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I'm waiting for Maxim.” — Я жду ___","opts":["Макси́му","Макси́м","Макси́ма"],"answer":2},{"q":"“on Saturday” — (суббо́та)","opts":["в суббо́ту","в суббо́та","в суббо́те"],"answer":0},{"q":"“every day” —","opts":["в день","ка́ждый день","ка́ждый дня"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's week in one paragraph — who she sees and when. Read it aloud; every living he-word here wears the genitive ending.","title":"Ка́ждый день в Москве́","sentences":["Ка́ждый день я ви́жу дру́га в кафе́.","В понеде́льник я жду Ива́на, а во вто́рник — Макси́ма.","Я хорошо́ зна́ю Ле́ну и её бра́та.","В сре́ду мы весь день в го́роде.","Ве́чером я жду дру́га у метро́.","Как хорошо́ ви́деть дру́га ка́ждый день!"],"translation":"Every day I see a friend at the café. On Monday I wait for Ivan, and on Tuesday for Maxim. I know Lena and her brother well. On Wednesday we're in the city all day. In the evening I wait for a friend by the metro. How good it is to see a friend every day!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — people and days.","prompt":"Say who you see or wait for, and when. Use a living he-word in the accusative (like <b>бра́та</b>, <b>дру́га</b>) and one time phrase (a day, <b>ка́ждый день</b>, or <b>весь день</b>).","hint":"A living he-word copies the genitive: <b>ви́жу бра́та</b>, <b>жду дру́га</b>. Things don't change (<b>ви́жу дом</b>), and she-words take -у (<b>ви́жу ма́му</b>) 🙂","example":"Ка́ждый день я ви́жу дру́га в кафе́. В понеде́льник я жду бра́та у метро́. А в суббо́ту я до́ма весь день."}},"14":{"day":14,"week":"02","themeRu":"Review 2","themeEn":"Повторе́ние №2","intro":"Six cases. A month ago that number might have frightened you — today you own every one. This is your second checkpoint: no new grammar, just the whole system laid out so you can feel it click. And your friends have thrown Эмма a welcome-home dinner, where every case you've learned is sitting right there at the table.","introAudio":"Six cases. A month ago that number might have frightened you — today you own every one. This is your second checkpoint: no new grammar, just the whole system laid out so you can feel it click. And your friends have thrown Emma a welcome-home dinner, where every case you've learned is sitting right there at the table.","goals":["Send it to a person: <b>пишу́ дру́гу</b>, <b>звоню́ ма́ме</b> — the receiver in the dative («to whom?»)","Name your tool and your job: <b>пишу́ ру́чкой</b>, <b>рабо́таю инжене́ром</b> — the instrumental","Talk and think <em>about</em> someone: <b>ду́маю о бра́те</b> — the prepositional with <b>о</b>","Run one noun through all six cases: <b>брат, бра́та, бра́ту, бра́та, бра́том, о бра́те</b>"],"learned":["Sent it to a person: <b>пишу́ дру́гу</b>, <b>звоню́ ма́ме</b> — the receiver in the dative","Named your tool and your job: <b>пишу́ ру́чкой</b>, <b>рабо́таю инжене́ром</b> — the instrumental","Talked and thought <em>about</em> someone: <b>ду́маю о бра́те</b> — the prepositional with <b>о</b>","Ran one noun through all six cases: <b>брат, бра́та, бра́ту, бра́та, бра́том, о бра́те</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Before we gather the whole system, a warm-up on where it all began — last week's genitive, the «of / no» case. Then the four cases you met this week complete the set.","introAudio":"Before we gather the whole system, a warm-up on where it all began — last week's genitive, the of and no case. Then the four cases you met this week complete the set.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all revision — treat it like greeting old friends.","q":"“I have no time.” — У меня́ нет ___","opts":["вре́мя","вре́мени","вре́менем"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>нет</b> always pulls the genitive: <b>нет вре́мени</b>. (вре́мя is a special it-word — its genitive is вре́мени.)"},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm going home from work.” — Я иду́ домо́й ___ рабо́ты","opts":["с","из","от"],"answer":0,"explain":"Work pairs with <b>на</b> (на рабо́те / на рабо́ту), so “from work” is <b>с рабо́ты</b> — с for the na-words, из for the v-words."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“five books”</b> (пять + кни́ги → …)","want":"пять книг","answer":"пять книг"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “on the first of May” (пе́рвое + ма́й):","accept":["первого мая","первого мая."],"placeholder":"пе́рвого…","explain":"A date puts BOTH the ordinal and the month in the genitive, with no preposition: <b>пе́рвого ма́я</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“It's Ivan's car.” — Э́то маши́на ___","opts":["Ива́н","Ива́на","Ива́ну"],"answer":1,"explain":"Possession = the owner in the genitive: <b>маши́на Ива́на</b>. Ива́н → Ива́на."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The dative — «to whom?»","title":"<em>Пишу́ дру́гу</em> — the receiver of the action","paras":["When something is aimed <em>at a person</em> — you write, call, help, or give a gift TO them — that person steps into the <b>dative</b>, the «to whom?» case. He/it-words take <b>-у/-ю</b>, she-words take <b>-е</b>: друг → дру́г<b>у</b>, ма́ма → ма́м<b>е</b>.","A handful of everyday verbs reach straight for the dative, where English quietly uses a plain object: <b>звони́ть</b> (to call), <b>помога́ть</b> (to help), <b>дари́ть</b> (to give). You call “to a friend”, you help “to a brother”.","The same case carries need and direction-to-a-person: <b>мне на́до рабо́тать</b> (I need to work) and <b>к врачу́</b> (to the doctor) — к + dative points you at a person."],"audio":"When something is aimed at a person — you write, call, help, or give a gift to them — that person steps into the dative, the 'to whom' case. He and it-words take -u or -yu, she-words take -e: drug becomes drugu, mama becomes mame. A handful of everyday verbs reach straight for the dative where English uses a plain object: zvonit, to call; pomogat, to help; darit, to give. You call to a friend, you help to a brother. The same case carries need and direction to a person: mne nado rabotat, I need to work; and k vrachu, to the doctor — k plus the dative points you at a person.","table":{"rows":[["друг → дру́гу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друг, другу\">🔊</button>","friend → to a friend (he-word, -у)"],["брат → бра́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брат, брату\">🔊</button>","brother → to the brother (he-word, -у)"],["ма́ма → ма́ме <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мама, маме\">🔊</button>","Mum → to Mum (she-word, -е)"],["врач → к врачу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"врач, к врачу\">🔊</button>","doctor → to the doctor (к + dative)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I call Mum every day.","en":"Я звоню́ <b>ма́ме</b> ка́ждый день.","say":"Я звоню маме каждый день."},{"ru":"Ivan helps his brother.","en":"Ива́н помога́ет <b>бра́ту</b>.","say":"Иван помогает брату."},{"ru":"I need to go to the doctor.","en":"Мне на́до идти́ <b>к врачу́</b>.","say":"Мне надо идти к врачу.","gloss":"к + dative = towards a person"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я звоню́ бра́та.","right":"Я звоню́ бра́ту.","why":"<b>звони́ть</b> takes the dative — the person you call is a “to-whom”, not a plain object. звоню́ бра́ту."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“I help my brother.” — Я помога́ю ___","opts":["бра́ту","бра́та","бра́том"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>помога́ть</b> takes the dative — you help “to whom”: помога́ю <b>бра́ту</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm calling Mum.”</b>","want":"Я звоню́ ма́ме","answer":"Я звоню́ ма́ме"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type “to the doctor” (к + врач):","accept":["к врачу","к врачу."],"placeholder":"к…","explain":"Direction to a person = <b>к</b> + dative: врач → к врачу́."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The instrumental — «with what? as what?»","title":"<em>Пишу́ ру́чкой, рабо́таю инжене́ром</em>","paras":["The tool you use and the way you travel go into the <b>instrumental</b> — and here English's little words “with” and “by” simply vanish into the ending: <b>пишу́ ру́чкой</b> (I write with a pen), <b>е́ду авто́бусом</b> (I go by bus). He/it-words take <b>-ом/-ем</b>, she-words <b>-ой/-ей</b>.","The word <b>с</b> (“with”) comes back only for <em>company</em> — a person you're with: <b>с дру́гом</b> (with a friend), <b>с Ле́ной</b> (with Lena). Tool = bare instrumental; company = с + instrumental.","Your job wears the instrumental too, after <b>рабо́тать / быть / стать</b>: <b>рабо́таю инжене́ром</b>, <b>был врачо́м</b>. Only bare “I am an engineer” in the present stays nominative — <b>я инжене́р</b>."],"audio":"The tool you use and the way you travel go into the instrumental — and here English's little words 'with' and 'by' simply vanish into the ending: ya pishu ruchkoy, I write with a pen; ya edu avtobusom, I go by bus. He and it-words take -om or -em, she-words -oy or -ey. The word s, 'with', comes back only for company, a person you're with: s drugom, with a friend; s Lenoy, with Lena. Tool is the bare instrumental; company is s plus the instrumental. Your job wears the instrumental too, after rabotat, byt, or stat: rabotayu inzhenerom, I work as an engineer; byl vrachom, was a doctor. Only bare 'I am an engineer' in the present stays nominative — ya inzhener.","table":{"rows":[["ру́чка → ру́чкой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ручка, ручкой\">🔊</button>","pen → with a pen (tool, no с)"],["авто́бус → авто́бусом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"автобус, автобусом\">🔊</button>","bus → by bus (means, no с)"],["друг → с дру́гом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друг, с другом\">🔊</button>","friend → with a friend (company, с)"],["врач → врачо́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"врач, врачом\">🔊</button>","doctor → (works) as a doctor"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm going by bus.","en":"Я е́ду <b>авто́бусом</b>.","say":"Я еду автобусом."},{"ru":"She works as a doctor.","en":"Она́ рабо́тает <b>врачо́м</b>.","say":"Она работает врачом."},{"ru":"I'm going to the cinema with a friend.","en":"Я иду́ в кино́ <b>с дру́гом</b>.","say":"Я иду в кино с другом."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я пишу́ с ру́чкой.","right":"Я пишу́ ру́чкой.","why":"A tool is the bare instrumental — no <b>с</b>. с is only for company (с дру́гом). English “with a pen” doesn't translate its “with”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm going by bus.” — Я е́ду ___","opts":["авто́бус","авто́бусом","авто́буса"],"answer":1,"explain":"“by bus” is the bare instrumental — no preposition: <b>авто́бусом</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I write with a pen.”</b>","want":"Я пишу́ ру́чкой","answer":"Я пишу́ ру́чкой"},{"type":"choice","q":"“She works as an engineer.” — Она́ рабо́тает ___","opts":["инжене́ром","инжене́р","инжене́ра"],"answer":0,"explain":"After <b>рабо́тать</b> the job goes into the instrumental: <b>инжене́ром</b>. (“She is an engineer” with no verb would be nominative: она́ инжене́р.)"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · All six, on one word","title":"<em>Брат</em> through the whole system","paras":["Here's the click. Take one word — <b>брат</b> — and let it walk through all six cases. Each case answers its own question and wears its own tail; the “of / to / with / about” is baked right into that tail, no extra English words needed.","Two you met only this week close the set. <b>About</b> a person is the prepositional with <b>о</b>: <b>ду́маю о бра́те</b> (I think about my brother) — always riding a little preposition. And a living he-word as an <em>object</em> copies its genitive: <b>ви́жу бра́та</b> (I see my brother), never “ви́жу брат”.","Read the grid aloud once. You're not memorising a table — you're hearing six familiar shapes of one small word, and feeling how the ending, not the word order, tells you who did what to whom."],"audio":"Here's the click. Take one word, brat, brother, and let it walk through all six cases. Each case answers its own question and wears its own tail; the of, to, with, and about are baked right in. Two you met only this week close the set. About a person is the prepositional with o: dumayu o brate, I think about my brother, always riding a little preposition. And a living he-word as an object copies its genitive: vizhu brata, I see my brother, never vizhu brat. Read the grid aloud once — you're hearing six familiar shapes of one small word, and feeling how the ending, not the word order, tells you who did what to whom.","table":{"rows":[["брат <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брат\">🔊</button>","Nominative — кто? the subject: «брат до́ма»"],["бра́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брата\">🔊</button>","Genitive — кого́? of / no: «кни́га бра́та»"],["бра́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брату\">🔊</button>","Dative — кому́? to: «пишу́ бра́ту»"],["бра́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брата\">🔊</button>","Accusative — кого́? object: «ви́жу бра́та» (= genitive!)"],["бра́том <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"братом\">🔊</button>","Instrumental — кем? with: «с бра́том»"],["о бра́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"о брате\">🔊</button>","Prepositional — о ком? about: «ду́маю о бра́те»"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"This is my brother's book.","en":"Э́то кни́га <b>бра́та</b>.","say":"Это книга брата.","gloss":"genitive"},{"ru":"I'm writing to my brother.","en":"Я пишу́ <b>бра́ту</b>.","say":"Я пишу брату.","gloss":"dative"},{"ru":"I see my brother.","en":"Я ви́жу <b>бра́та</b>.","say":"Я вижу брата.","gloss":"accusative = genitive (animate)"},{"ru":"I'm thinking about my brother.","en":"Я ду́маю <b>о бра́те</b>.","say":"Я думаю о брате.","gloss":"prepositional with о"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я ду́маю бра́та.","right":"Я ду́маю о бра́те.","why":"“Think about” isn't a plain object in Russian — it's <b>ду́мать о</b> + prepositional: о бра́те. The о is not optional."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which means “I'm thinking about my brother”?","opts":["Я ду́маю бра́та.","Я ду́маю о бра́те.","Я ду́маю бра́ту."],"answer":1,"explain":"“think about” = <b>ду́мать о</b> + prepositional: <b>о бра́те</b>. Not a bare object, not the dative."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I see my brother.” — a living he-word object copies the genitive: Я ви́жу ___","opts":["бра́та","брат","бра́ту"],"answer":0,"explain":"Animate masculine accusative = genitive: <b>ви́жу бра́та</b>, just like кни́га бра́та. Things don't change (ви́жу стол)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я рабо́таю инжене́ром.","sub":"say what you do for a living (swap in your own job if you like)","want":"я работаю инженером"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ivan and Lena have cooked a welcome-home dinner for Эмма. Listen for the cases flying past — «to whom», «with what», «about whom» — every one now yours. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, приве́т! Э́тот у́жин — для тебя́.","ru":"Emma, hi! This dinner is for you."},{"who":"A","en":"Как краси́во! Кто гото́вил э́тот у́жин?","ru":"How lovely! Who cooked this dinner?"},{"who":"B","en":"Мы с Ле́ной. Я о́чень люблю́ гото́вить.","ru":"Lena and I. I really love to cook. (мы с Ле́ной = “Lena and I”)"},{"who":"A","en":"А э́то что? Пода́рок? От кого́?","ru":"And what's this? A gift? From whom?"},{"who":"B","en":"От бра́та! Он рабо́тает инжене́ром здесь, в Москве́.","ru":"From my brother! He works as an engineer here in Moscow."},{"who":"A","en":"Как интере́сно! На́до сказа́ть ему́ спаси́бо.","ru":"How interesting! I should say thank you to him."},{"who":"B","en":"Обяза́тельно! А мы все ду́маем о тебе́ ка́ждый день.","ru":"Definitely! And we all think about you every day. (обяза́тельно = for sure)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо. Я так ра́да сно́ва ви́деть Ива́на и Ле́ну!","ru":"Thank you. I'm so glad to see Ivan and Lena again!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words from the week the cases came together. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"письмо́","ru":"letter","ex":"Я пишу́ письмо́ дру́гу."},{"en":"звони́ть","ru":"to call (phone)","ex":"Я звоню́ ма́ме."},{"en":"помога́ть","ru":"to help","ex":"Он помога́ет бра́ту."},{"en":"дари́ть","ru":"to give (a gift)","ex":"Я дарю́ пода́рок дру́гу."},{"en":"пода́рок","ru":"gift, present","ex":"Э́то пода́рок для тебя́."},{"en":"на́до","ru":"(one) needs to, must","ex":"Мне на́до рабо́тать."},{"en":"врач","ru":"doctor","ex":"Я иду́ к врачу́."},{"en":"ру́чка","ru":"pen","ex":"Я пишу́ ру́чкой."},{"en":"авто́бус","ru":"bus","ex":"Я е́ду авто́бусом."},{"en":"инжене́р","ru":"engineer","ex":"Он рабо́тает инжене́ром."},{"en":"рабо́та","ru":"work, job","ex":"Мы говори́м о рабо́те."},{"en":"ду́мать","ru":"to think","ex":"Я ду́маю о тебе́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to its case — one from every corner of the week.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["пишу́ дру́гу","I write to a friend (dative)"],["рабо́таю инжене́ром","I work as an engineer (instrumental)"],["ду́маю о бра́те","I think about my brother (prepositional)"],["ви́жу бра́та","I see my brother (animate accusative)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions across the whole week. If one bites, the rule above still has the answer.","items":[{"q":"“I'm calling my brother.” — Я звоню́ ___","opts":["брат","бра́та","бра́ту"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I write with a pen.” — Я пишу́ ___","opts":["ру́чку","ру́чкой","с ру́чкой"],"answer":1},{"q":"“She works as a doctor.” — Она́ рабо́тает ___","opts":["врач","врача́","врачо́м"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I'm thinking about you.” — Я ду́маю ___","opts":["о тебе́","тебя́","тебе́"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I see Ivan.” — Я ви́жу ___","opts":["Ива́н","Ива́на","Ива́ну"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I need to go to the doctor.” — Мне на́до идти́ ___","opts":["к врачу́","у врача́","врача́"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I'm going to the cinema with a friend.” — Я иду́ в кино́ ___","opts":["дру́гом","с дру́гом","с друг"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is the dative of брат («to the brother»)?","opts":["бра́та","бра́ту","бра́том"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note after the dinner — read it aloud. Every case in it is one you can now make yourself.","title":"Ве́чер с друзья́ми","sentences":["Сего́дня ве́чером я у Ива́на и Ле́ны.","Ива́н гото́вил у́жин, а Ле́на была́ на рабо́те.","На столе́ — пода́рок от бра́та Ива́на. Он рабо́тает инжене́ром.","Я пишу́ ему́ письмо́ и говорю́ спаси́бо.","Мы весь ве́чер говори́м о Москве́, о рабо́те и о друзья́х.","Я так ра́да сно́ва ви́деть Ива́на и Ле́ну!"],"translation":"Tonight I'm at Ivan and Lena's. Ivan was cooking dinner, and Lena was at work. On the table is a gift from Ivan's brother — he works as an engineer. I write him a letter and say thank you. All evening we talk about Moscow, about work and about friends. I'm so glad to see Ivan and Lena again!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — take one person through the week's cases.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about a friend or a family member: what they do (instrumental — рабо́тает …ом), that you call or write to them (dative), and that you often think about them (о + prepositional).","hint":"Lean on the patterns: <b>рабо́тает врачо́м</b>, <b>звоню́ дру́гу</b>, <b>ду́маю о ма́ме</b>. Every «to / with / about» is baked into the ending 🙂","example":"Мой брат — врач, он рабо́тает в Москве́. Я звоню́ ему́ ка́ждый день и пишу́ ему́ пи́сьма. Я ча́сто ду́маю о нём. И я о́чень люблю́ бра́та."}},"15":{"day":15,"week":"03","themeRu":"Process or result?","themeEn":"Де́лать / сде́лать","intro":"You've built the whole case system — now meet the other great engine of Russian. English has one verb for “to read”; Russian keeps two, and asks you to pick between them every time you speak. It's called <b>aspect</b>, and today Эмма spots it hiding in plain sight.","introAudio":"You've built the whole case system — now meet the other great engine of Russian. English has one verb for 'to read'; Russian keeps two, and asks you to pick between them every time you speak. It's called aspect, and today Emma spots it hiding in plain sight.","goals":["Meet Russian <b>aspect</b>: one English verb = a Russian pair, <b>де́лать / сде́лать</b>","Tell the two apart: imperfective = process/habit, perfective = one finished result","Spot the pair — a prefix usually builds the perfective: <b>чита́ть → прочита́ть</b>, <b>писа́ть → написа́ть</b>","Learn the big rule: the perfective has <b>no present tense</b> — only past or future"],"learned":["Met Russian <b>aspect</b>: one English verb = a Russian pair, <b>де́лать / сде́лать</b>","Told the two apart: imperfective = process/habit, perfective = one finished result","Spotted the pair — a prefix usually builds the perfective: <b>чита́ть → прочита́ть</b>","Learned the big rule: the perfective has <b>no present tense</b> — only past or future"],"review":{"intro":"The cases stay always-on. A quick lap through this week's newest three — dative, prepositional «о», and the animate accusative — then we open a whole new door.","introAudio":"The cases stay always-on. A quick lap through this week's newest three — dative, prepositional o, and the animate accusative — then we open a whole new door.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Old friends first.","q":"“I'm writing to a friend.” — Я пишу́ ___","opts":["дру́гу","дру́га","дру́гом"],"answer":0,"explain":"The receiver goes into the dative: <b>пишу́ дру́гу</b>. (дру́га = genitive/accusative, дру́гом = instrumental.)"},{"type":"choice","q":"“We're talking about work.” — Мы говори́м ___","opts":["рабо́ту","о рабо́те","рабо́ты"],"answer":1,"explain":"“talk about” = <b>говори́ть о</b> + prepositional: <b>о рабо́те</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I see Ivan.”</b> (a living he-word)","want":"Я ви́жу Ива́на","answer":"Я ви́жу Ива́на"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · One meaning, two verbs","title":"<em>Де́лать / сде́лать</em> — aspect, not time","paras":["Here's a surprise Russian has been keeping. Almost every verb comes as a <b>pair</b> — two verbs where English has one. <b>де́лать</b> and <b>сде́лать</b> both mean “to do”. The difference isn't <em>when</em> — it's <b>aspect</b>, a way of seeing the action.","The <b>imperfective</b> (де́лать, чита́ть) shows the action as a <em>process</em>, a habit, or the general idea — the film rolling. The <b>perfective</b> (сде́лать, прочита́ть) shows the whole action as <em>one finished result</em> — a single snapshot, done.","Same event, two lenses: <b>Я чита́л ве́чером</b> (I was reading in the evening — a process) vs <b>Я прочита́л кни́гу</b> (I read the book, cover to cover — a result). You'll choose a lens every time you use a verb."],"audio":"Here's a surprise Russian has been keeping. Almost every verb comes as a pair — two verbs where English has one. Delat and sdelat both mean 'to do'. The difference isn't when — it's aspect, a way of seeing the action. The imperfective, delat, chitat, shows the action as a process, a habit, or the general idea — the film rolling. The perfective, sdelat, prochitat, shows the whole action as one finished result — a single snapshot, done. Same event, two lenses: ya chital vecherom, I was reading in the evening, a process; ya prochital knigu, I read the book cover to cover, a result. You'll choose a lens every time you use a verb.","table":{"rows":[["де́лать → сде́лать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"делать, сделать\">🔊</button>","to do (process) → to get done (result)"],["чита́ть → прочита́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читать, прочитать\">🔊</button>","to read → to read through"],["писа́ть → написа́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"писать, написать\">🔊</button>","to write → to write (finish)"],["говори́ть → сказа́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"говорить, сказать\">🔊</button>","to talk → to say (one thing)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I was reading in the evening. (process)","en":"Я <b>чита́л</b> ве́чером.","say":"Я читал вечером."},{"ru":"I read the book (cover to cover). (result)","en":"Я <b>прочита́л</b> кни́гу.","say":"Я прочитал книгу."},{"ru":"What are you doing? (process, now)","en":"Что ты <b>де́лаешь</b>?","say":"Что ты делаешь?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"де́лать = past, сде́лать = present","right":"де́лать = process, сде́лать = result","why":"The pair isn't about time. Both live in the past and the future; they differ in <b>aspect</b> — a rolling process vs one finished result."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"<b>Де́лать</b> and <b>сде́лать</b> differ in…","opts":["time (past vs present)","aspect (process vs finished result)","gender"],"answer":1,"explain":"Not time — <b>aspect</b>. де́лать = the process; сде́лать = one finished result."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I read the book (through).”</b> — the finished result","want":"Я прочита́л кни́гу","answer":"Я прочита́л кни́гу"},{"type":"choice","q":"“I was reading in the evening.” (a process) —","opts":["Я прочита́л ве́чером.","Я чита́л ве́чером.","Я чита́ю ве́чером."],"answer":1,"explain":"A process in the past = imperfective <b>чита́л</b>. прочита́л would say you finished it; чита́ю is the present."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Spotting the pair","title":"A prefix builds the perfective — <em>про-, на-, с-</em>","paras":["How do you find the two halves of a pair? Most often the perfective is just the imperfective plus a little <b>prefix</b> that means “all the way”: чита́ть → <b>про</b>чита́ть, писа́ть → <b>на</b>писа́ть, де́лать → <b>с</b>де́лать. Same verb, one small piece added, and now it means “done”.","A few pairs are two different-looking words you simply learn together — the classic is <b>говори́ть → сказа́ть</b> (to talk → to say). No shared root, but they're partners all the same.","In a good dictionary the two always sit side by side. Once you can see the pair, you're free to choose the lens you need."],"audio":"How do you find the two halves of a pair? Most often the perfective is just the imperfective plus a little prefix that means 'all the way': chitat becomes prochitat, pisat becomes napisat, delat becomes sdelat. Same verb, one small piece added, and now it means done. A few pairs are two different-looking words you simply learn together — the classic is govorit and skazat, to talk and to say. No shared root, but partners all the same. In a good dictionary the two always sit side by side. Once you can see the pair, you're free to choose the lens you need.","table":{"rows":[["чита́ть → <b>про</b>чита́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читать, прочитать\">🔊</button>","read → read through (prefix про-)"],["писа́ть → <b>на</b>писа́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"писать, написать\">🔊</button>","write → write up (prefix на-)"],["де́лать → <b>с</b>де́лать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"делать, сделать\">🔊</button>","do → get done (prefix с-)"],["говори́ть → сказа́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"говорить, сказать\">🔊</button>","talk → say (a different-looking twin)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"I wrote a letter (finished it).","en":"Я <b>написа́л</b> письмо́.","say":"Я написал письмо."},{"ru":"He said the truth (one thing).","en":"Он <b>сказа́л</b> пра́вду.","say":"Он сказал правду.","gloss":"говори́ть → сказа́ть"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"One Russian verb covers English “read”.","right":"чита́ть AND прочита́ть — a pair.","why":"English “read” hides a choice. Russian gives you two verbs and asks which lens you mean — process (чита́ть) or result (прочита́ть)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which pair is <em>two different-looking words</em>, not just a prefix?","opts":["де́лать / сде́лать","чита́ть / прочита́ть","говори́ть / сказа́ть"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first two just add a prefix; <b>говори́ть / сказа́ть</b> is the odd pair — two different words you learn together."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type the perfective of <b>чита́ть</b> (add про-):","accept":["прочитать","прочита́ть"],"placeholder":"про…","explain":"чита́ть → <b>прочита́ть</b> — to read all the way through."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The perfective has no present","title":"The present belongs to the imperfective — <em>чита́ю</em>","paras":["Now a rule with no exceptions: the <b>perfective has no present tense</b>. The present belongs entirely to the imperfective. <b>Я чита́ю</b> means “I'm reading, right now” — imperfective.","The perfective can only look <em>back</em> (a past: <b>прочита́л</b>) or <em>forward</em> (a future you'll build in a couple of days) — but never at this very moment. There is simply no “I'm finishing-it now” form.","So when someone asks <b>Что ты де́лаешь?</b> (“What are you doing?”), the answer is always imperfective: <b>Я чита́ю</b>, <b>Я де́лаю уро́ки</b>. A finished result can't be happening now."],"audio":"Now a rule with no exceptions: the perfective has no present tense. The present belongs entirely to the imperfective. Ya chitayu means I'm reading, right now — imperfective. The perfective can only look back, a past, prochital, or forward, a future you'll build in a couple of days — but never at this very moment. There's simply no 'I'm finishing it now' form. So when someone asks chto ty delaesh, what are you doing, the answer is always imperfective: ya chitayu, ya delayu uroki. A finished result can't be happening now.","table":{"rows":[["Я чита́ю. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я читаю\">🔊</button>","present — “I'm reading now” (imperfective only)"],["Я чита́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я читал\">🔊</button>","past process — “I was reading” (imperfective)"],["Я прочита́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я прочитал\">🔊</button>","past result — “I read it through” (perfective)"],["✗ present of прочита́ть","does not exist — perfective is past or future only"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"What are you doing? — I'm reading.","en":"Что ты де́лаешь? — Я <b>чита́ю</b>.","say":"Что ты делаешь? Я читаю."},{"ru":"I'm doing my homework right now.","en":"Я сейча́с <b>де́лаю</b> уро́ки.","say":"Я сейчас делаю уроки."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Что ты де́лаешь? — Я прочита́л.","right":"Что ты де́лаешь? — Я чита́ю.","why":"“What are you doing?” asks about now — the process. Only the imperfective lives in the present: <b>чита́ю</b>. прочита́л is a finished past, not a “now”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“What are you doing (right now)?” must be answered with…","opts":["the perfective (сде́лал)","the imperfective (де́лаю)","either one"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present is imperfective territory only: <b>де́лаю</b>. The perfective has no “now”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“What are you doing?”</b>","want":"Что ты де́лаешь","answer":"Что ты де́лаешь"},{"type":"choice","q":"The perfective <b>сде́лать</b> exists in…","opts":["the present only","the past or the future, never the present","all three tenses"],"answer":1,"explain":"A perfective lives in the past (сде́лал) or the future (coming soon) — <b>never the present</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Choosing the lens","title":"Ask “process or result?”, not “when?”","paras":["So how do you choose? Not by <em>when</em> — by what you <b>mean</b>. Ask one question: <b>process, or result?</b> Was the action going on, repeated, a habit? Then imperfective. Was it one whole thing, finished, complete? Then perfective.","<b>Я де́лал уро́ки</b> — I was doing my homework (the evening rolling by). <b>Я сде́лал уро́ки</b> — I did the homework, it's complete. Same yesterday evening, two different pictures.","This is the point English speakers keep missing: your instinct is to match a <em>tense</em>, but Russian is asking about the <b>shape</b> of the action. Tense tells you when; aspect tells you how you see it."],"audio":"So how do you choose? Not by when — by what you mean. Ask one question: process, or result? Was the action going on, repeated, a habit? Then imperfective. Was it one whole thing, finished, complete? Then perfective. Ya delal uroki, I was doing my homework, the evening rolling by. Ya sdelal uroki, I did the homework, it's complete. Same yesterday evening, two different pictures. This is the point English speakers keep missing: your instinct is to match a tense, but Russian is asking about the shape of the action. Tense tells you when; aspect tells you how you see it.","table":{"rows":[["Я де́лал уро́ки. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я делал уроки\">🔊</button>","I was doing homework — process (imperfective)"],["Я сде́лал уро́ки. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я сделал уроки\">🔊</button>","I did / finished the homework — result (perfective)"],["Я писа́л письмо́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я писал письмо\">🔊</button>","I was writing a letter — process"],["Я написа́л письмо́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я написал письмо\">🔊</button>","I wrote the letter (it's done) — result"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I was doing my homework all evening. (process)","en":"Я <b>де́лал</b> уро́ки весь ве́чер.","say":"Я делал уроки весь вечер."},{"ru":"I did my homework — done! (result)","en":"Я <b>сде́лал</b> уро́ки — гото́во!","say":"Я сделал уроки, готово!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ка́ждый день я сде́лал уро́ки.","right":"Ка́ждый день я де́лал уро́ки.","why":"“Every day” is repetition — a process that keeps coming back → imperfective <b>де́лал</b>. One finished result (сде́лал) can't happen daily."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Every day I did my homework.” (repetition) —","opts":["Ка́ждый день я сде́лал уро́ки.","Ка́ждый день я де́лал уро́ки."],"answer":1,"explain":"Repetition = a process coming back again and again → imperfective <b>де́лал</b>. A single finished result can't happen “every day”."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я прочита́л кни́гу.","sub":"say you've read the book — all the way through (a finished result)","want":"я прочитал книгу"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I was writing a letter” — a process (писа́л + письмо́):","accept":["я писал письмо","я писала письмо","я писа́л письмо́"],"placeholder":"Я писа́л…","explain":"A process in the past = imperfective <b>писа́л(а)</b>. написа́л would mean you finished it."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is doing homework at the café when she spots something odd in her textbook — two verbs that both seem to mean “do”. Ivan explains. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, я не понима́ю: «де́лать» и «сде́лать» — э́то одно́ и то же?","ru":"Ivan, I don't understand: “делать” and “сделать” — are they the same thing? (одно́ и то же = the same)"},{"who":"B","en":"Почти́. «Де́лать» — э́то проце́сс, а «сде́лать» — результа́т.","ru":"Almost. “делать” is the process, and “сделать” is the result."},{"who":"A","en":"Проце́сс и́ли результа́т… А как э́то рабо́тает?","ru":"Process or result… And how does that work?"},{"who":"B","en":"Вот приме́р: «Я де́лал уро́ки ве́чером» — проце́сс. «Я сде́лал уро́ки» — всё, гото́во!","ru":"Here’s an example: “I was doing homework in the evening” — process. “I did the homework” — that’s it, done! (гото́во = done)"},{"who":"A","en":"А, поняла́! Оди́н англи́йский глаго́л — два ру́сских.","ru":"Ah, I get it! One English verb — two Russian ones. (глаго́л = verb)"},{"who":"B","en":"И́менно! «Сде́лал» — гото́во, а «де́лаю» — сейча́с.","ru":"Exactly! “сделал” — done, and “делаю” — right now. (и́менно = exactly)"},{"who":"A","en":"Тепе́рь я ви́жу разни́цу. Ру́сский язы́к лю́бит выбира́ть!","ru":"Now I see the difference. The Russian language loves to choose! (разни́ца = difference)"},{"who":"B","en":"Ты де́лаешь успе́хи, Э́мма!","ru":"You're making progress, Emma! (де́лать успе́хи = to make progress)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the twin verbs. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"де́лать / сде́лать","ru":"to do (process / result)","ex":"Я сде́лал все уро́ки."},{"en":"чита́ть / прочита́ть","ru":"to read (through)","ex":"Я прочита́л кни́гу."},{"en":"писа́ть / написа́ть","ru":"to write (up)","ex":"Я написа́л письмо́."},{"en":"говори́ть / сказа́ть","ru":"to talk / to say","ex":"Он сказа́л пра́вду."},{"en":"вид","ru":"aspect (of a verb)","ex":"У глаго́ла два ви́да."},{"en":"глаго́л","ru":"verb","ex":"«Чита́ть» — э́то глаго́л."},{"en":"проце́сс","ru":"process","ex":"«Де́лать» — э́то проце́сс."},{"en":"результа́т","ru":"result","ex":"«Сде́лать» — э́то результа́т."},{"en":"гото́во","ru":"done, ready","ex":"Всё гото́во!"},{"en":"пра́вда","ru":"truth; really?","ex":"Ты сказа́л пра́вду."},{"en":"уро́ки","ru":"homework, lessons","ex":"Я де́лаю уро́ки."},{"en":"ка́ждый день","ru":"every day","ex":"Я чита́ю ка́ждый день."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each sentence to its lens — process or result.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я чита́л кни́гу","I was reading the book (process)"],["Я прочита́л кни́гу","I read the book through (result)"],["Я де́лаю уро́ки","I'm doing homework (right now)"],["Я сде́лал уро́ки","I've done the homework (finished)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the twin verbs. If one bites, the rule above still has it.","items":[{"q":"Which verb shows the <b>process</b> (imperfective)?","opts":["сде́лать","де́лать","гото́во"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which verb sees <b>one finished result</b> (perfective)?","opts":["чита́ть","писа́ть","прочита́ть"],"answer":2},{"q":"The perfective <b>сде́лать</b> has…","opts":["a present tense","no present — only past or future","only a present tense"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Every day I read a book.” — Ка́ждый день я ___ кни́гу.","opts":["чита́ю","прочита́л","сде́лал"],"answer":0},{"q":"“What are you doing?” asks about…","opts":["a finished result → perfective","the process right now → imperfective","the future"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's diary the day the twin verbs clicked — read it aloud. She's telling it as a woman (поняла́, чита́ла).","title":"Оди́н смысл, два глаго́ла","sentences":["Сего́дня я поняла́ ва́жную вещь о ру́сском языке́.","У ка́ждого глаго́ла есть два ви́да: проце́сс и результа́т.","«Я чита́ла кни́гу» — э́то проце́сс, я чита́ла до́лго.","«Я прочита́ла кни́гу» — гото́во, вот результа́т!","Оди́н англи́йский глаго́л, а по-ру́сски их два.","Ру́сский язы́к лю́бит то́чность, и мне э́то нра́вится."],"translation":"Today I understood an important thing about the Russian language. Every verb has two aspects: process and result. “I was reading a book” — that's the process, I read for a long time. “I read the book through” — done, here's the result! One English verb, but in Russian there are two of them. The Russian language loves precision, and I like that."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — feel the two lenses.","prompt":"Pick a verb pair (де́лать/сде́лать, чита́ть/прочита́ть, писа́ть/написа́ть) and write two sentences about yesterday: one where the action was a process, one where you finished it. Then say why you chose each aspect.","hint":"Process → imperfective (<b>чита́л(а)</b>, was reading). One finished result → perfective (<b>прочита́л(а)</b>, read it through). Same day, two pictures 🙂","example":"Вчера́ я до́лго чита́л кни́гу — э́то был проце́сс. Пото́м я прочита́л всю кни́гу — гото́во, результа́т! Оди́н смысл, но два ви́да."}},"16":{"day":16,"week":"03","themeRu":"Was doing vs did","themeEn":"Чита́л / прочита́л","intro":"Yesterday you learned Russian keeps two verbs for one; today you put them to work in the <b>past</b>. Was it a process that rolled on — or one thing you got done? Эмма tells Ivan about her first study day, and the difference is written all over it.","introAudio":"Yesterday you learned Russian keeps two verbs for one; today you put them to work in the past. Was it a process that rolled on — or one thing you got done? Emma tells Ivan about her first study day, and the difference is written all over it.","goals":["Tell the past two ways: <b>чита́л</b> (was reading / used to) vs <b>прочита́л</b> (read it to the end)","Read the signals: <b>весь день, ка́ждый день</b> → imperfective; <b>уже́, бы́стро</b> → perfective","Say what you didn't finish: <b>чита́л, но не прочита́л</b>","Beat the trap: English “I read it” hides the aspect — Russian makes you choose"],"learned":["Told the past two ways: <b>чита́л</b> (was reading) vs <b>прочита́л</b> (read it through)","Read the signals: <b>весь день, ка́ждый день</b> → imperfective; <b>уже́, бы́стро</b> → perfective","Said what you didn't finish: <b>чита́л, но не прочита́л</b>","Beat the trap: English “I read it” hides the aspect — Russian makes you choose"],"review":{"intro":"Aspect is now always-on, so we start with yesterday's big rule, then keep two cases warm. Then into the past.","introAudio":"Aspect is now always-on, so we start with yesterday's big rule, then keep two cases warm. Then into the past.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"The perfective <b>сде́лать</b> has…","opts":["a present tense","no present tense — past or future only","three tenses"],"answer":1,"explain":"The present belongs to the imperfective (де́лаю). A perfective is <b>past or future only</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I have a lot of books.” — У меня́ мно́го ___","opts":["книг","кни́ги","кни́гам"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>мно́го</b> takes the genitive plural: <b>книг</b> (zero ending). кни́ги is singular/nominative-plural."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I write with a pen.”</b> (instrumental, no с)","want":"Я пишу́ ру́чкой","answer":"Я пишу́ ру́чкой"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The past, two ways","title":"<em>Чита́л</em> — was reading · <em>прочита́л</em> — read it through","paras":["In the past, both aspects use the same <b>-л/-ла/-ло/-ли</b> endings you built in A1 — but the verb you pick changes the whole picture. <b>чита́л</b> means “I was reading” or “I used to read” — a process or a habit, and it doesn't promise you finished.","<b>прочита́л</b> means “I read it — all the way to the end” — one completed result. Same past ending, different verb, different meaning.","So the two sit side by side in time but tell different stories: <b>Ве́чером я чита́л</b> (in the evening I was reading) vs <b>Я прочита́л всю кни́гу</b> (I read the whole book, cover to cover)."],"audio":"In the past, both aspects use the same -l, -la, -lo, -li endings you built in A1 — but the verb you pick changes the whole picture. Chital means 'I was reading' or 'I used to read' — a process or a habit, and it doesn't promise you finished. Prochital means 'I read it, all the way to the end' — one completed result. Same past ending, different verb, different meaning. Vecherom ya chital, in the evening I was reading; ya prochital vsyu knigu, I read the whole book, cover to cover.","table":{"rows":[["Я чита́л кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я читал книгу\">🔊</button>","I was reading / used to read the book (process)"],["Я прочита́л кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я прочитал книгу\">🔊</button>","I read the book (to the end) (result)"],["Я писа́л письмо́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я писал письмо\">🔊</button>","I was writing a letter (process)"],["Я написа́л письмо́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я написал письмо\">🔊</button>","I wrote the letter (finished it) (result)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"In the evening I was reading. (process)","en":"Ве́чером я <b>чита́л</b>.","say":"Вечером я читал."},{"ru":"I read the whole book. (result)","en":"Я <b>прочита́л</b> всю кни́гу.","say":"Я прочитал всю книгу."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я чита́л кни́гу. = “I finished the book.”","right":"Я прочита́л кни́гу. = “I finished the book.”","why":"чита́л only says the reading went on — it doesn't promise a finish. To claim you reached the end, use the perfective <b>прочита́л</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“I read the whole book (finished it).” —","opts":["Я чита́л кни́гу.","Я прочита́л кни́гу."],"answer":1,"explain":"Reached the end = one completed result → perfective <b>прочита́л</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I was writing a letter.”</b> (process)","want":"Я писа́л письмо́","answer":"Я писа́л письмо́"},{"type":"choice","q":"<b>Я чита́л</b> tells you the reading…","opts":["definitely finished","went on — maybe finished, maybe not","never started"],"answer":1,"explain":"The imperfective reports the process, not the finish line. For “finished”, you'd need прочита́л."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Words that call the imperfective","title":"<em>Весь день, ка́ждый день</em> — duration & habit","paras":["Some words point straight at the <b>imperfective</b>. <b>Duration</b> — весь день, весь ве́чер, до́лго (all day, all evening, for a long time) — a process stretched out over time.","<b>Repetition</b> — ка́ждый день, ча́сто, обы́чно (every day, often, usually) — a habit that came back again and again.","If the action filled up time, or kept repeating, reach for the imperfective: <b>Я весь день де́лал уро́ки</b>, <b>Она́ ка́ждый день чита́ла газе́ту</b>."],"audio":"Some words point straight at the imperfective. Duration — ves den, ves vecher, dolgo: all day, all evening, for a long time, a process stretched out over time. Repetition — kazhdy den, chasto, obychno: every day, often, usually, a habit that came back again and again. If the action filled up time, or kept repeating, reach for the imperfective: ya ves den delal uroki, I was doing homework all day; ona kazhdy den chitala gazetu, she read a newspaper every day.","table":{"rows":[["весь день / весь ве́чер <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"весь день, весь вечер\">🔊</button>","all day / evening → duration (imperfective)"],["до́лго <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"долго\">🔊</button>","for a long time → duration (imperfective)"],["ка́ждый день <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"каждый день\">🔊</button>","every day → repetition (imperfective)"],["ча́сто / обы́чно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"часто, обычно\">🔊</button>","often / usually → habit (imperfective)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I was doing homework all day.","en":"Я весь день <b>де́лал</b> уро́ки.","say":"Я весь день делал уроки."},{"ru":"She read a newspaper every day.","en":"Она́ ка́ждый день <b>чита́ла</b> газе́ту.","say":"Она каждый день читала газету.","gloss":"газе́та = newspaper"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Весь день я сде́лал уро́ки.","right":"Весь день я де́лал уро́ки.","why":"“All day” is duration — the process filled the day → imperfective <b>де́лал</b>. сде́лал is one finished moment, not a day-long stretch."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“All evening I was reading a book.” — Весь ве́чер я ___ кни́гу.","opts":["чита́л","прочита́л"],"answer":0,"explain":"“All evening” = duration → imperfective <b>чита́л</b>. прочита́л would be one finished result, not a stretch of time."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type “every day I read” (past habit: ка́ждый день + чита́л/чита́ла):","accept":["каждый день я читал","каждый день я читала"],"placeholder":"Ка́ждый день я…","explain":"A past habit = repetition → imperfective <b>чита́л(а)</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Words that call the perfective","title":"<em>Уже́, бы́стро, наконе́ц</em> — one done thing","paras":["Other words point at the <b>perfective</b> — one sharp, completed event. <b>уже́</b> (already — it's done), <b>бы́стро</b> (quickly — got it done), <b>вдруг</b> (suddenly), <b>наконе́ц</b> (finally — completed at last).","A chain of finished steps also strings perfectives together: “I <em>wrote</em> the letter, <em>did</em> the homework, and <em>read</em> the news” — each one a small result clicked into place.","<b>Я уже́ прочита́л письмо́</b> (I've already read the letter). <b>Она́ бы́стро написа́ла отве́т</b> (she quickly wrote a reply). Done, done."],"audio":"Other words point at the perfective — one sharp, completed event. Uzhe, already, it's done. Bystro, quickly, got it done. Vdrug, suddenly. Nakonets, finally, completed at last. A chain of finished steps also strings perfectives together: I wrote the letter, did the homework, and read the news — each one a small result clicked into place. Ya uzhe prochital pismo, I've already read the letter. Ona bystro napisala otvet, she quickly wrote a reply. Done, done.","table":{"rows":[["уже́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уже\">🔊</button>","already → it's done (perfective)"],["бы́стро <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"быстро\">🔊</button>","quickly → got it done (perfective)"],["вдруг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вдруг\">🔊</button>","suddenly → one sharp event (perfective)"],["наконе́ц <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"наконец\">🔊</button>","finally → completed at last (perfective)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"I've already read the letter.","en":"Я уже́ <b>прочита́л</b> письмо́.","say":"Я уже прочитал письмо."},{"ru":"She quickly wrote a reply.","en":"Она́ бы́стро <b>написа́ла</b> отве́т.","say":"Она быстро написала ответ.","gloss":"отве́т = reply, answer"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я наконе́ц чита́л письмо́.","right":"Я наконе́ц прочита́л письмо́.","why":"наконе́ц (“finally”) celebrates a finish line → perfective <b>прочита́л</b>. The imperfective чита́л has no finish to celebrate."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which word points to the <b>perfective</b> (one completed event)?","opts":["ка́ждый день","весь день","наконе́ц"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>наконе́ц</b> (“finally”) crowns a finish → perfective. ка́ждый день and весь день stretch or repeat → imperfective."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I've already read the letter.”</b> (finished)","want":"Я уже́ прочита́л письмо́","answer":"Я уже́ прочита́л письмо́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The hidden choice","title":"<em>Чита́л, но не прочита́л</em> — the trap English hides","paras":["Here's the trap. English “I read the book” tells you <em>when</em> — the past — but hides the aspect: did you finish? Russian won't let you dodge. <b>Я чита́л</b> may leave it open; <b>Я прочита́л</b> promises the last page.","And Russian can say something English struggles with in one breath: <b>Я чита́л кни́гу, но не прочита́л</b> — “I was reading the book, but didn't finish it.” The imperfective reports the effort; the perfective would claim the result.","So before you speak, ask the Rule-15 question — process or result? — and let the marker words (весь день vs уже́) back you up."],"audio":"Here's the trap. English 'I read the book' tells you when — the past — but hides the aspect: did you finish? Russian won't let you dodge. Ya chital may leave it open; ya prochital promises the last page. And Russian can say something English struggles with in one breath: ya chital knigu, no ne prochital — I was reading the book, but didn't finish it. The imperfective reports the effort; the perfective would claim the result. So before you speak, ask the question from yesterday — process or result? — and let the marker words, ves den versus uzhe, back you up.","table":{"rows":[["Я чита́л кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я читал книгу\">🔊</button>","I was reading the book (maybe unfinished)"],["Я прочита́л кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я прочитал книгу\">🔊</button>","I read the book (finished it)"],["Я чита́л, но не прочита́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я читал, но не прочитал\">🔊</button>","I was reading, but didn't finish"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"I was doing my homework, but didn't get it all done.","en":"Я <b>де́лал</b> уро́ки, но не всё <b>сде́лал</b>.","say":"Я делал уроки, но не всё сделал."},{"ru":"She was writing the letter for two hours.","en":"Она́ <b>писа́ла</b> письмо́ два часа́.","say":"Она писала письмо два часа.","gloss":"два часа́ = for two hours (duration)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я прочита́л кни́гу, но не прочита́л.","right":"Я чита́л кни́гу, но не прочита́л.","why":"If you didn't reach the end, the reading was a process → imperfective <b>чита́л</b>. прочита́л already claims the finish, so it can't be “didn't finish”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I was reading the book, but didn't finish it.” —","opts":["Я чита́ю кни́гу.","Я чита́л кни́гу, но не прочита́л.","Я прочита́л кни́гу."],"answer":1,"explain":"Process + no finish → imperfective <b>чита́л</b> … не <b>прочита́л</b>. чита́ю is the present; прочита́л alone claims you finished."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I read the whole letter” — finished it (прочита́л + всё письмо́):","accept":["я прочитал всё письмо","я прочитала всё письмо","я прочитал все письмо","я прочитала все письмо"],"placeholder":"Я прочита́л…","explain":"Reached the end → perfective <b>прочита́л(а)</b>."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я чита́л, но не прочита́л.","sub":"admit you were reading, but didn't finish","want":"я читал но не прочитал"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ivan asks Эмма how her first study day went. She has a lot to report — some of it finished, some of it… not quite. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну как, Э́мма? Что ты де́лала вчера́?","ru":"So how was it, Emma? What did you do yesterday?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ой, мно́го! Я весь день чита́ла ру́сские те́ксты.","ru":"Oh, a lot! I was reading Russian texts all day."},{"who":"B","en":"И как, прочита́ла всё?","ru":"And so — did you read it all?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, не всё. Оди́н текст чита́ла до́лго, но не прочита́ла.","ru":"No, not all. One text I was reading for a long time, but didn't finish."},{"who":"B","en":"Ничего́! А пото́м?","ru":"Never mind! And then? (ничего́ = never mind, it's OK)"},{"who":"A","en":"Пото́м я бы́стро написа́ла письмо́ ма́ме и сде́лала все уро́ки.","ru":"Then I quickly wrote a letter to Mum and did all my homework."},{"who":"B","en":"Молоде́ц! Ты уже́ говори́шь, как настоя́щая студе́нтка.","ru":"Well done! You already talk like a real student. (молоде́ц = well done)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо! Вчера́ был до́лгий день, но хоро́ший.","ru":"Thanks! Yesterday was a long day, but a good one."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for telling yesterday. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"вчера́","ru":"yesterday","ex":"Вчера́ я чита́л весь день."},{"en":"весь день","ru":"all day","ex":"Я рабо́тал весь день."},{"en":"до́лго","ru":"for a long time","ex":"Я до́лго писа́л письмо́."},{"en":"уже́","ru":"already","ex":"Я уже́ прочита́л кни́гу."},{"en":"бы́стро","ru":"quickly, fast","ex":"Она́ бы́стро сде́лала уро́ки."},{"en":"наконе́ц","ru":"finally, at last","ex":"Наконе́ц я прочита́л всю кни́гу!"},{"en":"пото́м","ru":"then, after that","ex":"Пото́м я написа́л отве́т."},{"en":"журна́л","ru":"magazine (not “journal”!)","ex":"Я чита́л журна́л ве́чером."},{"en":"газе́та","ru":"newspaper","ex":"Он чита́л газе́ту у́тром."},{"en":"отве́т","ru":"answer, reply","ex":"Я написа́л дли́нный отве́т."},{"en":"текст","ru":"text","ex":"Э́то тру́дный текст."},{"en":"ничего́","ru":"never mind; nothing","ex":"Не прочита́л? Ничего́!"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each past sentence to its picture — process or done.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я чита́л весь день","I was reading all day (process)"],["Я уже́ прочита́л письмо́","I've already read the letter (done)"],["Она́ ка́ждый день чита́ла","She read every day (habit)"],["Он бы́стро написа́л отве́т","He quickly wrote a reply (done)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on the past two ways. If one bites, the rule above still has it.","items":[{"q":"“I was reading all day.” — Я ___ весь день.","opts":["прочита́л","чита́л","чита́ю"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I've already read the letter (finished it).” — Я уже́ ___ письмо́.","opts":["прочита́л","чита́л","чита́ю"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which word points to the imperfective (duration / habit)?","opts":["наконе́ц","бы́стро","ка́ждый день"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I was reading the book, but didn't finish.” —","opts":["Я чита́ю кни́гу.","Я чита́л, но не прочита́л.","Я прочита́л кни́гу."],"answer":1},{"q":"English “I read the book yesterday” in Russian…","opts":["forces you to choose чита́л or прочита́л","is always прочита́л","has no past"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's account of yesterday — read it aloud. She tells it as a woman (чита́ла, написа́ла).","title":"Что я де́лала вчера́","sentences":["Вчера́ у меня́ был большо́й день.","У́тром я до́лго чита́ла ру́сский текст, но не прочита́ла его́.","Пото́м я бы́стро написа́ла письмо́ ма́ме.","По́сле обе́да я сде́лала все уро́ки.","Ве́чером я ка́ждый час пила́ чай и чита́ла журна́л.","День был до́лгий, но я сде́лала мно́го. Молоде́ц я!"],"translation":"Yesterday I had a big day. In the morning I was reading a Russian text for a long time, but didn't finish it. Then I quickly wrote a letter to Mum. After lunch I did all my homework. In the evening I drank tea every hour and read a magazine. The day was long, but I got a lot done. Well done me!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — tell yesterday, honestly.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about yesterday. Include at least one thing you were doing (imperfective — a process or a habit) and one thing you finished (perfective — a result). If you started something but didn't finish it, say so: <b>чита́л(а), но не прочита́л(а)</b>.","hint":"Duration and habit words (весь день, до́лго, ка́ждый день) call the imperfective; “done” words (уже́, бы́стро, наконе́ц) call the perfective 🙂","example":"Вчера́ я весь день был до́ма. У́тром я до́лго чита́л ру́сский текст, но не прочита́л его́. Пото́м я бы́стро написа́л письмо́ дру́гу и сде́лал все уро́ки. Э́то был до́лгий, но хоро́ший день."}},"17":{"day":17,"week":"03","themeRu":"I will be doing","themeEn":"Бу́ду чита́ть","intro":"You can tell the past two ways — now let's step into the future. Russian's first future is a team of two words: a helper, <b>бу́ду</b>, plus a verb, and it's the one you reach for whenever you'll <em>be doing</em> something. Эмма maps out her whole term over tea.","introAudio":"You can tell the past two ways — now let's step into the future. Russian's first future is a team of two words: a helper, budu, plus a verb, and it's the one you reach for whenever you'll be doing something. Emma maps out her whole term over tea.","goals":["Build the future of “to be”: <b>я бу́ду, ты бу́дешь, он бу́дет</b> …","Make the compound future: <b>бу́ду + imperfective</b> — <b>бу́ду чита́ть</b>, <b>бу́дешь рабо́тать</b>","Talk about plans ahead: <b>Ка́ждый день я бу́ду учи́ть слова́</b>; ask <b>Что ты бу́дешь де́лать?</b>","Lock the trap: <b>бу́ду</b> takes ONLY an imperfective — <b>бу́ду прочита́ть</b> ❌"],"learned":["Built the future of “to be”: <b>я бу́ду, ты бу́дешь, он бу́дет</b> …","Made the compound future: <b>бу́ду + imperfective</b> — <b>бу́ду чита́ть</b>, <b>бу́дешь рабо́тать</b>","Talked about plans ahead, and asked <b>Что ты бу́дешь де́лать?</b>","Locked the trap: <b>бу́ду</b> takes ONLY an imperfective — never <b>бу́ду прочита́ть</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Aspect stays always-on. Two quick past choices — one process, one result — then a case, and we're into the future.","introAudio":"Aspect stays always-on. Two quick past choices — one process, one result — then a case, and we're into the future.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"“All day I was doing homework.” — Весь день я ___ уро́ки.","opts":["сде́лал","де́лал"],"answer":1,"explain":"“All day” = duration → imperfective <b>де́лал</b>. сде́лал is one finished moment."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm calling my brother.” — Я звоню́ ___","opts":["бра́ту","бра́та","бра́том"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>звони́ть</b> takes the dative — the person you call: <b>бра́ту</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I've already read the book.”</b> (finished)","want":"Я уже́ прочита́л кни́гу","answer":"Я уже́ прочита́л кни́гу"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The future of «to be»","title":"<em>Я бу́ду, ты бу́дешь</em> — “will be”","paras":["To reach the future, Russian first needs the future of <b>быть</b> (“to be”) — and it's a normal set of endings you'll recognise: <b>я бу́ду, ты бу́дешь, он/она́ бу́дет, мы бу́дем, вы бу́дете, они́ бу́дут</b>.","On its own it already means “will be”: <b>Я бу́ду до́ма ве́чером</b> (I'll be home in the evening), <b>За́втра бу́дет хо́лодно</b> (tomorrow it'll be cold).","Notice the shift from A1: the present drops “am” — <b>Я до́ма</b>. In speech «За́втра я до́ма» works for a plan, but the clear, neutral future adds the verb: <b>Я бу́ду до́ма</b>. When you want the future to be unmistakable, reach for <b>бу́ду</b>."],"audio":"To reach the future, Russian first needs the future of byt, 'to be' — and it's a normal set of endings you'll recognise: ya budu, ty budesh, on or ona budet, my budem, vy budete, oni budut. On its own it already means 'will be': ya budu doma vecherom, I'll be home in the evening; zavtra budet kholodno, tomorrow it'll be cold. Notice the shift from A1: the present drops 'am', ya doma. In speech zavtra ya doma works for a plan, but the clear, neutral future adds the verb, ya budu doma.","table":{"rows":[["я бу́ду <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я буду\">🔊</button>","I will be"],["ты бу́дешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты будешь\">🔊</button>","you will be"],["он / она́ бу́дет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он будет, она будет\">🔊</button>","he / she will be"],["мы бу́дем · вы бу́дете · они́ бу́дут <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы будем, вы будете, они будут\">🔊</button>","we / you / they will be"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'll be home in the evening.","en":"Я <b>бу́ду</b> до́ма ве́чером.","say":"Я буду дома вечером."},{"ru":"Tomorrow it'll be cold.","en":"За́втра <b>бу́дет</b> хо́лодно.","say":"Завтра будет холодно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"За́втра я бу́дет до́ма.","right":"За́втра я бу́ду до́ма.","why":"The helper agrees with the subject: with <b>я</b> it’s <b>бу́ду</b>, never бу́дет (that’s for он/она́). «За́втра я до́ма» is fine in speech, but «За́втра я бу́ду до́ма» makes the future unmistakable."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“I'll be home tomorrow.” — За́втра я ___ до́ма.","opts":["бу́ду","бу́дет","бу́дешь"],"answer":0,"explain":"я → <b>бу́ду</b>. (бу́дет = he/she, бу́дешь = you.)"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type “you will be” (ты …):","accept":["ты будешь","будешь"],"placeholder":"ты…","explain":"ты → <b>бу́дешь</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The compound future","title":"<em>Бу́ду + infinitive</em> — бу́ду чита́ть","paras":["Now the everyday future: <b>бу́ду + an imperfective infinitive</b>. бу́ду carries the person and the tense; the infinitive carries the meaning. <b>Я бу́ду чита́ть</b> (I will read / be reading), <b>Ты бу́дешь рабо́тать</b> (you'll work), <b>Мы бу́дем говори́ть по-ру́сски</b> (we'll speak Russian).","Only the helper changes for the person — бу́ду, бу́дешь, бу́дет, бу́дем … — while the infinitive stays exactly the same: <b>… чита́ть</b>.","And it's always an <em>infinitive</em> after бу́ду, never a second conjugated verb. Two conjugated verbs in a row is the giveaway of an English brain at work."],"audio":"Now the everyday future: budu plus an imperfective infinitive. Budu carries the person and the tense; the infinitive carries the meaning. Ya budu chitat, I will read or be reading; ty budesh rabotat, you'll work; my budem govorit po-russki, we'll speak Russian. Only the helper changes for the person — budu, budesh, budet, budem — while the infinitive stays exactly the same: chitat. And it's always an infinitive after budu, never a second conjugated verb. Two conjugated verbs in a row is the giveaway of an English brain at work.","table":{"rows":[["Я бу́ду чита́ть. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я буду читать\">🔊</button>","I will read / be reading"],["Ты бу́дешь рабо́тать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты будешь работать\">🔊</button>","you will work"],["Он бу́дет учи́ть слова́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он будет учить слова\">🔊</button>","he will learn words"],["Мы бу́дем говори́ть по-ру́сски. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мы будем говорить по-русски\">🔊</button>","we will speak Russian"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Tomorrow I'll be working.","en":"За́втра я <b>бу́ду рабо́тать</b>.","say":"Завтра я буду работать."},{"ru":"In the evening we'll watch a film.","en":"Ве́чером мы <b>бу́дем смотре́ть</b> фильм.","say":"Вечером мы будем смотреть фильм."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я бу́ду рабо́таю.","right":"Я бу́ду рабо́тать.","why":"After <b>бу́ду</b> comes the plain infinitive, never a conjugated form. бу́ду already shows “I / future”; рабо́тать just names the action."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“You will work.” — Ты ___ рабо́тать.","opts":["бу́дет","бу́дешь","бу́ду"],"answer":1,"explain":"ты → <b>бу́дешь</b> + the infinitive рабо́тать."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I will be reading.”</b>","want":"Я бу́ду чита́ть","answer":"Я бу́ду чита́ть"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “we will speak Russian” (мы + говори́ть):","accept":["мы будем говорить по-русски","мы будем говорить по русски"],"placeholder":"Мы бу́дем…","explain":"<b>бу́дем</b> + the infinitive говори́ть: мы бу́дем говори́ть по-ру́сски."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Plans, habits, stretches of time","title":"<em>Что ты бу́дешь де́лать?</em>","paras":["The compound future is for actions seen as a <b>process</b>, a <b>habit</b>, or a <b>plan</b> — the imperfective's world, now aimed forward. Duration: <b>Я бу́ду рабо́тать весь день</b>. Repetition: <b>Ка́ждый день я бу́ду учи́ть слова́</b>. A plan: <b>Ле́том я бу́ду отдыха́ть</b>.","And here's the question you'll hear all the time: <b>Что ты бу́дешь де́лать?</b> — “What will you do / be doing?” — бу́дешь + де́лать.","Keep the helper agreeing with the subject: <b>я бу́ду, ты бу́дешь, мы бу́дем</b>. The infinitive never moves."],"audio":"The compound future is for actions seen as a process, a habit, or a plan — the imperfective's world, now aimed forward. Duration: ya budu rabotat ves den, I'll work all day. Repetition: kazhdy den ya budu uchit slova, every day I'll learn words. A plan: letom ya budu otdykhat, in summer I'll rest. And here's the question you'll hear all the time: chto ty budesh delat — what will you be doing — budesh plus delat. Keep the helper agreeing with the subject: ya budu, ty budesh, my budem. The infinitive never moves.","table":{"rows":[["За́втра я бу́ду рабо́тать весь день. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Завтра я буду работать весь день\">🔊</button>","tomorrow — a stretch of time (duration)"],["Ка́ждый день я бу́ду учи́ть слова́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Каждый день я буду учить слова\">🔊</button>","every day — a habit (repetition)"],["Ле́том я бу́ду отдыха́ть. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Летом я буду отдыхать\">🔊</button>","in summer — a plan"],["Что ты бу́дешь де́лать? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Что ты будешь делать?\">🔊</button>","What will you be doing? (the question)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"What will you do on Saturday?","en":"Что ты <b>бу́дешь де́лать</b> в суббо́ту?","say":"Что ты будешь делать в субботу?"},{"ru":"I'll study Russian every day.","en":"Я <b>бу́ду учи́ть</b> ру́сский ка́ждый день.","say":"Я буду учить русский каждый день."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ка́ждый день я бу́дет учи́ть слова́.","right":"Ка́ждый день я бу́ду учи́ть слова́.","why":"The helper agrees with the subject: я → <b>бу́ду</b>, not бу́дет. The infinitive (учи́ть) never changes."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“What will you be doing tomorrow?” — Что ты ___ де́лать за́втра?","opts":["бу́дет","бу́дешь","бу́ду"],"answer":1,"explain":"ты → <b>бу́дешь</b> + де́лать. That's the everyday “what are your plans?” question."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Every day I will study Russian.”</b>","want":"Ка́ждый день я бу́ду учи́ть ру́сский","answer":"Ка́ждый день я бу́ду учи́ть ру́сский"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я бу́ду учи́ть ру́сский ка́ждый день.","sub":"promise yourself you'll study Russian every day","want":"я буду учить русский каждый день"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Only the imperfective rides with бу́ду","title":"<em>Бу́ду чита́ть</em> ✅ · <em>бу́ду прочита́ть</em> ❌","paras":["One iron rule locks this future: <b>бу́ду takes ONLY an imperfective infinitive</b>. <b>Я бу́ду чита́ть</b> ✅ — but <b>Я бу́ду прочита́ть</b> ❌ is simply not Russian. The perfective refuses to ride with бу́ду.","Why? бу́ду means “I'll be in the middle of / keep doing” — a process — while the perfective is all about finishing. They pull opposite ways, so they can't share a phrase.","There <em>is</em> a separate, one-word future for “I'll get it done” — that's your very next lesson. For now, every бу́ду pairs with an imperfective, and you never conjugate <b>быть</b> as if it carried the action itself."],"audio":"One iron rule locks this future: budu takes only an imperfective infinitive. Ya budu chitat is correct; ya budu prochitat is simply not Russian. The perfective refuses to ride with budu. Why? Budu means 'I'll be in the middle of, keep doing' — a process — while the perfective is all about finishing. They pull opposite ways, so they can't share a phrase. There is a separate, one-word future for 'I'll get it done', and that's your very next lesson. For now, every budu pairs with an imperfective, and you never conjugate byt as if it carried the action itself.","table":{"rows":[["Я бу́ду чита́ть. ✅ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я буду читать\">🔊</button>","бу́ду + imperfective — correct"],["Я бу́ду прочита́ть. ✗","бу́ду + perfective — impossible"],["Ты бу́дешь рабо́тать. ✅ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты будешь работать\">🔊</button>","бу́ду + imperfective — correct"],["Ты бу́дешь сде́лать. ✗","бу́ду + perfective — impossible"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'll be watching this film in the evening.","en":"Ве́чером я <b>бу́ду смотре́ть</b> э́тот фильм.","say":"Вечером я буду смотреть этот фильм."},{"ru":"We'll be cooking dinner together.","en":"Мы <b>бу́дем гото́вить</b> у́жин вме́сте.","say":"Мы будем готовить ужин вместе."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я бу́ду прочита́ть кни́гу.","right":"Я бу́ду чита́ть кни́гу.","why":"<b>бу́ду</b> pairs only with the imperfective (чита́ть). The perfective прочита́ть can't ride with бу́ду — it builds its future a different way (next lesson)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which one is possible Russian?","opts":["Я бу́ду прочита́ть.","Я бу́ду чита́ть.","Я бу́ду сде́лать."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>бу́ду</b> + imperfective only → бу́ду чита́ть. The perfectives (прочита́ть, сде́лать) can't follow бу́ду."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I will write letters (every week).” — бу́ду + which infinitive?","opts":["писа́ть","написа́ть"],"answer":0,"explain":"A repeated process → imperfective <b>писа́ть</b>: бу́ду писа́ть. написа́ть is perfective and can't follow бу́ду."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I will be working” (я + бу́ду + рабо́тать):","accept":["я буду работать"],"placeholder":"Я бу́ду…","explain":"<b>бу́ду</b> + the imperfective infinitive рабо́тать."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Over tea, Ivan asks Эмма about the term ahead. She lays out her plans — all of them things she'll <em>be doing</em>, week after week. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, что ты бу́дешь де́лать в э́том семе́стре?","ru":"Emma, what will you be doing this semester? (семе́стр = semester, term)"},{"who":"A","en":"Мно́го! Ка́ждый день я бу́ду учи́ть но́вые слова́.","ru":"A lot! Every day I'll learn new words."},{"who":"B","en":"Здо́рово! А по вечера́м?","ru":"Great! And in the evenings?"},{"who":"A","en":"По вечера́м я бу́ду чита́ть ру́сские кни́ги и смотре́ть фи́льмы.","ru":"In the evenings I'll read Russian books and watch films."},{"who":"B","en":"А в выходны́е?","ru":"And on the weekend?"},{"who":"A","en":"В выходны́е мы с тобо́й бу́дем гуля́ть по Москве́!","ru":"On the weekend you and I will stroll around Moscow! (гуля́ть = to stroll)"},{"who":"B","en":"Отли́чный план! Я то́же бу́ду помога́ть тебе́ с ру́сским.","ru":"Great plan! I'll also help you with your Russian."},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо, Ива́н. Э́тот семе́стр бу́дет прекра́сный!","ru":"Thank you, Ivan. This semester will be wonderful!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for making plans. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"бу́ду / бу́дешь","ru":"I will / you will (be)","ex":"Я бу́ду до́ма ве́чером."},{"en":"за́втра","ru":"tomorrow","ex":"За́втра я бу́ду рабо́тать."},{"en":"план","ru":"plan","ex":"Э́то мой план на неде́лю."},{"en":"учи́ть","ru":"to learn, to study","ex":"Я бу́ду учи́ть слова́ ка́ждый день."},{"en":"слова́","ru":"words","ex":"Я учу́ но́вые слова́."},{"en":"гуля́ть","ru":"to stroll, to walk (for fun)","ex":"Мы бу́дем гуля́ть по Москве́."},{"en":"смотре́ть","ru":"to watch, to look","ex":"Ве́чером я бу́ду смотре́ть фильм."},{"en":"фильм","ru":"film, movie","ex":"Э́то о́чень интере́сный фильм."},{"en":"выходны́е","ru":"weekend, days off","ex":"В выходны́е я бу́ду отдыха́ть."},{"en":"семе́стр","ru":"semester, term","ex":"Э́тот семе́стр бу́дет тру́дный."},{"en":"неде́ля","ru":"week","ex":"На э́той неде́ле мно́го рабо́ты."},{"en":"отдыха́ть","ru":"to rest, to relax","ex":"Ле́том я бу́ду отдыха́ть."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each future phrase to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я бу́ду чита́ть","I will read / be reading"],["Ты бу́дешь рабо́тать","you will work"],["Что ты бу́дешь де́лать?","What will you be doing?"],["Мы бу́дем гуля́ть","we will go for walks"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on the бу́ду future. If one bites, the rule above still has it.","items":[{"q":"“I'll be home tomorrow.” — За́втра я ___ до́ма.","opts":["бу́дет","бу́дешь","бу́ду"],"answer":2},{"q":"“You will work.” — Ты ___ рабо́тать.","opts":["бу́дешь","бу́ду","бу́дет"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which infinitive can follow бу́ду?","opts":["прочита́ть","чита́ть","сде́лать"],"answer":1},{"q":"“What will you be doing?” —","opts":["Что ты де́лал?","Что ты бу́дешь де́лать?","Что ты бу́дешь сде́лать?"],"answer":1},{"q":"<b>Бу́ду</b> takes…","opts":["only an imperfective infinitive","only a perfective infinitive","a conjugated verb"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's plan for the term — read it aloud. Every verb after бу́ду is imperfective, just as it must be.","title":"Мои́ пла́ны на семе́стр","sentences":["Э́то бу́дет о́чень ва́жный семе́стр.","Ка́ждый день я бу́ду учи́ть но́вые слова́ и чита́ть те́ксты.","По вечера́м я бу́ду смотре́ть ру́сские фи́льмы.","В выходны́е мы с Ива́ном бу́дем гуля́ть по го́роду.","Я зна́ю: бу́дет тру́дно, но я бу́ду мно́го рабо́тать.","Я ду́маю, ру́сский язы́к ско́ро бу́дет мои́м дру́гом."],"translation":"This will be a very important semester. Every day I'll learn new words and read texts. In the evenings I'll watch Russian films. On weekends Ivan and I will stroll around the city. I know it will be hard, but I'll work a lot. I think Russian will soon be my friend."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — plan the week ahead.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about your plans, all with <b>бу́ду + an imperfective verb</b>. Say what you'll do every day (a habit), what you'll be doing at the weekend (a stretch of time), and one thing you'll be that you aren't yet (бу́ду + a noun).","hint":"Only the helper changes for the person — <b>я бу́ду, ты бу́дешь, мы бу́дем</b> — and it always takes the imperfective infinitive. Never бу́ду + a perfective 🙂","example":"За́втра я бу́ду рабо́тать весь день. Ка́ждый ве́чер я бу́ду чита́ть ру́сские кни́ги и учи́ть слова́. В выходны́е я бу́ду отдыха́ть и гуля́ть. Ско́ро я бу́ду говори́ть по-ру́сски хорошо́!"}},"18":{"day":18,"week":"03","themeRu":"I'll get it done","themeEn":"Прочита́ю, напишу́","intro":"Yesterday Emma promised her manager the report by Friday — <b>я напишу́</b>. One little verb, and the whole week is planned. Today you meet Russian's second future: take a <b>perfective</b> verb, give it present-tense endings, and it points straight at a finished result still to come. This is how you promise to get things done.","introAudio":"Yesterday Emma promised her manager the report by Friday — ya napishu. One little verb, and the whole week is planned. Today you meet Russian's second future: take a perfective verb, give it present-tense endings, and it points straight at a finished result still to come. This is how you promise to get things done.","goals":["Turn a perfective verb into the future: <b>напишу́</b> = “I will write (and finish)”","Conjugate it with endings you already own: <b>прочита́ю, сде́лаю, скажу́</b>","Choose between two futures — <b>бу́ду писа́ть</b> (process) vs <b>напишу́</b> (result)","Make real promises: <b>Я тебе́ позвоню́</b>, <b>Я всё сде́лаю</b>"],"learned":["Turned a perfective verb into the future: <b>напишу́</b> = “I will write (and finish)”","Conjugated it with familiar endings: <b>прочита́ю, сде́лаю, скажу́</b>","Chose between two futures — <b>бу́ду писа́ть</b> vs <b>напишу́</b>","Made real promises: <b>Я тебе́ позвоню́</b>, <b>Я всё сде́лаю</b>"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on the week so far — the aspect pair, the past, and the <b>бу́ду</b>-future. Today's new future grows straight out of them.","introAudio":"One minute on the week so far — the aspect pair, the past, and the budu-future. Today's new future grows straight out of them.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is all this week's Russian — a quick warm-up.","q":"“I was reading all evening” (a woman, a process) —","opts":["Я весь ве́чер чита́ла.","Я весь ве́чер прочита́ла.","Я весь ве́чер бу́ду чита́ть."],"answer":0,"explain":"“All evening” = an ongoing process → imperfective past <b>чита́ла</b>. прочита́ла would mean she finished it; бу́ду чита́ть is the future."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Tomorrow I'll be working” —","opts":["За́втра я бу́дет рабо́тать.","За́втра я бу́ду рабо́тать.","За́втра я рабо́тал."],"answer":1,"explain":"A future process → <b>бу́ду</b> + the imperfective infinitive: бу́ду рабо́тать. бу́дет is the wrong helper for я; рабо́тал is the past."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Yesterday I read the book (finished it).”</b>","want":"Вчера́ я прочита́л кни́гу","answer":"Вчера́ я прочита́л кни́гу"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · A perfective verb has no present","title":"<em>Напишу́</em> — one form, and it means the future","paras":["Here's the surprise at the heart of aspect: a <b>perfective</b> verb has <b>no present tense</b>. You can't be “in the middle of” a whole, finished action. So when you give a perfective verb the same personal endings you'd use for the present — <b>напишу́, напи́шешь, напи́шет</b> — it doesn't mean now. It means the <b>future</b>: “I will write (and finish).”","Compare the pair you know. Imperfective <b>пишу́</b> = “I am writing” (right now, in progress). Perfective <b>напишу́</b> = “I will write and get it done.” Same endings, opposite time — the aspect decides.","So one perfective word carries a whole English future-with-a-result: <b>прочита́ю</b> = “I'll read it (through)”, <b>сде́лаю</b> = “I'll do it (and finish)”, <b>скажу́</b> = “I'll tell (you).”"],"audio":"Here's the surprise at the heart of aspect: a perfective verb has no present tense. You can't be in the middle of a whole, finished action. So when you give a perfective verb the same personal endings you'd use for the present — napishu, napishesh, napishet — it doesn't mean now. It means the future: I will write and finish. Compare the pair you know. Imperfective pishu means I am writing, right now. Perfective napishu means I will write and get it done. Same endings, opposite time — the aspect decides. So one perfective word carries a whole English future with a result: prochitayu, I'll read it through; sdelayu, I'll do it and finish; skazhu, I'll tell you.","table":{"rows":[["пишу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пишу\">🔊</button>","I am writing (now) — imperfective"],["напишу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"напишу\">🔊</button>","I will write & finish — perfective"],["чита́ю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читаю\">🔊</button>","I am reading (now) — imperfective"],["прочита́ю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прочитаю\">🔊</button>","I will read it (through) — perfective"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I'll write you a letter today.","en":"Сего́дня я <b>напишу́</b> тебе́ письмо́.","say":"Сегодня я напишу тебе письмо."},{"ru":"I'll read this book by Friday.","en":"Я <b>прочита́ю</b> э́ту кни́гу к пя́тнице.","say":"Я прочитаю эту книгу к пятнице.","gloss":"к пя́тнице = by Friday (к + dative, day 9)"},{"ru":"I'll tell you everything in the evening.","en":"Ве́чером я всё <b>скажу́</b>.","say":"Вечером я всё скажу."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я напишу́» = “I am writing now.”","right":"«Я напишу́» = “I will write (and finish).”","why":"A perfective verb has no present. Its present-looking form always points to the <b>future</b>. For “I'm writing now”, use imperfective <b>пишу́</b>."},{"wrong":"Что ты де́лаешь? — Я напишу́ письмо́.","right":"Что ты де́лаешь? — Я пишу́ письмо́.","why":"“What are you doing?” asks about now → present <b>пишу́</b>. напишу́ answers “what <em>will</em> you do?”, not “what are you doing?”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"«Я напишу́ письмо́» means —","opts":["I am writing a letter now.","I will write a letter (and finish it).","I wrote a letter."],"answer":1,"explain":"напишу́ is a perfective verb in its present-looking form — that always means the <b>future</b> with a result. “Writing now” would be пишу́."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'll read this book (through).”</b>","want":"Я прочита́ю э́ту кни́гу","answer":"Я прочита́ю э́ту кни́гу"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"“I am writing a letter now.” Type it — this one really is the present:","accept":["я пишу письмо сейчас","я сейчас пишу письмо","я пишу письмо"],"placeholder":"Я …","explain":"“Now, in progress” → imperfective present <b>пишу́</b>, never напишу́."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Same endings you already know","title":"<em>Прочита́ю, напишу́, скажу́</em> — nothing new to conjugate","paras":["Good news: you don't learn new endings. A perfective verb takes the <b>very same personal endings</b> as the present — the -ю/-ешь and -у/-ишь sets from A1. прочита́ть follows чита́ть: <b>прочита́ю, прочита́ешь, прочита́ет</b>. сде́лать follows де́лать: <b>сде́лаю, сде́лаешь</b>.","A few pair up with a small sound change you've already met: написа́ть → <b>напишу́, напи́шешь</b> (с→ш), сказа́ть → <b>скажу́, ска́жешь</b> (з→ж). Watch the stress hop back from напишу́ to напи́шешь.","Second-conjugation ones keep their -ишь: позвони́ть → <b>позвоню́, позвони́шь</b>; купи́ть → <b>куплю́, ку́пишь</b> (the same п→пл you saw in the present)."],"audio":"Good news: you don't learn new endings. A perfective verb takes the very same personal endings as the present — the -yu, -esh, and -u, -ish sets from A1. Prochitat follows chitat: prochitayu, prochitayesh, prochitayet. Sdelat follows delat: sdelayu, sdelayesh. A few pair up with a small sound change you've already met: napisat gives napishu, napishesh; skazat gives skazhu, skazhesh. Watch the stress hop back from napishu to napishesh. Second-conjugation ones keep their -ish: pozvonit gives pozvonyu, pozvonish; kupit gives kuplyu, kupish.","table":{"rows":[["прочита́ю, прочита́ешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прочитаю, прочитаешь\">🔊</button>","I'll read / you'll read (through)"],["напишу́, напи́шешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"напишу, напишешь\">🔊</button>","I'll write / you'll write (& finish)"],["скажу́, ска́жешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"скажу, скажешь\">🔊</button>","I'll tell / you'll tell"],["позвоню́, позвони́шь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"позвоню, позвонишь\">🔊</button>","I'll call / you'll call"],["куплю́, ку́пишь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"куплю, купишь\">🔊</button>","I'll buy / you'll buy"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I'll call you tomorrow.","en":"Я тебе́ за́втра <b>позвоню́</b>.","say":"Я тебе завтра позвоню."},{"ru":"We'll do everything ourselves.","en":"Мы всё <b>сде́лаем</b> са́ми.","say":"Мы всё сделаем сами.","gloss":"са́ми = (we) ourselves"},{"ru":"Will you buy bread?","en":"Ты <b>ку́пишь</b> хлеб?","say":"Ты купишь хлеб?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я бу́ду написа́ть письмо́.","right":"Я напишу́ письмо́.","why":"You never put a perfective inside <b>бу́ду</b> (day 17). The perfective already carries the future by itself: напишу́."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“You'll call” — ты ___","opts":["позвони́шь","позвоню́","звони́шь"],"answer":0,"explain":"ты takes the -ишь ending: <b>позвони́шь</b>. позвоню́ is “I”; звони́шь is the present “you are calling (now)”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Finish: «За́втра мы ___ всё.» (we'll get everything done)","opts":["де́лаем","сде́лаем","бу́дем сде́лать"],"answer":1,"explain":"A finished future result → perfective <b>сде́лаем</b>. де́лаем is the present; бу́дем сде́лать is impossible (no perfective after бу́ду)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'll write you a letter.”</b>","want":"Я напишу́ тебе́ письмо́","answer":"Я напишу́ тебе́ письмо́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Two futures, one choice","title":"<em>Бу́ду чита́ть</em> vs <em>прочита́ю</em> — process or result","paras":["Russian gives you two futures, and they split exactly the way aspect always does. <b>бу́ду + imperfective</b> paints a <b>process</b> or a repeated/ongoing plan: <b>Ве́чером я бу́ду чита́ть</b> — “I'll be reading this evening.” The <b>perfective future</b> names a single <b>finished result</b>: <b>Я прочита́ю статью́</b> — “I'll read this article (through).”","Ask the same question as always: am I picturing the <b>activity</b> (бу́ду чита́ть) or the <b>done thing</b> (прочита́ю)? Time markers help — <b>весь ве́чер, ка́ждый день</b> lean imperfective; <b>за́втра, к пя́тнице, бы́стро</b> lean perfective.","One warning carries over from yesterday: бу́ду only ever takes an <b>imperfective</b> infinitive. Never <b>бу́ду написа́ть</b> — the perfective stands on its own."],"audio":"Russian gives you two futures, and they split the way aspect always does. Budu plus imperfective paints a process or a repeated plan: vecherom ya budu chitat — I'll be reading this evening. The perfective future names a single finished result: ya prochitayu statyu — I'll read this article through. Ask the same question as always: am I picturing the activity, budu chitat, or the done thing, prochitayu? Time markers help — ves vecher, kazhdy den lean imperfective; zavtra, k pyatnitse, bystro lean perfective. One warning carries over from yesterday: budu only ever takes an imperfective infinitive. Never budu napisat — the perfective stands on its own.","table":{"rows":[["Ве́чером я бу́ду чита́ть. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Вечером я буду читать.\">🔊</button>","I'll be reading this evening (process)"],["Я прочита́ю статью́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я прочитаю статью.\">🔊</button>","I'll read the article (through) (result)"],["Я бу́ду писа́ть весь день. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я буду писать весь день.\">🔊</button>","I'll be writing all day (process)"],["Я напишу́ отчёт к пя́тнице. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я напишу отчёт к пятнице.\">🔊</button>","I'll finish the report by Friday (result)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"What will you be doing tonight? — I'll be reading.","en":"Что ты бу́дешь де́лать ве́чером? — Бу́ду <b>чита́ть</b>.","say":"Что ты будешь делать вечером? Буду читать."},{"ru":"I'll read the article (through) and call you.","en":"Я <b>прочита́ю</b> статью́ и позвоню́ тебе́.","say":"Я прочитаю статью и позвоню тебе."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"За́втра я бу́ду прочита́ть кни́гу.","right":"За́втра я прочита́ю кни́гу.","why":"бу́ду never takes a perfective. For the finished result, the perfective future stands alone: <b>прочита́ю</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'll be working all day” (a process) —","opts":["Весь день я бу́ду рабо́тать.","Весь день я рабо́таю.","Весь день я сде́лаю."],"answer":0,"explain":"“All day” = an ongoing process → <b>бу́ду рабо́тать</b>. рабо́таю is the present “now”; сде́лаю names a finished result, not an all-day activity."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “Tomorrow I'll read this book (through)”:","accept":["завтра я прочитаю эту книгу","я прочитаю эту книгу завтра","я завтра прочитаю эту книгу"],"placeholder":"За́втра я …","explain":"A finished result in the future → perfective <b>прочита́ю</b>, with no бу́ду."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я бу́ду чита́ть весь ве́чер.","sub":"say you'll be reading all evening (a process)","want":"я буду читать весь вечер"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Making promises","title":"<em>Я всё сде́лаю</em> — the future of getting things done","paras":["This future is the language of <b>promises and plans</b>. When you commit to a result — I'll do it, I'll call, I'll buy it, I'll tell you — Russian reaches for the perfective future. It sounds firm and finished, exactly what a promise needs.","You'll hear it everywhere in daily life: <b>Я тебе́ позвоню́</b> (I'll call you), <b>Я всё сде́лаю</b> (I'll do it all), <b>Я куплю́ хлеб</b> (I'll get the bread), <b>Я скажу́ Ива́ну</b> (I'll tell Ivan).","Notice how the other cases keep working inside these promises: <b>тебе́, Ива́ну</b> are dative (I'll call / tell <em>to</em> you / Ivan), <b>хлеб</b> is accusative. New future, same case machinery underneath."],"audio":"This future is the language of promises and plans. When you commit to a result — I'll do it, I'll call, I'll buy it, I'll tell you — Russian reaches for the perfective future. It sounds firm and finished, exactly what a promise needs. You'll hear it everywhere: ya tebe pozvonyu, I'll call you; ya vsyo sdelayu, I'll do it all; ya kuplyu khleb, I'll get the bread; ya skazhu Ivanu, I'll tell Ivan. Notice how the other cases keep working: tebe, Ivanu are dative; khleb is accusative. New future, same case machinery underneath.","examples":[{"ru":"I promise: I'll do everything.","en":"Я обеща́ю: я всё <b>сде́лаю</b>.","say":"Я обещаю: я всё сделаю."},{"ru":"I'll call you in the evening, okay?","en":"Я <b>позвоню́</b> тебе́ ве́чером, хорошо́?","say":"Я позвоню тебе вечером, хорошо?"},{"ru":"I'll tell Ivan everything myself.","en":"Я сам всё <b>скажу́</b> Ива́ну.","say":"Я сам всё скажу Ивану.","gloss":"сам = myself (a man); a woman says сама́"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я тебе́ звоню́ за́втра.","right":"Я тебе́ за́втра позвоню́.","why":"A promise to call = a finished future action → perfective <b>позвоню́</b>. звоню́ is “I'm calling (right now)”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A firm promise: “I'll buy the bread.” —","opts":["Я покупа́ю хлеб.","Я куплю́ хлеб.","Я бу́ду купи́ть хлеб."],"answer":1,"explain":"A promised result → perfective future <b>куплю́</b>. покупа́ю is the present habit; бу́ду купи́ть is impossible (no perfective after бу́ду)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'll call you in the evening.”</b>","want":"Я позвоню́ тебе́ ве́чером","answer":"Я позвоню́ тебе́ ве́чером"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"It's Sunday night at the café. Emma has a big week ahead, and Lena teases her about her long list. Listen to everything Emma promises to get done.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, кака́я больша́я неде́ля! Ты всё успе́ешь?","ru":"Emma, what a big week! Will you manage everything? (успе́ешь = will manage)"},{"who":"A","en":"Успе́ю! В понеде́льник я напишу́ отчёт.","ru":"I'll manage! On Monday I'll write (finish) the report."},{"who":"B","en":"А кни́га для университе́та?","ru":"And the book for university?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я прочита́ю её во вто́рник. Всю, до конца́.","ru":"I'll read it (through) on Tuesday. All of it, to the end."},{"who":"B","en":"И ты обеща́ла позвони́ть ма́ме.","ru":"And you promised to call your mum."},{"who":"A","en":"Да, я позвоню́ ей сего́дня ве́чером и куплю́ ей пода́рок.","ru":"Yes, I'll call her tonight and buy her a present."},{"who":"B","en":"Ну ты и геро́й! А отдыха́ть когда́?","ru":"Well, you're a hero! And when will you rest? (отдыха́ть = to rest)"},{"who":"A","en":"В суббо́ту. В суббо́ту я не сде́лаю ничего́!","ru":"On Saturday. On Saturday I'll do nothing!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for planning and promising. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"напишу́","ru":"I will write (написа́ть)","ex":"Я напишу́ письмо́."},{"en":"прочита́ю","ru":"I will read (through) (прочита́ть)","ex":"Я прочита́ю кни́гу."},{"en":"сде́лаю","ru":"I will do, get done (сде́лать)","ex":"Я всё сде́лаю."},{"en":"скажу́","ru":"I will say, tell (сказа́ть)","ex":"Я скажу́ пра́вду."},{"en":"позвоню́","ru":"I will call (позвони́ть)","ex":"Я тебе́ позвоню́."},{"en":"куплю́","ru":"I will buy (купи́ть)","ex":"Я куплю́ хлеб."},{"en":"обеща́ть","ru":"to promise","ex":"Я обеща́ю тебе́."},{"en":"отчёт","ru":"report","ex":"Я пишу́ отчёт."},{"en":"пра́вда","ru":"truth; (it's) true","ex":"Э́то пра́вда?"},{"en":"успе́ть","ru":"to manage, have time","ex":"Я успе́ю всё."},{"en":"до конца́","ru":"to the end","ex":"Я прочита́ю до конца́."},{"en":"ве́чером","ru":"in the evening","ex":"Ве́чером я позвоню́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the promise to its meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я напишу́ отчёт.","I'll write the report."],["Я тебе́ позвоню́.","I'll call you."],["Я всё сде́лаю.","I'll do everything."],["Я прочита́ю кни́гу.","I'll read the book (through)."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the two futures. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Я напишу́ письмо́» means —","opts":["I am writing a letter now.","I will write a letter (and finish).","I used to write letters."],"answer":1},{"q":"“You'll buy bread” — ты ___","opts":["ку́пишь","куплю́","покупа́ешь"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which is the process future (“I'll be reading all evening”)?","opts":["Я прочита́ю весь ве́чер.","Я бу́ду чита́ть весь ве́чер.","Я чита́ю весь ве́чер."],"answer":1},{"q":"“I'll do everything” (a promised result) —","opts":["Я де́лаю всё.","Я бу́ду сде́лать всё.","Я всё сде́лаю."],"answer":2},{"q":"Which sentence is impossible in Russian?","opts":["Я напишу́ письмо́.","Я бу́ду писа́ть письмо́.","Я бу́ду написа́ть письмо́."],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's plan for the week, pinned to the fridge. Every verb is a promise — a perfective future. Read it aloud.","title":"Моя́ неде́ля","sentences":["В понеде́льник я напишу́ отчёт.","Во вто́рник я прочита́ю кни́гу до конца́.","В сре́ду я куплю́ пода́рок для ма́мы.","Ве́чером я позвоню́ ей и всё скажу́.","А в суббо́ту я не сде́лаю ничего́ — бу́ду отдыха́ть!"],"translation":"On Monday I'll write (finish) the report. On Tuesday I'll read the book to the end. On Wednesday I'll buy a present for Mum. In the evening I'll call her and tell her everything. And on Saturday I'll do nothing — I'll be resting!"},"essay":{"intro":"Now make your own promises.","prompt":"Name three things you'll get done tomorrow, using the perfective future (a finished result each time). Then name one thing you'll simply be doing — process, with бу́ду + imperfective.","hint":"Perfective future = one finished result: напишу́, прочита́ю, куплю́, позвоню́, сде́лаю. For the process, reach for бу́ду + imperfective 🙂","example":"За́втра я напишу́ два письма́, куплю́ проду́кты и позвоню́ дру́гу. А ве́чером я бу́ду чита́ть но́вую кни́гу."}},"19":{"day":19,"week":"03","themeRu":"Set off, arrive, leave","themeEn":"Пойти́, прийти́, уйти́","intro":"Emma spends her days crossing Moscow — setting off, arriving, leaving again. In A1 you had <b>идти́</b> for the walking itself. Today you snap little prefixes onto it, and each one adds a direction: <b>по-</b> (set off), <b>при-</b> (arrive), <b>у-</b> (leave). Three prefixes, and the whole coming-and-going of a day is yours.","introAudio":"Emma spends her days crossing Moscow — setting off, arriving, leaving again. In A1 you had idti for the walking itself. Today you snap little prefixes onto it, and each one adds a direction: po- for set off, pri- for arrive, u- for leave. Three prefixes, and the whole coming-and-going of a day is yours.","goals":["Snap a prefix onto <b>идти́</b> to add direction — and make it perfective","Set off with <b>по-</b>: <b>Я пойду́ в кино́</b>, <b>Пошли́!</b>","Arrive with <b>при-</b>: <b>Он пришёл домо́й</b>","Leave with <b>у-</b>: <b>Она́ уже́ ушла́</b>"],"learned":["Snapped a prefix onto <b>идти́</b> to add direction and make it perfective","Set off with <b>по-</b>: <b>Я пойду́ в кино́</b>, <b>Пошли́!</b>","Arrived with <b>при-</b>: <b>Он пришёл домо́й</b>","Left with <b>у-</b>: <b>Она́ уже́ ушла́</b>"],"review":{"intro":"A quick look back before we add prefixes: plain motion from A1, and this week's aspect. In a moment пойти́ and прийти́ will grow right out of идти́.","introAudio":"A quick look back before we add prefixes: plain motion from A1, and this week's aspect. In a moment poyti and priyti will grow right out of idti.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All A1 and this week — a short warm-up.","q":"“Where are you going?” (asking the direction) —","opts":["Куда́ ты идёшь?","Где ты идёшь?","Отку́да ты идёшь?"],"answer":0,"explain":"Movement toward a goal → <b>куда́</b> + идёшь. где asks a static “where”; отку́да asks “from where”."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'll be reading all evening” (a process) —","opts":["Я прочита́ю весь ве́чер.","Я бу́ду чита́ть весь ве́чер.","Я чита́ю весь ве́чер."],"answer":1,"explain":"A future process → <b>бу́ду</b> + imperfective. прочита́ю names a finished result; чита́ю is the present."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm going to the cinema.”</b> (right now, on foot)","want":"Я иду́ в кино́","answer":"Я иду́ в кино́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · A prefix adds a direction","title":"<em>Идти́</em> → <em>пойти́, прийти́, уйти́</em>","paras":["In A1, <b>идти́</b> meant simply “to be walking”. Now watch what a tiny prefix does: it clips onto the front and pins a <b>direction</b> onto the movement. <b>по</b>йти́ = set off, <b>при</b>йти́ = arrive/come, <b>у</b>йти́ = leave/go away.","Two things happen at once. First, the prefix adds meaning (which way?). Second, it makes the verb <b>perfective</b> — a single, complete move. So these behave exactly like this week's perfectives: they live in the <b>past</b> (пошёл, пришёл, ушёл) or the <b>future</b> (пойду́, приду́, уйду́), never the present.","Where English uses “go” plus a little word — go <em>off</em>, <em>come</em>, go <em>away</em> — Russian melts the whole idea into the prefix. One word carries what English spreads over two."],"audio":"In A1, idti meant simply to be walking. Now watch what a tiny prefix does: it clips onto the front and pins a direction onto the movement. Po-yti, set off; pri-yti, arrive or come; u-yti, leave or go away. Two things happen at once. First, the prefix adds meaning — which way. Second, it makes the verb perfective, a single complete move. So these behave like this week's perfectives: they live in the past — poshyol, prishyol, ushyol — or the future — poydu, pridu, uydu — never the present. Where English uses go plus a little word — go off, come, go away — Russian melts the whole idea into the prefix. One word carries what English spreads over two.","table":{"rows":[["идти́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"идти\">🔊</button>","to be walking (A1, no direction)"],["пойти́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пойти\">🔊</button>","to set off, head somewhere (по-)"],["прийти́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прийти\">🔊</button>","to arrive, to come (при-)"],["уйти́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уйти\">🔊</button>","to leave, to go away (у-)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'll set off to the cinema tonight.","en":"Ве́чером я <b>пойду́</b> в кино́.","say":"Вечером я пойду в кино."},{"ru":"Ivan came / arrived home early.","en":"Ива́н <b>пришёл</b> домо́й ра́но.","say":"Иван пришёл домой рано."},{"ru":"Lena has already left.","en":"Ле́на уже́ <b>ушла́</b>.","say":"Лена уже ушла."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Сейча́с я пойду́ по у́лице.","right":"Сейча́с я иду́ по у́лице.","why":"пойду́ is perfective → it can only be future (“I'll set off”). For “I'm walking down the street right now”, use plain <b>иду́</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"«Ве́чером я пойду́ в кино́» means —","opts":["I'm going to the cinema now.","I'll set off to the cinema tonight.","I went to the cinema."],"answer":1,"explain":"пойду́ is perfective → future: “I'll set off”. “Going right now” is иду́; “went” is пошёл."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Ivan arrived home.”</b>","want":"Ива́н пришёл домо́й","answer":"Ива́н пришёл домо́й"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · <em>По-</em> — set off","title":"<em>Пойду́, пошёл</em> — heading out","paras":["<b>по-</b> is the “off we go” prefix: it marks the <b>start</b> of a trip, the moment you head somewhere. In the future it's your everyday way to say “I'll go”: <b>пойду́, пойдёшь, пойдёт, пойдём, пойдёте, пойду́т</b>.","In the past it's <b>пошёл</b> (he), <b>пошла́</b> (she), <b>пошли́</b> (they) — “set off / went”. And <b>Пошли́!</b> on its own is the warm, everyday “Let's go!”. <b>Пойдём</b> does the same job: “Let's go.”","Destination still works just as in A1 — <b>в/на + accusative</b>: <b>пойду́ в кино́, пойдём на рабо́ту</b>. The prefix says “set off”; the case says “to where”."],"audio":"Po- is the off-we-go prefix: it marks the start of a trip, the moment you head somewhere. In the future it's your everyday way to say I'll go: poydu, poydyosh, poydyot, poydyom, poydyote, poydut. In the past it's poshyol for he, poshla for she, poshli for they — set off, went. And poshli on its own is the warm everyday let's go. Poydyom does the same job. Destination works just as in A1 — v or na plus accusative: poydu v kino, poydyom na rabotu. The prefix says set off; the case says to where.","table":{"rows":[["Я пойду́ в парк. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я пойду в парк.\">🔊</button>","I'll set off to the park"],["Он пошёл на рабо́ту. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он пошёл на работу.\">🔊</button>","He went / set off to work"],["Она́ пошла́ домо́й. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она пошла домой.\">🔊</button>","She went home"],["Пошли́! / Пойдём! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Пошли! Пойдём!\">🔊</button>","Let's go!"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Let's go to the café!","en":"<b>Пошли́</b> в кафе́!","say":"Пошли в кафе!"},{"ru":"Tomorrow we'll go to the museum.","en":"За́втра мы <b>пойдём</b> в музе́й.","say":"Завтра мы пойдём в музей."},{"ru":"After work she set off home.","en":"По́сле рабо́ты она́ <b>пошла́</b> домо́й.","say":"После работы она пошла домой.","gloss":"по́сле рабо́ты = after work (genitive, day 4)"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Let's go to the park!” —","opts":["Пошли́ в парк!","Иду́ в парк!","Хожу́ в парк!"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Пошли́!</b> (or Пойдём!) is the everyday “Let's go!”. иду́ = “I'm walking”; хожу́ = “I go (regularly)”."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “He went to work” (set off, a man):","accept":["он пошёл на работу","он пошел на работу"],"placeholder":"Он …","explain":"по- + past “he” → <b>пошёл</b>; destination на рабо́ту (accusative)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Tomorrow we'll go to the museum.”</b>","want":"За́втра мы пойдём в музе́й","answer":"За́втра мы пойдём в музе́й"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · <em>При-</em> — arrive, come","title":"<em>Пришёл, приду́</em> — getting there","paras":["<b>при-</b> is the “arrive / come” prefix — it marks reaching the destination. Past: <b>пришёл</b> (he), <b>пришла́</b> (she), <b>пришли́</b> (they). Future: <b>приду́, придёшь, придёт, придём, придёте, приду́т</b>.","One spelling note: the infinitive is <b>прийти́</b> (with й), but the living forms drop it — <b>приду́, пришёл</b>. You'll use the forms far more than the infinitive, so let your ear lead.","<b>при-</b> is about arriving <em>here</em>, at the goal: <b>Он пришёл домо́й</b> (he got home), <b>Я приду́ в семь</b> (I'll come at seven). It's the natural mirror of the next prefix, which is all about leaving."],"audio":"Pri- is the arrive-or-come prefix — it marks reaching the destination. Past: prishyol for he, prishla for she, prishli for they. Future: pridu, pridyosh, pridyot, pridyom, pridyote, pridut. One spelling note: the infinitive is priyti, with a y, but the living forms drop it — pridu, prishyol. You'll use the forms far more than the infinitive, so let your ear lead. Pri- is about arriving here, at the goal: on prishyol domoy, he got home; ya pridu v sem, I'll come at seven. It's the natural mirror of the next prefix, which is all about leaving.","table":{"rows":[["Он пришёл домо́й. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он пришёл домой.\">🔊</button>","He arrived / came home"],["Она́ пришла́ ра́но. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она пришла рано.\">🔊</button>","She arrived early"],["Го́сти пришли́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Гости пришли.\">🔊</button>","The guests have arrived"],["Я приду́ в семь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я приду в семь.\">🔊</button>","I'll come at seven"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"When will you come? — I'll come at eight.","en":"Когда́ ты <b>придёшь</b>? — Приду́ в во́семь.","say":"Когда ты придёшь? Приду в восемь."},{"ru":"Emma came to the café early.","en":"Э́мма <b>пришла́</b> в кафе́ ра́но.","say":"Эмма пришла в кафе рано."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Вчера́ он приду́ домо́й.","right":"Вчера́ он пришёл домо́й.","why":"“Yesterday” needs the past. при- past for “he” is <b>пришёл</b>; приду́ is the future “I'll come”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“The guests have arrived.” —","opts":["Го́сти пойду́т.","Го́сти пришли́.","Го́сти хо́дят."],"answer":1,"explain":"при- = arrive → past plural <b>пришли́</b>. пойду́т = “they'll set off”; хо́дят = “they go (regularly)”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Finish: «За́втра он ___ в семь.» (he'll come)","opts":["придёт","пришёл","пойдёт"],"answer":0,"explain":"“He'll come” = при- future, 3rd person: <b>придёт</b>. пришёл is the past; пойдёт = “he'll set off”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'll come at seven.”</b>","want":"Я приду́ в семь","answer":"Я приду́ в семь"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · <em>У-</em> — leave, go away","title":"<em>Ушёл, уйду́</em> — and gone","paras":["<b>у-</b> is the “away” prefix: someone <b>leaves</b>, disappears from here. Past: <b>ушёл</b> (he), <b>ушла́</b> (she), <b>ушли́</b> (they). Future: <b>уйду́, уйдёшь, уйдёт, уйдём, уйдёте, уйду́т</b>.","Feel the contrast with при-. <b>при-</b> = arrive <em>here</em>; <b>у-</b> = leave <em>here</em>, gone elsewhere. <b>Он пришёл</b> (he's here now) vs <b>Он ушёл</b> (he's gone). Same door, opposite directions.","A very common everyday use: <b>Её нет — она́ ушла́</b> (She's not here — she's left). Pair it with the genitive of absence from day 2, and you sound completely natural."],"audio":"U- is the away prefix: someone leaves, disappears from here. Past: ushyol for he, ushla for she, ushli for they. Future: uydu, uydyosh, uydyot, uydyom, uydyote, uydut. Feel the contrast with pri-. Pri- means arrive here; u- means leave here, gone elsewhere. On prishyol, he's here now, versus on ushyol, he's gone. Same door, opposite directions. A very common everyday use: yeyo nyet — ona ushla, she's not here, she's left. Pair it with the genitive of absence from day 2, and you sound completely natural.","table":{"rows":[["Он ушёл на рабо́ту. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он ушёл на работу.\">🔊</button>","He's left for work"],["Она́ ушла́ домо́й. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она ушла домой.\">🔊</button>","She went home / left"],["Все ушли́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Все ушли.\">🔊</button>","Everyone has left"],["Я ско́ро уйду́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я скоро уйду.\">🔊</button>","I'll leave soon"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Lena isn't here — she's already left.","en":"Ле́ны нет — она́ уже́ <b>ушла́</b>.","say":"Лены нет — она уже ушла.","gloss":"Ле́ны нет = Lena's not here (genitive, day 2)"},{"ru":"I'll leave soon, I'm tired.","en":"Я ско́ро <b>уйду́</b>, я уста́л.","say":"Я скоро уйду, я устал.","gloss":"уста́л = tired (a man); a woman says уста́ла"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он ушёл в кино́. (meaning: set off to the cinema)","right":"Он пошёл в кино́.","why":"у- means “left / went <em>away</em> from here”, not “set off <em>to</em> a place”. To head to the cinema, use по-: <b>пошёл</b>. Choose the prefix by direction, not by the English word “went”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“She's not here — she's left.” —","opts":["Она́ пришла́.","Она́ ушла́.","Она́ пошла́."],"answer":1,"explain":"“Left / gone away” → у-: <b>ушла́</b>. пришла́ = arrived (she's here); пошла́ = set off (started a trip)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Он ушёл на рабо́ту.","sub":"say that he's left for work","want":"он ушёл на работу"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"A busy afternoon at the flat. Ivan has just got home, Emma is about to head out, and Lena has already vanished. Each arrival and departure rides on one little prefix.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т! Я то́лько что пришёл с рабо́ты.","ru":"Hi! I've just got home from work. (то́лько что = just now)"},{"who":"A","en":"О, ты ра́но! А я как раз пойду́ в магази́н.","ru":"Oh, you're early! And I'm just about to head to the shop."},{"who":"B","en":"А где Ле́на?","ru":"And where's Lena?"},{"who":"A","en":"Её нет. Она́ уже́ ушла́ к подру́ге.","ru":"She's not here. She's already left to see a friend. (к подру́ге = to a friend)"},{"who":"B","en":"Ушла́? А когда́ придёт?","ru":"She's left? And when will she come back?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ско́ро придёт. А мы пока́ пойдём в кафе́?","ru":"She'll come soon. Shall we head to the café meanwhile? (пока́ = for now)"},{"who":"B","en":"Пошли́! Я как раз голо́дный.","ru":"Let's go! I happen to be hungry. (голо́дный = hungry)"},{"who":"A","en":"Отли́чно! Пойдём.","ru":"Great! Let's go."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for coming and going. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"пойти́","ru":"to set off, to go (по-)","ex":"Я пойду́ в кино́."},{"en":"прийти́","ru":"to arrive, to come (при-)","ex":"Он пришёл домо́й."},{"en":"уйти́","ru":"to leave, to go away (у-)","ex":"Она́ уже́ ушла́."},{"en":"пошли́!","ru":"let's go!","ex":"Пошли́ в парк!"},{"en":"пойдём","ru":"let's go / we'll go","ex":"Пойдём вме́сте!"},{"en":"пришёл","ru":"(he) arrived, came","ex":"Ива́н пришёл ра́но."},{"en":"ушла́","ru":"(she) left","ex":"Ле́на ушла́ на рабо́ту."},{"en":"приду́","ru":"I'll come","ex":"Я приду́ в семь."},{"en":"обра́тно","ru":"back (returning)","ex":"Он пришёл обра́тно."},{"en":"ско́ро","ru":"soon","ex":"Я ско́ро приду́."},{"en":"ра́но","ru":"early","ex":"Ты пришёл ра́но."},{"en":"по́здно","ru":"late","ex":"Она́ ушла́ по́здно."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Он пришёл домо́й.","He arrived home."],["Она́ ушла́.","She left."],["Пошли́ в кафе́!","Let's go to the café!"],["Я приду́ в семь.","I'll come at seven."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the three prefixes. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Он пришёл» means —","opts":["He set off.","He arrived.","He left."],"answer":1},{"q":"“Let's go!” (everyday) —","opts":["Пошли́!","Хожу́!","Иду́!"],"answer":0},{"q":"“She's left / gone away” —","opts":["Она́ пришла́.","Она́ пошла́.","Она́ ушла́."],"answer":2},{"q":"“I'm walking down the street right now” —","opts":["Я пойду́ по у́лице.","Я иду́ по у́лице.","Я ушёл по у́лице."],"answer":1},{"q":"To head TO the cinema (set off), use —","opts":["ушёл в кино́","пошёл в кино́","пришёл из кино́"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A page from Emma's diary — one ordinary day of setting off, arriving and leaving. Read it aloud; every motion verb wears a prefix.","title":"Оди́н день","sentences":["У́тром я пошла́ на ры́нок.","Я пришла́ домо́й в час.","Пото́м пришёл Ива́н, и мы пошли́ в парк.","Ве́чером Ива́н ушёл на рабо́ту.","А я ско́ро пойду́ спать — я уста́ла."],"translation":"In the morning I set off to the market. I came home at one. Then Ivan came, and we went to the park. In the evening Ivan left for work. And soon I'll go to sleep — I'm tired."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to map a day of coming and going.","prompt":"Describe one day: where did you set off to (пошёл/пошла́), when did you arrive (пришёл/пришла́), and when did you leave (ушёл/ушла́)? Use at least three prefixed motion verbs.","hint":"по- = set off, при- = arrive, у- = leave. Past for a man ends in -шёл, for a woman in -шла́: пошёл / пошла́ 🙂","example":"У́тром я пошёл в университе́т. Я пришёл ра́но. По́сле уро́ка я пошёл в кафе́, а пото́м ушёл домо́й. За́втра я сно́ва пойду́ в университе́т."}},"20":{"day":20,"week":"03","themeRu":"Enter, exit, arrive by","themeEn":"Войти́, вы́йти, прие́хать","intro":"A train pulls into the station, doors open, a stranger steps out — <b>вы́шел из ваго́на</b>. Yesterday's prefixes gave you по-, при-, у-. Today two more join the walking verb — <b>в-</b> (enter) and <b>вы-</b> (exit) — and the transport verb <b>е́хать</b> takes prefixes too, so you can finally say someone <b>прие́хал из Москвы́</b>: arrived, by train, from another city.","introAudio":"A train pulls into the station, doors open, a stranger steps out — vyshel iz vagona. Yesterday's prefixes gave you po-, pri-, u-. Today two more join the walking verb — v- for enter and vy- for exit — and the transport verb yekhat takes prefixes too, so you can finally say someone priyekhal iz Moskvy: arrived, by train, from another city.","goals":["Go in and out on foot: <b>войти́ в дом</b>, <b>вы́йти из до́ма</b>","Arrive and leave by transport: <b>прие́хать</b>, <b>уе́хать</b>","Split every prefix by foot vs wheels: <b>пришёл</b> vs <b>прие́хал</b>","Pair <b>при-</b> with <b>из + genitive</b>: <b>Он прие́хал из Москвы́</b>"],"learned":["Went in and out on foot: <b>войти́ в дом</b>, <b>вы́йти из до́ма</b>","Arrived and left by transport: <b>прие́хать</b>, <b>уе́хать</b>","Split every prefix by foot vs wheels: <b>пришёл</b> vs <b>прие́хал</b>","Paired <b>при-</b> with <b>из + genitive</b>: <b>Он прие́хал из Москвы́</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's prefixes first — по-, при-, у- on foot — plus the genitive of “from” from week 1. Both feed straight into today.","introAudio":"Yesterday's prefixes first — po-, pri-, u- on foot — plus the genitive of from from week 1. Both feed straight into today.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. A quick look back before the new material.","q":"“He's already left (on foot).” —","opts":["Он уже́ ушёл.","Он уже́ пришёл.","Он уже́ пойдёт."],"answer":0,"explain":"у- = leave / gone away → <b>ушёл</b>. пришёл = arrived; пойдёт = “he'll set off”."},{"type":"choice","q":"“from Moscow” (out of) —","opts":["в Москву́","из Москвы́","о Москве́"],"answer":1,"explain":"“Out of / from” a place → <b>из + genitive</b>: из Москвы́. в Москву́ = “to Moscow”; о Москве́ = “about Moscow”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“The guests have arrived.”</b> (on foot)","want":"Го́сти пришли́","answer":"Го́сти пришли́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · <em>В-</em> and <em>вы-</em> — in and out","title":"<em>Войти́ / вы́йти</em> — through the door on foot","paras":["Two more prefixes join <b>идти́</b>, and they're a neat pair: <b>в-</b> = go <b>in</b> (enter), <b>вы-</b> = go <b>out</b> (exit). <b>войти́</b> (enter) and <b>вы́йти</b> (exit) work like yesterday's — perfective, living in past and future.","They team up with the cases you already know. Enter <b>into</b> something → <b>в + accusative</b>: <b>войти́ в дом</b>. Exit <b>out of</b> something → <b>из + genitive</b> (week 1!): <b>вы́йти из до́ма</b>.","One stress quirk worth a glance: in <b>вы́-</b> verbs the prefix grabs the stress. So it's <b>вы́йду, вы́шел, вы́шла</b> — stress on <b>вы́</b>, not the ending. войти́ keeps end-stress: <b>войду́, вошёл, вошла́</b>."],"audio":"Two more prefixes join idti, and they're a neat pair: v- means go in, enter; vy- means go out, exit. Voyti, enter, and vyyti, exit, work like yesterday's — perfective, living in past and future. They team up with the cases you already know. Enter into something → v plus accusative: voyti v dom. Exit out of something → iz plus genitive, from week one: vyyti iz doma. One stress quirk worth a glance: in vy- verbs the prefix grabs the stress. So it's vyydu, vyshel, vyshla — stress on vy, not the ending. Voyti keeps end-stress: voydu, voshyol, voshla.","table":{"rows":[["Он вошёл в дом. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он вошёл в дом.\">🔊</button>","He went into the house (в + accusative)"],["Она́ вошла́ в ко́мнату. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она вошла в комнату.\">🔊</button>","She entered the room"],["Он вы́шел из до́ма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он вышел из дома.\">🔊</button>","He went out of the house (из + genitive)"],["Она́ вы́шла на у́лицу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она вышла на улицу.\">🔊</button>","She went out onto the street"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Ivan came into the room quietly.","en":"Ива́н ти́хо <b>вошёл</b> в ко́мнату.","say":"Иван тихо вошёл в комнату.","gloss":"ти́хо = quietly"},{"ru":"She stepped out of the house.","en":"Она́ <b>вы́шла</b> из до́ма.","say":"Она вышла из дома."},{"ru":"I'll go out for a minute.","en":"Я <b>вы́йду</b> на мину́ту.","say":"Я выйду на минуту.","gloss":"на мину́ту = for a minute"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он вы́шел в до́ма.","right":"Он вы́шел из до́ма.","why":"“Out of” a place → <b>из + genitive</b>: из до́ма. в + accusative (в дом) would mean “into the house”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“He went into the house.” —","opts":["Он вошёл в дом.","Он вы́шел из до́ма.","Он ушёл из до́ма."],"answer":0,"explain":"“Into” on foot → в-: <b>вошёл в дом</b> (в + accusative). вы́шел из до́ма = went out; ушёл = left / away."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“She went out of the house.”</b>","want":"Она́ вы́шла из до́ма","answer":"Она́ вы́шла из до́ма"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · <em>Прие́хать, уе́хать</em> — by transport","title":"Wheels, not feet: <em>прие́хал, уе́хал</em>","paras":["The same arrive/leave idea, but now by <b>transport</b>. In A1 <b>е́хать</b> meant “to go by vehicle”. Add при- and у-: <b>прие́хать</b> = arrive (by train, car, plane), <b>уе́хать</b> = leave / go away (by transport, usually far or for a while).","Forms are gentle — е́хать is regular here. Future: <b>прие́ду, прие́дешь, прие́дет</b>; <b>уе́ду, уе́дешь</b>. Past: <b>прие́хал / прие́хала / прие́хали</b>; <b>уе́хал / уе́хала / уе́хали</b>.","So a friend who walks over <b>пришёл</b>; a friend who takes the train <b>прие́хал</b>. Same при-, different verb — feet or wheels."],"audio":"The same arrive-or-leave idea, but now by transport. In A1 yekhat meant to go by vehicle. Add pri- and u-: priyekhat, arrive by train, car or plane; uyekhat, leave or go away by transport, usually far or for a while. Forms are gentle — yekhat is regular here. Future: priyedu, priyedesh, priyedet; uyedu, uyedesh. Past: priyekhal, priyekhala, priyekhali; uyekhal, uyekhala, uyekhali. So a friend who walks over prishyol; a friend who takes the train priyekhal. Same pri-, different verb — feet or wheels.","table":{"rows":[["Он прие́хал из Москвы́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он приехал из Москвы.\">🔊</button>","He arrived from Moscow (by train)"],["Друг прие́хал на маши́не. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Друг приехал на машине.\">🔊</button>","A friend arrived by car"],["Брат уе́хал в Петербу́рг. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Брат уехал в Петербург.\">🔊</button>","My brother left for Petersburg"],["Я прие́ду за́втра. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я приеду завтра.\">🔊</button>","I'll arrive tomorrow"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Emma arrived in Moscow a month ago.","en":"Э́мма <b>прие́хала</b> в Москву́ ме́сяц наза́д.","say":"Эмма приехала в Москву месяц назад.","gloss":"ме́сяц наза́д = a month ago"},{"ru":"When will you arrive? — I'll arrive on Friday.","en":"Когда́ ты <b>прие́дешь</b>? — Прие́ду в пя́тницу.","say":"Когда ты приедешь? Приеду в пятницу."},{"ru":"My brother has gone away to Petersburg.","en":"Брат <b>уе́хал</b> в Петербу́рг.","say":"Брат уехал в Петербург."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он прие́хал в Москву́ пешко́м.","right":"Он пришёл в Москву́ пешко́м.","why":"прие́хать is by <b>transport</b>. “On foot” (пешко́м) needs the foot verb: <b>пришёл</b>. Choose -ехать vs -йти by how you travel."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“He arrived from Moscow (by train).” —","opts":["Он пришёл из Москвы́.","Он прие́хал из Москвы́.","Он уе́хал из Москвы́."],"answer":1,"explain":"Arrived by transport → при- on е́хать: <b>прие́хал</b>. пришёл is on foot; уе́хал = left / went away."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “My brother left for Petersburg” (by transport):","accept":["брат уехал в петербург","мой брат уехал в петербург"],"placeholder":"Брат …","explain":"у- + е́хать, past “he” → <b>уе́хал</b>; destination в Петербу́рг (accusative)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'll arrive tomorrow.”</b>","want":"Я прие́ду за́втра","answer":"Я прие́ду за́втра"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Foot vs wheels","title":"<em>Пришёл</em> or <em>прие́хал</em>? Every prefix splits in two","paras":["Here's the tidy pattern behind it all. <b>Every</b> direction-prefix can sit on the foot verb (<b>-йти</b>) <em>or</em> the transport verb (<b>-ехать</b>). The prefix keeps its meaning; the root tells us <b>how</b> you moved.","So при- = arrive: on foot <b>пришёл</b>, by transport <b>прие́хал</b>. у- = leave: on foot <b>ушёл</b>, by transport <b>уе́хал</b>. по- = set off: <b>пошёл</b> / <b>пое́хал</b>. English hides this — “he came”, “he left” — but Russian always shows the means.","Rule of thumb: a short hop across the room or down the street → feet (<b>-йти</b>). Another city, the airport, a real journey → wheels (<b>-ехать</b>)."],"audio":"Here's the tidy pattern behind it all. Every direction-prefix can sit on the foot verb, -yti, or the transport verb, -yekhat. The prefix keeps its meaning; the root tells us how you moved. So pri- means arrive: on foot prishyol, by transport priyekhal. U- means leave: on foot ushyol, by transport uyekhal. Po- means set off: poshyol or poyekhal. English hides this — he came, he left — but Russian always shows the means. Rule of thumb: a short hop across the room or down the street → feet. Another city, the airport, a real journey → wheels.","table":{"rows":[["пришёл / прие́хал <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пришёл, приехал\">🔊</button>","arrived — on foot / by transport"],["ушёл / уе́хал <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ушёл, уехал\">🔊</button>","left — on foot / by transport"],["пошёл / пое́хал <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пошёл, поехал\">🔊</button>","set off — on foot / by transport"],["вошёл / вы́шел <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вошёл, вышел\">🔊</button>","went in / went out (on foot)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Lena came on foot, and Ivan came by car.","en":"Ле́на <b>пришла́</b>, а Ива́н <b>прие́хал</b> на маши́не.","say":"Лена пришла, а Иван приехал на машине."},{"ru":"We set off to the park (on foot).","en":"Мы <b>пошли́</b> в парк.","say":"Мы пошли в парк."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он прие́хал в ко́мнату.","right":"Он вошёл в ко́мнату.","why":"You don't drive into a room. A short move on foot → <b>вошёл</b>. Keep -ехать for real journeys."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Emma arrived from London by train. She ___","opts":["Она́ пришла́ из Ло́ндона","Она́ вы́шла из Ло́ндона","Она́ прие́хала из Ло́ндона"],"answer":2,"explain":"A journey from another city → transport verb: <b>прие́хала</b> (arrived). пришла́ is on foot; вы́шла = stepped out."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “We set off to the park” (on foot):","accept":["мы пошли в парк"],"placeholder":"Мы …","explain":"по- on foot, past “we” → <b>пошли́</b>; в парк (accusative)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“A friend arrived by car.”</b>","want":"Друг прие́хал на маши́не","answer":"Друг прие́хал на маши́не"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · <em>При-</em> meets <em>из</em>","title":"<em>Прие́хал из Москвы́</em> — arriving from somewhere","paras":["When you arrive <b>from</b> a place, при- naturally pairs with <b>из + genitive</b> — the very “from / out of” you learned in week 1. <b>Он прие́хал из Москвы́</b> (from Moscow), <b>Она́ прие́хала из Ло́ндона</b> (from London).","Both halves agree on the logic: <b>из</b> marks the starting point you came out of, <b>при-</b> marks that you've reached here. Where you're heading <em>to</em> still uses <b>в + accusative</b>: <b>прие́хал в Москву́</b>.","So one sentence can hold both: <b>Он прие́хал в Москву́ из Петербу́рга</b> — arrived in Moscow from Petersburg. From-place in the genitive, to-place in the accusative, journey done."],"audio":"When you arrive from a place, pri- naturally pairs with iz plus genitive — the very from-or-out-of you learned in week one. On priyekhal iz Moskvy, from Moscow; ona priyekhala iz Londona, from London. Both halves agree on the logic: iz marks the starting point you came out of, pri- marks that you've reached here. Where you're heading to still uses v plus accusative: priyekhal v Moskvu. So one sentence can hold both: on priyekhal v Moskvu iz Peterburga — arrived in Moscow from Petersburg. From-place in the genitive, to-place in the accusative, journey done.","examples":[{"ru":"A new student arrived from Kazan.","en":"Но́вый студе́нт <b>прие́хал из Каза́ни</b>.","say":"Новый студент приехал из Казани.","gloss":"Каза́нь → из Каза́ни (genitive)"},{"ru":"Emma arrived in Moscow from London.","en":"Э́мма <b>прие́хала в Москву́ из Ло́ндона</b>.","say":"Эмма приехала в Москву из Лондона."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он прие́хал с Москвы́.","right":"Он прие́хал из Москвы́.","why":"A city you come out of takes <b>из</b>, not с (day 3: из pairs with в-places, с with на-places). из Москвы́."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“She arrived from London.” —","opts":["Она́ прие́хала в Ло́ндон.","Она́ прие́хала из Ло́ндона.","Она́ прие́хала о Ло́ндоне."],"answer":1,"explain":"“From / out of” → из + genitive: <b>из Ло́ндона</b>. в Ло́ндон = “to London”; о Ло́ндоне = “about London”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which fits? «Он прие́хал ___ Петербу́рга.» (from the city)","opts":["из","с","в"],"answer":0,"explain":"A city → <b>из + genitive</b>: из Петербу́рга. с pairs with на-places; в would mean “to / into”."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Он прие́хал из Москвы́.","sub":"say that he arrived from Moscow (by train)","want":"он приехал из москвы"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is at the station to meet Maxim, Ivan's brother, arriving by train from Petersburg. Watch the arrival unfold — the train, the carriage, the first hello.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Вот и по́езд! Он прие́хал во́время.","ru":"There's the train! It arrived on time. (во́время = on time)"},{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма? Приве́т! Я то́лько что вы́шел из ваго́на.","ru":"Emma? Hi! I've just got out of the carriage."},{"who":"A","en":"Приве́т, Макси́м! Ты прие́хал из Петербу́рга?","ru":"Hi, Maxim! You've come from Petersburg?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, пря́мо из Петербу́рга. Е́хал всю ночь.","ru":"Yes, straight from Petersburg. I travelled all night. (пря́мо = straight; всю ночь = all night)"},{"who":"A","en":"Ты, наве́рное, уста́л. Пошли́, вы́йдем на у́лицу.","ru":"You're probably tired. Let's go, we'll step outside. (наве́рное = probably)"},{"who":"B","en":"Пошли́! А куда́ мы пото́м пойдём?","ru":"Let's go! And where will we go afterwards?"},{"who":"A","en":"Снача́ла домо́й. Ива́н уже́ пришёл с рабо́ты и ждёт.","ru":"First home. Ivan has already got back from work and is waiting. (снача́ла = first)"},{"who":"B","en":"Отли́чно. Пое́хали!","ru":"Great. Let's go (by car)! (пое́хали = let's go, by transport)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for stations, trains and doorways. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"войти́","ru":"to enter, go in (в-)","ex":"Он вошёл в дом."},{"en":"вы́йти","ru":"to exit, go out (вы-)","ex":"Она́ вы́шла из ко́мнаты."},{"en":"прие́хать","ru":"to arrive (by transport)","ex":"Я прие́хал из Москвы́."},{"en":"уе́хать","ru":"to leave, go away (by transport)","ex":"Брат уе́хал в Ло́ндон."},{"en":"вошёл","ru":"(he) came in","ex":"Он ти́хо вошёл."},{"en":"вы́шла","ru":"(she) went out","ex":"Ле́на вы́шла на у́лицу."},{"en":"прие́хал","ru":"(he) arrived (by transport)","ex":"Друг прие́хал у́тром."},{"en":"по́езд","ru":"train","ex":"По́езд из Москвы́."},{"en":"вокза́л","ru":"(railway) station","ex":"Мы на вокза́ле."},{"en":"ваго́н","ru":"carriage, train car","ex":"Я вошёл в ваго́н."},{"en":"биле́т","ru":"ticket","ex":"Вот мой биле́т."},{"en":"пешко́м","ru":"on foot","ex":"Я пришёл пешко́м."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Он вошёл в дом.","He went into the house."],["Она́ вы́шла из до́ма.","She went out of the house."],["Он прие́хал из Москвы́.","He arrived from Moscow."],["Брат уе́хал.","My brother has left."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on doors, trains and “from”. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Он прие́хал из Москвы́» means —","opts":["He walked from Moscow.","He arrived from Moscow (by transport).","He left for Moscow."],"answer":1},{"q":"“He went out of the house” (on foot) —","opts":["Он вы́шел из до́ма.","Он вошёл в дом.","Он уе́хал из до́ма."],"answer":0},{"q":"A friend takes the train to visit. He ___","opts":["пришёл","прие́хал","вошёл"],"answer":1},{"q":"“into the room” — войти́ ___","opts":["из ко́мнаты","в ко́мнату","о ко́мнате"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which “from” fits a city after прие́хал?","opts":["с Москвы́","о Москве́","из Москвы́"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"At the station, Emma waits for a friend's train. Read her note about who arrived, went in and left.","title":"На вокза́ле","sentences":["Я на вокза́ле. Жду по́езд из Каза́ни.","По́езд прие́хал в час.","Мой друг вы́шел из ваго́на.","Мы вы́шли на у́лицу и пошли́ в кафе́.","А ве́чером он уе́хал обра́тно в Каза́нь."],"translation":"I'm at the station. I'm waiting for the train from Kazan. The train arrived at one. My friend got out of the carriage. We went out onto the street and went to a café. And in the evening he went back to Kazan."},"essay":{"intro":"Tell the story of an arrival.","prompt":"Describe someone arriving from another city: where did they come from (прие́хал из…), did they get out or go in (вы́шел, вошёл), and where did you go together? Use foot verbs and transport verbs where each fits.","hint":"Feet vs wheels: a journey between cities → -ехать (прие́хал), a step through a door → -йти (вошёл, вы́шел). “From a city” = из + genitive 🙂","example":"Мой друг прие́хал из Петербу́рга. Он вы́шел из ваго́на, и мы пошли́ домо́й. До́ма мы вошли́ в кварти́ру. За́втра он уе́дет обра́тно."}},"21":{"day":21,"week":"03","themeRu":"Review 3","themeEn":"Повторе́ние №3","intro":"Three weeks in — and what a week this was. You turned Russian's aspect into a working tool, built two futures, and learned to melt whole directions into a single prefix. Today is a victory lap: no new grammar, just everything from days 15–20 pulled together in Emma's Moscow.","introAudio":"Three weeks in — and what a week this was. You turned Russian's aspect into a working tool, built two futures, and learned to melt whole directions into a single prefix. Today is a victory lap: no new grammar, just everything from days fifteen to twenty pulled together in Emma's Moscow.","goals":["Choose aspect with confidence: process <b>чита́л</b> vs result <b>прочита́л</b>","Build both futures: <b>бу́ду чита́ть</b> (process) and <b>прочита́ю</b> (result)","Move with prefixes: <b>пошёл, пришёл, ушёл, вошёл, вы́шел</b>","Split foot from wheels: <b>пришёл</b> vs <b>прие́хал из Москвы́</b>"],"learned":["Chose aspect with confidence: process <b>чита́л</b> vs result <b>прочита́л</b>","Built both futures: <b>бу́ду чита́ть</b> and <b>прочита́ю</b>","Moved with prefixes: <b>пошёл, пришёл, ушёл, вошёл, вы́шел</b>","Split foot from wheels: <b>пришёл</b> vs <b>прие́хал из Москвы́</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Warm up across the whole week — aspect, the two futures, and prefixed motion. Then three rules pull it all together.","introAudio":"Warm up across the whole week — aspect, the two futures, and prefixed motion. Then three rules pull it all together.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of it is this week's Russian — no new grammar today.","q":"“I was reading all evening” (a woman, a process) —","opts":["Я весь ве́чер прочита́ла.","Я весь ве́чер чита́ла.","Я весь ве́чер прочита́ю."],"answer":1,"explain":"“All evening” = an ongoing process → imperfective <b>чита́ла</b>. прочита́ла = finished it; прочита́ю = the future."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Tomorrow I'll be working” (a process) —","opts":["За́втра я бу́ду рабо́тать.","За́втра я бу́дет рабо́тать.","За́втра я рабо́тал."],"answer":0,"explain":"A future process → <b>бу́ду</b> + imperfective рабо́тать. бу́дет is the wrong helper for я; рабо́тал is the past."},{"type":"choice","q":"“He arrived from Moscow (by train).” —","opts":["Он пришёл из Москвы́.","Он прие́хал из Москвы́.","Он вы́шел из Москвы́."],"answer":1,"explain":"A journey by transport → <b>прие́хал</b>; из + genitive из Москвы́. пришёл is on foot."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“She has already left.”</b> (on foot)","want":"Она́ уже́ ушла́","answer":"Она́ уже́ ушла́"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “Tomorrow I'll read this book (through)”:","accept":["завтра я прочитаю эту книгу","я прочитаю эту книгу завтра"],"placeholder":"За́втра я …","explain":"A finished future result → perfective <b>прочита́ю</b>, with no бу́ду."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Aspect — process or result","title":"<em>Чита́л</em> vs <em>прочита́л</em> — the choice under everything","paras":["This week's big idea: every action asks you to choose. The <b>imperfective</b> paints the <b>process</b>, the habit, the general fact — <b>чита́л, де́лал, писа́л</b>. The <b>perfective</b> names a single <b>finished result</b> — <b>прочита́л, сде́лал, написа́л</b>. Same event, two camera angles.","In the past, markers point the way. <b>весь день, ка́ждый день, до́лго</b> pull imperfective (process). <b>уже́, вчера́ бы́стро, всё</b> pull perfective (done). <b>Я чита́л кни́гу</b> (was reading, maybe unfinished) vs <b>Я прочита́л кни́гу</b> (read it, finished).","English hides the choice — “I read the book” could be either. Russian makes you commit. Pick the <b>result</b> when you reached the end; pick the <b>process</b> when you're painting the doing."],"audio":"This week's big idea: every action asks you to choose. The imperfective paints the process, the habit, the general fact — chital, delal, pisal. The perfective names a single finished result — prochital, sdelal, napisal. Same event, two camera angles. In the past, markers point the way. Ves den, kazhdy den, dolgo pull imperfective. Uzhe, vchera bystro, vsyo pull perfective. Ya chital knigu, was reading, maybe unfinished, versus ya prochital knigu, read it, finished. English hides the choice — I read the book could be either. Russian makes you commit. Pick the result when you reached the end; pick the process when you're painting the doing.","table":{"rows":[["чита́л / прочита́л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читал, прочитал\">🔊</button>","was reading / read (finished)"],["де́лал / сде́лал <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"делал, сделал\">🔊</button>","was doing / did (finished)"],["писа́л / написа́л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"писал, написал\">🔊</button>","was writing / wrote (finished)"],["говори́л / сказа́л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"говорил, сказал\">🔊</button>","was speaking / said (once)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Yesterday I read the book to the end.","en":"Вчера́ я <b>прочита́л</b> кни́гу до конца́.","say":"Вчера я прочитал книгу до конца."},{"ru":"I was writing the report all day.","en":"Я <b>писа́л</b> отчёт весь день.","say":"Я писал отчёт весь день."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я весь день сде́лал дома́шнее зада́ние.","right":"Я весь день де́лал дома́шнее зада́ние.","why":"“All day” = a stretch of process → imperfective <b>де́лал</b>. The perfective сде́лал names one finished moment, which clashes with “all day”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I did (finished) my homework yesterday.” —","opts":["Вчера́ я де́лал дома́шнее зада́ние.","Вчера́ я сде́лаю дома́шнее зада́ние.","Вчера́ я сде́лал дома́шнее зада́ние."],"answer":2,"explain":"A finished result → perfective past <b>сде́лал</b>. де́лал = was doing (process); сде́лаю = the future."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which marker calls for the imperfective (process)?","opts":["уже́","весь день","бы́стро"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>весь день</b> stretches out a process → imperfective. уже́ and бы́стро point to a finished result (perfective)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Two futures","title":"<em>Бу́ду чита́ть</em> vs <em>прочита́ю</em>","paras":["Aspect shapes the future too. For a <b>process</b> or plan, use <b>бу́ду + imperfective</b>: <b>Ве́чером я бу́ду чита́ть</b>. For a single <b>finished result</b>, use the <b>perfective future</b> alone: <b>Я прочита́ю статью́</b>.","Remember the perfective's secret: it has no present. Its present-looking form <b>прочита́ю, напишу́, сде́лаю</b> always means the future. And <b>бу́ду</b> only ever takes an <b>imperfective</b> — never бу́ду прочита́ю.","So two clean tools: <b>бу́ду</b> + imperfective for “I'll be doing”, and the perfective future for “I'll get it done”. Same question as always — process or result?"],"audio":"Aspect shapes the future too. For a process or plan, use budu plus imperfective: vecherom ya budu chitat. For a single finished result, use the perfective future alone: ya prochitayu statyu. Remember the perfective's secret: it has no present. Its present-looking form — prochitayu, napishu, sdelayu — always means the future. And budu only ever takes an imperfective — never budu prochitayu. So two clean tools: budu plus imperfective for I'll be doing, and the perfective future for I'll get it done. Same question as always — process or result?","table":{"rows":[["Я бу́ду чита́ть весь ве́чер. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я буду читать весь вечер.\">🔊</button>","I'll be reading all evening (process)"],["Я прочита́ю статью́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я прочитаю статью.\">🔊</button>","I'll read the article (through) (result)"],["За́втра я бу́ду рабо́тать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Завтра я буду работать.\">🔊</button>","Tomorrow I'll be working (process)"],["Я напишу́ письмо́ и позвоню́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я напишу письмо и позвоню.\">🔊</button>","I'll write the letter and call (result)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I'll do everything tomorrow.","en":"За́втра я всё <b>сде́лаю</b>.","say":"Завтра я всё сделаю."},{"ru":"On Saturday I'll be resting all day.","en":"В суббо́ту я <b>бу́ду отдыха́ть</b> весь день.","say":"В субботу я буду отдыхать весь день."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"За́втра я бу́ду написа́ть письмо́.","right":"За́втра я напишу́ письмо́.","why":"бу́ду never takes a perfective. For the finished result, the perfective future stands alone: <b>напишу́</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'll be reading all evening” (a process) —","opts":["Я прочита́ю весь ве́чер.","Я чита́ю весь ве́чер.","Я бу́ду чита́ть весь ве́чер."],"answer":2,"explain":"A future process → <b>бу́ду чита́ть</b>. прочита́ю names a finished result; чита́ю is the present."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'll call you in the evening.”</b>","want":"Я позвоню́ тебе́ ве́чером","answer":"Я позвоню́ тебе́ ве́чером"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Motion with prefixes","title":"<em>Пошёл, пришёл, прие́хал</em> — direction in a prefix","paras":["A prefix on a motion verb does two jobs: it adds a <b>direction</b> and makes the verb <b>perfective</b>. <b>по-</b> set off, <b>при-</b> arrive, <b>у-</b> leave, <b>в-</b> go in, <b>вы-</b> go out. <b>пошёл, пришёл, ушёл, вошёл, вы́шел</b>.","Each prefix splits by <b>how</b> you travel: on foot uses <b>-йти</b> (пришёл), by transport uses <b>-ехать</b> (прие́хал). A step to the shop vs a train from another city.","And directions pair with the cases you know: destination <b>в/на + accusative</b> (пошёл в парк), arriving from a place <b>из + genitive</b> (прие́хал из Москвы́). One prefix, and the whole journey lines up."],"audio":"A prefix on a motion verb does two jobs: it adds a direction and makes the verb perfective. Po- set off, pri- arrive, u- leave, v- go in, vy- go out. Poshyol, prishyol, ushyol, voshyol, vyshel. Each prefix splits by how you travel: on foot uses -yti, prishyol; by transport uses -yekhat, priyekhal. A step to the shop versus a train from another city. And directions pair with the cases you know: destination v or na plus accusative, poshyol v park; arriving from a place iz plus genitive, priyekhal iz Moskvy. One prefix, and the whole journey lines up.","table":{"rows":[["пошёл / пое́хал <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пошёл, поехал\">🔊</button>","set off — on foot / by transport"],["пришёл / прие́хал <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пришёл, приехал\">🔊</button>","arrived — on foot / by transport"],["ушёл / уе́хал <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ушёл, уехал\">🔊</button>","left — on foot / by transport"],["вошёл / вы́шел <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вошёл, вышел\">🔊</button>","went in / went out (on foot)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Emma arrived in Moscow from London.","en":"Э́мма <b>прие́хала</b> в Москву́ из Ло́ндона.","say":"Эмма приехала в Москву из Лондона."},{"ru":"He came home, went into the kitchen and then left.","en":"Он <b>пришёл</b> домо́й, <b>вошёл</b> в ку́хню, а пото́м <b>ушёл</b>.","say":"Он пришёл домой, вошёл в кухню, а потом ушёл.","gloss":"ку́хня → в ку́хню (kitchen)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он прие́хал в ко́мнату.","right":"Он вошёл в ко́мнату.","why":"You don't drive into a room — a step on foot → <b>вошёл</b>. Keep -ехать (прие́хал) for real journeys between places."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A friend visits by train. He ___","opts":["пришёл","прие́хал","вошёл"],"answer":1,"explain":"By transport → <b>прие́хал</b>. пришёл is on foot; вошёл = came in (through a door)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“He arrived from Moscow.”</b> (by train)","want":"Он прие́хал из Москвы́","answer":"Он прие́хал из Москвы́"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Она́ ушла́ на рабо́ту.","sub":"say that she's left for work","want":"она ушла на работу"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Sunday again at Lena's café. Ivan asks Emma how her big week went — and her answer sweeps up everything from days 15 to 20: what she finished, where she went, and what's still to come.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну как неде́ля? Ты всё успе́ла?","ru":"So how was the week? Did you manage everything? (успе́ла = managed, a woman)"},{"who":"A","en":"Почти́ всё! Я написа́ла отчёт и прочита́ла кни́гу до конца́.","ru":"Almost everything! I wrote the report and read the book to the end. (почти́ = almost)"},{"who":"B","en":"Молоде́ц! А во вто́рник ты уе́хала?","ru":"Well done! And on Tuesday you left? (молоде́ц = well done)"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, я уе́хала в Петербу́рг, а прие́хала обра́тно в пя́тницу.","ru":"Yes, I left for Petersburg, and came back on Friday."},{"who":"B","en":"И как Петербу́рг?","ru":"And how was Petersburg?"},{"who":"A","en":"Прекра́сно! Но я всю ночь е́хала в по́езде и о́чень уста́ла.","ru":"Wonderful! But I travelled all night on the train and got very tired. (прекра́сно = wonderful)"},{"who":"B","en":"А что ты бу́дешь де́лать за́втра?","ru":"And what will you be doing tomorrow?"},{"who":"A","en":"За́втра я ничего́ не бу́ду де́лать. Бу́ду отдыха́ть!","ru":"Tomorrow I won't do anything. I'll be resting!"},{"who":"B","en":"Пра́вильно. А пото́м мы пойдём гуля́ть.","ru":"Right. And then we'll go for a walk. (гуля́ть = to stroll)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words that carry the whole of week 3. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"сде́лать","ru":"to do, get done (perfective)","ex":"Я всё сде́лаю."},{"en":"прочита́ть","ru":"to read (through) (perfective)","ex":"Я прочита́л кни́гу."},{"en":"написа́ть","ru":"to write (finish) (perfective)","ex":"Я написа́л письмо́."},{"en":"бу́ду","ru":"(I) will be (+ imperfective)","ex":"За́втра бу́ду рабо́тать."},{"en":"пойти́","ru":"to set off (по-)","ex":"Мы пойдём в кино́."},{"en":"прийти́","ru":"to arrive on foot (при-)","ex":"Он пришёл ра́но."},{"en":"уйти́","ru":"to leave on foot (у-)","ex":"Она́ ушла́."},{"en":"прие́хать","ru":"to arrive by transport","ex":"Я прие́хал из Москвы́."},{"en":"уе́хать","ru":"to leave by transport","ex":"Брат уе́хал."},{"en":"войти́","ru":"to go in (в-)","ex":"Он вошёл в дом."},{"en":"вы́йти","ru":"to go out (вы-)","ex":"Она́ вы́шла."},{"en":"ско́ро","ru":"soon","ex":"Я ско́ро приду́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я прочита́л кни́гу.","I read the book (finished it)."],["Я бу́ду чита́ть.","I'll be reading."],["Он прие́хал из Москвы́.","He arrived from Moscow."],["Она́ ушла́.","She left."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions across the whole week — aspect, both futures, and prefixed motion.","items":[{"q":"«Я прочита́л кни́гу» —","opts":["I was reading a book.","I read the book (finished it).","I will read a book."],"answer":1},{"q":"“All day I was doing homework” —","opts":["Весь день я сде́лал дома́шнее зада́ние.","Весь день я де́лал дома́шнее зада́ние.","Весь день я сде́лаю дома́шнее зада́ние."],"answer":1},{"q":"“Tomorrow I'll be working” (a process) —","opts":["За́втра я порабо́таю.","За́втра я бу́дет рабо́тать.","За́втра я бу́ду рабо́тать."],"answer":2},{"q":"“I'll get it done” (a promise) —","opts":["Я сде́лаю.","Я де́лаю.","Я бу́ду сде́лать."],"answer":0},{"q":"Which sentence is impossible in Russian?","opts":["Я бу́ду чита́ть.","Я прочита́ю.","Я бу́ду прочита́ю."],"answer":2},{"q":"«Он ушёл» means —","opts":["He arrived.","He left / went away.","He came in."],"answer":1},{"q":"A friend comes by train from Kazan. He ___","opts":["пришёл из Каза́ни","прие́хал из Каза́ни","вы́шел из Каза́ни"],"answer":1},{"q":"“She went out of the house” (on foot) —","opts":["Она́ вошла́ в дом.","Она́ уе́хала из до́ма.","Она́ вы́шла из до́ма."],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's week in five lines — aspect, both futures, and prefixes all working together. Read it aloud.","title":"Моя́ неде́ля в Москве́","sentences":["На э́той неде́ле я мно́го рабо́тала.","Я написа́ла отчёт и прочита́ла кни́гу.","Во вто́рник я уе́хала в Петербу́рг, а в пя́тницу прие́хала обра́тно.","За́втра я ничего́ не бу́ду де́лать — бу́ду отдыха́ть.","А в воскресе́нье мы пойдём гуля́ть в парк."],"translation":"This week I worked a lot. I wrote the report and read the book. On Tuesday I left for Petersburg, and on Friday I came back. Tomorrow I won't do anything — I'll be resting. And on Sunday we'll go for a walk in the park."},"essay":{"intro":"Your week, your way — put the whole of week 3 to work.","prompt":"Tell about your week: what you did (choose aspect — process or result), where you went (prefixed motion), and what you'll get done or be doing next. Aim for five or six sentences.","hint":"Process past = чита́л/де́лал; finished result = прочита́л/сде́лал. Motion rides a prefix: пошёл, прие́хал, ушёл. Future: бу́ду + imperfective (process) or the perfective alone (result) 🙂","example":"На э́той неде́ле я мно́го рабо́тал и написа́л большо́й отчёт. В сре́ду я уе́хал к дру́гу, а в пя́тницу прие́хал обра́тно. За́втра я всё сде́лаю, а пото́м бу́ду отдыха́ть. Ве́чером мы пойдём в кино́."}},"22":{"day":22,"week":"04","themeRu":"Say it, do it!","themeEn":"Скажи́! Де́лай!","intro":"Back in Moscow, Emma keeps needing two things: to ask the way, and to hand out a little advice of her own. Both need the <b>imperative</b> — the “do it!” form. <b>Скажи́!</b> Tell me! <b>Иди́!</b> Go! <b>Купи́!</b> Buy! Today you'll build these commands the Russian way — not from the dictionary word, but from the verb you already conjugate every single day.","introAudio":"Back in Moscow, Emma keeps needing two things: to ask the way, and to hand out a little advice of her own. Both need the imperative — the do-it form. Skazhi! Tell me. Idi! Go. Kupi! Buy. Today you'll build these commands the Russian way — not from the dictionary word, but from the verb you already conjugate every single day.","goals":["Give a command: <b>Чита́й!</b> “Read!”, <b>Де́лай!</b> “Do it!”, <b>Скажи́!</b> “Say it!”","Build the imperative from the <b>present-tense</b> stem, not from the infinitive","Choose the ending: <b>-й</b> after a vowel, <b>-и</b> after a consonant, <b>-ь</b> for a few like <b>будь</b>","Soften it with <b>-те</b>: <b>Скажи́те, пожа́луйста</b> — “Tell me, please”"],"learned":["Gave a command: <b>Чита́й!</b>, <b>Де́лай!</b>, <b>Скажи́!</b>","Built the imperative from the present-tense stem, not the infinitive","Chose the ending: <b>-й</b> after a vowel, <b>-и</b> after a consonant, <b>-ь</b> for a few like <b>будь</b>","Made polite requests with <b>-те</b>: <b>Скажи́те, пожа́луйста</b>"],"review":{"intro":"One minute first on the Russian that feeds today. The imperative grows straight out of the present tense, so let's wake that up — plus a case and a bought loaf of bread you'll be commanding in a moment.","introAudio":"One minute first on the Russian that feeds today. The imperative grows straight out of the present tense, so let's wake that up — plus a case and a bought loaf of bread you'll be commanding in a moment.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of this is Russian you already own.","q":"“They speak Russian” — pick the natural “they” form of говори́ть:","opts":["они́ говоря́т","они́ говори́т","они́ говоря́ть"],"answer":0,"explain":"говори́ть is an -и- verb: <b>они́ говоря́т</b>. говори́т is the “he” form; говоря́ть with -ть isn't a word."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Ivan bought bread yesterday.”</b> (perfective past)","want":"Ива́н купи́л хлеб вчера́","answer":"Ива́н купи́л хлеб вчера́"},{"type":"choice","q":"“I call my brother every evening.” — «Я звоню́ ___ ка́ждый ве́чер.»","opts":["бра́ту","бра́та","бра́том"],"answer":0,"explain":"звони́ть takes the <b>dative</b>: звоню́ <b>бра́ту</b> (to whom). бра́та is genitive/accusative, бра́том instrumental."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Build it from the “they” form","title":"Commands come from the present stem: <em>чита́ют → чита́й</em>","paras":["Here's the Russian logic, and it isn't the English one. To make a command, you don't reach for the dictionary word (<b>чита́ть, де́лать</b>). You start from the verb you already say every day — the <b>они́</b> (“they”) form — and chop off its ending.","<b>чита́ть</b>: они́ чита́<b>ют</b> → the stem is <b>чита́-</b>. It ends in a vowel, so you just add <b>-й</b>: <b>чита́й!</b> Same for <b>де́лают → де́лай</b>, <b>слу́шают → слу́шай</b>, <b>ду́мают → ду́май</b>.","So the command was hiding inside the present tense all along. Find the “they” form, cut the ending, and you're most of the way to a command."],"audio":"Here's the Russian logic, and it isn't the English one. To make a command, you don't reach for the dictionary word, chitat, delat. You start from the verb you already say every day — the oni, they, form — and chop off its ending. Chitat: oni chitayut, the stem is chita. It ends in a vowel, so you just add -y: chitay! Same for delayut, delay; slushayut, slushay; dumayut, dumay. So the command was hiding inside the present tense all along. Find the they form, cut the ending, and you're most of the way to a command.","table":{"rows":[["они́ чита́ют → чита́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они читают, читай\">🔊</button>","they read → read!"],["они́ де́лают → де́лай <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они делают, делай\">🔊</button>","they do → do it!"],["они́ слу́шают → слу́шай <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они слушают, слушай\">🔊</button>","they listen → listen!"],["они́ ду́мают → ду́май <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они думают, думай\">🔊</button>","they think → think!"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Read this text!","en":"<b>Чита́й</b> э́тот текст!","say":"Читай этот текст!"},{"ru":"Do your homework!","en":"<b>Де́лай</b> дома́шнее зада́ние!","say":"Делай домашнее задание!","gloss":"дома́шнее зада́ние = homework"},{"ru":"Listen to me carefully.","en":"<b>Слу́шай</b> меня́ внима́тельно.","say":"Слушай меня внимательно.","gloss":"внима́тельно = carefully"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чита́ть э́то!","right":"Чита́й э́то!","why":"The infinitive <b>чита́ть</b> is the dictionary word, not a command. Build from они́ чита́<b>ют</b> → <b>чита́й</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“Listen to the music!” — «___ му́зыку!» (слу́шать → они́ слу́шают)","opts":["Слу́шать","Слу́шай","Слу́шают"],"answer":1,"explain":"они́ слу́шают → drop -ют, vowel stem → add -й: <b>Слу́шай!</b>"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Do your homework!”</b>","want":"Де́лай дома́шнее зада́ние","answer":"Де́лай дома́шнее зада́ние"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Stem ends in a consonant → <em>-и</em>","title":"<em>Скажи́! Иди́! Купи́!</em> — the bright <em>-и</em> command","paras":["When you chop the ending and the stem ends in a <b>consonant</b>, add <b>-и́</b>. <b>говоря́т → говор- → говори́!</b> (speak!), <b>иду́т → ид- → иди́!</b> (go!), <b>ку́пят → куп- → купи́!</b> (buy!). The stress lands on that <b>-и́</b>.","Watch the trap: you build from the present, not the infinitive, so <b>сказа́ть</b> (“to say”) does NOT give “сказа́й”. Its present is <b>ска́жут</b> / я <b>скажу́</b> → <b>скажи́!</b> Same road: <b>писа́ть → пи́шут → пиши́!</b>, <b>смотре́ть → смо́трят → смотри́!</b>","Say them like small bright arrows, stress on the tail: <b>скажи́! иди́! купи́! пиши́! смотри́!</b>"],"audio":"When you chop the ending and the stem ends in a consonant, add stressed -i. Govoryat, govor, govori! Speak! Idut, id, idi! Go! Kupyat, kup, kupi! Buy! The stress lands on that -i. Watch the trap: you build from the present, not the infinitive, so skazat, to say, does not give skazay. Its present is skazhut, ya skazhu, so skazhi! Same road: pishut, pishi; smotryat, smotri. Say them like small bright arrows, stress on the tail: skazhi, idi, kupi, pishi, smotri.","table":{"rows":[["они́ говоря́т → говори́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они говорят, говори\">🔊</button>","they speak → speak!"],["они́ иду́т → иди́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они идут, иди\">🔊</button>","they go → go!"],["они́ ска́жут → скажи́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они скажут, скажи\">🔊</button>","they say → say / tell!"],["они́ пи́шут → пиши́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они пишут, пиши\">🔊</button>","they write → write!"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Say it in Russian!","en":"<b>Скажи́</b> э́то по-ру́сски!","say":"Скажи это по-русски!"},{"ru":"Go straight, then to the right.","en":"<b>Иди́</b> пря́мо, пото́м напра́во.","say":"Иди прямо, потом направо."},{"ru":"Buy some bread, please.","en":"<b>Купи́</b> хлеб, пожа́луйста.","say":"Купи хлеб, пожалуйста."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Сказа́й мне!","right":"Скажи́ мне!","why":"“сказа́й” is built from the infinitive — a dead end. The command grows from the present: ска́жут / скажу́ → <b>скажи́</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Go straight!” (идти́ → они́ иду́т)","opts":["Идёт пря́мо!","Иди́ пря́мо!","Идти́ пря́мо!"],"answer":1,"explain":"они́ иду́т → ид- (consonant) → add -и: <b>Иди́!</b> Идёт is “he goes”; идти́ is the infinitive."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. The stress marks are only for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “Buy some bread” (купи́ть → они́ ку́пят):","accept":["купи хлеб","купи хлеб."],"placeholder":"купи́…","explain":"они́ ку́пят → куп- → купи́ (stress on the tail): <b>Купи́ хлеб</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“Tell me where you are!” — «___ мне, где ты!» (сказа́ть → они́ ска́жут)","opts":["Сказа́й","Скажи́","Ска́жут"],"answer":1,"explain":"Not from the infinitive: ска́жут → скаж- → <b>скажи́!</b> Ска́жут is “they'll say”."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · A few take <em>-ь</em> — and <em>будь, дай</em>","title":"<em>Будь!</em> <em>Дай!</em> — short commands worth keeping whole","paras":["A smaller group ends the stem in a consonant but keeps the stress on the <b>stem</b>, not the tail. These take a soft <b>-ь</b> instead of -и. The one you'll use most is <b>будь!</b> — “be!” (from <b>быть</b>): <b>Будь здоро́в!</b> “Be well!”, <b>Будь до́ма в семь.</b> “Be home at seven.”","Two everyday commands are simplest just learned whole: <b>дай!</b> “give!” (from <b>дать</b>) and its polite <b>да́йте!</b> — <b>Дай мне ру́ку.</b> “Give me your hand.” You already met <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста</b> back in A1; now you know it's a command.","Don't over-worry the <b>-ь</b> group — it's small. Your two workhorses stay <b>-й</b> (after a vowel) and <b>-и</b> (after a consonant)."],"audio":"A smaller group ends the stem in a consonant but keeps the stress on the stem, not the tail. These take a soft soft-sign instead of -i. The one you'll use most is bud! Be! From byt. Bud zdorov, be well; bud doma v sem, be home at seven. Two everyday commands are simplest just learned whole: day, give, from dat, and its polite dayte. Day mne ruku, give me your hand. You already met dayte pozhaluysta back in A1; now you know it's a command. Don't over-worry the soft-sign group — it's small. Your two workhorses stay -y after a vowel and -i after a consonant.","table":{"rows":[["будь / бу́дьте <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"будь, будьте\">🔊</button>","be!"],["дай / да́йте <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дай, дайте\">🔊</button>","give!"],["не забу́дь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не забудь\">🔊</button>","don't forget!"],["встань / вста́ньте <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"встань, встаньте\">🔊</button>","stand up! / get up!"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Be well! (a warm goodbye)","en":"<b>Будь</b> здоро́в!","say":"Будь здоров!","gloss":"здоро́в = well, healthy (m)"},{"ru":"Give me your hand.","en":"<b>Дай</b> мне ру́ку.","say":"Дай мне руку."},{"ru":"Don't forget your keys!","en":"<b>Не забу́дь</b> ключи́!","say":"Не забудь ключи!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Бу́ди здоро́в!","right":"Будь здоро́в!","why":"<b>быть</b> keeps its stress on the stem, so the command is the short <b>будь</b> — a soft -ь, never -и."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Be well!” —","opts":["Будь здоро́в!","Быть здоро́в!","Бу́ди здоро́в!"],"answer":0,"explain":"быть → the short stem-stressed command <b>будь</b>. Быть is the infinitive; “бу́ди” isn't a form here."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Give me your hand.”</b>","want":"Дай мне ру́ку","answer":"Дай мне ру́ку"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Add <em>-те</em> to be polite","title":"<em>Скажи́те, пожа́луйста</em> — the same command, softened","paras":["To speak to a stranger, to someone older, or to more than one person, add <b>-те</b> to the command: <b>скажи́ → скажи́те</b>, <b>иди́ → иди́те</b>, <b>дай → да́йте</b>, <b>чита́й → чита́йте</b>, <b>будь → бу́дьте</b>. Add <b>пожа́луйста</b> and it becomes a warm request.","One thing matters here: this <b>-те</b> is <b>politeness</b>, not me switching to “вы” with you. I still speak to you as <b>ты</b>. When Emma stops a stranger, though, she says <b>Скажи́те, пожа́луйста…</b> — the grown-up, polite way to ask a person you don't know.","So one verb gives you two levels: <b>Купи́ хлеб</b> to a friend, <b>Купи́те, пожа́луйста</b> to be formal; <b>Скажи́</b> to Иван, <b>Скажи́те</b> to a stranger in the street."],"audio":"To speak to a stranger, to someone older, or to more than one person, add -te to the command: skazhi, skazhite; idi, idite; day, dayte; chitay, chitayte; bud, budte. Add pozhaluysta and it becomes a warm request. One thing matters here: this -te is politeness, not me switching to vy with you. I still speak to you as ty. When Emma stops a stranger, though, she says skazhite pozhaluysta — the grown-up, polite way to ask a person you don't know. So one verb gives you two levels: kupi khleb to a friend, kupite pozhaluysta to be formal; skazhi to Ivan, skazhite to a stranger in the street.","table":{"rows":[["скажи́ → скажи́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"скажи, скажите\">🔊</button>","tell (a friend / politely)"],["иди́ → иди́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"иди, идите\">🔊</button>","go"],["дай → да́йте <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дай, дайте\">🔊</button>","give"],["чита́й → чита́йте <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читай, читайте\">🔊</button>","read"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Excuse me, could you tell me — where is Red Square?","en":"Извини́те, <b>скажи́те</b>, пожа́луйста, где Кра́сная пло́щадь?","say":"Извините, скажите, пожалуйста, где Красная площадь?"},{"ru":"Come on in, please!","en":"<b>Проходи́те</b>, пожа́луйста!","say":"Проходите, пожалуйста!","gloss":"проходи́те = come on in (polite)"},{"ru":"Give me a cup of tea, please.","en":"<b>Да́йте</b>, пожа́луйста, ча́шку ча́я.","say":"Дайте, пожалуйста, чашку чая."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"“-те means Ми́ра is now using вы with me.”","right":"-те is politeness to a stranger; Ми́ра still says ты.","why":"The polite <b>-те</b> lives inside the <b>material</b> Emma uses with strangers. I keep talking to you as <b>ты</b>: <b>чита́й, де́лай, скажи́</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A stranger in the street — how do you ask “tell me, please”?","opts":["Скажи́, пожа́луйста","Скажи́те, пожа́луйста","Сказа́ть, пожа́луйста"],"answer":1,"explain":"Polite <b>-те</b>: <b>Скажи́те, пожа́луйста</b>. Скажи́ is fine to a friend; сказа́ть is the infinitive."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Come on in, please!”</b> (проходи́ть — polite)","want":"Проходи́те пожа́луйста","answer":"Проходи́те пожа́луйста"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and ask it out loud.","target":"Скажи́те, пожа́луйста, где метро́?","sub":"stop a passer-by and ask the way to the metro","want":"скажите пожалуйста где метро"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “Give me, please” politely (дать):","accept":["дайте пожалуйста","дайте, пожалуйста","дайте пожалуйста."],"placeholder":"да́йте…","explain":"Polite command of дать: <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста</b>."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is looking for a bookshop near the metro. She stops a passer-by — then relays the tip to Ivan by phone. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Извини́те, скажи́те, пожа́луйста, где кни́жный магази́н?","ru":"Excuse me, could you tell me where the bookshop is? (кни́жный магази́н = bookshop)"},{"who":"B","en":"Иди́те пря́мо, пото́м напра́во. Э́то совсе́м ря́дом.","ru":"Go straight, then right. It's very close by. (ря́дом = nearby)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо! А где здесь метро́?","ru":"Thank you! And where's the metro around here?"},{"who":"B","en":"Метро́ вон там. Купи́ биле́т в ка́ссе.","ru":"The metro's over there. Buy a ticket at the ticket office. (ка́сса = ticket office)"},{"who":"A","en":"Большо́е спаси́бо! Вы о́чень до́брый.","ru":"Thank you very much! You're very kind."},{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, слу́шай: иди́ пря́мо, пото́м напра́во — и там кни́жный.","ru":"Ivan, listen: go straight, then right — and the bookshop's there."},{"who":"B","en":"Отли́чно! Купи́ мне ту но́вую кни́гу, пожа́луйста.","ru":"Great! Buy me that new book, please. (ту = that, feminine)"},{"who":"A","en":"Хорошо́! Скажи́ то́лько, каку́ю и́менно.","ru":"Okay! Just tell me which one exactly. (каку́ю и́менно = which one exactly)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for commands and directions. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"скажи́ / скажи́те","ru":"say, tell (command)","ex":"Скажи́ пра́вду."},{"en":"иди́ / иди́те","ru":"go (command)","ex":"Иди́ сюда́!"},{"en":"купи́ / купи́те","ru":"buy (command)","ex":"Купи́ молоко́."},{"en":"де́лай / де́лайте","ru":"do (command)","ex":"Де́лай, как я."},{"en":"слу́шай / слу́шайте","ru":"listen (command)","ex":"Слу́шай внима́тельно."},{"en":"смотри́ / смотри́те","ru":"look (command)","ex":"Смотри́ напра́во!"},{"en":"будь / бу́дьте","ru":"be (command)","ex":"Будь здоро́в!"},{"en":"дай / да́йте","ru":"give (command)","ex":"Дай мне ру́ку."},{"en":"пря́мо","ru":"straight (ahead)","ex":"Иди́те пря́мо."},{"en":"напра́во","ru":"to the right","ex":"Поверни́те напра́во."},{"en":"нале́во","ru":"to the left","ex":"Метро́ нале́во."},{"en":"ря́дом","ru":"nearby, close by","ex":"Магази́н совсе́м ря́дом."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian command to its English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Скажи́те, пожа́луйста","Tell me, please"],["Иди́те пря́мо","Go straight"],["Купи́ хлеб","Buy some bread"],["Будь здоро́в","Be well"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on today's commands. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“Read this!” (чита́ть) —","opts":["Чита́ть э́то!","Чита́й э́то!","Чита́ю э́то!"],"answer":1},{"q":"To a stranger: “Tell me, please” —","opts":["Скажи́те, пожа́луйста","Скажи́, пожа́луйста","Сказа́ть, пожа́луйста"],"answer":0},{"q":"The command from сказа́ть (they say = ска́жут) is —","opts":["сказа́й","скажи́","ска́жете"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Go straight!” —","opts":["Идти́ пря́мо!","Идёт пря́мо!","Иди́ пря́мо!"],"answer":2},{"q":"The polite <b>-те</b> means —","opts":["Ми́ра is now using вы with you","it's a polite command; Ми́ра still says ты","it is the past tense"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A quick note from Emma, telling Ivan the way to the café. Read it aloud — every command is one you can now build.","title":"Скажи́ ему́ доро́гу","sentences":["Ива́н, слу́шай меня́ внима́тельно.","Иди́ пря́мо до магази́на, пото́м поверни́ напра́во.","Купи́ там ко́фе и приходи́ к нам.","И не забу́дь ключи́!","Скажи́ то́лько, когда́ ты бу́дешь ря́дом."],"translation":"Ivan, listen to me carefully. Go straight as far as the shop, then turn right. Buy some coffee there and come to us. And don't forget the keys! Just tell me when you'll be nearby."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to give directions.","prompt":"A friend is coming to visit you. In Russian, give three or four commands: how to get to you, and one thing to buy or bring. Use the imperative.","hint":"Build each command from the “they” form: <b>иду́т → иди́</b>, <b>ку́пят → купи́</b>, <b>чита́ют → чита́й</b>. Add <b>-те</b> only if you want to be formal 🙂","example":"Слу́шай! Иди́ пря́мо, пото́м нале́во. Купи́ ко́фе в магази́не. И не забу́дь торт! Скажи́, когда́ ты бу́дешь ря́дом."}},"23":{"day":23,"week":"04","themeRu":"Don't be late!","themeEn":"Не опа́здывай!","intro":"Yesterday you learned to give a command. Today you learn to aim it. Russian makes you choose the <b>aspect</b> even here: perfective for one clean, finished action — <b>Купи́ хлеб!</b> — and imperfective for an invitation or a habit — <b>Входи́те! Чита́й ка́ждый день!</b> And a real twist waits inside the little word <b>не</b>.","introAudio":"Yesterday you learned to give a command. Today you learn to aim it. Russian makes you choose the aspect even here: perfective for one clean, finished action — kupi khleb! — and imperfective for an invitation or a habit — vkhodite! chitay kazhdy den! And a real twist waits inside the little word nye.","goals":["Command one finished action with the <b>perfective</b>: <b>Купи́! Позвони́! Прочита́й!</b>","Invite and encourage with the <b>imperfective</b>: <b>Входи́те! Отдыха́й! Звони́!</b>","Say “don't…” with the <b>imperfective</b>: <b>Не опа́здывай! Не забыва́й!</b>","Feel the twist: <b>Смотри́, не опозда́й!</b> — a one-off warning, not a rule"],"learned":["Commanded one finished action with the perfective: <b>Купи́! Позвони́! Прочита́й!</b>","Invited and encouraged with the imperfective: <b>Входи́те! Отдыха́й! Звони́!</b>","Said “don't…” with the imperfective: <b>Не опа́здывай! Не забыва́й!</b>","Felt the twist: <b>Смотри́, не опозда́й!</b> — a one-off warning, not a rule"],"review":{"intro":"A minute on the machinery behind today: aspect (the perfective vs the imperfective), yesterday's commands, and a case that keeps earning its keep.","introAudio":"A minute on the machinery behind today: aspect, the perfective versus the imperfective, yesterday's commands, and a case that keeps earning its keep.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is aspect and cases you already own.","q":"“I'll read the whole book (and finish it).” —","opts":["Я бу́ду чита́ть кни́гу","Я прочита́ю кни́гу","Я чита́ю кни́гу"],"answer":1,"explain":"One finished result in the future = perfective <b>прочита́ю</b>. Бу́ду чита́ть is the ongoing process; чита́ю is present."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Tell me, please, where the metro is.”</b> (polite command)","want":"Скажи́те пожа́луйста где метро́","answer":"Скажи́те пожа́луйста где метро́"},{"type":"choice","q":"“I always help my sister.” — «Я всегда́ помога́ю ___.»","opts":["сестре́","сестры́","сестро́й"],"answer":0,"explain":"помога́ть takes the <b>dative</b>: помога́ю <b>сестре́</b> (to whom). сестры́ is genitive, сестро́й instrumental."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · One finished action → perfective","title":"<em>Купи́ хлеб!</em> — do it once, do it fully","paras":["When you want a single, specific, <b>completed</b> result, reach for the perfective. <b>Купи́ хлеб</b> (buy the bread — one shopping act), <b>Сде́лай э́то</b> (get it done), <b>Прочита́й письмо́</b> (read the letter through), <b>Позвони́ мне ве́чером</b> (call me tonight — one call).","You build these exactly as yesterday — from the <b>они́</b> form of the <b>perfective</b> verb: ку́пят → <b>купи́</b>, сде́лают → <b>сде́лай</b>, напи́шут → <b>напиши́</b>, позвоня́т → <b>позвони́</b>.","The feeling is “this thing, done”. A perfective command points at one clean outcome you want to see finished."],"audio":"When you want a single, specific, completed result, reach for the perfective. Kupi khleb, buy the bread, one shopping act; sdelay eto, get it done; prochitay pismo, read the letter through; pozvoni mne vecherom, call me tonight, one call. You build these exactly as yesterday — from the oni form of the perfective verb: kupyat, kupi; sdelayut, sdelay; napishut, napishi; pozvonyat, pozvoni. The feeling is: this thing, done. A perfective command points at one clean outcome you want to see finished.","table":{"rows":[["они́ ку́пят → купи́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они купят, купи\">🔊</button>","buy! (once)"],["они́ сде́лают → сде́лай <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они сделают, сделай\">🔊</button>","do it! (get it done)"],["они́ напи́шут → напиши́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они напишут, напиши\">🔊</button>","write it! (down)"],["они́ позвоня́т → позвони́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они позвонят, позвони\">🔊</button>","call! (once)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Buy some bread, please.","en":"<b>Купи́</b> хлеб, пожа́луйста.","say":"Купи хлеб, пожалуйста."},{"ru":"Call me tonight.","en":"<b>Позвони́</b> мне ве́чером.","say":"Позвони мне вечером."},{"ru":"Write down the address.","en":"<b>Напиши́</b> а́дрес.","say":"Напиши адрес."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чита́й э́то письмо́ сейча́с!","right":"Прочита́й э́то письмо́!","why":"One specific letter, read through to the end → perfective <b>прочита́й</b>. Imperfective чита́й asks for the process, as if you should sit reading it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“Call me tonight (one call).” —","opts":["Звони́ мне ве́чером","Позвони́ мне ве́чером","Звони́ть мне ве́чером"],"answer":1,"explain":"One finished call = perfective <b>позвони́</b>. Звони́ (imperfective) means “keep calling / stay in touch”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Buy some bread, please.”</b>","want":"Купи́ хлеб пожа́луйста","answer":"Купи́ хлеб пожа́луйста"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Invitation or process → imperfective","title":"<em>Входи́те! Чита́й ка́ждый день!</em>","paras":["The imperfective command opens a door: it invites, encourages, or asks for something <b>ongoing or repeated</b>. The warmest one lives at the threshold — <b>Входи́те! Проходи́те!</b> (Come in! Come on through!) — always imperfective, open-ended and kind.","Use it for habits too: <b>Чита́й ка́ждый день</b> (read every day), <b>Отдыха́й</b> (rest, take it easy), <b>Звони́</b> (keep in touch). No single finish line — just the door held open.","A note on the welcome: the perfective <b>войди́те</b> does exist, but it sounds like a curt, one-time order (a boss, a soldier). To a guest you say the warm imperfective <b>входи́те</b>."],"audio":"The imperfective command opens a door: it invites, encourages, or asks for something ongoing or repeated. The warmest one lives at the threshold — vkhodite! prokhodite! Come in! Come on through! Always imperfective, open-ended and kind. Use it for habits too: chitay kazhdy den, read every day; otdykhay, rest, take it easy; zvoni, keep in touch. No single finish line — just the door held open. A note on the welcome: the perfective voydite does exist, but it sounds like a curt, one-time order. To a guest you say the warm imperfective vkhodite.","table":{"rows":[["входи́те / проходи́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"входите, проходите\">🔊</button>","come in! come on through!"],["чита́й ка́ждый день <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читай каждый день\">🔊</button>","read every day"],["отдыха́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"отдыхай\">🔊</button>","rest, take it easy"],["звони́! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"звони\">🔊</button>","call / keep in touch!"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Come in, please!","en":"<b>Входи́те</b>, пожа́луйста!","say":"Входите, пожалуйста!"},{"ru":"Read a little every day.","en":"<b>Чита́й</b> немно́го ка́ждый день.","say":"Читай немного каждый день.","gloss":"немно́го = a little"},{"ru":"Call me, don't disappear!","en":"<b>Звони́</b>, не пропада́й!","say":"Звони, не пропадай!","gloss":"не пропада́й = stay in touch (don't vanish)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Войди́те, пожа́луйста! (to a guest)","right":"Входи́те, пожа́луйста!","why":"A warm welcome uses the imperfective <b>входи́те</b>. The perfective войди́те sounds like a curt one-off order."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A guest is at your door. You say —","opts":["Входи́те, пожа́луйста!","Войди́те, пожа́луйста!","Входи́ть, пожа́луйста!"],"answer":0,"explain":"Invitation → imperfective <b>Входи́те</b>. Войди́те is a curt one-off order; входи́ть is the infinitive."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are only for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “read every day” (чита́ть — a habit):","accept":["читай каждый день","читай каждый день."],"placeholder":"чита́й…","explain":"A repeated habit → imperfective <b>Чита́й ка́ждый день</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Come on in, please!”</b> (проходи́ть)","want":"Проходи́те пожа́луйста","answer":"Проходи́те пожа́луйста"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · “Don't…” → imperfective","title":"<em>Не опа́здывай!</em> — negatives go imperfective","paras":["Here's the twist English never warns you about. A prohibition — “don't…” — almost always takes the <b>imperfective</b>, no matter which aspect you'd use to say “do it”. <b>Не опа́здывай!</b> (don't be late), <b>Не забыва́й меня́!</b> (don't forget me), <b>Не покупа́й э́то!</b> (don't buy that).","So the pair flips. <b>Купи́ хлеб</b> (buy bread — perfective) becomes <b>Не покупа́й хлеб</b> (don't buy bread — imperfective). You don't simply stick <b>не</b> in front of купи́.","The rule of thumb: to forbid an action, name it as a <b>process</b> and wave it off — that's the imperfective's job."],"audio":"Here's the twist English never warns you about. A prohibition — don't — almost always takes the imperfective, no matter which aspect you'd use to say do it. Ne opazdyvay! Don't be late. Ne zabyvay menya! Don't forget me. Ne pokupay eto! Don't buy that. So the pair flips. Kupi khleb, buy bread, perfective, becomes ne pokupay khleb, don't buy bread, imperfective. You don't simply stick ne in front of kupi. The rule of thumb: to forbid an action, name it as a process and wave it off — that's the imperfective's job.","table":{"rows":[["Не опа́здывай! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не опаздывай\">🔊</button>","Don't be late!"],["Не забыва́й меня́! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не забывай меня\">🔊</button>","Don't forget me!"],["Не покупа́й э́то! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не покупай это\">🔊</button>","Don't buy that!"],["Не говори́ так! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не говори так\">🔊</button>","Don't talk like that!"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Don't be late tomorrow!","en":"<b>Не опа́здывай</b> за́втра!","say":"Не опаздывай завтра!"},{"ru":"Don't buy that — it's expensive.","en":"<b>Не покупа́й</b> э́то — э́то до́рого.","say":"Не покупай это — это дорого.","gloss":"до́рого = (it's) expensive"},{"ru":"Don't forget me!","en":"<b>Не забыва́й</b> меня́!","say":"Не забывай меня!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Не купи́ э́то.","right":"Не покупа́й э́то.","why":"A prohibition takes the <b>imperfective</b>: <b>не покупа́й</b>. Не + perfective (не купи́) isn't an ordinary “don't” — it slides into a warning (next rule)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Don't be late!” (the standing rule) —","opts":["Не опозда́й!","Не опа́здывай!","Не опа́здывать!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A prohibition → imperfective <b>Не опа́здывай!</b> Не опозда́й is the one-off warning; не опа́здывать is the infinitive."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Don't forget me!”</b>","want":"Не забыва́й меня́","answer":"Не забыва́й меня́"},{"type":"choice","q":"“Buy bread” is <b>Купи́ хлеб</b>. So “Don't buy bread” is —","opts":["Не купи́ хлеб","Не покупа́й хлеб","Не покупа́ть хлеб"],"answer":1,"explain":"The negative flips to imperfective: <b>Не покупа́й хлеб</b>. You don't just add не to купи́."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · <em>Смотри́, не…</em> — mind you don't","title":"<em>Не купи́!</em> isn't “don't buy” — it's a warning","paras":["So what does <b>не + perfective</b> mean, if not an ordinary “don't”? It's a <b>warning</b> about one accidental slip — “mind you don't…”, “be careful not to…”. Russians often flag it with <b>смотри́</b> (“watch out”): <b>Смотри́, не опозда́й!</b> (careful you don't end up late!), <b>Смотри́, не забу́дь!</b> (mind you don't forget!).","Feel the two side by side. <b>Не опа́здывай</b> (imperfective) is the standing rule — don't make a habit of being late. <b>Смотри́, не опозда́й</b> (perfective) is the anxious one-off — this once, don't slip.","So the same little <b>не</b> aims two different arrows: with the imperfective it forbids in general, with the perfective it warns about a single moment."],"audio":"So what does ne plus perfective mean, if not an ordinary don't? It's a warning about one accidental slip — mind you don't, be careful not to. Russians often flag it with smotri, watch out: smotri, ne opozday! Careful you don't end up late. Smotri, ne zabud! Mind you don't forget. Feel the two side by side. Ne opazdyvay, imperfective, is the standing rule — don't make a habit of being late. Smotri, ne opozday, perfective, is the anxious one-off — this once, don't slip. So the same little ne aims two arrows: with the imperfective it forbids in general, with the perfective it warns about a single moment.","table":{"rows":[["Не опа́здывай. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не опаздывай\">🔊</button>","Don't be late. (the rule)"],["Смотри́, не опозда́й! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"смотри, не опоздай\">🔊</button>","Mind you don't be late! (this once)"],["Не забыва́й. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не забывай\">🔊</button>","Don't forget. (in general)"],["Смотри́, не забу́дь! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"смотри, не забудь\">🔊</button>","Careful you don't forget!"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Careful you don't miss the train!","en":"Смотри́, <b>не опозда́й</b> на по́езд!","say":"Смотри, не опоздай на поезд!","gloss":"на по́езд = for / onto the train"},{"ru":"Mind you don't forget the tickets!","en":"Смотри́, <b>не забу́дь</b> биле́ты!","say":"Смотри, не забудь билеты!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Не забу́дь меня́! (meaning “don't forget me, ever”)","right":"Не забыва́й меня́!","why":"For the ongoing plea use imperfective <b>не забыва́й</b>. Не забу́дь is the one-off warning: “mind you don't forget — now.”"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You're worried your friend might miss the train this once. You say —","opts":["Не опа́здывай!","Смотри́, не опозда́й!","Не опа́здывать!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A one-off worry → не + perfective: <b>Смотри́, не опозда́й!</b> Не опа́здывай is the general rule."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Не опа́здывай, пожа́луйста!","sub":"gently remind a friend not to be late","want":"не опаздывай пожалуйста"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “Don't forget me!” (an ongoing plea):","accept":["не забывай меня","не забывай меня."],"placeholder":"не забыва́й…","explain":"Ongoing plea → imperfective <b>Не забыва́й меня́</b>."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma arrives at Lena's flat for dinner. Lena is all warmth and small instructions — come in, relax, and one thing to sort out for tomorrow. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, приве́т! Входи́, проходи́!","ru":"Emma, hi! Come in, come on through!"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо! Ой, как у вас хорошо́.","ru":"Thanks! Oh, how nice it is at your place."},{"who":"B","en":"Дай мне ку́ртку. И отдыха́й, ты уста́ла.","ru":"Give me your jacket. And relax, you're tired. (ку́ртка = jacket; уста́ла = tired, of a woman)"},{"who":"A","en":"Что купи́ть на за́втра? Хлеб?","ru":"What should I buy for tomorrow? Bread?"},{"who":"B","en":"Купи́ хлеб и молоко́. Но не покупа́й торт — я сама́ сде́лаю.","ru":"Buy bread and milk. But don't buy a cake — I'll make one myself. (сама́ = (I) myself)"},{"who":"A","en":"Хорошо́! А у́жин за́втра в семь?","ru":"Okay! And dinner tomorrow is at seven? (у́жин = dinner)"},{"who":"B","en":"Да. Смотри́, не опозда́й! Ива́н всегда́ опа́здывает.","ru":"Yes. Mind you're not late! Ivan is always late."},{"who":"A","en":"Я не опозда́ю! И не забу́ду хлеб.","ru":"I won't be late! And I won't forget the bread."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for inviting, asking and gently warning. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"позвони́","ru":"call! (one call)","ex":"Позвони́ мне ве́чером."},{"en":"напиши́","ru":"write (it) down!","ex":"Напиши́ мне а́дрес."},{"en":"входи́ / входи́те","ru":"come in!","ex":"Входи́те, пожа́луйста!"},{"en":"проходи́ / проходи́те","ru":"come on through!","ex":"Проходи́ в ко́мнату."},{"en":"приходи́ / приходи́те","ru":"come over! (invitation)","ex":"Приходи́ к нам ве́чером."},{"en":"отдыха́й / отдыха́йте","ru":"rest, relax!","ex":"Отдыха́й, ты уста́л."},{"en":"не опа́здывай","ru":"don't be late!","ex":"Не опа́здывай на рабо́ту."},{"en":"не забыва́й","ru":"don't forget! (keep in mind)","ex":"Не забыва́й друзе́й."},{"en":"смотри́","ru":"watch out; mind you…","ex":"Смотри́, не опозда́й!"},{"en":"опозда́ть","ru":"to be late (once)","ex":"Я не хочу́ опозда́ть."},{"en":"у́жин","ru":"dinner, supper","ex":"У́жин в семь часо́в."},{"en":"молоко́","ru":"milk","ex":"Не забу́дь молоко́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to its English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Входи́те, пожа́луйста","Come in, please"],["Не опа́здывай!","Don't be late!"],["Позвони́ мне ве́чером","Call me tonight"],["Смотри́, не забу́дь!","Mind you don't forget!"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on aspect in commands. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"One call tonight: “Call me tonight.” —","opts":["Звони́ мне ве́чером","Позвони́ мне ве́чером","Звони́ть мне ве́чером"],"answer":1},{"q":"A guest at the door: “Come in, please!” —","opts":["Входи́те, пожа́луйста!","Войди́те, пожа́луйста!","Входи́ть, пожа́луйста!"],"answer":0},{"q":"“Don't be late!” (the standing rule) —","opts":["Не опозда́й!","Не опа́здывать!","Не опа́здывай!"],"answer":2},{"q":"“Buy bread” is Купи́ хлеб. So “Don't buy bread” is —","opts":["Не купи́ хлеб","Не покупа́й хлеб","Не покупа́ть хлеб"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Смотри́, не опозда́й!» means —","opts":["the general rule: never be late","a one-off warning: careful you don't end up late","you are already late"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Lena texts Emma the plan for tomorrow. Read it aloud — every command is one you can now aim.","title":"Не опозда́й на у́жин!","sentences":["Э́мма, приходи́ к нам за́втра в семь.","Купи́ хлеб и молоко́, но не покупа́й торт.","Смотри́, не опозда́й — у нас у́жин ро́вно в семь.","И не забу́дь ключи́!","Позвони́, когда́ бу́дешь ря́дом, и отдыха́й сего́дня."],"translation":"Emma, come over to us tomorrow at seven. Buy bread and milk, but don't buy a cake. Mind you're not late — dinner is at seven sharp. And don't forget your keys! Call when you're nearby, and rest today."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to welcome and to warn.","prompt":"Write a short message to a friend visiting tomorrow. Invite them in, ask them to buy or bring one thing (perfective), tell them one thing NOT to do (imperfective), and add a gentle one-off warning with Смотри́, не…","hint":"Invitations and “don't…” go <b>imperfective</b> (<b>входи́, не опа́здывай</b>); one finished action goes <b>perfective</b> (<b>купи́, позвони́</b>). A one-off warning: <b>Смотри́, не опозда́й!</b> 🙂","example":"Приходи́ за́втра в семь! Купи́, пожа́луйста, торт. Но не опа́здывай и не забыва́й ключи́. Смотри́, не опозда́й — я о́чень жду!"}},"24":{"day":24,"week":"04","themeRu":"Bigger, better","themeEn":"Бо́льше, лу́чше","intro":"Emma has been in Moscow long enough to start comparing. Is it bigger than London? Colder? Is her Russian better than it was? Today you get the <b>comparative</b> — Russian's “bigger, better, older” — and here's the good news for an English speaker: it's usually just <b>one word</b>, with no “more” in front.","introAudio":"Emma has been in Moscow long enough to start comparing. Is it bigger than London? Colder? Is her Russian better than it was? Today you get the comparative — Russian's bigger, better, older — and here's the good news for an English speaker: it's usually just one word, with no more in front.","goals":["Make the comparative in one word: <b>интере́снее</b> (more interesting), <b>быстре́е</b> (faster)","Use the common irregulars: <b>бо́льше, ме́ньше, лу́чше, ху́же, ста́рше</b>","Say “than” two ways: bare genitive <b>ста́рше бра́та</b>, or <b>ста́рше, чем брат</b>","Drop the English “more”: never <b>бо́лее хорошо́</b> — just <b>лу́чше</b>"],"learned":["Made the comparative in one word: <b>интере́снее</b>, <b>быстре́е</b>","Used the common irregulars: <b>бо́льше, ме́ньше, лу́чше, ху́же, ста́рше</b>","Said “than” two ways: bare genitive <b>ста́рше бра́та</b>, or <b>ста́рше, чем брат</b>","Dropped the English “more”: <b>лу́чше</b>, not <b>бо́лее хорошо́</b>"],"review":{"intro":"A quick warm-up on the pieces today leans on: yesterday's warnings, the genitive (you'll reuse it for “than”), and adjective agreement.","introAudio":"A quick warm-up on the pieces today leans on: yesterday's warnings, the genitive, which you'll reuse for than, and adjective agreement.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All review, all yours already.","q":"A worried one-off: “Mind you don't forget the tickets!” —","opts":["Не забыва́й биле́ты!","Смотри́, не забу́дь биле́ты!","Не забы́ть биле́ты!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A single-slip warning = смотри́ + perfective: <b>Смотри́, не забу́дь!</b> Не забыва́й is the general rule; не забы́ть is the infinitive."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“the centre of Moscow”</b> (genitive)","want":"центр Москвы́","answer":"центр Москвы́"},{"type":"choice","q":"“an interesting book” — «Э́то ___ кни́га» (кни́га is a she-word)","opts":["интере́сный","интере́сная","интере́сное"],"answer":1,"explain":"The adjective agrees with the she-word кни́га: <b>интере́сная</b>. This same agreement matters again tomorrow."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Add <em>-ее</em> — one word, no “more”","title":"<em>интере́сный → интере́снее</em>","paras":["To compare, English adds a word (“more interesting”) or a tail (“faster”). Russian nearly always just adds the tail <b>-ее</b> to the adjective's stem — and then the word stops changing for gender or case: <b>интере́сный → интере́снее</b>, <b>краси́вый → краси́вее</b>, <b>бы́стрый → быстре́е</b>, <b>тёплый → тепле́е</b>.","One form covers both “more interesting” and “more interestingly”: <b>Э́та кни́га интере́снее</b> (this book is more interesting), <b>Он говори́т быстре́е</b> (he speaks faster).","Stress watch — this is A2, so it matters: short adjectives usually pull the stress onto the ending (<b>быстре́е, тепле́е, важне́е</b>); longer ones keep it where it was (<b>интере́снее, краси́вее</b>). Let your ear follow the acute."],"audio":"To compare, English adds a word, more interesting, or a tail, faster. Russian nearly always just adds the tail -eye to the adjective's stem — and then the word stops changing for gender or case: interesny, interesneye; krasivy, krasiveye; bystry, bystreye; tyoply, tepleye. One form covers both more interesting and more interestingly: eta kniga interesneye, this book is more interesting; on govorit bystreye, he speaks faster. Stress watch — this is A2, so it matters: short adjectives usually pull the stress onto the ending, bystreye, tepleye, vazhneye; longer ones keep it where it was, interesneye, krasiveye. Let your ear follow the acute.","table":{"rows":[["интере́сный → интере́снее <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"интересный, интереснее\">🔊</button>","interesting → more interesting"],["бы́стрый → быстре́е <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"быстрый, быстрее\">🔊</button>","fast → faster"],["тёплый → тепле́е <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тёплый, теплее\">🔊</button>","warm → warmer"],["холо́дный → холодне́е <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"холодный, холоднее\">🔊</button>","cold → colder"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This book is more interesting.","en":"Э́та кни́га <b>интере́снее</b>.","say":"Эта книга интереснее."},{"ru":"Today it's warmer than yesterday.","en":"Сего́дня <b>тепле́е</b>, чем вчера́.","say":"Сегодня теплее, чем вчера."},{"ru":"He speaks Russian faster now.","en":"Тепе́рь он говори́т по-ру́сски <b>быстре́е</b>.","say":"Теперь он говорит по-русски быстрее."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́та кни́га бо́лее интере́сная.","right":"Э́та кни́га интере́снее.","why":"In everyday speech the one-word <b>интере́снее</b> is what you reach for. “бо́лее интере́сная” is grammatical but bookish and heavier — save it for formal writing."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"Which is the everyday one-word comparative of интере́сный?","opts":["бо́лее интере́сный","интере́снее","интере́сная"],"answer":1,"explain":"The one-word comparative is <b>интере́снее</b>. “бо́лее интере́сный” is the heavy two-word form; интере́сная is just the feminine positive."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Today it's warmer than yesterday.”</b>","want":"Сего́дня тепле́е чем вчера́","answer":"Сего́дня тепле́е чем вчера́"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are only for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “faster” (from бы́стрый):","accept":["быстрее"],"placeholder":"быстре́е","explain":"бы́стрый → <b>быстре́е</b>, stress pulled onto the ending."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Learn these by heart: <em>бо́льше, лу́чше…</em>","title":"<em>бо́льше, ме́ньше, лу́чше, ху́же, ста́рше</em>","paras":["A handful of the most common comparatives don't take -ее — you simply know them. The top five: <b>большо́й → бо́льше</b> (bigger / more), <b>ма́ленький → ме́ньше</b> (smaller / less), <b>хоро́ший → лу́чше</b> (better), <b>плохо́й → ху́же</b> (worse), <b>ста́рый → ста́рше</b> (older).","Two of these double as quantity words: <b>бо́льше</b> = “more”, <b>ме́ньше</b> = “less / fewer” — <b>Купи́ бо́льше хле́ба</b> (buy more bread), <b>говори́ ме́ньше</b> (talk less).","These are the ones you'll say every single day, so let them become reflexes: <b>лу́чше, ху́же, бо́льше, ме́ньше, ста́рше</b>."],"audio":"A handful of the most common comparatives don't take -eye — you simply know them. The top five: bolshoy, bolshe, bigger or more; malenky, menshe, smaller or less; khoroshy, luchshe, better; plokhoy, khuzhe, worse; stary, starshe, older. Two of these double as quantity words: bolshe means more, menshe means less or fewer — kupi bolshe khleba, buy more bread; govori menshe, talk less. These are the ones you'll say every single day, so let them become reflexes: luchshe, khuzhe, bolshe, menshe, starshe.","table":{"rows":[["большо́й → бо́льше <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"большой, больше\">🔊</button>","big → bigger / more"],["ма́ленький → ме́ньше <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"маленький, меньше\">🔊</button>","small → smaller / less"],["хоро́ший → лу́чше <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хороший, лучше\">🔊</button>","good → better"],["плохо́й → ху́же <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"плохой, хуже\">🔊</button>","bad → worse"],["ста́рый → ста́рше <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"старый, старше\">🔊</button>","old → older"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Moscow is bigger than London.","en":"Москва́ <b>бо́льше</b>, чем Ло́ндон.","say":"Москва больше, чем Лондон."},{"ru":"I speak Russian better than before.","en":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски <b>лу́чше</b>, чем ра́ньше.","say":"Я говорю по-русски лучше, чем раньше."},{"ru":"The weather today is worse than yesterday.","en":"Пого́да сего́дня <b>ху́же</b>, чем вчера́.","say":"Погода сегодня хуже, чем вчера."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́то бо́лее хорошо́.","right":"Э́то лу́чше.","why":"“good → better” has its own single word: <b>лу́чше</b>. Never build “бо́лее хорошо́” — the irregular form replaces it entirely."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“better” (from хоро́ший) —","opts":["лу́чше","бо́лее хорошо́","хоро́шее"],"answer":0,"explain":"The irregular comparative is <b>лу́чше</b>. “бо́лее хорошо́” never happens; хоро́шее is just the neuter positive."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I speak better than before.”</b>","want":"Я говорю́ лу́чше чем ра́ньше","answer":"Я говорю́ лу́чше чем ра́ньше"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · “than” as the bare genitive","title":"<em>ста́рше бра́та</em> — “older than the brother”","paras":["Here's Russian's neatest trick for “than”. Put the thing you compare against straight into the <b>genitive</b> — with no extra word at all: <b>ста́рше бра́та</b> (older than the brother), <b>бо́льше Москвы́</b> (bigger than Moscow), <b>лу́чше меня́</b> (better than me).","You've made this genitive since week 1: брат → бра́т<b>а</b>, Москва́ → Москв<b>ы́</b>, я → <b>меня́</b>, ты → <b>тебя́</b>. The comparative just leans on the case you already own.","This works when “than” is followed by a single noun or pronoun: <b>Ива́н ста́рше меня́.</b> <b>Ле́на моло́же Ива́на.</b>"],"audio":"Here's Russian's neatest trick for than. Put the thing you compare against straight into the genitive — with no extra word at all: starshe brata, older than the brother; bolshe Moskvy, bigger than Moscow; luchshe menya, better than me. You've made this genitive since week one: brat, brata; Moskva, Moskvy; ya, menya; ty, tebya. The comparative just leans on the case you already own. This works when than is followed by a single noun or pronoun: Ivan starshe menya; Lena molozhe Ivana.","table":{"rows":[["ста́рше бра́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"старше брата\">🔊</button>","older than the brother"],["моло́же Ива́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"моложе Ивана\">🔊</button>","younger than Ivan"],["бо́льше Москвы́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"больше Москвы\">🔊</button>","bigger than Moscow"],["лу́чше меня́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лучше меня\">🔊</button>","better than me"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Ivan is older than me.","en":"Ива́н <b>ста́рше меня́</b>.","say":"Иван старше меня."},{"ru":"Lena is younger than Ivan.","en":"Ле́на <b>моло́же Ива́на</b>.","say":"Лена моложе Ивана."},{"ru":"London is older than Moscow.","en":"Ло́ндон <b>ста́рше Москвы́</b>.","say":"Лондон старше Москвы."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ива́н ста́рше я.","right":"Ива́н ста́рше меня́.","why":"After a comparative the bare “than” word steps into the <b>genitive</b>: я → <b>меня́</b>. The nominative я can't stand here."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“older than the brother” (bare genitive) —","opts":["ста́рше бра́та","ста́рше брат","ста́рше бра́ту"],"answer":0,"explain":"“than the brother” = genitive <b>бра́та</b>. брат is nominative; бра́ту is dative."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “older than me” (я → меня́):","accept":["старше меня"],"placeholder":"ста́рше…","explain":"Bare genitive of я is <b>меня́</b>: ста́рше меня́."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Lena is younger than Ivan.”</b>","want":"Ле́на моло́же Ива́на","answer":"Ле́на моло́же Ива́на"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Or say it with <em>чем</em>","title":"<em>ста́рше, чем брат</em> — the always-safe route","paras":["The genitive route is neat but has limits. The always-safe alternative is <b>чем</b> + the plain (nominative) form, with a comma before чем: <b>ста́рше, чем брат</b>, <b>бо́льше, чем Ло́ндон</b>, <b>лу́чше, чем я</b>.","Two things to know. First, <b>чем</b> is your friend when the second half isn't a simple noun: “warmer than yesterday” must be <b>тепле́е, чем вчера́</b> — you can't put вчера́ into the genitive. Second, after <b>чем</b> a pronoun stays nominative: <b>чем я</b>, <b>чем он</b> — not меня́.","So you have two roads to the same meaning: <b>ста́рше бра́та = ста́рше, чем брат</b>. When in doubt, take <b>чем</b>."],"audio":"The genitive route is neat but has limits. The always-safe alternative is chem plus the plain, nominative, form, with a comma before chem: starshe, chem brat; bolshe, chem London; luchshe, chem ya. Two things to know. First, chem is your friend when the second half isn't a simple noun: warmer than yesterday must be tepleye, chem vchera — you can't put vchera into the genitive. Second, after chem a pronoun stays nominative: chem ya, chem on, not menya. So you have two roads to the same meaning: starshe brata equals starshe, chem brat. When in doubt, take chem.","table":{"rows":[["ста́рше, чем брат <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"старше, чем брат\">🔊</button>","older than the brother"],["бо́льше, чем Ло́ндон <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"больше, чем Лондон\">🔊</button>","bigger than London"],["тепле́е, чем вчера́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"теплее, чем вчера\">🔊</button>","warmer than yesterday"],["лу́чше, чем я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лучше, чем я\">🔊</button>","better than me"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Moscow is bigger than London.","en":"Москва́ <b>бо́льше, чем Ло́ндон</b>.","say":"Москва больше, чем Лондон."},{"ru":"Today it's colder than yesterday.","en":"Сего́дня <b>холодне́е, чем вчера́</b>.","say":"Сегодня холоднее, чем вчера."},{"ru":"She reads better than me.","en":"Она́ чита́ет <b>лу́чше, чем я</b>.","say":"Она читает лучше, чем я."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"тепле́е вчера́","right":"тепле́е, чем вчера́","why":"вчера́ (“yesterday”) can't take a genitive ending, so the bare route is closed. Use <b>чем</b>: тепле́е, чем вчера́."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“warmer than yesterday” — which is right?","opts":["тепле́е, чем вчера́","тепле́е вчера́","бо́лее тепло́ вчера́"],"answer":0,"explain":"вчера́ can't go genitive, so use <b>чем</b>: тепле́е, чем вчера́ — one-word comparative + чем."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Москва́ бо́льше, чем Ло́ндон.","sub":"compare the two cities out loud","want":"москва больше чем лондон"},{"type":"choice","q":"After <b>чем</b>, “than me” is —","opts":["чем меня́","чем я","чем мне"],"answer":1,"explain":"After чем the pronoun stays nominative: <b>чем я</b>. (The genitive меня́ belongs to the other route: лу́чше меня́.)"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"On a bench above the river, Emma and Ivan compare their two cities — and, of course, each other. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну что, Москва́ бо́льше, чем Ло́ндон?","ru":"So then, is Moscow bigger than London?"},{"who":"A","en":"Мо́жет быть. Но Ло́ндон ста́рше Москвы́.","ru":"Maybe. But London is older than Moscow. (мо́жет быть = maybe)"},{"who":"B","en":"Зато́ у нас метро́ краси́вее!","ru":"But on the other hand our metro is more beautiful! (зато́ = but then, on the other hand)"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то пра́вда. И зимо́й здесь холодне́е, чем до́ма.","ru":"That's true. And in winter it's colder here than at home. (зимо́й = in winter)"},{"who":"B","en":"Ты говори́шь по-ру́сски лу́чше, чем ра́ньше.","ru":"You speak Russian better than before."},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо! А ты ста́рше меня́?","ru":"Thanks! And are you older than me?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, на два го́да. А Ле́на моло́же нас.","ru":"Yes, by two years. And Lena is younger than us. (на два го́да = by two years)"},{"who":"A","en":"Зна́чит, я моло́же всех вас! Ну, зато́ я тепе́рь москви́чка.","ru":"So I'm younger than all of you! Well, at least I'm a Muscovite now. (москви́чка = a Muscovite woman)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for comparing people, places and weather. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"бо́льше","ru":"bigger; more","ex":"Москва́ бо́льше Ло́ндона."},{"en":"ме́ньше","ru":"smaller; less","ex":"Э́та ко́мната ме́ньше."},{"en":"лу́чше","ru":"better","ex":"Здесь лу́чше, чем там."},{"en":"ху́же","ru":"worse","ex":"Пого́да ста́ла ху́же."},{"en":"ста́рше","ru":"older","ex":"Брат ста́рше меня́."},{"en":"моло́же","ru":"younger","ex":"Сестра́ моло́же меня́."},{"en":"интере́снее","ru":"more interesting","ex":"Э́та кни́га интере́снее."},{"en":"быстре́е","ru":"faster","ex":"Метро́ быстре́е авто́буса."},{"en":"тепле́е","ru":"warmer","ex":"Сего́дня тепле́е, чем вчера́."},{"en":"холодне́е","ru":"colder","ex":"Зимо́й здесь холодне́е."},{"en":"краси́вее","ru":"more beautiful","ex":"Метро́ в Москве́ краси́вее."},{"en":"чем","ru":"than","ex":"Он ста́рше, чем я."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to its English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Москва́ бо́льше Ло́ндона","Moscow is bigger than London"],["Он ста́рше меня́","He's older than me"],["Сего́дня тепле́е, чем вчера́","Today is warmer than yesterday"],["Я говорю́ лу́чше","I speak better"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on the comparative. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The one-word comparative of интере́сный is —","opts":["интере́снее","бо́лее интере́сный","интере́сная"],"answer":0},{"q":"“better” (from хоро́ший) —","opts":["бо́лее хорошо́","лу́чше","хоро́шее"],"answer":1},{"q":"“older than the brother” (bare genitive) —","opts":["ста́рше брат","ста́рше бра́ту","ста́рше бра́та"],"answer":2},{"q":"“warmer than yesterday” —","opts":["тепле́е, чем вчера́","тепле́е вчера́","бо́лее тепло́ вчера́"],"answer":0},{"q":"After <b>чем</b>, “than me” is —","opts":["чем меня́","чем мне","чем я"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma writes home, comparing her two cities. Read it aloud — every comparison is one you can now make.","title":"Москва́ и Ло́ндон","sentences":["Москва́ бо́льше, чем мой Ло́ндон, и здесь всё быстре́е.","Метро́ краси́вее, но зимо́й намно́го холодне́е.","Ива́н ста́рше меня́ на два го́да, а Ле́на моло́же.","Тепе́рь я говорю́ по-ру́сски лу́чше, чем ра́ньше.","Ду́маю, э́тот го́род интере́снее с ка́ждым днём."],"translation":"Moscow is bigger than my London, and everything here is faster. The metro is more beautiful, but in winter it's much colder. Ivan is older than me by two years, and Lena is younger. Now I speak Russian better than before. I think this city gets more interesting with every day."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to compare.","prompt":"Compare two places you know (two cities, or home and here) and two people (yourself and a friend or sibling). Use at least three comparatives and both ways of saying “than”.","hint":"One word, no “more”: <b>бо́льше, лу́чше, интере́снее</b>. For “than”, use the bare genitive (<b>ста́рше бра́та</b>) or <b>чем</b> + the plain form (<b>ста́рше, чем брат</b>) 🙂","example":"Москва́ бо́льше, чем мой го́род, и намно́го интере́снее. Здесь зимо́й холодне́е, но ле́том тепле́е. Мой брат ста́рше меня́ на три го́да, а я говорю́ по-ру́сски лу́чше, чем он."}},"25":{"day":25,"week":"04","themeRu":"The most…","themeEn":"Са́мый интере́сный","intro":"One winner, above all the rest — that's today. Comparing got you “bigger, better”; the <b>superlative</b> gives you “the biggest, the best”. It's wonderfully simple: put <b>са́мый</b> in front of the adjective. <b>Са́мый интере́сный</b> — the most interesting. The only catch, an easy one, is that <b>са́мый</b> dresses to match its noun.","introAudio":"One winner, above all the rest — that's today. Comparing got you bigger, better; the superlative gives you the biggest, the best. It's wonderfully simple: put samy in front of the adjective. Samy interesny — the most interesting. The only catch, an easy one, is that samy dresses to match its noun.","goals":["Say “the most…” with <b>са́мый</b> + adjective: <b>са́мый интере́сный го́род</b>","Agree <b>са́мый</b> with its noun: <b>са́мая, са́мое, са́мые</b> — and in cases too","Use the one-word best / worst: <b>лу́чший друг</b>, <b>ху́дший день</b>","Reach for <b>са́мое гла́вное</b> — “the main thing”"],"learned":["Said “the most…” with <b>са́мый</b> + adjective: <b>са́мый интере́сный го́род</b>","Agreed <b>са́мый</b> with its noun: <b>са́мая, са́мое, са́мые</b> — and in cases","Used the one-word best / worst: <b>лу́чший друг</b>, <b>ху́дший день</b>","Reached for <b>са́мое гла́вное</b> — “the main thing”"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on what today grows from: yesterday's comparatives, adjective agreement, and a place-in-the-prepositional so you're ready to say “in the biggest room”.","introAudio":"One minute on what today grows from: yesterday's comparatives, adjective agreement, and a place in the prepositional so you're ready to say in the biggest room.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of this is yours already.","q":"“better than before” —","opts":["лу́чше, чем ра́ньше","бо́лее хорошо́, чем ра́ньше","лу́чший, чем ра́ньше"],"answer":0,"explain":"The comparative of хоро́ший is the one word <b>лу́чше</b>: лу́чше, чем ра́ньше. “бо́лее хорошо́” isn't used; лу́чший is “best”, coming up today."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“in the centre of the city”</b> (в + prepositional, then genitive)","want":"в це́нтре го́рода","answer":"в це́нтре го́рода"},{"type":"choice","q":"“a big room” — «___ ко́мната» (кни́га-type she-word)","opts":["больша́я","большо́й","большо́е"],"answer":0,"explain":"The adjective agrees with the she-word ко́мната: <b>больша́я</b>. You'll add са́мая in front of exactly this in a moment."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · “The most…” = <em>са́мый</em> + adjective","title":"<em>са́мый интере́сный</em> — a two-word superlative","paras":["For “the most / the -est”, Russian doesn't bend the adjective — it sets <b>са́мый</b> (“the most”) in front of the ordinary adjective: <b>са́мый интере́сный</b> (the most interesting), <b>са́мый большо́й</b> (the biggest), <b>са́мый краси́вый</b> (the most beautiful).","Unlike the comparative (one frozen word), the superlative is two words, and both stay full adjectives: са́мый интере́сный <b>го́род</b>. There's no “more/most” juggling — <b>са́мый</b> does the whole job.","Hold the two levels side by side: <b>интере́снее</b> = more interesting (than something), <b>са́мый интере́сный</b> = the most interesting (of all)."],"audio":"For the most or the -est, Russian doesn't bend the adjective — it sets samy, the most, in front of the ordinary adjective: samy interesny, the most interesting; samy bolshoy, the biggest; samy krasivy, the most beautiful. Unlike the comparative, one frozen word, the superlative is two words, and both stay full adjectives: samy interesny gorod. There's no more-most juggling — samy does the whole job. Hold the two levels side by side: interesneye, more interesting than something; samy interesny, the most interesting of all.","table":{"rows":[["интере́снее → са́мый интере́сный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"интереснее, самый интересный\">🔊</button>","more interesting → the most interesting"],["большо́й → са́мый большо́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"большой, самый большой\">🔊</button>","big → the biggest"],["краси́вый → са́мый краси́вый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"красивый, самый красивый\">🔊</button>","beautiful → the most beautiful"],["ва́жный → са́мый ва́жный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"важный, самый важный\">🔊</button>","important → the most important"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This is the most interesting city.","en":"Э́то <b>са́мый интере́сный</b> го́род.","say":"Это самый интересный город."},{"ru":"There's the biggest park here.","en":"Здесь <b>са́мый большо́й</b> парк.","say":"Здесь самый большой парк."},{"ru":"Today is the most important day.","en":"Сего́дня <b>са́мый ва́жный</b> день.","say":"Сегодня самый важный день."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́то бо́лее са́мый интере́сный го́род.","right":"Э́то са́мый интере́сный го́род.","why":"<b>са́мый</b> by itself means “the most”. Don't stack бо́лее or another “most” on top — one са́мый is the whole superlative."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“the most interesting city” (го́род, a he-word) —","opts":["са́мый интере́сный го́род","бо́лее интере́сный го́род","са́мая интере́сная го́род"],"answer":0,"explain":"Superlative = <b>са́мый</b> + adjective, both matching the he-word го́род: <b>са́мый интере́сный го́род</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“This is the biggest park.”</b>","want":"Э́то са́мый большо́й парк","answer":"Э́то са́мый большо́й парк"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are only for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “the most important day” (день):","accept":["самый важный день"],"placeholder":"са́мый…","explain":"<b>са́мый ва́жный день</b> — са́мый + adjective + noun, all masculine."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · <em>са́мый</em> dresses to match its noun","title":"<em>са́мая, са́мое, са́мые</em> — and in every case","paras":["<b>са́мый</b> is itself an adjective, so it changes shape for its noun exactly like мой or но́вый. She-word → <b>са́мая</b>, it-word → <b>са́мое</b>, plural → <b>са́мые</b>: <b>са́мая краси́вая ста́нция</b>, <b>са́мое гла́вное сло́во</b>, <b>са́мые вку́сные пирожки́</b>.","And here's the A2 point. When the whole phrase goes into a case, <b>both</b> words take that case: “in the biggest room” = <b>в са́мой большо́й ко́мнате</b> — са́мой and большо́й both prepositional, both feminine.","So it's double agreement: match the gender, match the case. The adjective habits you built in A1 do all the work."],"audio":"Samy is itself an adjective, so it changes shape for its noun exactly like moy or novy. She-word, samaya; it-word, samoye; plural, samye: samaya krasivaya stantsiya; samoye glavnoye slovo; samye vkusnye pirozhki. And here's the A2 point. When the whole phrase goes into a case, both words take that case: in the biggest room is v samoy bolshoy komnate — samoy and bolshoy both prepositional, both feminine. So it's double agreement: match the gender, match the case. The adjective habits you built in A1 do all the work.","table":{"rows":[["са́мая краси́вая ста́нция <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"самая красивая станция\">🔊</button>","the most beautiful station (she-word)"],["са́мое гла́вное сло́во <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"самое главное слово\">🔊</button>","the most important word (it-word)"],["са́мые вку́сные пирожки́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"самые вкусные пирожки\">🔊</button>","the tastiest pies (plural)"],["в са́мой большо́й ко́мнате <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в самой большой комнате\">🔊</button>","in the biggest room (prep., she-word)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"This is the most beautiful metro station.","en":"Э́то <b>са́мая краси́вая</b> ста́нция метро́.","say":"Это самая красивая станция метро.","gloss":"ста́нция = station"},{"ru":"Lena lives in the biggest room.","en":"Ле́на живёт <b>в са́мой большо́й</b> ко́мнате.","say":"Лена живёт в самой большой комнате."},{"ru":"These are the tastiest pies.","en":"Э́то <b>са́мые вку́сные</b> пирожки́.","say":"Это самые вкусные пирожки.","gloss":"пирожки́ = little pies"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"в са́мый большо́й ко́мнате","right":"в са́мой большо́й ко́мнате","why":"Inside “in the room” everything goes prepositional and feminine: <b>са́мой большо́й ко́мнате</b>. са́мый has to change too, not only the noun."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“the most beautiful station” (ста́нция, a she-word) —","opts":["са́мый краси́вый","са́мая краси́вая","са́мое краси́вое"],"answer":1,"explain":"Match the she-word ста́нция: <b>са́мая краси́вая</b> ста́нция."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Lena lives in the biggest room.”</b>","want":"Ле́на живёт в са́мой большо́й ко́мнате","answer":"Ле́на живёт в са́мой большо́й ко́мнате"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · <em>лу́чший</em> = best, <em>ху́дший</em> = worst","title":"One-word best and worst — mind the <em>лу́чше / лу́чший</em> twins","paras":["A couple of superlatives carry their own single word, no са́мый needed: <b>лу́чший</b> (best) and <b>ху́дший</b> (worst). <b>мой лу́чший друг</b> (my best friend), <b>ху́дший день в году́</b> (the worst day of the year). They're adjectives, so they agree: <b>лу́чшая подру́га</b> (best friend, of a woman).","Now the trap you set up yesterday. <b>лу́чше</b> and <b>лу́чший</b> look like twins but do different jobs: <b>лу́чше</b> = <b>better</b> (comparative — one beats another): Я пишу́ лу́чше. <b>лу́чший</b> = <b>best</b> (superlative — tops them all): Я лу́чший.","You'll also hear <b>са́мый лу́чший</b> in warm everyday speech (“the very best”) — fine to recognise; the clean form is simply <b>лу́чший</b>."],"audio":"A couple of superlatives carry their own single word, no samy needed: luchshy, best, and khudshy, worst. Moy luchshy drug, my best friend; khudshy den v godu, the worst day of the year. They're adjectives, so they agree: luchshaya podruga, best friend of a woman. Now the trap you set up yesterday. Luchshe and luchshy look like twins but do different jobs: luchshe is better, the comparative, one beats another — ya pishu luchshe. Luchshy is best, the superlative, tops them all — ya luchshy. You'll also hear samy luchshy in warm everyday speech, the very best — fine to recognise; the clean form is simply luchshy.","table":{"rows":[["лу́чше → лу́чший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лучше, лучший\">🔊</button>","better → best"],["ху́же → ху́дший <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хуже, худший\">🔊</button>","worse → worst"],["мой лу́чший друг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мой лучший друг\">🔊</button>","my best friend"],["лу́чшая подру́га <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лучшая подруга\">🔊</button>","best friend (of a woman)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Ivan is my best friend.","en":"Ива́н — мой <b>лу́чший</b> друг.","say":"Иван — мой лучший друг."},{"ru":"It was the best day.","en":"Э́то был <b>лу́чший</b> день.","say":"Это был лучший день."},{"ru":"I write better, but he's the best.","en":"Я пишу́ <b>лу́чше</b>, но он <b>лу́чший</b>.","say":"Я пишу лучше, но он лучший."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ива́н — мой лу́чше друг.","right":"Ива́н — мой лу́чший друг.","why":"“best friend” takes the superlative <b>лу́чший</b>. лу́чше means “better” and can't sit in front of a noun like this."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“my best friend” —","opts":["мой лу́чше друг","мой лу́чший друг","мой са́мый друг"],"answer":1,"explain":"Best (superlative) = <b>лу́чший</b>: мой лу́чший друг. лу́чше is “better”; “са́мый друг” isn't a phrase."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I write better than before.” — better =","opts":["лу́чший","лу́чше","са́мый лу́чший"],"answer":1,"explain":"“better” is the comparative <b>лу́чше</b>. лу́чший is “best”; са́мый лу́чший is “the very best” (superlative)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Ты мой лу́чший друг.","sub":"tell a friend they are your best friend","want":"ты мой лучший друг"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · <em>са́мое гла́вное</em> — the main thing","title":"The neuter superlative standing alone as “the … thing”","paras":["Russian loves to turn a neuter superlative into a “the … thing” all by itself. <b>са́мое гла́вное</b> = “the main thing”, <b>са́мое интере́сное</b> = “the most interesting part”, <b>са́мое лу́чшее</b> = “the best bit”. No noun follows — the neuter <b>-ое</b> stands alone.","You'll hear it open a thought: <b>Са́мое гла́вное — я здесь.</b> (The main thing is, I'm here.) <b>Са́мое интере́сное впереди́.</b> (The most interesting part is ahead.)","It's a small, warm phrase that makes you sound fluent. Keep <b>са́мое гла́вное</b> ready for the end of any story."],"audio":"Russian loves to turn a neuter superlative into a the-thing all by itself. Samoye glavnoye, the main thing; samoye interesnoye, the most interesting part; samoye luchsheye, the best bit. No noun follows — the neuter -oye stands alone. You'll hear it open a thought: samoye glavnoye — ya zdes, the main thing is, I'm here; samoye interesnoye vperedi, the most interesting part is ahead. It's a small, warm phrase that makes you sound fluent. Keep samoye glavnoye ready for the end of any story.","table":{"rows":[["са́мое гла́вное <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"самое главное\">🔊</button>","the main thing"],["са́мое интере́сное <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"самое интересное\">🔊</button>","the most interesting part"],["са́мое ва́жное <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"самое важное\">🔊</button>","the most important thing"],["са́мое лу́чшее <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"самое лучшее\">🔊</button>","the very best bit"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The main thing is, I'm happy here.","en":"<b>Са́мое гла́вное</b> — я здесь сча́стлива.","say":"Самое главное — я здесь счастлива.","gloss":"сча́стлива = happy (of a woman)"},{"ru":"The most interesting part is still ahead.","en":"<b>Са́мое интере́сное</b> ещё впереди́.","say":"Самое интересное ещё впереди.","gloss":"впереди́ = ahead"},{"ru":"That's the most important thing.","en":"Э́то <b>са́мое ва́жное</b>.","say":"Это самое важное."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"са́мый гла́вный (meaning “the main thing”)","right":"са́мое гла́вное","why":"For an abstract “thing”, use the <b>neuter</b>: <b>са́мое гла́вное</b>. са́мый гла́вный would need a he-word noun right after it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“The main thing is, I'm here.” —","opts":["Са́мый гла́вный — я здесь.","Са́мое гла́вное — я здесь.","Са́мая гла́вная — я здесь."],"answer":1,"explain":"The abstract “thing” is neuter: <b>Са́мое гла́вное</b>. The masculine/feminine forms would each need a matching noun."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “the main thing” (neuter):","accept":["самое главное"],"placeholder":"са́мое…","explain":"Neuter superlative standing alone: <b>са́мое гла́вное</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“That's the most important thing.”</b>","want":"Э́то са́мое ва́жное","answer":"Э́то са́мое ва́жное"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma has a list of favourites now. Over tea, she tells Ivan what's the best, the biggest, the most beautiful in her Moscow. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну, Э́мма, что здесь са́мое интере́сное?","ru":"So, Emma, what's the most interesting thing here?"},{"who":"A","en":"Метро́! Э́то са́мая краси́вая ста́нция в ми́ре.","ru":"The metro! This is the most beautiful station in the world. (в ми́ре = in the world)"},{"who":"B","en":"А са́мый большо́й парк ты уже́ ви́дела?","ru":"And have you seen the biggest park yet? (ви́дела = seen, of a woman)"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Но моё са́мое люби́мое ме́сто — ва́ше кафе́.","ru":"Yes! But my most favourite place is your café. (люби́мое = favourite)"},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, у Ле́ны лу́чший ко́фе в го́роде?","ru":"So Lena has the best coffee in the city?"},{"who":"A","en":"То́чно! И она́ мой са́мый до́брый друг здесь.","ru":"Exactly! And she's my kindest friend here. (то́чно = exactly)"},{"who":"B","en":"А что для тебя́ са́мое гла́вное в Москве́?","ru":"And what's the main thing about Moscow for you?"},{"who":"A","en":"Са́мое гла́вное — вы. С ва́ми э́то мой лу́чший год.","ru":"The main thing is — you. With you two, this is my best year. (с ва́ми = with you)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for crowning your favourites. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"са́мый","ru":"the most (he-word)","ex":"Э́то са́мый большо́й парк."},{"en":"са́мая","ru":"the most (she-word)","ex":"Са́мая краси́вая ста́нция."},{"en":"са́мое","ru":"the most (it-word)","ex":"Са́мое интере́сное ме́сто."},{"en":"лу́чший","ru":"best","ex":"Мой лу́чший друг."},{"en":"ху́дший","ru":"worst","ex":"Э́то был ху́дший день."},{"en":"са́мое гла́вное","ru":"the main thing","ex":"Са́мое гла́вное — здоро́вье."},{"en":"люби́мый","ru":"favourite, beloved","ex":"Э́то моё люби́мое кафе́."},{"en":"изве́стный","ru":"famous, well-known","ex":"Са́мый изве́стный музе́й."},{"en":"ста́нция","ru":"station","ex":"Краси́вая ста́нция метро́."},{"en":"парк","ru":"park","ex":"Са́мый большо́й парк в го́роде."},{"en":"ва́жный","ru":"important","ex":"Э́то са́мый ва́жный день."},{"en":"в ми́ре","ru":"in the world","ex":"Са́мое краси́вое метро́ в ми́ре."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to its English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["са́мый большо́й парк","the biggest park"],["мой лу́чший друг","my best friend"],["са́мое гла́вное","the main thing"],["в са́мой большо́й ко́мнате","in the biggest room"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on the superlative. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“the most interesting city” —","opts":["са́мый интере́сный го́род","бо́лее интере́сный го́род","са́мая интере́сная го́род"],"answer":0},{"q":"“in the biggest room” —","opts":["в са́мый большо́й ко́мнате","в са́мая больша́я ко́мната","в са́мой большо́й ко́мнате"],"answer":2},{"q":"“my best friend” —","opts":["мой лу́чше друг","мой лу́чший друг","мой бо́лее лу́чший друг"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I write better” (comparative) —","opts":["Я пишу́ лу́чший","Я пишу́ лу́чше","Я пишу́ са́мый лу́чший"],"answer":1},{"q":"“the main thing” (the abstract “thing”) —","opts":["са́мое гла́вное","са́мый гла́вный","са́мая гла́вная"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma writes down her Moscow favourites. Read it aloud — every superlative is one you can now build.","title":"Са́мое гла́вное","sentences":["В Москве́ мно́го краси́вых мест, но у меня́ есть люби́мые.","Метро́ здесь — са́мое краси́вое в ми́ре.","Са́мый большо́й парк здесь — Го́рький парк, а лу́чший ко́фе — у Ле́ны.","Ива́н — мой лу́чший друг, и он ста́рше меня́.","Но са́мое гла́вное — я здесь сча́стлива."],"translation":"In Moscow there are many beautiful places, but I have my favourites. The metro here is the most beautiful in the world. The biggest park here is Gorky Park, and the best coffee is at Lena's. Ivan is my best friend, and he's older than me. But the main thing is — I'm happy here."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to crown your favourites.","prompt":"Describe your city or your life in superlatives. Name the biggest, the most beautiful, and the best of something. End with Са́мое гла́вное — … (the main thing is…).","hint":"“The most” = <b>са́мый</b> + adjective, and both agree with the noun (<b>са́мая больша́я ко́мната</b>). Best / worst have their own words: <b>лу́чший, ху́дший</b> 🙂","example":"Мой го́род не са́мый большо́й, но са́мый ую́тный. Здесь са́мая краси́вая пло́щадь и лу́чший парк. Моя́ ма́ма — мой лу́чший друг. Но са́мое гла́вное — я до́ма."}},"26":{"day":26,"week":"04","themeRu":"The -ся verbs","themeEn":"Учи́ться, находи́ться","intro":"A month in, Emma has a rhythm: a university across town, classes that start and finish, new faces she keeps running into. To talk about any of it she needs a new kind of verb — the ones wearing a little tail, <b>-ся</b>. Today you'll meet them as a family: <b>учи́ться</b> (to study), <b>находи́ться</b> (to be located), <b>интересова́ться</b> (to be interested).","introAudio":"A month in, Emma has a rhythm: a university across town, classes that start and finish, new faces she keeps running into. To talk about any of it she needs a new kind of verb — the ones wearing a little tail, -sya. Today you'll meet them as a family: uchitsya, to study; nakhoditsya, to be located; interesovatsya, to be interested.","goals":["Add the reflexive tail: <b>-ся</b> after a consonant, <b>-сь</b> after a vowel — <b>я учу́сь</b>, <b>он у́чится</b>","Use the everyday <b>-ся</b> verbs that aren't “self” at all: <b>находи́ться</b> (be located), <b>начина́ться / конча́ться</b> (begin / end)","Say what you're into with <b>интересова́ться</b> + the instrumental: <b>интересу́юсь му́зыкой</b>","Hear that <b>-ться</b> and <b>-тся</b> are one sound [ца] — and know which one to write"],"learned":["Added the reflexive tail: <b>-ся</b> after a consonant, <b>-сь</b> after a vowel — <b>я учу́сь</b>, <b>он у́чится</b>","Used the <b>-ся</b> verbs that aren't “self”: <b>находи́ться</b>, <b>начина́ться / конча́ться</b>","Said what you're into with <b>интересова́ться</b> + the instrumental","Told <b>-ться</b> from <b>-тся</b> — one sound [ца], two different jobs"],"review":{"intro":"One case from last month earns a curtain-call today: the <b>instrumental</b>, “with what / by what”. Warm it up — a brand-new verb is about to lean on it hard.","introAudio":"One case from last month earns a curtain call today: the instrumental, with-what, by-what. Warm it up — a brand-new verb is about to lean on it hard.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of this is Russian you already own.","q":"“I go by bus.” — Я е́ду ___","opts":["авто́бус","авто́бусом","авто́буса"],"answer":1,"explain":"By-transport is the bare <b>instrumental</b>, with no little “by”: авто́бус → <b>авто́бусом</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“It's the most interesting book.” — Э́то ___ кни́га.","opts":["са́мый интере́сный","са́мая интере́сная","бо́лее интере́сная"],"answer":1,"explain":"кни́га is a she-word, so <b>са́мая интере́сная</b> agrees with it. бо́лее интере́сная would be the comparative “more interesting”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Tomorrow I'll read the book”</b> (and finish it):","want":"За́втра я прочита́ю кни́гу","answer":"За́втра я прочита́ю кни́гу"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The reflexive tail","title":"<em>-ся / -сь</em> — the ending rides on the very end","paras":["Some Russian verbs carry an extra little tail: <b>-ся</b>. You build the verb exactly as you always have, then glue <b>-ся</b> onto the very end. <b>учи́ть</b> → <b>я учу́</b> → <b>я учу́сь</b> (I study). Nothing slips in between the verb and its tail — it's all one word.","The tail has two shapes, chosen purely by sound. After a <b>consonant</b> it's <b>-ся</b>; after a <b>vowel</b> it softens to <b>-сь</b>. So <b>он у́чится</b> (ends in т, a consonant) but <b>я учу́сь</b> (ends in у, a vowel). In the past too: <b>он учи́лся</b>, <b>она́ учи́лась</b>.","A warning up front: this tail is <em>not</em> the English “-self”. Very few of these verbs mean doing something to yourself — think of <b>-ся</b> as a fixed part of the verb you simply learn attached. The next rule shows why."],"audio":"Some Russian verbs carry an extra little tail: -sya. You build the verb exactly as always, then glue -sya onto the very end. Uchit becomes ya uchu becomes ya uchus, I study. Nothing slips in between the verb and its tail — it's all one word. The tail has two shapes, chosen by sound. After a consonant it's -sya; after a vowel it softens to -s. So on uchitsya, ending in a consonant, but ya uchus, ending in a vowel. In the past too: on uchilsya, ona uchilas. A warning up front: this tail is not the English -self. Very few of these verbs mean doing something to yourself — learn -sya as a fixed part of the verb.","table":{"rows":[["я учу́сь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я учусь\">🔊</button>","I study (vowel → -сь)"],["ты у́чишься <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты учишься\">🔊</button>","you study (stress hops back)"],["он / она́ у́чится <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он учится\">🔊</button>","he / she studies (-тся)"],["мы у́чимся, они́ у́чатся <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы учимся, они учатся\">🔊</button>","we study, they study"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I study at the university.","en":"Я <b>учу́сь</b> в университе́те.","say":"Я учусь в университете."},{"ru":"Where do you study?","en":"Где ты <b>у́чишься</b>?","say":"Где ты учишься?"},{"ru":"She went to school here.","en":"Она́ <b>учи́лась</b> здесь в шко́ле.","say":"Она училась здесь в школе."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я учу́ сь в шко́ле.","right":"Я учу́сь в шко́ле.","why":"The tail is glued on — never a separate word. English “-self” stands apart; Russian <b>-ся/-сь</b> never does."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“I study at the university.” — Я ___ в университе́те.","opts":["учу́сь","у́чится","учи́ться"],"answer":0,"explain":"“I” → <b>учу́сь</b>, with <b>-сь</b> after the vowel. у́чится is “he/she”, учи́ться is the infinitive."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“He studies at school.”</b>","want":"Он у́чится в шко́ле","answer":"Он у́чится в шко́ле"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it in Russian. The stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “I study” (from учи́ться):","accept":["я учусь","учусь"],"placeholder":"я…","explain":"я учу́сь — the tail is <b>-сь</b> because учу́ ends in a vowel."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Not “self” at all","title":"<em>Находи́ться, начина́ться, конча́ться</em> — everyday tailed verbs","paras":["Most <b>-ся</b> verbs simply <em>are</em> tailed — the meaning has nothing to do with “self”. Three you'll use daily: <b>находи́ться</b> = to be located, <b>начина́ться</b> = to begin, <b>конча́ться</b> = to end.","<b>Находи́ться</b> answers <em>where</em> something sits: <b>Где нахо́дится метро́?</b> — “Where is the metro?” For buildings and places it sounds warmer and more Russian than a bare <b>где</b> + noun.","<b>Начина́ться</b> and <b>конча́ться</b> are for things that begin and end on their own — a lesson, a film, a day: <b>Уро́к начина́ется в де́вять и конча́ется в час.</b> Both mostly live in the он/она́/они shapes: <b>начина́ется, начина́ются</b>."],"audio":"Most -sya verbs simply are tailed — the meaning has nothing to do with self. Three you'll use daily: nakhoditsya, to be located; nachinatsya, to begin; konchatsya, to end. Nakhoditsya answers where something sits: gde nakhoditsya metro? Where is the metro? For places it sounds warmer and more Russian than a bare gde plus noun. Nachinatsya and konchatsya are for things that begin and end on their own — a lesson, a film, a day: urok nachinaetsya v devyat i konchaetsya v chas. Both mostly live in the he, she, they shapes.","table":{"rows":[["Где нахо́дится банк? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Где находится банк?\">🔊</button>","Where is the bank?"],["Апте́ка нахо́дится ря́дом. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Аптека находится рядом.\">🔊</button>","The pharmacy is nearby."],["Фильм начина́ется в семь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Фильм начинается в семь.\">🔊</button>","The film starts at seven."],["Уро́к конча́ется в час. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Урок кончается в час.\">🔊</button>","The lesson ends at one."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Where is the university located?","en":"Где <b>нахо́дится</b> университе́т?","say":"Где находится университет?"},{"ru":"The lesson begins at nine.","en":"Уро́к <b>начина́ется</b> в де́вять.","say":"Урок начинается в девять."},{"ru":"Summer is ending.","en":"Ле́то <b>конча́ется</b>.","say":"Лето кончается."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Метро́ нахо́дит в це́нтре.","right":"Метро́ нахо́дится в це́нтре.","why":"Without the tail, <b>нахо́дит</b> means “finds” (someone finds something). For a place that <em>is located</em> somewhere, you need <b>нахо́дится</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Where is the metro?” — Где ___ метро́?","opts":["нахо́дится","нахо́дит","находи́ться"],"answer":0,"explain":"A place <em>is located</em> → <b>нахо́дится</b> (with the tail). нахо́дит means “finds”; находи́ться is the infinitive."},{"type":"choice","q":"“The lesson begins at nine.” — Уро́к ___ в де́вять.","opts":["начина́ет","начина́ется","конча́ется"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson begins on its own → <b>начина́ется</b>. начина́ет (no tail) means “someone starts something”; конча́ется means “ends”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“The lesson ends at one.”</b>","want":"Уро́к конча́ется в час","answer":"Уро́к конча́ется в час"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · What you're into","title":"<em>Интересова́ться</em> + the instrumental — “interested in”","paras":["To say what you're interested in, Russian reaches for <b>интересова́ться</b> — a tailed verb — and puts the topic in the <b>instrumental</b>, the “with what” case from week two: <b>Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой.</b> — literally “I busy myself with music.”","It bends <b>я интересу́юсь, ты интересу́ешься, он интересу́ется</b>, with the tail always on the very end. What matters most is what follows: the thing you're into lands in the <b>instrumental</b> — <b>му́зыкой, спо́ртом, исто́рией</b>.","Careful — English says “interested <em>in</em>”, but there's no little word for “in” here. The instrumental ending does the whole job: <b>интересу́юсь спо́ртом</b> (into sport), <b>интересу́юсь кни́гами</b> (into books)."],"audio":"To say what you're interested in, Russian reaches for interesovatsya — a tailed verb — and puts the topic in the instrumental, the with-what case from week two: ya interesuyus muzykoy, literally I busy myself with music. It bends ya interesuyus, ty interesueshsya, on interesuetsya, with the tail always on the very end. What matters most is what follows: the thing you're into lands in the instrumental — muzykoy, sportom, istoriey. Careful — English says interested in, but there's no little word for in here. The instrumental ending does the whole job: interesuyus sportom, interesuyus knigami.","table":{"rows":[["Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я интересуюсь музыкой.\">🔊</button>","music (she-word → -ой)"],["Он интересу́ется спо́ртом. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он интересуется спортом.\">🔊</button>","sport (he-word → -ом)"],["Мы интересу́емся исто́рией. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мы интересуемся историей.\">🔊</button>","history (soft → -ей)"],["Они́ интересу́ются кни́гами. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Они интересуются книгами.\">🔊</button>","books (plural → -ами)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm interested in Russian history.","en":"Я <b>интересу́юсь</b> ру́сской исто́рией.","say":"Я интересуюсь русской историей."},{"ru":"What are you interested in?","en":"Чем ты <b>интересу́ешься</b>?","say":"Чем ты интересуешься?","gloss":"чем = “in what” — the instrumental of что"},{"ru":"My brother is into sport.","en":"Мой брат <b>интересу́ется</b> спо́ртом.","say":"Мой брат интересуется спортом."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я интересу́юсь му́зыку.","right":"Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой.","why":"“Interested in” takes the <b>instrumental</b>, not the accusative. му́зыку (acc.) → <b>му́зыкой</b> (instr.)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm interested in music.” — Я интересу́юсь ___.","opts":["му́зыка","му́зыку","му́зыкой"],"answer":2,"explain":"The topic goes in the <b>instrumental</b>: му́зыка → <b>му́зыкой</b>. му́зыку is the accusative, wrong here."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I'm interested in history” (интересова́ться + instrumental):","accept":["я интересуюсь историей","интересуюсь историей"],"placeholder":"я интересу́юсь…","explain":"исто́рия → исто́рией (soft ending). Я интересу́юсь исто́рией."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я интересу́юсь спо́ртом.","sub":"say what you're into — sport","want":"я интересуюсь спортом"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · <em>-ться</em> or <em>-тся</em>?","title":"One sound [ца], two spellings — and how to choose","paras":["Here's the ear-trap. Both <b>-ться</b> (the infinitive) and <b>-тся</b> (the он/она́ form) are pronounced exactly the same: [ца]. <b>учи́ться</b> and <b>у́чится</b> sound like twins — but one has a soft sign <b>ь</b> and the other doesn't.","A one-second test tells them apart. Ask the verb a question. If it answers <b>что де́лать?</b> (“to do what?” — an infinitive), it keeps the soft sign: <b>учи́ться</b>. If it answers <b>что де́лает?</b> (“does what?” — he/she/it), no soft sign: <b>у́чится</b>. The <b>ь</b> of де́ла<b>ть</b> travels into the verb.","So: <b>Он хо́чет учи́ться</b> (after хо́чет comes an infinitive — keep <b>ь</b>) but <b>Он хорошо́ у́чится</b> (he does — drop <b>ь</b>). Two more tailed verbs to practise on: <b>познако́миться</b> (to meet, get to know) and <b>встреча́ться</b> (to meet up)."],"audio":"Here's the ear-trap. Both -tsya, the infinitive, and -tsya, the he-she form, are pronounced exactly the same: tsa. Uchitsya and uchitsya sound like twins — but one has a soft sign and the other doesn't. A one-second test tells them apart. Ask the verb a question. If it answers chto delat, to do what, an infinitive, it keeps the soft sign. If it answers chto delaet, does what, he or she, no soft sign. So: on khochet uchitsya, after khochet comes an infinitive, keep the soft sign; but on khorosho uchitsya, he does, drop it. Two more tailed verbs to practise on: poznakomitsya, to meet, and vstrechatsya, to meet up.","table":{"rows":[["Он лю́бит учи́ться. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он любит учиться.\">🔊</button>","…loves to study — infinitive, keep ь"],["Он хорошо́ у́чится. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он хорошо учится.\">🔊</button>","…studies well — he does, no ь"],["Я хочу́ познако́миться. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я хочу познакомиться.\">🔊</button>","…want to get to know you — infinitive, ь"],["Мы встреча́емся в семь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мы встречаемся в семь.\">🔊</button>","…we meet at seven — we do, -ся"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I want to meet your brother.","en":"Я хочу́ <b>познако́миться</b> с твои́м бра́том.","say":"Я хочу познакомиться с твоим братом.","gloss":"с + instrumental: “with your brother”"},{"ru":"We meet every day.","en":"Мы <b>встреча́емся</b> ка́ждый день.","say":"Мы встречаемся каждый день."},{"ru":"He studies well.","en":"Он хорошо́ <b>у́чится</b>.","say":"Он хорошо учится."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он у́читься здесь.","right":"Он у́чится здесь.","why":"“He studies” answers <b>что де́лает?</b> — no soft sign: <b>у́чится</b>. Keep the <b>ь</b> only for the infinitive (что де́лать?)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is right? “He studies here.”","opts":["Он у́чится здесь.","Он учи́ться здесь.","Он у́читься здесь."],"answer":0,"explain":"“He studies” = что де́лает? → no soft sign: <b>у́чится</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which is right? “I want to study.”","opts":["Я хочу́ учи́ться.","Я хочу́ у́чится.","Я хочу́ у́читься."],"answer":0,"explain":"After хочу́ comes an infinitive (что де́лать?) → keep the soft sign: <b>учи́ться</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Classes begin.”</b>","want":"Заня́тия начина́ются","answer":"Заня́тия начина́ются"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma and Ivan meet at Lena's café. He wants to hear how the new term is going. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну как, Э́мма? Где ты тепе́рь у́чишься?","ru":"Well, how's it going, Emma? Where do you study now?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я учу́сь в университе́те. Он нахо́дится в це́нтре.","ru":"I study at the university. It's in the centre."},{"who":"B","en":"А когда́ начина́ются заня́тия?","ru":"And when do classes begin?"},{"who":"A","en":"Пе́рвое заня́тие начина́ется в де́вять, а конча́ется в час.","ru":"The first class begins at nine and ends at one."},{"who":"B","en":"И чем ты интересу́ешься?","ru":"And what are you interested in?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я интересу́юсь ру́сской исто́рией. Э́то о́чень интере́сно.","ru":"I'm interested in Russian history. It's really interesting."},{"who":"B","en":"А с кем ты уже́ познако́милась?","ru":"And who have you met already?"},{"who":"A","en":"Со студе́нткой из Испа́нии. Мы встреча́емся ка́ждый день.","ru":"A student from Spain. We meet every day. (со студе́нткой = with a student)"},{"who":"B","en":"Здо́рово! Я о́чень рад за тебя́.","ru":"Great! I'm really glad for you. (рад — said by a man)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for study, places, and people you keep meeting. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"учи́ться","ru":"to study, be a student","ex":"Я учу́сь в университе́те."},{"en":"находи́ться","ru":"to be located","ex":"Где нахо́дится метро́?"},{"en":"начина́ться","ru":"to begin (of things)","ex":"Уро́к начина́ется в де́вять."},{"en":"конча́ться","ru":"to end (of things)","ex":"Фильм конча́ется по́здно."},{"en":"интересова́ться","ru":"to be interested in (+ instr.)","ex":"Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой."},{"en":"занима́ться","ru":"to do, be engaged in (+ instr.)","ex":"Он занима́ется спо́ртом."},{"en":"познако́миться","ru":"to meet, get acquainted","ex":"Прия́тно познако́миться!"},{"en":"встреча́ться","ru":"to meet up","ex":"Мы встреча́емся ка́ждый день."},{"en":"университе́т","ru":"university","ex":"Я иду́ в университе́т."},{"en":"заня́тие","ru":"class, lesson","ex":"Заня́тия начина́ются у́тром."},{"en":"ря́дом","ru":"nearby, next to","ex":"Апте́ка ря́дом."},{"en":"исто́рия","ru":"history; story","ex":"Я люблю́ ру́сскую исто́рию."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я учу́сь","I study"],["метро́ нахо́дится ря́дом","the metro is located nearby"],["уро́к начина́ется","the lesson begins"],["интересу́юсь му́зыкой","I'm interested in music"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“I study at university.” — я ___","opts":["учу́сь","у́чится","учи́ться"],"answer":0},{"q":"“Where is the metro located?” — Где ___ метро́?","opts":["нахо́дит","нахо́дится","начина́ется"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I'm interested in music.”","opts":["Я интересу́ю му́зыкой.","Я интересу́юсь му́зыку.","Я интересу́юсь му́зыкой."],"answer":2},{"q":"Which spelling is right — “He studies here.”","opts":["Он учи́ться здесь.","Он у́чится здесь.","Он у́читься здесь."],"answer":1},{"q":"“Classes end at one.” — Заня́тия ___ в час.","opts":["конча́ются","начина́ют","нахо́дятся"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note about her new routine — read it aloud. Every tailed verb in it is one you can now make yourself.","title":"Где я учу́сь","sentences":["Я учу́сь в университе́те в це́нтре Москвы́.","Он нахо́дится ря́дом с метро́.","Заня́тия начина́ются в де́вять и конча́ются в два.","Я интересу́юсь ру́сской исто́рией и му́зыкой.","Вчера́ я познако́милась с де́вушкой из Испа́нии.","Мы встреча́емся ка́ждый день по́сле заня́тий."],"translation":"I study at a university in the centre of Moscow. It's located next to the metro. Classes begin at nine and end at two. I'm interested in Russian history and music. Yesterday I met a girl from Spain. We meet every day after classes."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put the tailed verbs to work.","prompt":"Tell us about your studies or your day. Where do you study or work, and where is it located? When do things begin and end? What are you interested in?","hint":"Reach for the tailed verbs: <b>учу́сь</b>, <b>нахо́дится</b>, <b>начина́ется / конча́ется</b>, <b>интересу́юсь</b> + the instrumental. Remember the tail: <b>-сь</b> after a vowel, <b>-ся</b> after a consonant 🙂","example":"Я учу́сь в университе́те. Он нахо́дится в це́нтре го́рода. Заня́тия начина́ются в де́вять утра́ и конча́ются в три. Я интересу́юсь исто́рией и му́зыкой. По́сле заня́тий я встреча́юсь с друзья́ми."}},"27":{"day":27,"week":"04","themeRu":"I must, you may","themeEn":"До́лжен, мо́жно, нельзя́","intro":"Every new place comes with its own rules — what you must do, what you may do, what you simply mustn't. Emma is learning hers: the library, the metro, the dorm. Today you'll gather Russian's little words of duty and permission into one toolkit — <b>до́лжен</b> (must), <b>мо́жно</b> (may), <b>нельзя́</b> (mustn't) — and catch a twist where a verb's aspect flips the whole meaning.","introAudio":"Every new place comes with its own rules — what you must do, what you may do, what you simply mustn't. Emma is learning hers: the library, the metro, the dorm. Today you'll gather Russian's little words of duty and permission into one toolkit — dolzhen, must; mozhno, may; nelzya, mustn't — and catch a twist where a verb's aspect flips the whole meaning.","goals":["Say what you <em>must</em> do with <b>до́лжен / должна́ / должны́</b> + an infinitive — the “must” that agrees with you","Feel the split: personal <b>я до́лжен</b> vs impersonal <b>мне на́до / ну́жно</b> (dative, never changing)","Ask and give permission with <b>мо́жно</b>, and forbid with <b>нельзя́</b>","Hear how aspect flips <b>нельзя́</b>: <b>нельзя́ кури́ть</b> (not allowed) vs <b>нельзя́ купи́ть</b> (impossible)"],"learned":["Said what you must do with <b>до́лжен / должна́ / должны́</b> + an infinitive","Felt the split: personal <b>я до́лжен</b> vs impersonal <b>мне на́до / ну́жно</b>","Asked and gave permission with <b>мо́жно</b>, and forbade with <b>нельзя́</b>","Heard how aspect flips <b>нельзя́</b>: not allowed vs impossible"],"review":{"intro":"Today's toolkit grows straight out of one A2 day you've already done — the impersonal <b>на́до / ну́жно</b> with the dative. Warm that up, plus the aspect you'll lean on at the end.","introAudio":"Today's toolkit grows straight out of one A2 day you've already done — the impersonal nado, nuzhno with the dative. Warm that up, plus the aspect you'll lean on at the end.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is Russian from earlier in the level.","q":"“I need to go home.” — Мне ___ идти́ домо́й.","opts":["на́до","на́дой","до́лжен"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>на́до</b> + infinitive, fixed, with the person in the dative: <b>мне на́до идти́</b>. до́лжен can't follow мне — it needs the plain “я”."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'll read this letter (and finish it).” — Я ___ письмо́.","opts":["бу́ду чита́ть","прочита́ю","чита́ю"],"answer":1,"explain":"One finished result → perfective future <b>прочита́ю</b>. бу́ду чита́ть is the process; чита́ю is the present."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“She works as a doctor.”</b>","want":"Она́ рабо́тает врачо́м","answer":"Она́ рабо́тает врачо́м"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The “must” that agrees","title":"<em>До́лжен / должна́ / должны́</em> + an infinitive","paras":["To say “must”, Russian uses <b>до́лжен</b> + an infinitive — and here's the surprise: <b>до́лжен</b> is not frozen. It changes shape to match the person, exactly like an adjective. A man says <b>я до́лжен</b>, a woman <b>я должна́</b>, and any group <b>мы должны́</b>.","So the ending answers “who?”: <b>Он до́лжен рабо́тать</b> (he must work), <b>Она́ должна́ рабо́тать</b> (she must), <b>Они́ должны́ рабо́тать</b> (they must). The infinitive after it never changes — only <b>до́лжен</b> flexes.","English “must” is one stiff word for everyone. Russian makes “must” agree with you — a small habit to build, and the same agreement you already know from <b>мой / моя́ / мои́</b>."],"audio":"To say must, Russian uses dolzhen plus an infinitive — and here's the surprise: dolzhen is not frozen. It changes shape to match the person, exactly like an adjective. A man says ya dolzhen, a woman ya dolzhna, and any group my dolzhny. So the ending answers who: on dolzhen rabotat, he must work; ona dolzhna rabotat, she must; oni dolzhny rabotat, they must. The infinitive after it never changes — only dolzhen flexes. English must is one stiff word for everyone. Russian makes must agree with you — the same agreement you know from moy, moya, moi.","table":{"rows":[["я / ты / он до́лжен <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я должен\">🔊</button>","must — a man (he-word, -Ø)"],["я / ты / она́ должна́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я должна\">🔊</button>","must — a woman (she-word, -а)"],["мы / вы / они́ должны́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы должны\">🔊</button>","must — any group (plural, -ы)"],["Она́ должна́ идти́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она должна идти.\">🔊</button>","She must go."]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"I must work today. (a man)","en":"Я <b>до́лжен</b> рабо́тать сего́дня.","say":"Я должен работать сегодня."},{"ru":"She must study a lot. (a woman)","en":"Она́ <b>должна́</b> мно́го учи́ться.","say":"Она должна много учиться."},{"ru":"We must be at home at seven.","en":"Мы <b>должны́</b> быть до́ма в семь.","say":"Мы должны быть дома в семь."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Она́ до́лжен рабо́тать.","right":"Она́ должна́ рабо́тать.","why":"<b>до́лжен</b> agrees with the subject like an adjective. A she-word subject → <b>должна́</b>, never до́лжен."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"A woman says “I must work today.” — Я ___ рабо́тать сего́дня.","opts":["до́лжен","должна́","должны́"],"answer":1,"explain":"A woman → <b>должна́</b>. до́лжен is the man's form, должны́ is for a group."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“We must study.”</b>","want":"Мы должны́ учи́ться","answer":"Мы должны́ учи́ться"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it in Russian. Plain letters are fine — the stress marks are just for you.","q":"Type “He must work” (он + must + work):","accept":["он должен работать"],"placeholder":"он до́лжен…","explain":"A he-word subject → <b>до́лжен</b>: Он до́лжен рабо́тать."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Personal vs impersonal","title":"<em>Я до́лжен</em> vs <em>мне на́до</em> — two ways to feel “have to”","paras":["You now have two ways to say you have to do something, and they're built differently. <b>До́лжен</b> takes a plain subject (nominative) and agrees with it: <b>Я до́лжен идти́</b>. The impersonal <b>на́до / ну́жно</b> take the person in the <b>dative</b> and never change: <b>Мне на́до идти́</b>.","The feel differs a little too. <b>До́лжен</b> leans toward duty and responsibility (“I'm obliged to”); <b>на́до / ну́жно</b> lean toward practical need (“I need to”). Both are everyday Russian — pick whichever fits.","The trap is mixing the frames. It's <b>Я до́лжен</b> (plain я) but <b>Мне на́до</b> (dative мне). Never <span>“Мне до́лжен”</span> or <span>“Я на́до”</span> — each word keeps its own case."],"audio":"You now have two ways to say you have to do something, and they're built differently. Dolzhen takes a plain subject and agrees with it: ya dolzhen idti. The impersonal nado and nuzhno take the person in the dative and never change: mne nado idti. The feel differs a little too. Dolzhen leans toward duty and responsibility; nado and nuzhno lean toward practical need. Both are everyday Russian. The trap is mixing the frames. It's ya dolzhen, plain ya, but mne nado, dative mne. Never mne dolzhen or ya nado — each word keeps its own case.","table":{"rows":[["Я до́лжен идти́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я должен идти.\">🔊</button>","I must go. (plain я, agrees)"],["Мне на́до идти́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне надо идти.\">🔊</button>","I need to go. (dative мне, fixed)"],["Она́ должна́ рабо́тать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она должна работать.\">🔊</button>","She must work. (agrees)"],["Ей ну́жно рабо́тать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ей нужно работать.\">🔊</button>","She needs to work. (dative ей, fixed)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I have to rest.","en":"Мне <b>на́до</b> отдохну́ть.","say":"Мне надо отдохнуть.","gloss":"dative + fixed на́до"},{"ru":"You must read this book. (to a man)","en":"Ты <b>до́лжен</b> прочита́ть э́ту кни́гу.","say":"Ты должен прочитать эту книгу."},{"ru":"They need to go to the doctor.","en":"Им <b>ну́жно</b> идти́ к врачу́.","say":"Им нужно идти к врачу."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Мне до́лжен идти́.","right":"Я до́лжен идти́. / Мне на́до идти́.","why":"<b>до́лжен</b> wants a plain subject (я), not the dative. With <b>мне</b> you need the impersonal <b>на́до / ну́жно</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is correct Russian for “She must go”?","opts":["Ей должна́ идти́.","Она́ должна́ идти́.","Её на́до идти́."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>до́лжен</b> takes a plain subject and agrees: <b>Она́ должна́</b>. (For the dative you'd say Ей на́до идти́.)"},{"type":"choice","q":"“I need to rest” — with на́до it's…","opts":["Я на́до отдохну́ть.","Мне на́до отдохну́ть.","Мне до́лжен отдохну́ть."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>на́до</b> takes the dative: <b>Мне на́до отдохну́ть</b>. Not Я на́до (needs dative), not Мне до́лжен (до́лжен needs plain я)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“They must work.”</b>","want":"Они́ должны́ рабо́тать","answer":"Они́ должны́ рабо́тать"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · May and mustn't","title":"<em>Мо́жно</em> — “may I?” · <em>Нельзя́</em> — “you mustn't”","paras":["For permission Russian has two tiny impersonal words. <b>Мо́жно</b> = “it's allowed / may I”: <b>Мо́жно войти́?</b> (May I come in?), <b>Здесь мо́жно кури́ть.</b> (Smoking is allowed here.) Add a dative to name the person: <b>Мне мо́жно войти́?</b>","Its opposite is <b>нельзя́</b> = “it's not allowed / you mustn't”: <b>Здесь нельзя́ кури́ть.</b> (No smoking here.) Both stand alone as a full answer: <b>— Мо́жно? — Нет, нельзя́.</b>","These aren't the duty words. <b>До́лжен</b> and <b>на́до</b> say you <em>have to</em>; <b>мо́жно</b> and <b>нельзя́</b> say whether you <em>may</em>. Keep the two pairs apart and you can navigate any set of rules."],"audio":"For permission Russian has two tiny impersonal words. Mozhno means it's allowed, or may I: mozhno voyti? May I come in? Zdes mozhno kurit, smoking is allowed here. Add a dative to name the person: mne mozhno voyti? Its opposite is nelzya, it's not allowed, you mustn't: zdes nelzya kurit, no smoking here. Both stand alone as a full answer: mozhno? Nyet, nelzya. These aren't the duty words. Dolzhen and nado say you have to; mozhno and nelzya say whether you may. Keep the two pairs apart and you can navigate any set of rules.","table":{"rows":[["Мо́жно войти́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Можно войти?\">🔊</button>","May I come in?"],["Здесь мо́жно фотографи́ровать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Здесь можно фотографировать.\">🔊</button>","Photos are allowed here."],["Здесь нельзя́ кури́ть. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Здесь нельзя курить.\">🔊</button>","No smoking here."],["— Мо́жно? — Нет, нельзя́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Можно? Нет, нельзя.\">🔊</button>","— May I? — No, you mustn't."]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"May I sit here?","en":"<b>Мо́жно</b> здесь сесть?","say":"Можно здесь сесть?"},{"ru":"You mustn't talk loudly here.","en":"Здесь <b>нельзя́</b> гро́мко говори́ть.","say":"Здесь нельзя громко говорить."},{"ru":"— May I have some tea? — Yes, of course.","en":"— <b>Мо́жно</b> ча́ю? — Да, коне́чно.","say":"Можно чаю? Да, конечно.","gloss":"ча́ю = genitive of ча́й (some tea)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Здесь до́лжен кури́ть.","right":"Здесь мо́жно / нельзя́ кури́ть.","why":"<b>до́лжен</b> is “must (do)”, not “may / mustn't”. Permission and prohibition are <b>мо́жно</b> and <b>нельзя́</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“May I come in?” — ___ войти́?","opts":["Мо́жно","Нельзя́","До́лжен"],"answer":0,"explain":"Asking permission → <b>Мо́жно войти́?</b> нельзя́ forbids; до́лжен is “must”."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and ask it out loud.","target":"Здесь мо́жно кури́ть?","sub":"ask whether smoking is allowed here","want":"здесь можно курить"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “you mustn't smoke here” (здесь + mustn't + smoke):","accept":["здесь нельзя курить"],"placeholder":"здесь нельзя́…","explain":"Prohibition → <b>Здесь нельзя́ кури́ть</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · <em>Нельзя́</em> flips with aspect","title":"<em>Нельзя́ кури́ть</em> (forbidden) vs <em>нельзя́ купи́ть</em> (impossible)","paras":["Here's the twist that catches everyone. After <b>нельзя́</b>, the verb's <em>aspect</em> changes what the sentence means. With an <b>imperfective</b> verb, <b>нельзя́</b> = “it's not allowed”: <b>Здесь нельзя́ кури́ть.</b> — a standing rule, no smoking.","With a <b>perfective</b> verb, <b>нельзя́</b> shifts to “it's impossible / there's no way”: <b>Э́тот биле́т нельзя́ купи́ть.</b> — you can't buy this ticket (they're sold out). Same little word, opposite flavour — the aspect decides.","So <b>нельзя́ входи́ть</b> (impf.) = “no entry, you're not allowed in”, but <b>нельзя́ войти́</b> (pf.) = “you can't get in — the door's locked”. When you forbid, reach for the imperfective; when you mean “can't manage it”, the perfective."],"audio":"Here's the twist that catches everyone. After nelzya, the verb's aspect changes what the sentence means. With an imperfective verb, nelzya means it's not allowed: zdes nelzya kurit — a standing rule, no smoking. With a perfective verb, nelzya shifts to it's impossible, there's no way: etot bilet nelzya kupit — you can't buy this ticket, they're sold out. Same little word, opposite flavour — the aspect decides. So nelzya vkhodit, imperfective, means no entry, you're not allowed in; but nelzya voyti, perfective, means you can't get in, the door's locked. When you forbid, reach for the imperfective; when you mean can't manage it, the perfective.","table":{"rows":[["нельзя́ кури́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нельзя курить\">🔊</button>","not allowed to smoke (impf. → rule)"],["нельзя́ купи́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нельзя купить\">🔊</button>","impossible to buy (pf. → can't)"],["нельзя́ входи́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нельзя входить\">🔊</button>","no entry (impf. → forbidden)"],["нельзя́ войти́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нельзя войти\">🔊</button>","can't get in (pf. → impossible)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"You mustn't be late.","en":"<b>Нельзя́</b> опа́здывать.","say":"Нельзя опаздывать.","gloss":"опа́здывать (impf.) → a rule"},{"ru":"This word can't be translated.","en":"Э́то сло́во <b>нельзя́</b> перевести́.","say":"Это слово нельзя перевести.","gloss":"перевести́ (pf.) → impossible"},{"ru":"You mustn't talk here.","en":"Здесь <b>нельзя́</b> разгова́ривать.","say":"Здесь нельзя разговаривать."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Здесь нельзя́ закури́ть. (as a “no smoking” sign)","right":"Здесь нельзя́ кури́ть.","why":"For a standing rule use the <b>imperfective</b>: нельзя́ <b>кури́ть</b>. The perfective закури́ть would mean “there's no way to light up”, not “forbidden”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“No smoking here.” (a standing rule) — Здесь нельзя́ ___.","opts":["кури́ть","закури́ть","купи́ть"],"answer":0,"explain":"A standing prohibition → imperfective: нельзя́ <b>кури́ть</b>. The perfective would twist it to “can't manage to”."},{"type":"choice","q":"“You can't buy this ticket (it's impossible).” — Э́тот биле́т нельзя́ ___.","opts":["покупа́ть","продава́ть","купи́ть"],"answer":2,"explain":"Impossibility → perfective: нельзя́ <b>купи́ть</b> = “there's no way to buy it”. покупа́ть (impf.) would mean “buying it is not allowed”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“You mustn't be late.”</b> (a rule)","want":"Нельзя́ опа́здывать","answer":"Нельзя́ опа́здывать"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma's first visit to the university library. A librarian explains what she may and mustn't do. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Извини́те, здесь мо́жно занима́ться?","ru":"Excuse me, may I study here?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, коне́чно. Но здесь нельзя́ гро́мко говори́ть.","ru":"Yes, of course. But you mustn't talk loudly here."},{"who":"A","en":"Поня́тно. А мо́жно взять э́ту кни́гу домо́й?","ru":"I see. And may I take this book home?"},{"who":"B","en":"Нет, э́ту кни́гу нельзя́ взять. Она́ то́лько для чте́ния здесь.","ru":"No, this book can't be taken. It's for reading here only. (для чте́ния = for reading)"},{"who":"A","en":"Хорошо́. А что я должна́ сде́лать?","ru":"OK. And what must I do?"},{"who":"B","en":"Вы должны́ показа́ть студе́нческий биле́т.","ru":"You must show your student card. (студе́нческий биле́т = student card)"},{"who":"A","en":"Вот он. Спаси́бо большо́е!","ru":"Here it is. Thank you very much!"},{"who":"B","en":"Пожа́луйста. У нас заня́тия начина́ются в де́вять.","ru":"You're welcome. Our classes begin at nine."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for rules, duty, and permission. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"до́лжен / должна́","ru":"must (a man / a woman)","ex":"Я до́лжен идти́."},{"en":"мо́жно","ru":"one may, it's allowed","ex":"Здесь мо́жно сиде́ть?"},{"en":"нельзя́","ru":"one mustn't, not allowed","ex":"Здесь нельзя́ кури́ть."},{"en":"на́до","ru":"one needs to (+ dative)","ex":"Мне на́до рабо́тать."},{"en":"ну́жно","ru":"necessary, one needs to","ex":"Ну́жно отдохну́ть."},{"en":"пра́вило","ru":"rule","ex":"Э́то ва́жное пра́вило."},{"en":"кури́ть","ru":"to smoke","ex":"Он не ку́рит."},{"en":"отдыха́ть","ru":"to rest, relax","ex":"Я хочу́ отдыха́ть."},{"en":"библиоте́ка","ru":"library","ex":"Библиоте́ка ря́дом."},{"en":"ти́хо","ru":"quietly","ex":"Говори́те ти́хо."},{"en":"гро́мко","ru":"loudly","ex":"Нельзя́ гро́мко говори́ть."},{"en":"войти́","ru":"to come in, enter","ex":"Мо́жно войти́?"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я до́лжен идти́","I must go"],["здесь мо́жно кури́ть","smoking is allowed here"],["здесь нельзя́ шуме́ть","you mustn't make noise here"],["мне на́до рабо́тать","I need to work"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A man says “I must work.”","opts":["Я должна́ рабо́тать.","Я до́лжен рабо́тать.","Я должны́ рабо́тать."],"answer":1},{"q":"“She must study.”","opts":["Она́ должна́ учи́ться.","Она́ до́лжен учи́ться.","Ей должна́ учи́ться."],"answer":0},{"q":"“May I sit here?” — ___ здесь сесть?","opts":["Нельзя́","До́лжен","Мо́жно"],"answer":2},{"q":"“No smoking here.” (a rule) — Здесь нельзя́ ___.","opts":["кури́ть","закури́ть","купи́ть"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I need to go.” (impersonal) — ___ идти́.","opts":["Я на́до","Мне на́до","Мне до́лжен"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"The library's little rulebook, in Emma's own words — read it aloud.","title":"Пра́вила библиоте́ки","sentences":["Библиоте́ка нахо́дится в це́нтре университе́та.","Здесь мо́жно чита́ть и занима́ться.","Но здесь нельзя́ гро́мко говори́ть.","Ка́ждый студе́нт до́лжен показа́ть студе́нческий биле́т.","Э́ти кни́ги нельзя́ взять домо́й.","Мне о́чень нра́вится здесь занима́ться."],"translation":"The library is in the centre of the university. Here you may read and study. But you mustn't talk loudly here. Every student must show a student card. These books can't be taken home. I really like studying here."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — lay down some rules.","prompt":"Describe the rules of a place you know — your work, your home, a library, a gym. What must you do there? What may you do? What mustn't you do?","hint":"Use <b>до́лжен / должна́ / должны́</b> for “must” (it agrees with the subject), <b>мо́жно</b> for “may”, and <b>нельзя́</b> for “mustn't”. A standing rule after <b>нельзя́</b> takes the imperfective — <b>нельзя́ кури́ть</b> 🙂","example":"В на́шей библиоте́ке есть свои́ пра́вила. Здесь мо́жно чита́ть и занима́ться, но нельзя́ гро́мко говори́ть. Ка́ждый студе́нт до́лжен показа́ть биле́т. Кни́ги нельзя́ взять домо́й. Мне здесь о́чень нра́вится."}},"28":{"day":28,"week":"04","themeRu":"The one who…","themeEn":"…, кото́рый…","intro":"Emma's world is filling with people and places she wants to point out — not just <em>a friend</em>, but <em>the friend who lives nearby</em>; not just <em>a book</em>, but <em>the book she's reading</em>. For that you need a joining word: <b>кото́рый</b>. We'll take it gently — it sits right on the A2/B1 border — but by the end you'll link two thoughts into one the way Russians do.","introAudio":"Emma's world is filling with people and places she wants to point out — not just a friend, but the friend who lives nearby; not just a book, but the book she's reading. For that you need a joining word: kotoryy. We'll take it gently — it sits right on the A2 to B1 border — but by the end you'll link two thoughts into one the way Russians do.","goals":["Join two sentences with <b>кото́рый</b> — “who / which / that” — <b>челове́к, кото́рый живёт здесь</b>","Match the noun: <b>кото́рый / кото́рая / кото́рое / кото́рые</b> by gender and number","See the honest edge: gender comes from the noun, but the <em>case</em> comes from the clause — <b>кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю</b>","Keep two English habits in check: never drop it, and always put a comma before it"],"learned":["Joined two sentences with <b>кото́рый</b> — “who / which / that”","Matched the noun: <b>кото́рый / кото́рая / кото́рое / кото́рые</b> by gender and number","Saw the honest edge: gender from the noun, case from the clause — <b>кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю</b>","Kept two English habits in check: never dropped it, always a comma before it"],"review":{"intro":"A relative clause reuses everything — every case, and past aspect too. So today's warm-up gathers a few loose threads before we weave them together.","introAudio":"A relative clause reuses everything — every case, and past aspect too. So today's warm-up gathers a few loose threads before we weave them together.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of this is Russian you already own.","q":"“I see my brother.” — Я ви́жу ___.","opts":["брат","бра́та","бра́ту"],"answer":1,"explain":"A person as the object goes in the accusative, which copies the genitive for he-words: брат → <b>бра́та</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“He's older than me.” — Он ста́рше ___.","opts":["меня́","я","мне"],"answer":0,"explain":"“than” after a comparative can be the bare genitive: ста́рше <b>меня́</b> (older than me)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Don't be late!”</b> (to a friend)","want":"Не опа́здывай","answer":"Не опа́здывай"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · One sentence out of two","title":"<em>Кото́рый</em> — “who / which / that”","paras":["Often you want to add a detail about a noun: not just <em>a person</em>, but <em>the person who lives here</em>. Russian glues the two thoughts with <b>кото́рый</b> — one word covering English “who”, “which”, and “that”.","Watch it form: <b>Э́то челове́к. Он живёт здесь.</b> → <b>Э́то челове́к, кото́рый живёт здесь.</b> The second sentence tucks in behind a comma, and <b>он</b> turns into <b>кото́рый</b>. The comma is not optional in Russian.","In this first, most common shape, <b>кото́рый</b> is the <em>subject</em> of its own little clause — the one doing the verb (who <em>lives</em>, who <em>works</em>). That's exactly where we start."],"audio":"Often you want to add a detail about a noun: not just a person, but the person who lives here. Russian glues the two thoughts with kotoryy — one word covering English who, which, and that. Watch it form: eto chelovek. On zhivyot zdes. Becomes eto chelovek, kotoryy zhivyot zdes. The second sentence tucks in behind a comma, and on turns into kotoryy. The comma is not optional in Russian. In this first, most common shape, kotoryy is the subject of its own little clause — the one doing the verb, who lives, who works. That's exactly where we start.","table":{"rows":[["Э́то челове́к. Он живёт здесь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это человек. Он живёт здесь.\">🔊</button>","two sentences"],["Э́то челове́к, кото́рый живёт здесь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это человек, который живёт здесь.\">🔊</button>","→ one (он → кото́рый)"],["друг, кото́рый живёт в Москве́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друг, который живёт в Москве\">🔊</button>","the friend who lives in Moscow"],["студе́нт, кото́рый хорошо́ у́чится <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"студент, который хорошо учится\">🔊</button>","the student who studies well"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"This is the man who works here.","en":"Э́то челове́к, <b>кото́рый</b> рабо́тает здесь.","say":"Это человек, который работает здесь."},{"ru":"I have a friend who lives in Moscow.","en":"У меня́ есть друг, <b>кото́рый</b> живёт в Москве́.","say":"У меня есть друг, который живёт в Москве."},{"ru":"This is the bus that goes to the centre.","en":"Э́то авто́бус, <b>кото́рый</b> идёт в центр.","say":"Это автобус, который идёт в центр."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́то челове́к живёт здесь.","right":"Э́то челове́к, кото́рый живёт здесь.","why":"You can't just stack two verbs. To add “who lives here”, you need <b>кото́рый</b> and a comma — Russian never leaves it out."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“the friend who lives here” — друг, ___ живёт здесь.","opts":["кото́рый","кото́рая","кото́рые"],"answer":0,"explain":"друг is a he-word → <b>кото́рый</b>. Match the noun before the comma."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“This is the student who studies here.”</b>","want":"Э́то студе́нт, кото́рый у́чится здесь","answer":"Э́то студе́нт, кото́рый у́чится здесь"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it in Russian. The comma matters, but plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “the person who works here” (челове́к, …):","accept":["человек который работает здесь","человек, который работает здесь"],"placeholder":"челове́к, …","explain":"челове́к is a he-word → <b>кото́рый</b>: челове́к, кото́рый рабо́тает здесь."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · It matches the noun","title":"<em>Кото́рый / кото́рая / кото́рое / кото́рые</em>","paras":["<b>Кото́рый</b> has four shapes, and it takes the one that matches the noun it points back to, in gender and number. A he-word → <b>кото́рый</b>, a she-word → <b>кото́рая</b>, an it-word → <b>кото́рое</b>, a plural → <b>кото́рые</b>. Just like <b>мой / моя́ / моё / мои́</b>.","So the noun in front chooses the shape: <b>челове́к, кото́рый…</b> (he-word), <b>же́нщина, кото́рая…</b> (she-word), <b>письмо́, кото́рое…</b> (it-word), <b>лю́ди, кото́рые…</b> (plural).","Notice it looks <em>backward</em> at the noun, not forward at anything else. Find the noun just before the comma, name its gender, and <b>кото́рый</b> dresses to match."],"audio":"Kotoryy has four shapes, and it takes the one that matches the noun it points back to, in gender and number. A he-word becomes kotoryy, a she-word kotoraya, an it-word kotoroye, a plural kotoryye. Just like moy, moya, moyo, moi. So the noun in front chooses the shape: chelovek, kotoryy; zhenshchina, kotoraya; pismo, kotoroye; lyudi, kotoryye. Notice it looks backward at the noun, not forward at anything else. Find the noun just before the comma, name its gender, and kotoryy dresses to match.","table":{"rows":[["челове́к, кото́рый… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"человек, который\">🔊</button>","he-word → кото́рый"],["же́нщина, кото́рая… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"женщина, которая\">🔊</button>","she-word → кото́рая"],["письмо́, кото́рое… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"письмо, которое\">🔊</button>","it-word → кото́рое"],["лю́ди, кото́рые… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"люди, которые\">🔊</button>","plural → кото́рые"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"This is the girl who works at the café.","en":"Э́то де́вушка, <b>кото́рая</b> рабо́тает в кафе́.","say":"Это девушка, которая работает в кафе."},{"ru":"Here's the letter that came today.","en":"Вот письмо́, <b>кото́рое</b> пришло́ сего́дня.","say":"Вот письмо, которое пришло сегодня."},{"ru":"These are the people who study with me.","en":"Э́то лю́ди, <b>кото́рые</b> у́чатся со мной.","say":"Это люди, которые учатся со мной.","gloss":"со мной = with me (instrumental)"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"же́нщина, кото́рый рабо́тает тут","right":"же́нщина, кото́рая рабо́тает тут","why":"The noun is <b>же́нщина</b>, a she-word, so it must be <b>кото́рая</b>. Match the gender of the noun before the comma."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“the girl who works here” — де́вушка, ___ рабо́тает здесь.","opts":["кото́рый","кото́рая","кото́рое"],"answer":1,"explain":"де́вушка is a she-word → <b>кото́рая</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“the people who live here” — лю́ди, ___ живу́т здесь.","opts":["кото́рый","кото́рая","кото́рые"],"answer":2,"explain":"A plural noun (лю́ди) → <b>кото́рые</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Here's the letter that came today.”</b>","want":"Вот письмо́, кото́рое пришло́ сего́дня","answer":"Вот письмо́, кото́рое пришло́ сего́дня"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Gender from the noun, case from the clause","title":"Why it's <em>кото́рую</em> in <em>кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю</em>","paras":["Here's the part that makes <b>кото́рый</b> cleverer than English “that”. Its <em>gender and number</em> come from the noun it replaces — but its <em>case</em> comes from the job it does inside its own clause. Two different sources, and that's the honest edge of A2.","Look: <b>кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю</b> — “the book that I'm reading”. кни́га is a she-word, so we start from <b>кото́рая</b>; but inside the clause it's the <em>object</em> of чита́ю (I'm reading <em>it</em>), so it steps into the accusative: <b>кото́рую</b>. Feminine from the noun, accusative from the clause.","Same with a person you're waiting for: <b>друг, кото́рого я жду</b> — “the friend I'm waiting for”. Masculine from <b>друг</b>, but the accusative <b>кото́рого</b> because he's the object of жду. You don't have to produce every case yet — just see why the ending can shift."],"audio":"Here's the part that makes kotoryy cleverer than English that. Its gender and number come from the noun it replaces — but its case comes from the job it does inside its own clause. Two different sources, and that's the honest edge of A2. Look: kniga, kotoruyu ya chitayu — the book that I'm reading. Kniga is a she-word, so we start from kotoraya; but inside the clause it's the object of chitayu, so it steps into the accusative: kotoruyu. Feminine from the noun, accusative from the clause. Same with a person you're waiting for: drug, kotorogo ya zhdu — the friend I'm waiting for. Masculine from drug, but the accusative kotorogo because he's the object of zhdu.","table":{"rows":[["друг, кото́рый живёт здесь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друг, который живёт здесь\">🔊</button>","subject → кото́рый (nom.)"],["кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга, которую я читаю\">🔊</button>","object → кото́рую (acc., she-word)"],["друг, кото́рого я жду <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друг, которого я жду\">🔊</button>","object, a person → кото́рого (acc.)"],["фильм, кото́рый я люблю́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"фильм, который я люблю\">🔊</button>","object, a thing → кото́рый (unchanged)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"This is the book that I'm reading.","en":"Э́то кни́га, <b>кото́рую</b> я чита́ю.","say":"Это книга, которую я читаю."},{"ru":"The film that I love is Russian.","en":"Фильм, <b>кото́рый</b> я люблю́, — ру́сский.","say":"Фильм, который я люблю, русский."},{"ru":"This is the friend I'm waiting for.","en":"Э́то друг, <b>кото́рого</b> я жду.","say":"Это друг, которого я жду."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"кни́га, кото́рый я чита́ю","right":"кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю","why":"кни́га is a she-word and it's the <em>object</em> here (I read it) → accusative <b>кото́рую</b>. Gender from the noun, case from the job."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“the book that I'm reading” — кни́га, ___ я чита́ю.","opts":["кото́рый","кото́рую","кото́рая"],"answer":1,"explain":"She-word (from кни́га) + object of чита́ю (accusative) → <b>кото́рую</b>."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Э́то кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю.","sub":"say: this is the book that I'm reading","want":"это книга которую я читаю"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “the friend I'm waiting for” (друг, … жду):","accept":["друг которого я жду","друг, которого я жду"],"placeholder":"друг, …","explain":"A person as the object → accusative <b>кото́рого</b>: друг, кото́рого я жду."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Never drop it, always comma it","title":"English drops “that” — Russian keeps <em>кото́рый</em>","paras":["English lets you drop the linking word: “the book <em>I read</em>”, “the man <em>I saw</em>” — no “that” needed. Russian can't. <b>Кото́рый</b> must be there: <b>кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю</b>, <b>челове́к, кото́рого я ви́дел</b>. Feel a gap where English would drop it? Fill it with кото́рый.","And it always rides behind a <b>comma</b>. Wherever the extra clause begins, a comma opens it: <b>Я зна́ю де́вушку, кото́рая живёт здесь.</b> Miss the comma and it reads wrong to a Russian eye.","That's the whole toolkit for now — one connector, four shapes, a comma, and the quiet rule that its case can shift inside the clause. Enough to describe every person and place in Emma's new life."],"audio":"English lets you drop the linking word: the book I read, the man I saw — no that needed. Russian can't. Kotoryy must be there: kniga, kotoruyu ya chitayu; chelovek, kotorogo ya videl. Feel a gap where English would drop it? Fill it with kotoryy. And it always rides behind a comma. Wherever the extra clause begins, a comma opens it: ya znayu devushku, kotoraya zhivyot zdes. Miss the comma and it reads wrong to a Russian eye. That's the whole toolkit for now — one connector, four shapes, a comma, and the quiet rule that its case can shift inside the clause.","examples":[{"ru":"The book I'm reading is interesting.","en":"Кни́га, <b>кото́рую</b> я чита́ю, интере́сная.","say":"Книга, которую я читаю, интересная."},{"ru":"I know a girl who lives here.","en":"Я зна́ю де́вушку, <b>кото́рая</b> живёт здесь.","say":"Я знаю девушку, которая живёт здесь.","gloss":"де́вушку = accusative (I know her); кото́рая = subject of its own clause"},{"ru":"The friend I'm waiting for is late.","en":"Друг, <b>кото́рого</b> я жду, опа́здывает.","say":"Друг, которого я жду, опаздывает."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"кни́га я чита́ю","right":"кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю","why":"English can drop “that”; Russian can't. Put <b>кото́рую</b> and a comma back in: <b>кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is correct Russian for “the girl who lives here”?","opts":["де́вушка кото́рая живёт здесь","де́вушка, кото́рая живёт здесь","де́вушка живёт здесь"],"answer":1,"explain":"A comma opens the clause, and кото́рая can't be dropped: <b>де́вушка, кото́рая живёт здесь</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I know a girl who lives here.”</b>","want":"Я зна́ю де́вушку, кото́рая живёт здесь","answer":"Я зна́ю де́вушку, кото́рая живёт здесь"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ivan is looking through Emma's photos. She points out the new people and places in her life. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, кто э́то на фотогра́фии?","ru":"Emma, who's this in the photo? (фотогра́фия = photo)"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то де́вушка, кото́рая у́чится со мной. Её зову́т Мари́я.","ru":"That's the girl who studies with me. Her name is Maria."},{"who":"B","en":"Краси́вая. А э́то — твой университе́т?","ru":"Pretty. And this — is that your university?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, э́то университе́т, кото́рый нахо́дится в це́нтре.","ru":"Yes, that's the university, which is in the centre."},{"who":"B","en":"А кни́га? Э́то кни́га, кото́рую ты чита́ешь?","ru":"And the book? Is that the book you're reading?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да. Э́то кни́га, кото́рую написа́л мой преподава́тель.","ru":"Yes. It's the book that my teacher wrote. (преподава́тель = teacher)"},{"who":"B","en":"Здо́рово! У тебя́ тепе́рь интере́сная жизнь.","ru":"Great! You have an interesting life now."},{"who":"A","en":"Да. Мне о́чень нра́вятся лю́ди, кото́рые здесь живу́т.","ru":"Yes. I really like the people who live here."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the people and places around Emma. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"кото́рый","ru":"who, which, that (he-word)","ex":"челове́к, кото́рый живёт здесь"},{"en":"кото́рая","ru":"who, which, that (she-word)","ex":"де́вушка, кото́рая рабо́тает тут"},{"en":"кото́рое","ru":"who, which, that (it-word)","ex":"письмо́, кото́рое пришло́"},{"en":"кото́рые","ru":"who, which, that (plural)","ex":"лю́ди, кото́рые живу́т тут"},{"en":"челове́к","ru":"person","ex":"Э́то до́брый челове́к."},{"en":"же́нщина","ru":"woman","ex":"Э́то же́нщина из Москвы́."},{"en":"мужчи́на","ru":"man","ex":"Э́то мужчи́на, кото́рый рабо́тает тут."},{"en":"преподава́тель","ru":"teacher, lecturer","ex":"Наш преподава́тель — ру́сский."},{"en":"зда́ние","ru":"building","ex":"Э́то ста́рое зда́ние."},{"en":"сосе́д","ru":"neighbour","ex":"Мой сосе́д о́чень до́брый."},{"en":"лю́ди","ru":"people","ex":"Здесь до́брые лю́ди."},{"en":"жизнь","ru":"life","ex":"У меня́ интере́сная жизнь."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["челове́к, кото́рый живёт здесь","the person who lives here"],["де́вушка, кото́рая рабо́тает тут","the girl who works here"],["кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю","the book that I'm reading"],["лю́ди, кото́рые у́чатся тут","the people who study here"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“the person who lives here” — челове́к, ___ живёт здесь.","opts":["кото́рая","кото́рый","кото́рые"],"answer":1},{"q":"“the girl who works here” — де́вушка, ___ рабо́тает здесь.","opts":["кото́рая","кото́рый","кото́рое"],"answer":0},{"q":"“the book that I'm reading” — кни́га, ___ я чита́ю.","opts":["кото́рый","кото́рая","кото́рую"],"answer":2},{"q":"“the people who study here” — лю́ди, ___ у́чатся здесь.","opts":["кото́рые","кото́рый","кото́рая"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which is correct — “I know a girl who lives here”?","opts":["Я зна́ю де́вушка, кото́рая живёт здесь.","Я зна́ю де́вушку, кото́рая живёт здесь.","Я зна́ю де́вушку кото́рая живёт здесь."],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma writes about the people who fill her Moscow life now — read it aloud.","title":"Мои́ но́вые друзья́","sentences":["У меня́ тепе́рь мно́го друзе́й в Москве́.","Мари́я — э́то де́вушка, кото́рая у́чится со мной.","Ива́н — мой друг, кото́рый всегда́ помога́ет мне.","Кни́га, кото́рую я чита́ю, о́чень интере́сная.","Университе́т, кото́рый нахо́дится в це́нтре, о́чень краси́вый.","Мне нра́вятся лю́ди, кото́рые здесь живу́т."],"translation":"I now have a lot of friends in Moscow. Maria is the girl who studies with me. Ivan is my friend who always helps me. The book that I'm reading is very interesting. The university, which is in the centre, is very beautiful. I like the people who live here."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — point people and places out with кото́рый.","prompt":"Describe two or three people or places in your life using <b>кото́рый</b>. Who's the friend who helps you? What's the book that you're reading? Where's the café that you love?","hint":"Pin the noun first, then add <b>, кото́рый / кото́рая / кото́рое / кото́рые …</b> to match its gender and number. Don't drop it, and don't forget the comma before it 🙂","example":"У меня́ есть друг, кото́рый живёт в Москве́. Есть де́вушка, кото́рая у́чится со мной. Кни́га, кото́рую я сейча́с чита́ю, о́чень интере́сная. А кафе́, кото́рое нахо́дится ря́дом, — моё люби́мое ме́сто."}},"29":{"day":29,"week":"04","themeRu":"My life story","themeEn":"Моя́ жизнь","intro":"You've spent a month gathering the pieces — six cases, both pasts and futures, commands, comparisons. Today no new grammar arrives; today you learn to <em>connect</em>. Emma sits down to tell her whole story — where she started, why she came, where she's headed — and you'll pick up the little words that turn scattered sentences into one flowing tale: <b>снача́ла, пото́м, поэ́тому</b>.","introAudio":"You've spent a month gathering the pieces — six cases, both pasts and futures, commands, comparisons. Today no new grammar arrives; today you learn to connect. Emma sits down to tell her whole story — where she started, why she came, where she's headed — and you'll pick up the little words that turn scattered sentences into one flowing tale: snachala, potom, poetomu.","goals":["Sequence a story with <b>снача́ла</b> (first), <b>пото́м</b> (then), <b>по́сле э́того</b> (after that)","Link reason and result: <b>поэ́тому</b> (so, that's why) and <b>потому́ что</b> (because)","Keep your aspect steady across a story — imperfective for the backdrop, perfective for each finished step","Weave past, present and plans into one connected account of your life"],"learned":["Sequenced a story with <b>снача́ла, пото́м, по́сле э́того</b>","Linked reason and result with <b>поэ́тому</b> and <b>потому́ что</b>","Kept aspect steady across a story — imperfective backdrop, perfective steps","Wove past, present and plans into one connected account"],"review":{"intro":"A life story leans on everything at once — cases, past aspect, the future. So today's warm-up loosens up those exact muscles before Emma tells hers.","introAudio":"A life story leans on everything at once — cases, past aspect, the future. So today's warm-up loosens up those exact muscles before Emma tells hers.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. All of this is Russian you already own.","q":"“Tomorrow I'll be working.” — За́втра я ___.","opts":["бу́ду рабо́тать","рабо́таю","рабо́тал"],"answer":0,"explain":"An ongoing action in the future → <b>бу́ду</b> + imperfective: бу́ду рабо́тать."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm going to the doctor.” — Я иду́ ___.","opts":["к врачу́","врача́","врачо́м"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heading toward a person → <b>к</b> + dative: к врачу́."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“I lived in London.”</b> (a woman)","want":"Я жила́ в Ло́ндоне","answer":"Я жила́ в Ло́ндоне"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Telling it in order","title":"<em>Снача́ла… пото́м… по́сле э́того…</em>","paras":["A story is just sentences in an order — and Russian marks that order with a few small signposts. <b>Снача́ла</b> = “first / at first”, <b>пото́м</b> = “then / next”, <b>по́сле э́того</b> = “after that”. Drop them at the front of a sentence and a list of events becomes a timeline.","<b>Снача́ла я жила́ в Ло́ндоне, пото́м прие́хала в Москву́, а по́сле э́того начала́ рабо́тать.</b> — First I lived in London, then I came to Moscow, and after that I started working.","None of the grammar under them is new — it's your past tense and your cases. The signposts just line the sentences up so a listener can follow you from start to finish."],"audio":"A story is just sentences in an order — and Russian marks that order with a few small signposts. Snachala means first, or at first; potom means then, or next; posle etogo means after that. Drop them at the front of a sentence and a list of events becomes a timeline. Snachala ya zhila v Londone, potom priyekhala v Moskvu, a posle etogo nachala rabotat — first I lived in London, then I came to Moscow, and after that I started working. None of the grammar under them is new — it's your past tense and your cases. The signposts just line the sentences up.","table":{"rows":[["снача́ла <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сначала\">🔊</button>","first, at first"],["пото́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"потом\">🔊</button>","then, next"],["по́сле э́того <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"после этого\">🔊</button>","after that"],["Снача́ла ко́фе, пото́м рабо́та. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сначала кофе, потом работа.\">🔊</button>","First coffee, then work."]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"At first it was hard, but then it got easier.","en":"<b>Снача́ла</b> бы́ло тру́дно, но <b>пото́м</b> ста́ло ле́гче.","say":"Сначала было трудно, но потом стало легче.","gloss":"ле́гче = easier (comparative)"},{"ru":"First I'll have lunch, then I'll read.","en":"<b>Снача́ла</b> я пообе́даю, <b>пото́м</b> бу́ду чита́ть.","say":"Сначала я пообедаю, потом буду читать."},{"ru":"After that we went home.","en":"<b>По́сле э́того</b> мы пошли́ домо́й.","say":"После этого мы пошли домой."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я жила́ в Ло́ндоне. Я прие́хала в Москву́. Я начала́ рабо́тать.","right":"Снача́ла я жила́ в Ло́ндоне, пото́м прие́хала в Москву́, по́сле э́того начала́ рабо́тать.","why":"The bare sentences are all correct, but they don't <em>flow</em>. The signposts <b>снача́ла / пото́м / по́сле э́того</b> tie them into one story."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the question and tap the answer. A wrong tap is just part of learning.","q":"“First I lived in London…” — ___ я жила́ в Ло́ндоне.","opts":["Снача́ла","Пото́м","По́сле"],"answer":0,"explain":"The very first step → <b>Снача́ла</b>. пото́м and по́сле come later in the story."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Then I came to Moscow.”</b> (a woman)","want":"Пото́м я прие́хала в Москву́","answer":"Пото́м я прие́хала в Москву́"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type it in Russian. Plain letters are fine — the stress marks are just for you.","q":"Type the connector “after that”:","accept":["после этого"],"placeholder":"по́сле…","explain":"<b>по́сле э́того</b> — literally “after this”, our “after that”."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Reason and result","title":"<em>Поэ́тому</em> (so) vs <em>потому́ что</em> (because)","paras":["Two look-alikes that point opposite ways. <b>Поэ́тому</b> = “so / that's why” — it introduces the <em>result</em>. <b>Потому́ что</b> = “because” — it introduces the <em>reason</em>. Same facts, told from two ends.","<b>Я люблю́ Росси́ю, поэ́тому я прие́хала сюда́.</b> — I love Russia, <em>so</em> I came here (result). <b>Я прие́хала сюда́, потому́ что люблю́ Росси́ю.</b> — I came here <em>because</em> I love Russia (reason).","Pick by what comes next: is it the outcome (<b>поэ́тому</b>) or the cause (<b>потому́ что</b>)? English uses two different words too — just make sure you don't swap them."],"audio":"Two look-alikes that point opposite ways. Poetomu means so, or that's why — it introduces the result. Potomu chto means because — it introduces the reason. Same facts, told from two ends. Ya lyublyu Rossiyu, poetomu ya priyekhala syuda — I love Russia, so I came here. Ya priyekhala syuda, potomu chto lyublyu Rossiyu — I came here because I love Russia. Pick by what comes next: is it the outcome, poetomu, or the cause, potomu chto? Just make sure you don't swap them.","table":{"rows":[["…, поэ́тому… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"поэтому\">🔊</button>","…, so / that's why… (result)"],["…, потому́ что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"потому что\">🔊</button>","…, because… (reason)"],["Бы́ло по́здно, поэ́тому я оста́лась. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Было поздно, поэтому я осталась.\">🔊</button>","It was late, so I stayed."],["Я оста́лась, потому́ что бы́ло по́здно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я осталась, потому что было поздно.\">🔊</button>","I stayed because it was late."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I love Russia, so I came here.","en":"Я люблю́ Росси́ю, <b>поэ́тому</b> я прие́хала сюда́.","say":"Я люблю Россию, поэтому я приехала сюда."},{"ru":"I came here because I love the language.","en":"Я прие́хала сюда́, <b>потому́ что</b> люблю́ язы́к.","say":"Я приехала сюда, потому что люблю язык."},{"ru":"I was tired, that's why I stayed home.","en":"Я уста́ла, <b>поэ́тому</b> оста́лась до́ма.","say":"Я устала, поэтому осталась дома."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я прие́хала, поэ́тому люблю́ Росси́ю.","right":"Я прие́хала, потому́ что люблю́ Росси́ю.","why":"The love is the <em>reason</em> for coming, not the result — so it needs <b>потому́ что</b> (because), not поэ́тому (so)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I love Russia, so I came here.” — Я люблю́ Росси́ю, ___ я прие́хала сюда́.","opts":["пото́м","потому́ что","поэ́тому"],"answer":2,"explain":"Coming here is the <em>result</em> → <b>поэ́тому</b> (so, that's why)."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I came because I love the language.” — Я прие́хала, ___ люблю́ язы́к.","opts":["поэ́тому","снача́ла","потому́ что"],"answer":2,"explain":"Loving the language is the <em>reason</em> → <b>потому́ что</b> (because)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“It was late, so I stayed home.”</b> (a woman)","want":"Бы́ло по́здно, поэ́тому я оста́лась до́ма","answer":"Бы́ло по́здно, поэ́тому я оста́лась до́ма"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · One story, steady aspect","title":"Backdrop vs steps — <em>жила́</em> then <em>реши́ла</em>","paras":["The trap of a long story is letting the aspect wander. Hold it steady. Use the <b>imperfective</b> past for the backdrop — the ongoing, the repeated, the “used to”: <b>Я жила́ в Ло́ндоне</b> (I lived / used to live there). Use the <b>perfective</b> past for each single, finished step that moves the story: <b>Пото́м я реши́ла прие́хать</b> (then I decided to come).","So the two braid together: a stretch of backdrop (imperfective), then a step (perfective), then more backdrop, then the next step. <b>Снача́ла бы́ло тру́дно</b> (backdrop), <b>но пото́м я познако́милась с людьми́</b> (step).","This is the same choice you drilled on day 16 — now stretched across a whole paragraph. Keep asking: is this the scene, or a step that happened once? The answer picks your aspect."],"audio":"The trap of a long story is letting the aspect wander. Hold it steady. Use the imperfective past for the backdrop — the ongoing, the repeated, the used-to: ya zhila v Londone, I lived, or used to live, there. Use the perfective past for each single, finished step that moves the story: potom ya reshila priyekhat, then I decided to come. So the two braid together: a stretch of backdrop, then a step, then more backdrop, then the next step. Snachala bylo trudno, but potom ya poznakomilas s lyudmi. This is the same choice you drilled on day sixteen — now stretched across a whole paragraph. Keep asking: is this the scene, or a step that happened once?","table":{"rows":[["Я жила́ в Ло́ндоне. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я жила в Лондоне.\">🔊</button>","backdrop → imperfective жила́"],["Пото́м я реши́ла прие́хать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Потом я решила приехать.\">🔊</button>","one step → perfective реши́ла"],["Ка́ждый день я учи́ла слова́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Каждый день я учила слова.\">🔊</button>","repeated → imperfective учи́ла"],["В ма́е я сдала́ экза́мен. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"В мае я сдала экзамен.\">🔊</button>","one result → perfective сдала́"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I used to live in London and studied at school there.","en":"Я <b>жила́</b> в Ло́ндоне и <b>учи́лась</b> там в шко́ле.","say":"Я жила в Лондоне и училась там в школе."},{"ru":"Then one day I decided to come to Moscow.","en":"Пото́м одна́жды я <b>реши́ла</b> прие́хать в Москву́.","say":"Потом однажды я решила приехать в Москву.","gloss":"одна́жды = one day, once"},{"ru":"At first it was hard, but then I got used to it.","en":"Снача́ла <b>бы́ло</b> тру́дно, но пото́м я <b>привы́кла</b>.","say":"Сначала было трудно, но потом я привыкла.","gloss":"привы́кла = got used to it"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Пото́м я реша́ла прие́хать в Москву́.","right":"Пото́м я реши́ла прие́хать в Москву́.","why":"Deciding to come was one finished step → perfective <b>реши́ла</b>. The imperfective реша́ла would mean “kept on deciding”, which breaks the flow of the story."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"One finished step: “Then I decided to come.” — Пото́м я ___ прие́хать.","opts":["реша́ла","реши́ла","реша́ю"],"answer":1,"explain":"A single completed step → perfective <b>реши́ла</b>. реша́ла (impf.) would mean “kept deciding”."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Снача́ла бы́ло тру́дно, но пото́м ста́ло ле́гче.","sub":"say: at first it was hard, but then it got easier","want":"сначала было трудно но потом стало легче"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I lived in London” (a woman, the ongoing backdrop):","accept":["я жила в лондоне","жила в лондоне"],"placeholder":"я жила́…","explain":"The backdrop of a life → imperfective past: <b>Я жила́ в Ло́ндоне</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · From past to plans","title":"Endings carry it — put your life in one line","paras":["Now stitch the whole arc: where you were (past), where you are (present), where you're going (future). <b>Снача́ла я жила́ в Ло́ндоне, тепе́рь я живу́ в Москве́, а по́сле э́того го́да я бу́ду рабо́тать здесь.</b>","Because Russian marks who-does-what with <em>endings</em>, your word order can flex without breaking. <b>Ру́сский язы́к я начала́ учи́ть в Ло́ндоне</b> and <b>Я начала́ учи́ть ру́сский язы́к в Ло́ндоне</b> both work — the endings, not the order, hold the meaning together.","That's the quiet power you've built all level: six cases, two aspects, two futures, all pulling together. Tomorrow you'll gather it into one portrait — «о себе́»."],"audio":"Now stitch the whole arc: where you were, where you are, where you're going. Snachala ya zhila v Londone, teper ya zhivu v Moskve, a posle etogo goda ya budu rabotat zdes. Because Russian marks who-does-what with endings, your word order can flex without breaking. Russkiy yazyk ya nachala uchit v Londone, and ya nachala uchit russkiy yazyk v Londone, both work — the endings, not the order, hold the meaning together. That's the quiet power you've built all level: six cases, two aspects, two futures, all pulling together. Tomorrow you'll gather it into one portrait — o sebe.","table":{"rows":[["Снача́ла я жила́ в Ло́ндоне. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сначала я жила в Лондоне.\">🔊</button>","past — where I was"],["Тепе́рь я живу́ в Москве́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Теперь я живу в Москве.\">🔊</button>","present — where I am"],["Пото́м я бу́ду рабо́тать здесь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Потом я буду работать здесь.\">🔊</button>","future — where I'm going"],["По́сле э́того го́да я оста́нусь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"После этого года я останусь.\">🔊</button>","after this year I'll stay"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"After this year I want to work here.","en":"<b>По́сле</b> э́того го́да я хочу́ рабо́тать здесь.","say":"После этого года я хочу работать здесь."},{"ru":"I'll go on learning the language.","en":"Я <b>бу́ду</b> учи́ть язы́к да́льше.","say":"Я буду учить язык дальше.","gloss":"да́льше = further, on"},{"ru":"Now Moscow is my home.","en":"Тепе́рь Москва́ — мой дом.","say":"Теперь Москва мой дом."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"По́сле э́того год я бу́ду рабо́тать.","right":"По́сле э́того го́да я бу́ду рабо́тать.","why":"<b>по́сле</b> takes the genitive: год → <b>го́да</b>. The ending, not the order, carries “after this year”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“After this year I want to work here.” — ___ э́того го́да я хочу́ рабо́тать здесь.","opts":["По́сле","Снача́ла","Поэ́тому"],"answer":0,"explain":"“after” + genitive → <b>По́сле</b> э́того го́да."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'll go on learning Russian.”</b>","want":"Я бу́ду учи́ть ру́сский да́льше","answer":"Я бу́ду учи́ть ру́сский да́льше"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At a student gathering, someone new asks Emma to tell her story. She lays it out from the start. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, а отку́да ты? Расскажи́ о себе́.","ru":"Emma, where are you from? Tell me about yourself."},{"who":"A","en":"Снача́ла я жила́ в Ло́ндоне. Пото́м я начала́ учи́ть ру́сский.","ru":"First I lived in London. Then I started learning Russian."},{"who":"B","en":"И почему́ ру́сский?","ru":"And why Russian?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я о́чень люблю́ ру́сскую культу́ру, поэ́тому я прие́хала сюда́.","ru":"I really love Russian culture, so I came here."},{"who":"B","en":"И как тебе́ Москва́?","ru":"And how do you like Moscow?"},{"who":"A","en":"Снача́ла бы́ло тру́дно, но пото́м я познако́милась с людьми́ и полюби́ла го́род.","ru":"At first it was hard, but then I met people and grew to love the city. (с людьми́ = with people)"},{"who":"B","en":"А что да́льше?","ru":"And what next?"},{"who":"A","en":"По́сле э́того го́да я хочу́ рабо́тать здесь. Я бу́ду учи́ть язы́к да́льше.","ru":"After this year I want to work here. I'll go on learning the language."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for telling your story — connectors and the shape of a life. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"снача́ла","ru":"first, at first","ex":"Снача́ла я жил в Ло́ндоне."},{"en":"пото́м","ru":"then, next","ex":"Пото́м я прие́хал сюда́."},{"en":"по́сле э́того","ru":"after that","ex":"По́сле э́того я на́чал рабо́тать."},{"en":"поэ́тому","ru":"so, that's why","ex":"Бы́ло по́здно, поэ́тому я оста́лся."},{"en":"потому́ что","ru":"because","ex":"Я рад, потому́ что сдал экза́мен."},{"en":"культу́ра","ru":"culture","ex":"Я люблю́ ру́сскую культу́ру."},{"en":"реши́ть","ru":"to decide (pf.)","ex":"Я реши́л прие́хать."},{"en":"нача́ть","ru":"to begin, start (pf.)","ex":"Я на́чал учи́ть ру́сский."},{"en":"роди́ться","ru":"to be born","ex":"Я родила́сь в Ло́ндоне."},{"en":"де́тство","ru":"childhood","ex":"В де́тстве я жил у мо́ря."},{"en":"пла́ны","ru":"plans","ex":"У меня́ больши́е пла́ны."},{"en":"да́льше","ru":"further, next, on","ex":"Что бу́дет да́льше?"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["снача́ла","first, at first"],["пото́м","then, next"],["поэ́тому я оста́лся","so I stayed"],["потому́ что я люблю́ го́род","because I love the city"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Which pair puts a story in order — “first…, then…”?","opts":["пото́м … снача́ла","поэ́тому … потому́","снача́ла … пото́м"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I love the city, so I stayed.” — Я люблю́ го́род, ___ оста́лась.","opts":["потому́ что","поэ́тому","снача́ла"],"answer":1},{"q":"Backdrop of a story — “I used to live here.” — Я ___ здесь.","opts":["жила́","пожила́","бу́ду жить"],"answer":0},{"q":"One finished step — “Then I came to Moscow.” — Пото́м я ___ в Москву́.","opts":["е́хала","прие́хала","е́ду"],"answer":1},{"q":"“after this year” — ___ э́того го́да.","opts":["По́сле","Снача́ла","Пото́м"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's whole story, told in six lines — read it aloud. It's your model for the essay.","title":"Моя́ жизнь","sentences":["Я родила́сь в Ло́ндоне и жила́ там мно́го лет.","Снача́ла я не зна́ла ру́сский язы́к.","Пото́м я начала́ учи́ть его́, потому́ что люблю́ Росси́ю.","Год наза́д я прие́хала в Москву́ и познако́милась с Ива́ном.","Снача́ла бы́ло тру́дно, но по́сле э́того ста́ло ле́гче.","Тепе́рь Москва́ — мой дом, и я бу́ду жить здесь да́льше."],"translation":"I was born in London and lived there for many years. At first I didn't know Russian. Then I started learning it, because I love Russia. A year ago I came to Moscow and met Ivan. At first it was hard, but after that it got easier. Now Moscow is my home, and I'll go on living here."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — tell your own story, start to finish.","prompt":"Tell a short version of your life. Where were you born? What did you do first, and then? Why did you start learning Russian? And what's next?","hint":"String it with connectors: <b>снача́ла</b> … <b>пото́м</b> … <b>по́сле э́того</b> …, and use <b>поэ́тому</b> / <b>потому́ что</b> for the “so” and the “because”. Keep your aspect steady — imperfective for the backdrop, perfective for each finished step 🙂","example":"Я родила́сь в Ло́ндоне. Снача́ла я учи́лась в шко́ле, пото́м в университе́те. Я о́чень люби́ла ру́сскую культу́ру, поэ́тому начала́ учи́ть язы́к. По́сле э́того я прие́хала в Москву́. Здесь я познако́милась с хоро́шими людьми́. Тепе́рь я хочу́ рабо́тать здесь, и я бу́ду учи́ть ру́сский да́льше."}},"30":{"day":30,"week":"04","themeRu":"About me","themeEn":"О себе́","intro":"Thirty days ago you opened a book that turned out to be a door. Today Emma writes the piece every learner earns the right to write — <b>о себе́</b>, “about myself”: who she is, what she loves, where she's been and where she's going. Your task is the same. No new grammar today — just you, a blank page, and a whole level of Russian to pour into it.","introAudio":"Thirty days ago you opened a book that turned out to be a door. Today Emma writes the piece every learner earns the right to write — o sebe, about myself: who she is, what she loves, where she's been and where she's going. Your task is the same. No new grammar today — just you, a blank page, and a whole level of Russian to pour into it.","goals":["Introduce yourself fully — name, home, studies, work — in confident Russian","Put all six cases to work in one self-portrait, each doing its own job","Move through time: where you were, where you are, where you're going","Write <b>о себе́</b> — a warm, connected paragraph about your own life"],"learned":["Introduced yourself fully — name, home, studies, work","Put all six cases to work in one self-portrait","Moved through time: past, present and plans","Wrote <b>о себе́</b> — a connected paragraph about your own life"],"review":{"intro":"One last warm-up, and it reaches back across the whole level — a touch of each week you've climbed. Then the floor is yours.","introAudio":"One last warm-up, and it reaches back across the whole level — a touch of each week you've climbed. Then the floor is yours.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Every one of these is Russian you now own.","q":"“There's no time.” — ___ вре́мени.","opts":["Нет","Не","Нельзя́"],"answer":0,"explain":"Absence → <b>нет</b> + genitive: нет вре́мени. (вре́мя → вре́мени.)"},{"type":"choice","q":"“I work as a teacher.” (a woman) — Я рабо́таю ___.","opts":["учи́тель","учи́тельницей","учи́тельница"],"answer":1,"explain":"“work as” takes the instrumental: <b>учи́тельницей</b>. учи́тельница (nom.) would be “I am a teacher”, without рабо́таю."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'll write the letter (and finish it).” — Я ___ письмо́.","opts":["бу́ду писа́ть","напишу́","пишу́"],"answer":1,"explain":"One finished result → perfective future <b>напишу́</b>. бу́ду писа́ть is the process; пишу́ is the present."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Tell me, please.”</b> (to a friend)","want":"Скажи́ мне, пожа́луйста","answer":"Скажи́ мне, пожа́луйста"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the one word. Plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the comparative of <b>хорошо́</b> — “better”:","accept":["лучше"],"placeholder":"лу́чше…","explain":"The irregular comparative of хорошо́ is <b>лу́чше</b> — one word, no “more”."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Who I am now","title":"<em>О себе́</em> — the present-tense portrait","paras":["Start where you stand: the present. Name yourself, place yourself, say what fills your days. <b>Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. Я из Ло́ндона. Сейча́с я живу́ в Москве́ и учу́сь в университе́те.</b> Every tool here is one you've owned since A1 or built this month.","Then add what you love — a tailed verb and the instrumental: <b>Я интересу́юсь исто́рией и му́зыкой.</b> A self-portrait without your interests is just an ID card.","Notice there's no verb “to be” in the present — <b>я Э́мма</b>, <b>я из Ло́ндона</b> — no “am” anywhere. That quiet gap is pure Russian, and by now it feels natural."],"audio":"Start where you stand: the present. Name yourself, place yourself, say what fills your days. Menya zovut Emma. Ya iz Londona. Seychas ya zhivu v Moskve i uchus v universitete. Every tool here is one you've owned since A1 or built this month. Then add what you love — a tailed verb and the instrumental: ya interesuyus istoriey i muzykoy. A self-portrait without your interests is just an ID card. Notice there's no verb to be in the present — ya Emma, ya iz Londona — no am anywhere. That quiet gap is pure Russian, and by now it feels natural.","table":{"rows":[["Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Меня зовут Эмма.\">🔊</button>","My name is Emma."],["Я из Ло́ндона. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я из Лондона.\">🔊</button>","I'm from London. (из + genitive)"],["Я учу́сь в университе́те. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я учусь в университете.\">🔊</button>","I study at university."],["Я интересу́юсь исто́рией. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я интересуюсь историей.\">🔊</button>","I'm interested in history. (instrumental)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"My name is Emma, and I'm from London.","en":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма, и я из Ло́ндона.","say":"Меня зовут Эмма, и я из Лондона."},{"ru":"Now I live in Moscow and study at university.","en":"Сейча́с я живу́ в Москве́ и учу́сь в университе́те.","say":"Сейчас я живу в Москве и учусь в университете."},{"ru":"I'm interested in history and music.","en":"Я интересу́юсь исто́рией и му́зыкой.","say":"Я интересуюсь историей и музыкой."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я есть Э́мма из Ло́ндон.","right":"Я Э́мма из Ло́ндона.","why":"No “to be” in the present (drop есть), and “from” takes the genitive: Ло́ндон → <b>Ло́ндона</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm interested in music.” — Я интересу́юсь ___.","opts":["му́зыка","му́зыкой","му́зыку"],"answer":1,"explain":"интересова́ться takes the instrumental: <b>му́зыкой</b>."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I study at university” (я + study + at university):","accept":["я учусь в университете"],"placeholder":"я учу́сь…","explain":"<b>Я учу́сь в университе́те</b> — the tailed verb учи́ться plus в + prepositional."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“My name is Emma, I'm from London.”</b>","want":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма, я из Ло́ндона","answer":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма, я из Ло́ндона"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · My six cases in action","title":"One system, six jobs — the spine of your A2","paras":["Here's the backbone you built this month — the six cases, each answering its own question. You don't assemble them one lesson at a time anymore; you reach for whichever the meaning needs.","Watch each do its job: <b>кни́га бра́та</b> (whose — genitive), <b>пишу́ дру́гу</b> (to whom — dative), <b>чита́ю кни́гу</b> (what — accusative), <b>пишу́ ру́чкой</b> (with what — instrumental), <b>ду́маю о рабо́те</b> (about what — prepositional) — all anchored to <b>я</b> (who — nominative).","That reflex — feeling which ending a role needs — is what A2 gave you, more than any single table. It's why your word order can bend and the sentence still holds."],"audio":"Here's the backbone you built this month — the six cases, each answering its own question. You don't assemble them one lesson at a time anymore; you reach for whichever the meaning needs. Watch each do its job: kniga brata, whose, genitive; pishu drugu, to whom, dative; chitayu knigu, what, accusative; pishu ruchkoy, with what, instrumental; dumayu o rabote, about what, prepositional — all anchored to ya, who, nominative. That reflex — feeling which ending a role needs — is what A2 gave you, more than any single table.","table":{"rows":[["им. — кто? — <b>я</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я\">🔊</button>","nominative — the subject"],["род. — чего́? — кни́га <b>бра́та</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга брата\">🔊</button>","genitive — of / whose"],["дат. — кому́? — пишу́ <b>дру́гу</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пишу другу\">🔊</button>","dative — to whom"],["вин. — что? — чита́ю <b>кни́гу</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читаю книгу\">🔊</button>","accusative — the object"],["твор. — чем? — пишу́ <b>ру́чкой</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пишу ручкой\">🔊</button>","instrumental — with what"],["предл. — о чём? — ду́маю <b>о рабо́те</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"думаю о работе\">🔊</button>","prepositional — about what"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This is my brother's book.","en":"Э́то кни́га <b>бра́та</b>.","say":"Это книга брата.","gloss":"genitive — whose"},{"ru":"I'm writing to a friend with a pen.","en":"Я пишу́ <b>дру́гу</b> <b>ру́чкой</b>.","say":"Я пишу другу ручкой.","gloss":"dative + instrumental"},{"ru":"We're thinking about work.","en":"Мы ду́маем <b>о рабо́те</b>.","say":"Мы думаем о работе.","gloss":"prepositional — about"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я пишу́ друг.","right":"Я пишу́ дру́гу.","why":"“write to a friend” — the addressee is dative: друг → <b>дру́гу</b>. The ending, not a preposition, marks “to”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm writing to a friend.” — Я пишу́ ___.","opts":["друг","дру́га","дру́гу"],"answer":2,"explain":"The addressee goes in the dative: друг → <b>дру́гу</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“We're talking about work.” — Мы говори́м ___.","opts":["о рабо́те","о рабо́та","рабо́ту"],"answer":0,"explain":"“about” → <b>о</b> + prepositional: о рабо́те."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“This is my brother's book.”</b>","want":"Э́то кни́га бра́та","answer":"Э́то кни́га бра́та"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · My past and my plans","title":"Where I've been, where I'm going","paras":["A portrait gains depth when it moves through time. Behind you: the past, with its aspect — the backdrop (<b>жила́</b>) and the finished steps (<b>прие́хала</b>). Ahead: the future in two shapes — the ongoing plan (<b>бу́ду учи́ть</b>) and the promised result (<b>вы́учу</b>).","Thread them with yesterday's connectors: <b>Снача́ла я жила́ в Ло́ндоне, пото́м прие́хала в Москву́, а по́сле э́того го́да бу́ду рабо́тать здесь.</b> Past, present, plans — one line.","And that's everything: present, past, future; six cases; the tailed verbs; the words of duty and desire. Nothing left to introduce — only your own life to tell. Take the page and write it."],"audio":"A portrait gains depth when it moves through time. Behind you: the past, with its aspect — the backdrop, zhila, and the finished steps, priyekhala. Ahead: the future in two shapes — the ongoing plan, budu uchit, and the promised result, vyuchu. Thread them with yesterday's connectors: snachala ya zhila v Londone, potom priyekhala v Moskvu, a posle etogo goda budu rabotat zdes. Past, present, plans — one line. And that's everything: present, past, future; six cases; the tailed verbs; the words of duty and desire. Nothing left to introduce — only your own life to tell. Take the page and write it.","table":{"rows":[["Снача́ла я жила́ в Ло́ндоне. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сначала я жила в Лондоне.\">🔊</button>","past backdrop — imperfective"],["Пото́м я прие́хала в Москву́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Потом я приехала в Москву.\">🔊</button>","past step — perfective"],["Сейча́с я живу́ здесь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сейчас я живу здесь.\">🔊</button>","present — where I am"],["По́сле э́того го́да я бу́ду рабо́тать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"После этого года я буду работать.\">🔊</button>","future — my plan"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"I was born in London and lived there a long time.","en":"Я родила́сь в Ло́ндоне и до́лго жила́ там.","say":"Я родилась в Лондоне и долго жила там.","gloss":"до́лго = for a long time"},{"ru":"A year ago I came to Moscow.","en":"Год наза́д я прие́хала в Москву́.","say":"Год назад я приехала в Москву."},{"ru":"Next I want to work here and go on learning Russian.","en":"Да́льше я хочу́ рабо́тать здесь и учи́ть ру́сский.","say":"Дальше я хочу работать здесь и учить русский."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"За́втра я бу́ду прочита́ть кни́гу.","right":"За́втра я прочита́ю кни́гу.","why":"<b>бу́ду</b> only takes the imperfective. For a finished future result, use the perfective on its own: <b>прочита́ю</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Tomorrow I'll read the book (and finish it).” — За́втра я ___ кни́гу.","opts":["бу́ду чита́ть","прочита́ю","чита́ю"],"answer":1,"explain":"A finished future result → perfective <b>прочита́ю</b>. бу́ду чита́ть is the ongoing process."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud — with a little pride.","target":"Год наза́д я не зна́ла ру́сский, а тепе́рь зна́ю.","sub":"your own arc: a year ago I didn't know Russian, now I do","want":"год назад я не знала русский а теперь знаю"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"A year on, Emma and Ivan are back at Lena's café — where it all began. No goodbye this time. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Год наза́д ты почти́ не говори́ла по-ру́сски.","ru":"A year ago you hardly spoke Russian."},{"who":"A","en":"Да. Снача́ла бы́ло о́чень тру́дно.","ru":"Yes. At first it was really hard."},{"who":"B","en":"А тепе́рь ты говори́шь как ру́сская!","ru":"And now you speak like a Russian!"},{"who":"A","en":"Не как ру́сская. Но тепе́рь Москва́ — мой дом.","ru":"Not like a Russian. But now Moscow is my home."},{"who":"B","en":"И что да́льше? Ты остаёшься?","ru":"And what next? Are you staying? (остава́ться = to stay)"},{"who":"A","en":"По́сле э́того го́да я хочу́ рабо́тать здесь. У меня́ больши́е пла́ны.","ru":"After this year I want to work here. I have big plans."},{"who":"B","en":"Я знал, что ты вернёшься.","ru":"I knew you'd come back. (вернёшься = you'll return)"},{"who":"A","en":"Я обеща́ла — и я здесь. Э́то то́лько нача́ло.","ru":"I promised — and here I am. This is only the beginning."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for your own portrait — <b>о себе́</b>. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"о себе́","ru":"about myself, about oneself","ex":"Расскажи́ о себе́."},{"en":"жизнь","ru":"life","ex":"Э́то моя́ жизнь."},{"en":"семья́","ru":"family","ex":"У меня́ ма́ленькая семья́."},{"en":"рабо́та","ru":"work, job","ex":"Я иду́ на рабо́ту."},{"en":"учёба","ru":"studies","ex":"Учёба начина́ется в сентябре́."},{"en":"интере́сы","ru":"interests","ex":"У меня́ ра́зные интере́сы."},{"en":"пла́ны","ru":"plans","ex":"У меня́ больши́е пла́ны."},{"en":"бу́дущее","ru":"the future","ex":"Я ду́маю о бу́дущем."},{"en":"мечта́","ru":"dream","ex":"Э́то моя́ мечта́."},{"en":"родно́й язы́к","ru":"native language","ex":"Мой родно́й язы́к — англи́йский."},{"en":"по-ру́сски","ru":"in Russian","ex":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски."},{"en":"тепе́рь","ru":"now, by now","ex":"Тепе́рь Москва́ — мой дом."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — one last time.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["о себе́","about myself"],["Меня́ зову́т Э́мма","My name is Emma"],["я интересу́юсь му́зыкой","I'm interested in music"],["мой родно́й язы́к","my native language"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — a lap around the whole level. Take your time; you've earned every answer.","items":[{"q":"“There's no time.” — ___ вре́мени.","opts":["Нет","Не","Нельзя́"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I'm writing to my brother.” — Я пишу́ ___.","opts":["бра́та","бра́ту","бра́том"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I work as a doctor.” — Я рабо́таю ___.","opts":["врач","врача́","врачо́м"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I read the whole book (finished it).” — Я ___ кни́гу.","opts":["чита́л","прочита́л","чита́ю"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is right — “He studies well.”","opts":["Он у́чится хорошо́.","Он учи́ться хорошо́.","Он у́читься хорошо́."],"answer":0},{"q":"A woman says “I must work.”","opts":["Я до́лжен рабо́тать.","Я должна́ рабо́тать.","Мне до́лжен рабо́тать."],"answer":1},{"q":"“the book that I'm reading” — кни́га, ___ я чита́ю.","opts":["кото́рый","кото́рая","кото́рую"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I love Russia, so I came.” — Я люблю́ Росси́ю, ___ прие́хала.","opts":["потому́ что","поэ́тому","снача́ла"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's own «О себе́» — read it aloud, then write yours. Everything in it is Russian you can now make.","title":"О себе́","sentences":["Меня́ зову́т Э́мма, и мне два́дцать четы́ре го́да.","Я родила́сь в Ло́ндоне, но сейча́с живу́ в Москве́.","Я учу́сь в университе́те и интересу́юсь ру́сской исто́рией.","Год наза́д мне бы́ло тру́дно, но тепе́рь я говорю́ по-ру́сски ка́ждый день.","У меня́ есть друзья́, кото́рые живу́т здесь, и я о́чень их люблю́.","По́сле э́того го́да я хочу́ рабо́тать в Москве́ — э́то моя́ мечта́."],"translation":"My name is Emma, and I'm twenty-four years old. I was born in London, but now I live in Moscow. I study at university and I'm interested in Russian history. A year ago it was hard for me, but now I speak Russian every day. I have friends who live here, and I love them very much. After this year I want to work in Moscow — that's my dream."},"essay":{"intro":"The last page of the level — and it's about the only subject that's truly yours.","prompt":"Write your own «О себе́» — about yourself. Who are you and where are you from? Where do you live, study or work? What are you interested in? Where have you been, and where are you going? Pour in everything you've learned.","hint":"Move through time — <b>снача́ла / пото́м / тепе́рь / да́льше</b>. Reach for your cases, your aspect (backdrop vs finished step), your future, and a <b>кото́рый</b> or two. There's no rush and no single right answer — this page is yours 🙂","example":"Меня́ зову́т А́нна, и я из Ло́ндона. Снача́ла я жила́ там и учи́лась в шко́ле. Пото́м я начала́ учи́ть ру́сский, потому́ что о́чень люблю́ ру́сскую культу́ру. Год наза́д я прие́хала в Москву́ и познако́милась с людьми́, кото́рые тепе́рь мои́ друзья́. Снача́ла бы́ло тру́дно, но сейча́с ста́ло намно́го ле́гче. Днём я учу́сь в университе́те, а ве́чером рабо́таю в кафе́. Я интересу́юсь исто́рией и му́зыкой. По́сле э́того го́да я хочу́ оста́ться здесь и рабо́тать — э́то моя́ са́мая больша́я мечта́."}}};
